window.DATA = {"cert": {"id": "admin", "label": "Platform Administrator", "pass_pct": 68, "minutes": 105, "questions": 60}, "sections": [{"id": "config", "label": "Configuration et paramétrage", "weight": 15, "topics": [{"id": "adm_company", "title": "Paramètres de l'entreprise", "fiche": {"one_liner": "Company Settings define your org's regional defaults: Company Information, Default Locale, Language and Currency, Multiple Currencies (irreversible), Fiscal Year, Business Hours and Holidays.", "need": "A French manufacturer called Delmas Industries sells in France, Canada and the UK. It needs dates, numbers and currency shown correctly for each country, quotes in euros, Canadian dollars and pounds, a fiscal year that starts in April, and support case deadlines that count only working hours and skip public holidays.", "core": "Company Information holds the org's identity: name, address, primary contact, default locale, default language, default currency and time zone, plus license usage and the Organization ID. The default locale controls the format of dates, times, numbers and names. The default language controls the language of the interface and labels for users who have not chosen their own. Each user can override locale, language and time zone in their personal settings. Locale and language are separate: a user can see English labels with French date formats.\n\nMultiple Currencies is enabled with a checkbox (Activate Multiple Currencies) in Company Settings > Manage Currencies. Activation is irreversible: you cannot turn it off. Once active, you define the corporate currency (the currency used for reporting roll-ups and conversions), add active currencies, and set conversion rates. Each record can then carry its own currency, and users have a personal currency. Advanced Currency Management adds dated exchange rates, which matter for closed opportunities. It is a separate option that you enable after activating multicurrency. Dated exchange rates are not used for every object, so remember the idea rather than the detail.\n\nFiscal Year can be Standard or Custom. Standard follows the Gregorian calendar, and you only choose the start month and whether the year is named after its start or end year. Custom Fiscal Years are for 4-4-5 or 13-period calendars, or any year that is not a regular month-based year. They are enabled in Fiscal Year settings and defined in detail, and once you switch to custom, you cannot easily go back and the periods are managed manually.\n\nBusiness Hours define when your company works. Holidays are linked to Business Hours records. Together they drive Entitlement Management milestones, escalation rules and case escalation timing, so a deadline pauses outside working hours and on holidays.", "setup": ["Setup > Company Settings > Company Information: edit the default locale, language, currency, time zone and primary contact.", "Setup > Company Settings > Manage Currencies: activate Multiple Currencies, set the corporate currency, add currencies and conversion rates.", "Setup > Company Settings > Fiscal Year: choose Standard (start month) or Custom Fiscal Year.", "Setup > Company Settings > Business Hours: create business hours, then link holidays.", "Setup > Company Settings > Holidays: define each holiday (date, recurrence, affected business hours)."], "compare": [{"a": "Default Locale", "b": "Default Language", "diff": "Locale controls the format of dates, times, numbers and names. Language controls the text of the interface and labels. They are set independently."}, {"a": "Standard Fiscal Year", "b": "Custom Fiscal Year", "diff": "Standard is based on the Gregorian calendar with a chosen start month. Custom is for 4-4-5, 13-period or irregular calendars, and you define the periods yourself."}, {"a": "Multiple Currencies", "b": "Advanced Currency Management", "diff": "Multiple Currencies lets records use different currencies with one conversion rate each. Advanced Currency Management adds dated exchange rates, so past records keep the rate valid on their date."}], "signals": [{"if": "Sales in several countries and quotes in different currencies", "then": "Activate Multiple Currencies and set the corporate currency"}, {"if": "Fiscal year starts in a month other than January", "then": "Standard Fiscal Year with a custom start month"}, {"if": "4-4-5 or 13-period fiscal calendar", "then": "Custom Fiscal Year"}, {"if": "Support deadlines must exclude nights, weekends and public holidays", "then": "Business Hours and Holidays"}], "traps": ["Multiple Currencies cannot be deactivated once enabled, so a question asking how to undo it has no undo answer.", "Setting the default locale does not change the language of the interface, and the reverse is also true.", "A Holiday does nothing on its own: it only affects the Business Hours records it is linked to.", "Users can override locale, language and time zone personally, so the org default is only a starting point."], "scenario": {"situation": "A company with a head office in France opens a Canadian sales team that must quote in CAD. Reports must roll up in EUR. The CFO also asks whether you can switch off multicurrency after a three-month pilot if it does not work out.", "answer": "Activate Multiple Currencies, set EUR as the corporate currency, and add CAD with its conversion rate. Warn the CFO that activation is irreversible, so there is no switch-off after the pilot. Test in a sandbox first, because the change cannot be undone in production."}, "cards": [{"q": "What does Default Locale control?", "a": "The format of dates, times, numbers and names, not the language of the interface."}, {"q": "Can Multiple Currencies be deactivated after activation?", "a": "No. Activation is irreversible, so test in a sandbox first."}, {"q": "What is the corporate currency?", "a": "The currency used for roll-up and reporting conversions. Other currencies convert to it through conversion rates."}, {"q": "When do you use a Custom Fiscal Year?", "a": "When the fiscal calendar is not a standard month-based year, for example 4-4-5 or 13 periods."}, {"q": "How are Holidays used?", "a": "They are linked to Business Hours so that time-based processes, such as escalations and milestones, skip those days."}]}, "questions": [{"id": "630eb026180f42db8ab4c7f657d2ab69", "stem": "Northwind Outfitters sells in the United States and has just opened a subsidiary in Germany. Sales reps in both countries will use the same Salesforce org, and the company wants opportunities to be tracked in both USD and EUR with conversion rates between them. A system administrator is asked to prepare the org. What should the administrator keep in mind before taking action?", "options": ["Multiple currencies are enabled automatically when a user's locale is set to Germany (German).", "Multiple currencies require the Fiscal Year setting to be switched to Custom Fiscal Year first.", "Multiple currencies can be enabled and later disabled if the Germany subsidiary closes.", "Multiple currencies can be enabled in Company Information, but the activation cannot be undone once it is turned on."], "correct": [3], "explanations": ["Incorrect. A user's locale affects formats of dates, numbers and names, but it never enables multicurrency.", "Incorrect. Fiscal year settings are independent from currency settings, and no prerequisite links them.", "Incorrect. Once multicurrency is activated, it cannot be disabled, even if only one currency remains in use.", "Correct. Enabling multiple currencies is done in Company Settings and is irreversible, so the decision must be validated before activation."], "takeaway": "Activating multiple currencies is permanent, so confirm the need before enabling it."}, {"id": "614b61db1bba46ffba3fa7eb9c250fda", "stem": "Contoso Retail has a fiscal year that starts on February 1 and has quarters of 4, 4 and 5 weeks pattern variations that do not follow the calendar months. Sales leaders want forecasts and quarterly reports to align with these periods. Which setting should the administrator configure in Fiscal Year settings?", "options": ["Standard Fiscal Year, with the start month set to February", "Default Locale, changed to match the fiscal calendar", "Business Hours, with a start date set to February 1", "Custom Fiscal Year, defining the periods in a custom structure"], "correct": [3], "explanations": ["Incorrect. A Standard Fiscal Year follows the Gregorian calendar with months, so it cannot represent irregular 4-4-5 week periods.", "Incorrect. Locale controls formats such as dates and numbers, not fiscal periods.", "Incorrect. Business Hours define working times for support processes like Entitlements and Escalation Rules, not fiscal periods.", "Correct. A Custom Fiscal Year allows periods of varying lengths, such as week-based patterns, to match the company's reporting calendar."], "takeaway": "Use Custom Fiscal Year when fiscal periods are not based on standard calendar months."}, {"id": "02c3ff8cd145464e8f01705f0aba6446", "stem": "Fabrikam Support promises customers a response during weekdays from 8 AM to 6 PM. Cases should be escalated based on these working times, and the office is closed on national holidays. The administrator wants escalations not to count closed days. What should the administrator set up?", "options": ["A Validation Rule that blocks case updates on holidays", "Business Hours and a Holiday record associated with them", "A Default Time Zone for the company set to the office location", "A Fiscal Year with holidays entered as custom periods"], "correct": [1], "explanations": ["Incorrect. A Validation Rule would block users from working and does not affect escalation timing.", "Correct. Business Hours define the support schedule, and Holidays linked to them pause the clock for escalations and similar processes.", "Incorrect. The time zone helps interpret times, but it does not define working hours or closed days.", "Incorrect. Fiscal years are used for financial reporting and forecasting, not for support time calculations."], "takeaway": "Business Hours combined with Holidays define when support time counts for escalations and entitlements."}, {"id": "23aff1ca37b1446fb7d61fff24ac2606", "stem": "Adventure Works is rolling out Salesforce to teams in France and Canada. Users should see dates, times and numbers in their regional format, and the interface language should be French for the Paris team. Which two actions should the administrator take?", "options": ["Create a Custom Fiscal Year so dates display in the local format.", "Set the Locale on each user's record to the regional format that suits them.", "Set the Language on each user's record to French for the Paris team.", "Change the Default Currency in Company Information to Euro to switch the interface language.", "Edit Business Hours to apply French language labels to the interface."], "correct": [1, 2], "explanations": ["Incorrect. Fiscal year defines financial periods and has no effect on date display formats.", "Correct. Locale controls how dates, times, numbers and names are formatted for each user.", "Correct. The Language setting determines the language of the Salesforce interface for that user.", "Incorrect. Currency affects how monetary values are stored and displayed, not the language of the interface.", "Incorrect. Business Hours are about work schedules and do not control interface language."], "takeaway": "Locale controls regional formats and Language controls interface text, and both can be set per user."}, {"id": "2fb0db1bbef6478fa4d74266e43add53", "stem": "Tailspin Toys operates in several countries and has just enabled multiple currencies. Reports must show amounts in a consistent way, and sales reps in Japan will enter opportunities in JPY. Which two tasks does the administrator need to handle in the currency setup?", "options": ["Set Business Hours for each currency to control when exchange rates apply.", "Disable multiple currencies after the initial data load to simplify reporting.", "Define the corporate currency and add JPY as an active currency.", "Create one Fiscal Year per currency so that amounts are converted correctly.", "Maintain conversion rates between the corporate currency and other active currencies."], "correct": [2, 4], "explanations": ["Incorrect. Business Hours do not control exchange rates in any way.", "Incorrect. Multicurrency cannot be disabled once it has been enabled.", "Correct. The corporate currency is the base for conversions, and JPY must be added as an active currency so users can select it.", "Incorrect. Fiscal years are unrelated to currency conversion.", "Correct. Conversion rates must be maintained so that amounts convert to the corporate currency for reporting and roll-ups."], "takeaway": "After enabling multicurrency, set the corporate currency, activate the needed currencies and maintain conversion rates."}]}, {"id": "adm_users", "title": "Gérer les utilisateurs", "fiche": {"one_liner": "Create each user with a license and a profile, never delete them: deactivate or freeze them, transfer their records, use Login As to troubleshoot, and reset passwords from the user record.", "need": "A consulting firm hires a new analyst, and another consultant leaves the company. The admin must give the newcomer the right access on day one, then shut off the leaver's access immediately without losing their accounts, opportunities or history, and hand that work to a colleague. When the analyst reports a problem, the admin needs to see exactly what they see.", "core": "Every user needs a User license (which sets the pool of features available) and a profile (which sets baseline permissions and settings). The license determines which profiles can be assigned: a Salesforce license allows standard profiles such as Standard User, whereas a Salesforce Platform license is more limited. Extra access comes from permission sets (ensembles d'autorisations), not from creating many profiles. Each user also needs a unique username in email format, which must be unique across all Salesforce orgs, and an email address. A new user receives a welcome email with a link to set a password.\n\nUsers can never be deleted. Instead you deactivate them (clear the Active checkbox), which frees the license and blocks login. Records they own remain, and they still appear in reports and history. Deactivation can be blocked if the user is referenced in certain places, for example as the default workflow or case owner, or in some assignments. If you need to block access immediately but are not ready to deactivate, you freeze the user (Freeze button on the user record). Freezing prevents login but does not free the license. It is useful as a temporary step, for example while you reassign things that block deactivation.\n\nWhen a user leaves, ownership of their records is transferred. You can change owners with the Transfer Record tool or by editing the Owner field on records, and in some cases records are reassigned by list view or Data Loader. Login Access lets an admin log in as another user to reproduce a problem. The user must grant login access in their settings (Grant Account Login Access), unless the org has set \"Administrators Can Log in as Any User\". To reset a password, open the user and click Reset Password: Salesforce emails the user a link. Admins can also set a password directly, but the user's own reset is preferred for security.", "setup": ["Setup > Users > Users > New User: enter name, email, username, choose License and Profile, then save to send the welcome email.", "Setup > Users > Users, open the user, clear Active to deactivate, or click Freeze (in User Detail) to block login without freeing the license.", "Setup > Users > Users, open the user and click Login (visible when login access is allowed) to log in as that user, then Log out to return.", "Setup > Users > Users, open the user and click Reset Password to email a reset link. Password policies are in Setup > Security > Password Policies.", "Setup > Security > Login Access Policies: control whether administrators can log in as any user."], "compare": [{"a": "Deactivate a user", "b": "Freeze a user", "diff": "Deactivating clears Active, blocks login and frees the license. Freezing only blocks login temporarily and the license stays consumed."}, {"a": "License", "b": "Profile", "diff": "The license defines which features and profiles are available. The profile defines baseline permissions within that license. A profile can only be assigned if the license allows it."}, {"a": "Reset Password", "b": "Login As", "diff": "Reset Password sends the user a link to set a new password. Login As lets the admin see the org as that user, without knowing their password, for troubleshooting."}], "signals": [{"if": "Employee leaves, but their records and history must be kept", "then": "Deactivate the user (users cannot be deleted) and transfer their records"}, {"if": "Block access immediately while preparing to deactivate", "then": "Freeze the user"}, {"if": "Admin needs to see exactly what a user sees to reproduce an issue", "then": "Login As (Login Access)"}, {"if": "User forgot password", "then": "Reset Password from the user record"}], "traps": ["Deleting a user is not possible: the correct answer is always deactivate (or freeze).", "Freezing a user does not free their license; only deactivating does.", "Choosing a profile before the license: the license restricts which profiles are available.", "Login As requires the user to have granted access, unless the org allows administrators to log in as any user."], "scenario": {"situation": "A sales rep leaves the company today. The admin must stop their access immediately, keep their opportunities for reporting, free up their license for a replacement, and give their open opportunities to another rep.", "answer": "Freeze the user first if immediate blocking is needed and something prevents deactivation, then deactivate them to free the license, as users cannot be deleted. Transfer the open opportunities to the other rep by changing the owner. Historical data stays available for reporting."}, "cards": [{"q": "Can a Salesforce user be deleted?", "a": "No. Users can only be deactivated or frozen, which keeps their records and history intact."}, {"q": "What is the difference between freezing and deactivating a user?", "a": "Freezing blocks login but keeps the license in use. Deactivating blocks login and frees the license."}, {"q": "What two things must you choose when creating a user?", "a": "A license and a profile. The license determines which profiles can be selected."}, {"q": "When can an admin use Login As for a user?", "a": "When the user has granted login access, or when the org allows administrators to log in as any user."}, {"q": "How do you reset a user's password?", "a": "In Setup > Users > Users, open the user and click Reset Password. The user receives an email with a link to set a new one."}]}, "questions": [{"id": "e3c1763da9f44f83a2f02d79d10d40a2", "stem": "Northwind Outfitters has a sales representative, Maria, who has left the company. Her manager wants to make sure she can no longer log in, but Maria owns hundreds of open Opportunities and several Accounts that must be reassigned to her colleagues. The administrator also wants to keep the historical data. What should the administrator do?", "options": ["Delete Maria's user record from Setup and let Salesforce reassign her records automatically", "Deactivate Maria's user record and transfer her records to other users", "Change Maria's email address to an invalid one so she cannot receive login emails", "Remove Maria's permission set licenses and leave her user active"], "correct": [1], "explanations": ["Salesforce users cannot be deleted. Deletion is not an available option, and there is no automatic reassignment of records.", "Deactivating the user blocks login and releases the license while preserving history. Records can then be transferred to other users, so this meets every requirement.", "Changing the email does not prevent login. The user could still authenticate with the username and password, and this does not address record ownership.", "Removing permission set licenses does not stop the user from logging in, and it does not transfer ownership of the records."], "takeaway": "Users are never deleted in Salesforce: deactivate them to block access and free the license, then transfer their records."}, {"id": "70086a4f31e64bb283788c11a13a4b89", "stem": "Contoso Legal suspects that an employee's account may have been compromised. The administrator needs to immediately block this user from accessing Salesforce while the security team investigates, but the company does not want to free up the license or lose the user's role and assignments, since the employee will probably return to work shortly. Which action should the administrator take?", "options": ["Deactivate the user", "Freeze the user login", "Reset the user's password", "Change the user's profile to a profile with no object permissions"], "correct": [1], "explanations": ["Deactivating frees the license and is a heavier action. It is not the lightweight temporary block needed here, and it can be hard to reverse in a license-limited org.", "Freezing a user account prevents the user from logging in while the license stays consumed. It is the right option when you need immediate blocking, for example before deactivating or during an investigation.", "Resetting the password does not immediately stop active sessions or prevent the user from logging in again with the new password sent by email.", "Changing the profile removes access to data but does not block login, and it alters the user configuration, which the company wants to keep."], "takeaway": "Freeze a user to block login immediately without releasing the license, which is useful when you cannot deactivate right away."}, {"id": "6f4f73917c52451fa526663aaa09be68", "stem": "Fabrikam Consulting hires an external contractor who needs to view and edit only Accounts and Contacts for a three-month project. The administrator is creating the new user record. Which two items must be set on the user record before it can be saved?", "options": ["A license and a profile that is compatible with that license", "A role in the role hierarchy", "A manager in the Manager field", "A unique username in email address format", "A permission set group"], "correct": [0, 3], "explanations": ["Every user must have a user license, and a profile is required. The profile must be one that belongs to the selected license.", "The role is optional. A user without a role is still valid, though record visibility through the hierarchy will differ.", "The Manager field is optional and is used for things like approval routing, not for creating the user.", "The username must be unique across all Salesforce orgs and must be in email address format. It is a required field.", "Permission set groups are optional extras used to grant additional access beyond the profile."], "takeaway": "A new user requires at minimum a user license, a profile, and a globally unique username in email format, while role and manager are optional."}, {"id": "8a65488cdc314092ba0c7da6ff70ff4a", "stem": "Tailspin Retail's administrator receives a report from a sales user that a custom dashboard filter behaves oddly. To reproduce the issue exactly as the user sees it, the administrator wants to use the Login feature from the user's detail page in Setup. The administrator can see the Login link for some users but not for this one. Which two situations could explain this?", "options": ["The user is inactive", "The user has not been assigned a role", "The Administrators Can Log in as Any User setting is disabled and the user has not granted login access", "The user has no permission set assigned", "The user's profile has the API Enabled permission"], "correct": [0, 2], "explanations": ["The Login link is not available for inactive users, since you cannot log in as a deactivated account.", "A role is not required to log in as a user, so a missing role does not hide the link.", "If the org setting Administrators Can Log in as Any User is off, administrators can only log in as users who have granted login access, so the link is hidden otherwise.", "Permission sets are not required for the Login link to appear.", "API Enabled has no effect on whether an administrator can log in as the user."], "takeaway": "An administrator can log in as another user only if the org allows it or the user granted access, and only when that user is active."}, {"id": "73cbfdf98d09435e8315d54035149766", "stem": "Adventure Works has an employee who forgot her Salesforce password. The administrator opens her user record in Setup and wants to help her regain access. Which two statements about resetting the password are true?", "options": ["The administrator must delete and recreate the user to issue a new password", "The administrator can set a password directly for the user without sending an email", "The administrator must first freeze the user before a reset is possible", "The administrator can reset the password so that the user receives an email with a link to set a new one", "The administrator can view the user's current password in Setup"], "correct": [1, 3], "explanations": ["Users cannot be deleted, and recreating is never needed to reset a password.", "Administrators can use the Change Password option on the user record to set a password directly for the user, and no email is required.", "Freezing is not required for a password reset and would actually block login.", "Using Reset Password on the user record sends an email to the user with a link to create a new password. This is the standard method.", "Passwords are stored securely and are never visible to administrators."], "takeaway": "Administrators can reset a user's password by email or set a new one directly, but they can never see the existing password."}]}, {"id": "adm_login", "title": "Sécurité de connexion", "fiche": {"one_liner": "Login security controls who can log in, from where and when (profile login hours and IP ranges, org trusted IP ranges), how they prove identity (MFA, SSO, password policies) and how long sessions last (session settings, My Domain).", "need": "A consulting firm with 500 employees must protect client data. Back-office staff should only log in during office hours and from the office network. Everyone must prove their identity with more than a password, passwords must be strong and expire, idle sessions must close, and employees should sign in once with their corporate identity provider instead of managing a separate Salesforce password.", "core": "Two layers restrict access by context. On a profile, Login Hours block logins outside a set schedule: a user already logged in is kicked out when the window ends. Login IP Ranges on a profile are strict: a login from outside the range is denied outright. Trusted IP Ranges at the org level (Network Access) behave differently: logins from those IPs skip the identity verification challenge (verification code), but logins from other IPs are not blocked, they are just challenged. Important: the Login IP Ranges on the profile apply to every access, including API access, while Trusted IP Ranges only relax verification for the browser login flow.\n\nMulti-Factor Authentication (MFA) requires a second proof of identity beyond username and password, for example the Salesforce Authenticator app, a TOTP authenticator app, a security key (WebAuthn/U2F) or built-in authenticators such as Touch ID. Salesforce requires MFA for all users who log in through the UI with a username and password. It can be enforced through the permission \"Multi-Factor Authentication for User Interface Logins\", and an SSO login can satisfy MFA if the identity provider handles it. Password Policies (Setup) set the expiration period, minimum length, complexity, history and lockout after failed attempts. Session Settings control the session timeout (inactivity), whether sessions are locked to the IP address, and security levels.\n\nMy Domain gives the org its own URL (mycompany.my.salesforce.com). It is a prerequisite for single sign-on (SSO), which delegates authentication to an external identity provider using SAML or OpenID Connect, so users log in once with corporate credentials. It also lets you customize the login page and set login policies, and is required for deploying Lightning components.", "setup": ["Setup > Users > Profiles > select profile > Login Hours (set the allowed days and times)", "Setup > Users > Profiles > select profile > Login IP Ranges (define allowed IP ranges)", "Setup > Security > Network Access (Trusted IP Ranges for the org)", "Setup > Security > Password Policies, and Setup > Security > Session Settings", "Setup > Company Settings > My Domain, then Setup > Identity > Single Sign-On Settings"], "compare": [{"a": "Login IP Ranges on a profile", "b": "Trusted IP Ranges in Network Access (org)", "diff": "Profile Login IP Ranges deny logins from outside the range. Org Trusted IP Ranges only let users from those IPs skip the verification challenge and do not block anyone."}, {"a": "Login Hours", "b": "Session timeout", "diff": "Login Hours restrict when a user may be logged in at all, based on a schedule on the profile. Session timeout closes a session after a period of inactivity regardless of the time of day."}], "signals": [{"if": "Users may only log in from the office network, others must be blocked", "then": "Login IP Ranges on the profile"}, {"if": "Users may only access Salesforce during business hours", "then": "Login Hours on the profile"}, {"if": "Reduce verification prompts for users on the corporate network", "then": "Trusted IP Ranges (Network Access)"}, {"if": "Sign in with the corporate identity provider, one set of credentials", "then": "Single sign-on (SAML) with My Domain"}], "traps": ["Trusted IP Ranges do not block logins from other IPs, they only bypass the identity challenge. Blocking needs profile Login IP Ranges.", "Login Hours and Login IP Ranges are set per profile, not per user, and not on permission sets.", "An inactive user with a long session timeout is not a Login Hours problem: that is Session Settings.", "Password policies are org-wide settings; they do not apply to users who log in only via SSO, as the identity provider manages their passwords."], "scenario": {"situation": "A bank wants call center agents to log in only from the call center's IP range during their 8am to 6pm shift. Remote managers must still be able to log in anywhere. Agents all share one profile, managers another.", "answer": "On the agents' profile, set Login Hours (8am to 6pm) and Login IP Ranges (the call center range). Leave the managers' profile without those restrictions. Profile-level settings are enforced per profile, so the two groups can be treated differently, and the login is denied outside the allowed context."}, "cards": [{"q": "What happens when a user logs in from an IP outside the profile's Login IP Ranges?", "a": "The login is denied. The restriction is strict and also applies to API access."}, {"q": "What do org-level Trusted IP Ranges do?", "a": "Users logging in from those IPs are not asked for identity verification (verification code). Users from other IPs are not blocked, only challenged."}, {"q": "What does Multi-Factor Authentication add?", "a": "A second factor of identity (authenticator app, security key, built-in authenticator) beyond username and password."}, {"q": "Where do you set the session timeout?", "a": "Setup > Security > Session Settings. It closes the session after inactivity."}, {"q": "Why is My Domain linked to single sign-on?", "a": "My Domain gives the org a dedicated URL and is needed to configure SSO with an identity provider (SAML or OpenID Connect)."}]}, "questions": [{"id": "189b586e147547579f5cd1972558dc1c", "stem": "Northwind Logistics wants its warehouse employees to log in to Salesforce only during their shifts, from 6:00 AM to 6:00 PM. Other employees, such as managers, must keep unrestricted access at any time. The administrator wants to enforce this with minimal configuration. What should the administrator do?", "options": ["Add Trusted IP Ranges in Network Access for the warehouse employees", "Create a permission set that restricts login times and assign it to the warehouse employees", "Set Login Hours in the Session Settings for the whole organization", "Set Login Hours on the profile assigned to the warehouse employees"], "correct": [3], "explanations": ["Incorrect. Trusted IP Ranges control from where users can log in without identity verification, not when they can log in.", "Incorrect. Permission sets cannot restrict login hours. Login Hours are a profile setting.", "Incorrect. Session Settings control session timeout and security behavior, not time windows for login, and they apply organization-wide.", "Correct. Login Hours are defined on a profile, so only users with the warehouse profile are blocked outside the allowed time window and other profiles are unaffected."], "takeaway": "Login Hours and Login IP Ranges are restrictions configured on the profile, not on permission sets."}, {"id": "4b72fdec2c56474fa0aa5cdcf8647a15", "stem": "Contoso Energy has a sales team that works only from the corporate office. The security officer requires that these users can log in to Salesforce exclusively from the office network range 203.0.113.1 to 203.0.113.254. Remote users with other profiles must not be affected. What should the administrator configure?", "options": ["Enable Lock sessions to the IP address from which they originated in Session Settings", "Add the range to Trusted IP Ranges in the organization's Network Access settings", "Add the range as Login IP Ranges on the sales team's profile", "Require multi-factor authentication for the sales team's profile"], "correct": [2], "explanations": ["Incorrect. This setting ties an active session to the originating IP but does not restrict the addresses from which users may log in.", "Incorrect. Trusted IP Ranges let users from those addresses skip identity verification, but they do not block logins from other addresses.", "Correct. Login IP Ranges on a profile prevent users with that profile from logging in from any address outside the defined range.", "Incorrect. MFA adds a verification step but does not limit login to a specific network."], "takeaway": "Login IP Ranges on a profile block logins from outside the range, whereas organization Trusted IP Ranges only relax identity verification."}, {"id": "021e628caccf4de7864bd90f6c063295", "stem": "Fabrikam Consulting is adopting a corporate identity provider. Employees should sign in to Salesforce with their corporate credentials without a separate Salesforce password, and the company wants its own branded login URL such as fabrikam.my.salesforce.com. The administrator must prepare the org first. What should the administrator do?", "options": ["Configure My Domain, then set up SAML single sign-on with the identity provider", "Add the identity provider's addresses to Trusted IP Ranges and enable login with SSO", "Enable Delegated Authentication and share the default login.salesforce.com URL", "Create a Connected App for each employee and distribute the credentials"], "correct": [0], "explanations": ["Correct. My Domain provides the branded URL and is required to configure SSO, and SAML SSO lets employees authenticate through the corporate identity provider.", "Incorrect. Trusted IP Ranges do not provide federated authentication and there is no setting that enables SSO this way.", "Incorrect. Delegated Authentication is a different mechanism that requires development work, and the shared login URL does not give a branded login address.", "Incorrect. Connected Apps are not created per employee, and distributing credentials contradicts the goal of using corporate sign-in."], "takeaway": "Deploy My Domain before configuring SSO, since it gives the branded login URL and is required for single sign-on."}, {"id": "88a4fa5d89eb4a568c37cac9f601c48a", "stem": "Adventure Works wants to strengthen the security of its Salesforce org. The security team asks that users who log in with a Salesforce username and password must prove their identity with an additional verification factor. Which two actions meet this requirement?", "options": ["Set the password policy to require a minimum length of 20 characters", "Add the company network to Trusted IP Ranges for all users", "Enable the Require multi-factor authentication (MFA) for all direct UI logins to your Salesforce org setting in Session Settings", "Reduce the session timeout value to 15 minutes", "Assign the Multi-Factor Authentication for User Interface Logins permission to the users"], "correct": [2, 4], "explanations": ["Incorrect. A stronger password policy improves password strength but does not add a second verification factor.", "Incorrect. Trusted IP Ranges reduce verification prompts for users from those addresses, which is the opposite of the requirement.", "Correct. Salesforce provides a Session Settings option that requires MFA for all direct UI logins in the org.", "Incorrect. A shorter timeout ends inactive sessions sooner but does not require an additional factor at login.", "Correct. This permission, usually included in a permission set, requires MFA for the users who have it when they log in directly to the UI."], "takeaway": "MFA for direct UI logins is enforced through the user permission or the Session Settings option, not through password policies or IP settings."}, {"id": "7ece773a07aa4c71ad7f332f9e051657", "stem": "Tailspin Retail's security policy states that users must change their passwords regularly, cannot reuse recent passwords, and must have their account locked after repeated failed login attempts. The administrator needs to configure these requirements for all users in the org. Which two settings in Password Policies address this requirement?", "options": ["Trusted IP Ranges", "Lock sessions to the IP address from which they originated", "User passwords expire in", "Enforce password history", "Login Hours"], "correct": [2, 3], "explanations": ["Incorrect. Trusted IP Ranges are Network Access settings that affect identity verification, not password rules.", "Incorrect. This is a Session Settings option that protects active sessions, not a password policy.", "Correct. This Password Policies setting forces users to change their password after the chosen period.", "Correct. Enforce password history stops users from reusing a defined number of previous passwords.", "Incorrect. Login Hours is a profile setting that limits when users can log in and is unrelated to password rules."], "takeaway": "Password expiration, password history, complexity, and lockout behavior are all configured in Password Policies."}]}, {"id": "adm_perms", "title": "Profils, ensembles d'autorisations et groupes", "fiche": {"one_liner": "Each user has exactly one profile that sets the baseline access; permission sets and permission set groups add more, and muting permission sets subtract inside a group.", "need": "A company has 200 sales reps who all need the same basic access. Only 5 of them also need to export reports, and 2 managers need to delete accounts. Creating a separate profile for each variation becomes unmanageable. A minimal shared profile plus targeted permission sets gives each person exactly the access they need, and nothing more.", "core": "A profile is assigned to every user, and a user can have only one. It defines baseline settings: object permissions, field permissions, system permissions, app and tab visibility, page layout assignments, login hours and IP ranges. A permission set (ensembles d'autorisations) is a collection of permissions that is purely additive: it can grant access but never remove it. A user can have many permission sets, so they are the way to extend access for a subset of people.\n\nObject permissions are Create, Read, Edit and Delete (CRUD). Read is the foundation: Edit, Create and Delete all require Read. Two additional permissions work across all records of an object, regardless of sharing rules or the role hierarchy: View All (Afficher tout) and Modify All (Modifier tout). View All gives Read on every record, and Modify All gives Read, Edit, Delete and Transfer, and so also bypasses sharing. There are also the global equivalents View All Data and Modify All Data, which are system permissions. System permissions (for example Export Reports, Manage Users, API Enabled) control actions rather than data on an object.\n\nA permission set group (groupe d'ensembles d'autorisations) bundles several permission sets that match a job function, for example one group called Sales Manager. Assign the group to a user and they get everything in it. Inside a group you can add a muting permission set (ensemble de restrictions), which removes selected permissions, but only for the group it belongs to. It does not change the underlying permission sets or other assignments of the user.\n\nThe current best practice is to keep profiles minimal, with only the bare essentials such as login hours, IP ranges and default settings, and to grant the actual access through permission sets and groups. Salesforce is moving away from permissions on profiles, so new design should favor permission sets.", "setup": ["Setup > Users > Profiles: create or clone a profile and set object, field and system permissions, then assign it on the user record (one profile only).", "Setup > Users > Permission Sets: create a permission set, configure Object Settings and System Permissions, then use Manage Assignments to assign users.", "Setup > Users > Permission Set Groups: create a group, add permission sets, and check the group status (it must be Updated before use).", "In a permission set group, create a Muting Permission Set from the group's page and select the permissions to remove for that group only."], "compare": [{"a": "Profile", "b": "Permission set", "diff": "A profile is mandatory and unique per user and sets the baseline. A permission set is optional, can be assigned several times to a user, and only grants additional access."}, {"a": "Permission set group", "b": "Muting permission set", "diff": "A group bundles permission sets to grant access by job role. A muting permission set lives inside a group and subtracts permissions, but only for the users who get access through that group."}, {"a": "Modify All / View All (object level)", "b": "Modify All Data / View All Data (system level)", "diff": "The first pair applies to one object and bypasses sharing for it. The second pair applies to every object in the org."}], "signals": [{"if": "Only some users need an extra permission on top of their normal access", "then": "Permission set"}, {"if": "A job role needs a combination of several permission sets", "then": "Permission set group"}, {"if": "A group should have everything except one specific permission", "then": "Muting permission set inside the permission set group"}, {"if": "A user needs to see or edit all records of an object regardless of sharing", "then": "View All or Modify All on the object"}], "traps": ["A user can have only one profile, so \"assign two profiles\" is never the answer.", "Permission sets can only add access. You cannot use one to remove a permission granted by the profile.", "A muting permission set only works inside a permission set group, and only affects access obtained through that group.", "Edit, Create and Delete on an object require Read, and View All and Modify All override sharing settings for that object."], "scenario": {"situation": "All support agents share the Support profile. A new group of 10 senior agents must also delete cases and export reports. A few of them are on a team that must not export reports even though the rest of the seniors can. The admin wants to avoid creating new profiles.", "answer": "Create permission sets for deleting cases and exporting reports, bundle them into a Senior Agent permission set group, and assign it to the 10 agents. For the team that cannot export, add a muting permission set that removes Export Reports in a separate group. Profiles stay unchanged."}, "cards": [{"q": "How many profiles can a user have, and how many permission sets?", "a": "Exactly one profile and any number of permission sets or permission set groups."}, {"q": "Can a permission set remove access granted by a profile?", "a": "No. Permission sets are only additive. To remove access, change the profile or use a muting permission set inside a permission set group."}, {"q": "What does a muting permission set do?", "a": "It disables selected permissions within one permission set group, without altering the underlying permission sets or the user's other assignments."}, {"q": "What is the difference between Modify All on an object and Modify All Data?", "a": "Modify All on an object gives full access to all records of that one object, ignoring sharing. Modify All Data does so for every object in the org."}, {"q": "Why does current best practice favor minimal profiles?", "a": "Permission sets and groups are reusable and flexible, so access is granted in small, targeted pieces rather than through many near-duplicate profiles."}]}, "questions": [{"id": "2ebd29cf778042d2a7d5fe5b837d4b84", "stem": "Northwind Logistics has 200 sales reps who all use the Sales Rep profile. The sales director asks that 15 of these reps also be able to delete Opportunity records. No other user should gain this ability. The company wants to keep the number of profiles low. What should the administrator do?", "options": ["Create a second profile cloned from Sales Rep with Delete on Opportunity and assign it to the 15 reps as an additional profile", "Create a role for the 15 reps and grant Delete on Opportunity through the role hierarchy", "Create a permission set that grants Delete on Opportunity and assign it to the 15 reps", "Grant Delete on Opportunity in the Sales Rep profile and use sharing rules to limit who can delete"], "correct": [2], "explanations": ["Incorrect. A user can have only one profile, so a second profile cannot be assigned as an additional profile.", "Incorrect. Roles influence record visibility through the hierarchy, not object-level permissions such as Delete.", "Correct. Permission sets are additive and extend access for selected users without changing their profile or adding profiles.", "Incorrect. Changing the profile gives Delete to all 200 reps, and sharing rules grant record access, not object permissions such as Delete."], "takeaway": "Use permission sets to add object permissions to a subset of users, because each user has exactly one profile."}, {"id": "f8bc61d2d7ac4eda885763a89477a073", "stem": "Contoso Health created a permission set group named Service Manager that combines four permission sets. One of them, Case Tools, grants Delete on Case. Managers assigned to the group must keep every other permission from the group but must not be able to delete cases. What should the administrator do?", "options": ["Remove Case Tools from the group and recreate its other permissions in a new profile", "Deactivate the permission set group and assign the four permission sets individually", "Edit the Case Tools permission set to remove Delete on Case, since the group inherits it", "Create a muting permission set in the group that mutes Delete on Case"], "correct": [3], "explanations": ["Incorrect. This is heavy and unnecessary, and profiles cannot be added to a group to replace a permission set.", "Incorrect. Individual assignment brings back Delete on Case, since nothing mutes it, and defeats the purpose of the group.", "Incorrect. Editing Case Tools would affect every other group or user that uses it, not just this group.", "Correct. A muting permission set inside a permission set group disables selected permissions for that group only, without changing the underlying permission sets."], "takeaway": "A muting permission set in a permission set group removes specific permissions for that group without altering the original permission sets."}, {"id": "a933e966beaa4d2e8abfccf7552011d4", "stem": "At Fabrikam Retail, an analyst named Priya needs to view all Account records in the org for reporting, regardless of ownership or sharing settings. She must not be able to edit or delete any of them. Her profile gives Read access on Account. Which permission should the administrator grant to her?", "options": ["Modify All on the Account object", "View All Data system permission", "Read on Account together with the Transfer Records permission", "View All on the Account object"], "correct": [3], "explanations": ["Incorrect. Modify All also allows editing and deleting all Account records, which exceeds the requirement.", "Incorrect. View All Data would expose every object in the org, which is broader than the need for Account only.", "Incorrect. Transfer Records changes ownership and does not grant visibility of records owned by others.", "Correct. View All on an object gives read access to all records of that object regardless of sharing, without edit or delete rights."], "takeaway": "Use the object-level View All permission for read-only access to all records of one object, and reserve View All Data and Modify All Data for org-wide needs."}, {"id": "3eb819c6ecdb4b9fa191f5708d4f0daa", "stem": "Adventure Works is redesigning its security model. The architect proposes that every user gets a minimal-access profile and that additional access be granted in a way that is easy to reuse and maintain. Which two approaches align with this recommended practice? (Choose two.)", "options": ["Bundle related permission sets into permission set groups aligned with job functions", "Create one profile per job title that contains all object and field permissions that title requires", "Clone the System Administrator profile for each department and remove permissions that are not needed", "Give all users the same profile with every permission enabled and restrict access with page layouts", "Assign a minimal profile to users and grant job-specific access through permission sets"], "correct": [0, 4], "explanations": ["Correct. Permission set groups package permission sets by job function, making assignment simpler and consistent.", "Incorrect. A profile per job title leads to profile sprawl and is hard to maintain, which minimal profiles are meant to avoid.", "Incorrect. Starting from a powerful profile and subtracting is error-prone and increases the risk of excess access.", "Incorrect. Page layouts control the UI, not security, so users would still hold every permission.", "Correct. Minimal profiles plus permission sets keep the baseline small and add access only where needed."], "takeaway": "The recommended approach is minimal profiles for baseline access, with permission sets and permission set groups granting additional access."}, {"id": "b9b67521a7494c1d9520c8d443bf72db", "stem": "Tailspin Energy wants to let a team lead named Marcus reset passwords and unlock users on his team, and also manage who can access a specific app. The administrator wants to give Marcus these capabilities without changing his profile and without making him a full System Administrator. Which two actions can the administrator take? (Choose two.)", "options": ["Add Marcus to a role higher in the hierarchy so he inherits administrative abilities", "Assign Marcus a second profile that includes the Reset User Passwords and Unlock Users permission", "Include the system permission in a permission set group and assign the group to Marcus", "Enable Modify All Data on Marcus's profile so he can manage users", "Create a permission set that enables the Reset User Passwords and Unlock Users system permission and assign it to Marcus"], "correct": [2, 4], "explanations": ["Incorrect. Roles control record visibility, not system permissions for user management.", "Incorrect. A user can have only one profile, so a second profile cannot be assigned.", "Correct. A permission set group containing a permission set with that system permission also grants it to the assigned user.", "Incorrect. Modify All Data is far broader than needed and does not target the stated requirement, and it changes the profile.", "Correct. System permissions such as Reset User Passwords and Unlock Users can be granted through a permission set without changing the profile."], "takeaway": "System permissions can be added through permission sets or permission set groups, so a profile change or a full admin profile is not required."}]}, {"id": "adm_fls", "title": "Sécurité au niveau du champ", "fiche": {"one_liner": "Field-level security (FLS) controls whether a field is Visible or Read-Only per profile or permission set, and it applies everywhere: page layouts, reports, search, list views and the API.", "need": "At a staffing company, recruiters must see a candidate's Expected Salary, but consultants working on the same Candidate records must not see it at all. Hiding it only on the page would still leave it exposed in reports and the API. FLS blocks access to the field itself, for every access path.", "core": "Field-level security (FLS) is a per-field setting that defines, for each profile or permission set, whether the field is Visible (read access) and whether it is Read-Only (no edit). If Visible is unchecked, the user cannot see the field anywhere. If Visible is checked and Read-Only is checked, the user can see but not change it. If Visible is checked and Read-Only is unchecked, the user can read and edit.\n\nThe key point for the exam is the scope. FLS is enforced across the whole platform: record pages, reports and dashboards, list views, global search, related lists, Data Loader and the APIs, and (by default) Apex run in user mode. A page layout, by contrast, only controls what appears on the record page (the layout of fields and sections) and whether a field is Required or Read-Only on that page. Removing a field from a layout does not secure it: the user may still see it in a report or query it through the API.\n\nWhen FLS and the page layout conflict, the most restrictive setting wins. A field that is Editable in FLS but Read-Only in the layout is read-only on the page. A field that is on the layout but not Visible in FLS simply does not display. To grant access, the field must be allowed by FLS and placed on the layout (or the user will not see it on the page, though it remains accessible elsewhere).\n\nBest practice: keep the base profile restrictive and grant field access additively with permission sets (permission sets can only add access, never remove it). Note that FLS does not control which records a user sees: that is the role of the sharing model (OWD, roles, sharing rules). Also, a field required by the system (e.g. a universally required field) cannot be hidden through FLS.", "setup": ["Setup > Object Manager > [Object] > Fields & Relationships > [Field] > Set Field-Level Security (sets Visible and Read-Only for all profiles at once)", "Setup > Users > Profiles > [Profile] > Object Settings > [Object] > Field Permissions (Read / Edit per field for one profile, in the enhanced profile UI)", "Setup > Users > Permission Sets > [Permission Set] > Object Settings > [Object] > Field Permissions (grants additional Read or Edit access)", "To check the result: Setup > Object Manager > [Object] > Fields & Relationships > [Field] > View Field Accessibility, or Setup > Users > [User] > Field Accessibility"], "compare": [{"a": "Field-level security", "b": "Page layout (présentation de page)", "diff": "FLS secures the field everywhere (reports, search, API, list views). A page layout only changes what shows on the record page, so it is not a security control."}, {"a": "Field-level security", "b": "Object permissions (Read, Create, Edit, Delete) and sharing settings", "diff": "Object permissions decide what a user can do with the object, sharing decides which records they see, and FLS decides which fields within those records are visible or editable."}], "signals": [{"if": "Hide a sensitive field (salary, SSN, discount cost) from certain users everywhere, including reports", "then": "Field-level security (uncheck Visible) via profile or permission set"}, {"if": "Users may see a field but must not change it", "then": "Field-level security: Visible with Read-Only checked (or Read without Edit in a permission set)"}, {"if": "Give a few users from different profiles access to one extra field", "then": "Permission set with field permissions"}, {"if": "Reorganize which fields appear on the record page, or make a field required only on the page", "then": "Page layout, not FLS"}], "traps": ["Removing a field from the page layout does not protect it: it can still appear in reports, list views, search and API calls if FLS allows it.", "Field-level security does not control record access: seeing a record is decided by OWD, roles and sharing rules, not by FLS.", "Permission sets can only grant access: you cannot use one to remove field access given by the profile.", "When FLS and the layout disagree, the most restrictive wins: Edit in FLS but Read-Only on the layout means read-only on the page."], "scenario": {"situation": "A company adds a Discount Cost field on Opportunity. Sales Reps must not see it, but Sales Managers must see and edit it. An admin removes the field from the Sales Rep page layout, yet a rep builds a report and displays the field.", "answer": "Layout removal only hides the field on the page. Set field-level security so Visible is unchecked for the Sales Rep profile, and give Sales Managers Visible and editable access (via their profile or a permission set). FLS applies to reports, search and the API, so the field disappears for reps everywhere."}, "cards": [{"q": "What two settings does field-level security offer per profile or permission set?", "a": "Visible (the user can see the field) and Read-Only (the user can see but not edit it). If Visible is unchecked, the field is hidden entirely."}, {"q": "Why is a page layout not a security tool for a field?", "a": "It only controls the record page display. A field removed from the layout can still be seen in reports, list views, search and API if FLS grants access."}, {"q": "Where do you set FLS for one field across all profiles?", "a": "Setup > Object Manager > [Object] > Fields & Relationships > [Field] > Set Field-Level Security."}, {"q": "Where do you set FLS for one profile across many fields?", "a": "In the profile, under Object Settings > [Object] > Field Permissions, or in a permission set for additional access."}, {"q": "Field is Edit in FLS but Read-Only on the page layout. What can the user do on the record page?", "a": "Only read it: the most restrictive setting wins."}]}, "questions": [{"id": "6363e66b95b048a89e2e99da39edc798", "stem": "Northwind Logistics stores a Credit Score field on the Account object. Sales reps must no longer see this field anywhere in Salesforce, including reports, list views, global search results, and API integrations. A page layout change has already been tested by an admin. What should the administrator do to meet the requirement?", "options": ["Set the field to not visible for the Sales Rep profile using field-level security", "Add the field to a compact layout that sales reps cannot edit", "Create a Lightning page that hides the field with component visibility filters", "Remove the field from the Account page layout assigned to the Sales Rep profile"], "correct": [0], "explanations": ["Field-level security controls access to the field everywhere: record pages, reports, list views, search and API. Making it not visible for the profile satisfies the requirement.", "Compact layouts control which fields appear in highlights and mobile views. They do not restrict access to data.", "Component visibility filters on a Lightning page only affect what appears on that page. They do not enforce security on the field.", "Removing the field from the page layout only hides it on the record page. Reps could still see it in reports, search and through the API."], "takeaway": "Page layouts only control the UI on a record page, while field-level security enforces access across reports, search, list views and the API."}, {"id": "b72cb4a2cda0446586fb4c53cf7c7e2b", "stem": "Contoso Health wants all employees in the Support Agent profile to see the Contract Value field on Case as read-only. A few Support Managers using the same profile must be able to edit it. The admin wants to avoid creating a new profile. What is the best approach?", "options": ["Use a validation rule to block edits by anyone who is not a manager", "Make the field read-only on the page layout and let managers use a different layout", "Set the field to read-only in the Support Agent profile and give managers a permission set that grants Edit access to the field", "Set the field to read-only in the profile and clone the profile for managers"], "correct": [2], "explanations": ["A validation rule can block saves but it does not show the field as read-only and is not field-level security. It is a workaround for a need that FLS handles directly.", "A read-only page layout setting is not security. Users could still edit the field through the API, data import or other tools.", "The profile sets the baseline of read-only access, and the permission set adds Edit access for managers only. Permissions are additive, so this meets the need without a new profile.", "This works but requires creating a new profile, which the admin wants to avoid and which adds maintenance."], "takeaway": "Use the profile for baseline field access and permission sets to grant additional field access to specific users."}, {"id": "b09e4ae68ef245a98fb6912f78e5e929", "stem": "A Salesforce admin at Fabrikam sets the Discount Approved field on Opportunity to Visible but not Read-Only... Actually the field is marked Read-Only on the page layout for the Sales profile, and field-level security for that profile grants Edit access. A sales rep needs to update this field in bulk using Data Import Wizard. What will happen?", "options": ["The import can update the field because field-level security grants Edit access", "The import will fail because the page layout makes the field read-only", "The import can update the field only if the user also has the Modify All Data permission", "The import will ignore the field because Data Import Wizard respects page layouts"], "correct": [0], "explanations": ["Page layout read-only only applies to the UI. Because field-level security grants Edit access, the user can update the field through the import.", "Read-only on a page layout affects only the record page UI, not imports.", "Modify All Data is not required. Edit access from field-level security together with object permissions is enough.", "Data Import Wizard does not evaluate page layouts. It relies on object and field permissions."], "takeaway": "A field that is read-only on the page layout can still be edited through imports or the API if field-level security allows Edit."}, {"id": "b39b5e7173dc4451a4f81c9e228df42c", "stem": "Adventure Works stores a Salary Band field on the Contact object. The admin must make sure that only HR users can read this field. Which two actions can the administrator use to configure field-level security for the field? (Choose the two that apply.)", "options": ["Remove the field from the Contact search layout", "Change the organization-wide default for Contact to Private", "Edit the field's access in the Profile settings under Field-Level Security for the object", "From Object Manager, open the field and select Set Field-Level Security", "Edit the Contact page layout and select Read-Only on the field"], "correct": [2, 3], "explanations": ["Search layouts control which columns appear in search results. They do not secure the field.", "Organization-wide defaults control record access, not access to individual fields.", "Correct. Field-level security can also be configured from the profile (or a permission set), where you set access per object and field.", "Correct. In Object Manager, the Set Field-Level Security option on a field lets you set visibility and read-only access per profile.", "Read-only on the page layout is not field-level security and does not apply in reports or the API."], "takeaway": "Field-level security is set from the field in Object Manager, or from a profile or permission set, not from layouts or sharing settings."}, {"id": "a939afcfef454aab85f4f93e6bcd386f", "stem": "Litware wants the Annual Revenue field on Account to be hidden from users of the Partner Community profile. The admin proposes several changes. Which two statements about field-level security are true for this requirement? (Choose the two that apply.)", "options": ["If the field is not visible through field-level security, it will not appear in reports for those users", "If a profile has no access, a permission set assigned to a user can still grant Read access to the field", "Hiding the field on the page layout is enough to keep it out of reports", "Field-level security applies to the profile only and cannot be changed with permission sets", "Field-level security is only enforced on record detail pages"], "correct": [0, 1], "explanations": ["Correct. Without field access, users cannot see the field in reports, list views, search or the API.", "Correct. Permissions are additive, so a permission set can grant Read access even when the profile does not, which the admin must keep in mind.", "A page layout only affects the record page. The field could still be used in reports if FLS allows access.", "Permission sets can grant field permissions in addition to the profile.", "Field-level security is enforced across the platform, not only on detail pages."], "takeaway": "Field-level security is enforced everywhere and is additive, so profiles and permission sets together determine a user's final field access."}]}, {"id": "adm_owd", "title": "Paramètres par défaut de l'organisation et hiérarchie des rôles", "fiche": {"one_liner": "Organization-Wide Defaults (OWD) set the most restrictive record access baseline; the role hierarchy, sharing rules and manual sharing then open access up, never restrict it.", "need": "A company's sales reps should not see each other's opportunities, yet each sales manager must see the whole team's deals. Administrators set the baseline with Organization-Wide Defaults (OWD, 'paramètres de partage par défaut de l'organisation') at Private, then rely on the role hierarchy so managers automatically see their reports' records.", "core": "Organization-Wide Defaults define the baseline level of access to records for each object, for users who do not own the record. The options are Private, Public Read Only, Public Read/Write, and Controlled by Parent (for detail objects in a master-detail relationship, where access follows the parent record). Some objects also offer Public Read/Write/Transfer (Leads, Cases) and Public Full Access (Campaigns). The golden rule: OWD is the most restrictive level in the sharing model. Other tools can only grant more access, never less.\n\nOWD has two settings: Default Internal Access (for employees) and Default External Access (for external users such as community or portal users, and guests). External access can never be more permissive than internal access. The record owner always has full access to their own records, whatever the OWD.\n\nThe role hierarchy opens access upward: a user in a higher role can view, and with Read/Write access can edit, the records owned by users below them in the hierarchy. It never gives access to peers or downward. For standard objects, this is always on. For custom objects, the setting Grant Access Using Hierarchies can be unchecked, so that managers do not automatically see those records. Note that it is a per-object option, found on the OWD page.\n\nA role is not a profile. Roles control record visibility (which records). Profiles control what a user can do (object permissions, field permissions, apps, system permissions). Every user must have a profile, but a role is optional.", "setup": ["Setup > Security > Sharing Settings > Organization-Wide Defaults: click Edit, choose Default Internal Access and Default External Access per object.", "On the same page, check or uncheck Grant Access Using Hierarchies for each custom object (standard objects always use the hierarchy).", "Setup > Users > Roles > Set Up Roles: build the role hierarchy by dragging and adding roles.", "Setup > Users > Users: assign each user a Role (for record visibility) and a Profile (for permissions)."], "compare": [{"a": "Role", "b": "Profile", "diff": "A role determines which records a user can see through the hierarchy (record access). A profile determines what the user can do: object and field permissions, apps, tabs. A profile is mandatory, a role is optional."}, {"a": "Organization-Wide Defaults", "b": "Sharing rules", "diff": "OWD sets the most restrictive baseline for record access. Sharing rules are exceptions that open access to specific groups, roles or criteria. They can only extend access, never reduce it."}, {"a": "Default Internal Access", "b": "Default External Access", "diff": "Internal applies to employees, external to community or portal users and guests. External access must be equal to or more restrictive than internal access."}], "signals": [{"if": "Users must not see each other's records, but managers must see their team's", "then": "OWD Private plus the role hierarchy"}, {"if": "Child records should inherit the visibility of the parent (master-detail)", "then": "OWD Controlled by Parent"}, {"if": "Everyone can see all records but only the owner can edit", "then": "OWD Public Read Only"}, {"if": "Managers should not automatically see records of a custom object", "then": "Uncheck Grant Access Using Hierarchies for that object"}], "traps": ["OWD is the most restrictive level: you cannot use a sharing rule or the hierarchy to make access tighter than OWD.", "The role hierarchy opens access upward only, never to peers or to subordinates of other branches.", "Roles do not grant object or field permissions: that is the job of profiles and permission sets.", "Grant Access Using Hierarchies can be disabled only for custom objects; standard objects always follow the hierarchy."], "scenario": {"situation": "A company stores Invoice records in a custom object. OWD is Private. Regional sales reps must see only their own invoices, but the Finance director, placed high in the role hierarchy, must not automatically see every invoice. What should the admin do?", "answer": "Edit the OWD for the Invoice object and uncheck Grant Access Using Hierarchies. This option exists for custom objects, so higher roles no longer inherit access. The Finance director can then be given access through a sharing rule or another explicit sharing mechanism."}, "cards": [{"q": "What are the four main OWD options?", "a": "Private, Public Read Only, Public Read/Write, and Controlled by Parent (the last for detail objects in a master-detail relationship)."}, {"q": "Can a sharing rule make access more restrictive than OWD?", "a": "No. OWD is the most restrictive baseline. Sharing rules, the hierarchy and manual sharing can only open access further."}, {"q": "In which direction does the role hierarchy grant access?", "a": "Upward: users higher in the hierarchy can access the records of users below them, not the reverse and not across branches."}, {"q": "What does Grant Access Using Hierarchies do?", "a": "It lets the role hierarchy give managers access to records of a custom object. It can be unchecked per custom object in the OWD settings."}, {"q": "What is the difference between a role and a profile?", "a": "A role controls record visibility through the hierarchy. A profile controls permissions (objects, fields, apps, system). A profile is required, a role is optional."}]}, "questions": [{"id": "a30c04fe16a84baab417ef5ed997962b", "stem": "Northwind Logistics stores shipment quotes in the standard Opportunity object. The organization-wide default for Opportunity is Private. A sales rep in the Regional Sales role needs to see the opportunities owned by the rep in the Account Executive role, who reports directly to the Regional Sales role in the hierarchy (Regional Sales is the parent role). Both users have the same profile. What explains why the Regional Sales rep can see these records?", "options": ["The shared profile grants both users access to each other's Opportunity records.", "The role hierarchy gives users in higher roles access to records owned by users below them in the hierarchy.", "Opportunity is Controlled by Parent, so access follows the Account owner.", "The Private default allows users to see records owned by peers in the same profile."], "correct": [1], "explanations": ["Incorrect. Profiles control object permissions and field access, not which individual records a user can see.", "Correct. With the role hierarchy, users higher in the hierarchy inherit read and edit access to records owned by users below them, even when the default is Private.", "Incorrect. Opportunity is not Controlled by Parent in this scenario, and the stem states its default is Private.", "Incorrect. Private means only the owner and users above the owner in the role hierarchy can access the records, not peers."], "takeaway": "The role hierarchy opens record access upward, while profiles only control object and field permissions."}, {"id": "015d132d8cd242e5add4f2710930b5a8", "stem": "Contoso Energy created a custom object called Inspection. The organization-wide default is Private. Managers in senior roles must automatically see Inspection records owned by users who report to them in the role hierarchy, but they currently cannot. The administrator confirmed that the managers' profiles have Read access on the object. What should the administrator do?", "options": ["Change the managers' profiles to include View All Data.", "Select Grant Access Using Hierarchies for the Inspection object in the sharing settings.", "Set the Inspection default external access to Public Read Only.", "Create a new role for each manager under the Inspection owners."], "correct": [1], "explanations": ["Incorrect. View All Data would expose every record in the org to these managers, far beyond the hierarchy-based need.", "Correct. For custom objects, Grant Access Using Hierarchies can be disabled, and enabling it lets users in higher roles access records of subordinates.", "Incorrect. Public Read Only would open records to everyone, not only to managers above the owners, and external access is a separate setting.", "Incorrect. Adding roles does not fix the missing hierarchy access setting and would complicate the role structure."], "takeaway": "For custom objects, Grant Access Using Hierarchies must be selected for the role hierarchy to give managers access to subordinates' records."}, {"id": "af498ff291f1485f8efbfacef3cb11f3", "stem": "Fabrikam Services wants all internal employees to view every Account record, but only the owner and users above the owner in the role hierarchy should be able to edit them. Which organization-wide default for Account meets this requirement for internal users?", "options": ["Private", "Controlled by Parent", "Public Read/Write", "Public Read Only"], "correct": [3], "explanations": ["Incorrect. Private would prevent most employees from viewing Accounts they do not own.", "Incorrect. Controlled by Parent is not available for Account since it is not a detail object in a master-detail relationship.", "Incorrect. Public Read/Write lets all internal users edit every Account, which violates the edit restriction.", "Correct. Public Read Only lets all internal users view records, while edit access is limited to the owner and users above in the role hierarchy, plus any additional sharing."], "takeaway": "Public Read Only gives everyone view access, and edit stays with the owner and those above in the hierarchy."}, {"id": "5f903fd7aa284921a3b59616ccc8e1c6", "stem": "Adventure Works tracks Invoice Line Items as a custom object that is the detail side of a master-detail relationship with the Invoice object. The administrator wants access to a line item to depend entirely on a user's access to its parent invoice. Which two statements are true about this configuration?", "options": ["The administrator can set the Invoice Line Item default to Private independently of Invoice.", "Sharing rules can be created directly on the Invoice Line Item object.", "The line item owner field determines who can see each record.", "Users who can edit the parent Invoice can edit its line items.", "The organization-wide default for Invoice Line Item is set to Controlled by Parent."], "correct": [3, 4], "explanations": ["Incorrect. A master-detail detail object cannot have its own independent default; it is controlled by the parent.", "Incorrect. Sharing rules cannot be created on detail objects, since sharing is controlled by the master record.", "Incorrect. Detail records in master-detail have no owner field; access comes from the parent.", "Correct. Edit access on the parent grants the corresponding access to the detail records, since sharing is inherited from the master.", "Correct. A detail object in a master-detail relationship uses Controlled by Parent, so access derives from the master record."], "takeaway": "Detail objects in a master-detail relationship use Controlled by Parent, so access follows the master record and cannot be set separately."}, {"id": "e15911f31dfa4ec4be2f7cf5971239af", "stem": "Tailspin Retail is tightening security. The Case organization-wide default is Private. A support director wants to be sure the administrator understands what roles do before restructuring. Which two statements about roles and profiles are accurate for this org?", "options": ["Every user must be assigned exactly one profile, but assigning a role is optional.", "Removing a user from a role also removes the user's profile permissions.", "Roles determine record visibility through the hierarchy, while profiles determine object permissions and field access.", "Assigning a user to a higher role automatically grants that user a more permissive profile.", "A user's role determines which objects the user can create."], "correct": [0, 2], "explanations": ["Correct. A profile is required for every user, while a role is optional, though without one the user gets no hierarchy-based access.", "Incorrect. Profile permissions remain unchanged when the role is removed; only hierarchy-based record access is affected.", "Correct. Roles open record access upward in the hierarchy, whereas profiles control object permissions, field permissions, and other user permissions.", "Incorrect. Roles and profiles are independent, so a higher role does not change profile permissions.", "Incorrect. Object-level create permission comes from the profile or permission sets, not from the role."], "takeaway": "A profile is required and controls what users can do, while a role is optional and influences which records users can see."}]}, {"id": "adm_sharing", "title": "Règles de partage, partage manuel, équipes", "fiche": {"one_liner": "Sharing rules, manual sharing and teams only open up record access beyond the organization-wide defaults and role hierarchy; they never remove access. Restriction rules are the tool for narrowing it.", "need": "A consulting firm sets Accounts to Private so reps see only their own. But the Finance team must read every Account owned by Sales, and a rep sometimes needs a colleague's help on one big deal. Sharing rules cover the standing need, manual sharing and teams cover the one-off cases.", "core": "Start from the baseline: organization-wide defaults (OWD) set the most restrictive access, then the role hierarchy and sharing mechanisms open it up. Sharing can only grant additional access, never take it away. If a user already has access through another route (ownership, hierarchy, profile permission such as View All or Modify All), a sharing rule does not reduce it.\n\nSharing rules come in two types. Owner-based sharing rules share records owned by members of a public group, role or role and subordinates with another group, role or role and subordinates. Criteria-based sharing rules share records whose field values match criteria (for example Industry = Banking or Region = EMEA), whatever the owner. Targets are usually public groups (collections of users, roles, and other groups that you define once and reuse in sharing rules and in manual sharing), roles or roles and subordinates. You choose Read Only or Read/Write. A sharing rule cannot give more than Read/Write, and it only works if OWD is not already Public Read/Write.\n\nManual sharing is ad hoc: the record owner, a user above the owner in the hierarchy, or an administrator clicks Share on a single record and picks a user, group or role. It is lost if the owner changes. Account teams and opportunity teams are user-driven too: team members are added to a specific record with a team role and an access level, which suits a recurring group of collaborators per deal. Teams must be enabled in Setup first.\n\nRestriction rules are the exception to 'sharing only opens access'. They filter which records a given set of users can see, even if other mechanisms would grant access. They are used for confidentiality, for example hiding records from contractors. They are supported on a limited set of objects, so verify the object before choosing them.", "setup": ["Setup > Security > Sharing Settings: set organization-wide defaults per object, then create sharing rules in the object's Sharing Rules related list (owner-based or criteria-based).", "Setup > Users > Public Groups: create the group to use as the source or target of a sharing rule.", "Setup > Feature Settings > Sales > Account Teams (or Opportunity Teams): enable teams, then add team members on the record.", "On the record: click Sharing (Share) to grant manual sharing. It requires OWD below Public Read/Write for that object.", "Setup > Security > Restriction Rules: define the object, the user criteria and the record criteria that limit visibility."], "compare": [{"a": "Owner-based sharing rule", "b": "Criteria-based sharing rule", "diff": "Owner-based selects records by who owns them (a role or group). Criteria-based selects records by field values, regardless of owner."}, {"a": "Sharing rules", "b": "Restriction rules", "diff": "Sharing rules only add access. Restriction rules reduce which records users can see, overriding access granted by other means."}, {"a": "Manual sharing", "b": "Account or opportunity team", "diff": "Manual sharing is a one-off grant on one record and is lost on owner change. Teams are a structured, repeatable set of members with roles on a record."}], "signals": [{"if": "Every record where Region = EMEA must be visible to a support group, whoever owns it", "then": "Criteria-based sharing rule to a public group"}, {"if": "A rep needs to give a colleague access to one specific deal, exceptionally", "then": "Manual sharing (or an opportunity team member)"}, {"if": "A recurring group of people with different roles work on the same account", "then": "Account team"}, {"if": "Contractors must not see certain records even though they currently can", "then": "Restriction rule"}], "traps": ["Thinking a sharing rule can remove or limit access: it only grants more access, never less.", "Creating a sharing rule when OWD is already Public Read/Write: it adds nothing, since access is already open.", "Using a sharing rule to give access to a single record: manual sharing or a team is the right tool.", "Forgetting that manual shares are lost when the record owner changes, so they are not a permanent solution."], "scenario": {"situation": "Accounts are Private. All Accounts in the Healthcare industry, owned by anyone in Sales, must be readable by the Compliance team. The Compliance users are not in the role hierarchy above Sales. One rep also wants to let a colleague edit a single Account today.", "answer": "Create a public group for Compliance and a criteria-based sharing rule on Account where Industry equals Healthcare, shared with that group as Read Only. Criteria is right because the need depends on field values, not owners. For the colleague, use manual sharing on that single record with Read/Write."}, "cards": [{"q": "Can a sharing rule reduce a user's access to a record?", "a": "No. Sharing only grants additional access beyond OWD, hierarchy and ownership. Reducing access requires restriction rules or tighter OWD."}, {"q": "What is the difference between owner-based and criteria-based sharing rules?", "a": "Owner-based picks records by owner (role, group), criteria-based picks them by field values on the record."}, {"q": "What is the most permissive access a sharing rule can grant?", "a": "Read/Write. It cannot grant delete or transfer, and it cannot exceed what OWD allows."}, {"q": "What happens to manual sharing when the record owner changes?", "a": "The manual share is removed. It is therefore unsuitable for permanent access needs."}, {"q": "What is a restriction rule used for?", "a": "To limit which records a defined set of users can see, for confidentiality, even if other sharing mechanisms would grant access."}]}, "questions": [{"id": "c7a482cd424047c5bdcb1344e654a568", "stem": "Northwind Logistics has the Account organization-wide default set to Private. Sales representatives in the West region must be able to edit all accounts owned by any member of the West Sales role, but they must not see accounts owned by other regions. The administrator wants a declarative, maintainable solution. What should the administrator do?", "options": ["Ask each account owner to use Manual Sharing to share the record with their colleagues", "Create an owner-based sharing rule that shares accounts owned by the West Sales role with a public group containing the West Sales role, with Read/Write access", "Create a restriction rule on Account that limits West users to West-owned records", "Change the Account organization-wide default to Public Read/Only and add a validation rule for other regions"], "correct": [1], "explanations": ["Incorrect. Manual Sharing works record by record, depends on each owner, and is not maintainable or automatic for new records.", "Correct. An owner-based sharing rule grants Read/Write access to a defined group of users automatically, without affecting other regions.", "Incorrect. A restriction rule only removes access and cannot grant Read/Write access to colleagues' records.", "Incorrect. Opening the default to Public Read/Only gives every user visibility of all accounts, which violates the requirement, and a validation rule cannot control record access."], "takeaway": "Use owner-based sharing rules to automatically open access to records owned by specific roles or groups when the organization-wide default is restrictive."}, {"id": "3bf1679102434645b98411082e47da31", "stem": "Contoso Energy wants to give a sales manager read access to every Opportunity where the Stage is Negotiation and the Amount is greater than 500,000, regardless of who owns it. The Opportunity organization-wide default is Private. The manager is not above the owners in the role hierarchy. Which solution meets this requirement?", "options": ["A criteria-based sharing rule on Opportunity", "An Opportunity Team added to every qualifying opportunity by the manager", "A permission set with the View All Data permission", "An owner-based sharing rule on Opportunity"], "correct": [0], "explanations": ["Correct. A criteria-based sharing rule shares records that match field criteria, here Stage and Amount, with the manager's role or a public group.", "Incorrect. Opportunity Teams are added per record by users and do not automatically apply to all records matching criteria.", "Incorrect. View All Data grants access to all records in the org, far beyond the requirement.", "Incorrect. Owner-based rules depend on who owns the record, not on field values such as Stage or Amount."], "takeaway": "Use a criteria-based sharing rule when access must depend on field values rather than on record ownership."}, {"id": "36879b4c6e524914a86daf309a677ce2", "stem": "Fabrikam has the Account organization-wide default set to Private. An account owner needs to give a colleague in another department Read/Write access to one specific account for the duration of a project. No other accounts should be affected. What should the account owner use?", "options": ["Add the colleague to a public group used by the existing sharing rules", "Use the Share button on the account record to apply Manual Sharing", "Create a new criteria-based sharing rule for that account", "Change the colleague's profile to include the Modify All permission on Accounts"], "correct": [1], "explanations": ["Incorrect. Adding the user to a public group could expose many other records through existing rules.", "Correct. Manual Sharing lets the owner, or a user with sufficient access, share a single record with a user, group, or role.", "Incorrect. Sharing rules apply to all records matching the rule and are managed by administrators, not for a single temporary exception.", "Incorrect. Modify All on Accounts grants edit access to every account, which exceeds the requirement."], "takeaway": "Manual Sharing is the tool for ad hoc, record-level access to one record for specific users."}, {"id": "a5fd8f2deea54222a6ee52af1e5a9363", "stem": "Tailspin Insurance has Case organization-wide default set to Public Read/Write. Compliance now requires that support agents in the Trainee profile see only cases that belong to their own queue's region, even though other sharing already gives them broad access. Which two statements about the solution are correct?", "options": ["A restriction rule on Case can limit which records Trainee users can access", "Restriction rules can be used to grant Trainee users access to cases they cannot currently see", "A new sharing rule can be created to take away the trainees' existing access", "Restriction rules filter records that users would otherwise have access to, so they can reduce access granted by sharing", "Changing sharing rules to Read Only will remove the access already granted to the trainees"], "correct": [0, 3], "explanations": ["Correct. A restriction rule on a supported object such as Case lets an administrator define criteria limiting which records specific users can see.", "Incorrect. Restriction rules never grant access, they only narrow what a user can already see.", "Incorrect. Sharing rules never remove access, they only extend it.", "Correct. Restriction rules act as a filter over the records a user would otherwise access, which is how access is reduced.", "Incorrect. Access from the organization-wide default and other mechanisms remains, since sharing rules only add access and cannot lower it."], "takeaway": "Sharing rules and teams can only add access, so use restriction rules when access granted by other mechanisms must be narrowed."}, {"id": "98ab63967e8a4a9f835802e985ea48c3", "stem": "Adventure Works has the Opportunity organization-wide default set to Private. Regional sales managers must be able to view opportunities owned by members of the sales reps in their own region. The members change often as people join and leave teams. The administrator wants to minimize ongoing maintenance. What should the administrator do?", "options": ["Add the regional managers to the Opportunity Team of every opportunity in the region", "Ask each opportunity owner to use Sharing button and add the regional manager manually on every record", "Change the Opportunity organization-wide default to Public Read Only for all users", "Create a public group for each region's sales reps and a owner-based sharing rule that shares records owned by that group with the regional managers' group"], "correct": [3], "explanations": ["Opportunity Teams are added per record and require constant upkeep, which does not meet the minimal maintenance goal.", "Manual sharing is done record by record by users, does not scale, and is lost when the owner changes.", "Changing the OWD opens access to every user in the org, far beyond the managers' need.", "Correct. An owner-based sharing rule between public groups grants access automatically, and group membership changes are the only maintenance needed."], "takeaway": "Use owner-based sharing rules with public groups to extend access automatically to a changing set of users."}, {"id": "e3995493935a421b93ebb28ff9e32d39", "stem": "Litware has the Account organization-wide default set to Private. A sales rep owns a key account and adds a solution architect as an Account Team member with Read Only access. The architect's profile grants Read and Edit on Accounts, and the architect is not in any role above the owner. The architect tries to edit the account. What is the result?", "options": ["The architect can edit the account because the profile grants Edit", "The architect cannot edit the account because the team access level is Read Only and sharing never overrides this", "The architect cannot even view the account because Account Teams only apply to Opportunities", "The architect can edit the account only if a validation rule allows it"], "correct": [1], "explanations": ["Wrong. The object permission on the profile is necessary, but record access also depends on sharing. Here the only record access is Read Only.", "Correct. The profile permits editing in general, but the record-level access comes only from the team's Read Only level, so edit is not possible.", "Wrong. Account Teams exist for accounts, and the member does get read access.", "Wrong. Validation rules enforce data quality and do not grant record access."], "takeaway": "Effective access is limited by both object permissions and record-level access, so a Read Only share cannot be edited even when the profile allows Edit."}, {"id": "5458295380fb4df9bf6370de976267a0", "stem": "Wingtip Toys has the Case organization-wide default set to Private. A criteria-based sharing rule shares all cases with Priority equal to High to the Escalation Managers public group with Read Only access. A support lead creates a manual share giving a member of that group Read/Write on one high-priority case. What access does that member have on that case?", "options": ["Read Only, because the sharing rule is applied last and takes precedence", "Read/Write, because the most permissive access among the sharing sources is used", "No access, because manual sharing conflicts with the sharing rule and both are removed", "Read Only, because the most restrictive access among all sources is used"], "correct": [1], "explanations": ["Wrong. There is no ordering where the rule overrides the manual share.", "Correct. Sharing grants access additively, so the user gets the highest level granted by any source.", "Wrong. Overlapping shares do not cancel each other.", "Wrong. Salesforce does not apply the most restrictive result, because sharing only adds access."], "takeaway": "When several sharing mechanisms apply to the same user and record, the user gets the most permissive level."}, {"id": "d7c59d297b584bdaabc5b71599a88a4e", "stem": "Proseware wants sales managers to see accounts owned by their direct reports, and the Account organization-wide default is Private. The administrator also needs to extend access to accounts owned by the Partner Success team. Which two options can the administrator use to extend access? (The administrator must keep the OWD as Private.)", "options": ["Create a criteria-based sharing rule that shares accounts based on a field value such as Type equal to Partner", "Create an owner-based sharing rule that shares accounts owned by the Partner Success public group with the Sales Managers public group", "Set the Account OWD to Controlled by Parent for the Partner Success users", "Give the Sales Managers the Modify All Data permission on their profile", "Add a restriction rule so that the Sales Managers can see more records"], "correct": [0, 1], "explanations": ["Correct. Criteria-based rules grant access to records matching field values, such as Type equal to Partner, without changing the OWD.", "Correct. Owner-based rules can share records owned by a public group with another group and keep the OWD Private.", "Wrong. Controlled by Parent is not an option for Account and is not a way to open access for specific users.", "Wrong. Modify All Data gives access to every record in the org, which is far broader than needed.", "Wrong. Restriction rules only limit access to records and never expand it."], "takeaway": "Sharing rules, owner-based or criteria-based, extend access beyond the OWD, while restriction rules only narrow it."}, {"id": "b5c31401c4b6424cac6a5696a680add4", "stem": "Alpine Bank has the Case organization-wide default set to Public Read/Write. The compliance team creates a restriction rule on Case so that users with the Contractor permission set only see cases they own. A contractor is also a member of a public group that receives cases through a sharing rule. Which two statements are true?", "options": ["Restriction rules can be created only for the Account object", "The contractor sees only the cases the contractor owns, despite the sharing rule", "The restriction rule grants the contractor edit access to cases they own", "The sharing rule overrides the restriction rule because sharing grants access", "Restriction rules filter the records that sharing would otherwise make visible"], "correct": [1, 4], "explanations": ["Wrong. Restriction rules are supported on several objects, including custom objects, contracts, events, tasks, and cases, not only Account.", "Correct. The restriction rule limits the contractor to owned cases even though a sharing rule would otherwise expose more.", "Wrong. Restriction rules never grant access. They only reduce the set of visible records.", "Wrong. Restriction rules take precedence over the access granted by sharing and do not get overridden by it.", "Correct. Restriction rules act as a filter on top of the access that sharing and other mechanisms grant."], "takeaway": "Restriction rules narrow the records a user can see even when sharing would otherwise grant access."}]}, {"id": "adm_access_debug", "title": "Diagnostiquer un problème d'accès", "fiche": {"one_liner": "When a user cannot see or edit something, check access in order: object permissions, then field-level security, then record access, using Sharing, Field Accessibility, Setup Audit Trail and Login As.", "need": "At Acme Consulting, a sales rep named Claire says she cannot see an Opportunity her colleague Marc is working on, and cannot edit its Discount field. The admin needs a reliable method to find out whether the cause is the object, the field or the record, instead of changing settings at random and risking giving too much access.", "core": "Always diagnose in the same order, from the broadest level to the narrowest. First, object access: does the user's profile or a permission set grant Read, Create, Edit or Delete on the object? If not, nothing else matters, since the user will not even see the tab or the records. Second, field access: field-level security (FLS) decides whether each field is Visible and Read-Only for the user. Third, record access: even with object access, the user sees only records allowed by the org-wide defaults (OWD), the role hierarchy, sharing rules, manual sharing, teams and ownership.\n\nThe Sharing button on a record (Sharing in Lightning, shown on the record's action menu when the OWD is Private or Public Read Only for the object) lists who has access to that record and why: owner, role hierarchy, sharing rule, manual share. The role hierarchy gives users higher in the hierarchy the same access as their subordinates, but only if the object's \"Grant Access Using Hierarchies\" setting is enabled (it is for standard objects, and can be turned off for custom objects).\n\nField Accessibility (Check Field Accessibility in Setup) shows, for a chosen object, the access to each field by profile, record type or page layout. It helps you see how a field behaves across profiles, but remember that the final result combines FLS and page layout.\n\nSetup Audit Trail lists the latest configuration changes made in Setup (the last 6 months, viewable and downloadable as CSV for that period). It answers \"what changed recently and who did it?\". Finally, Login As lets an admin act as the user to reproduce the problem exactly, if the user has granted login access or the org allows admins to log in as any user. Actions are performed as that user, so be careful.", "setup": ["Setup > Users > Profiles, or Permission Sets: check object permissions (Read, Create, Edit, Delete) and field permissions.", "Setup > Security > Field Accessibility (Check Field Accessibility): choose the object, view by profiles or by fields.", "On the record, click Sharing (Sharing button, when available) to see who has access and why.", "Setup > Security > View Setup Audit Trail: review recent configuration changes and download them if needed.", "Setup > Users > Users, then Login next to the user's name (Login As), after checking that login access is allowed."], "compare": [{"a": "Field-level security (FLS)", "b": "Page layout", "diff": "FLS is real security: it controls visibility and editing everywhere (reports, API, layouts). A page layout only controls what appears on the page, so a field removed from the layout is hidden in the UI but not secured."}, {"a": "Object permissions (profile/permission set)", "b": "Record access (OWD, role hierarchy, sharing rules)", "diff": "Object permissions say what a user can do with an object at all. Record access determines which individual records the user can see. A user needs both to work with a record."}, {"a": "Setup Audit Trail", "b": "Field history tracking", "diff": "Setup Audit Trail records changes to configuration (who changed a profile, a field, a rule). Field history tracking records changes to data values in records."}], "signals": [{"if": "User cannot see a record that a colleague can see", "then": "Check record access: OWD, role hierarchy, sharing rules, then the Sharing button on the record."}, {"if": "User can see the record but a field is missing or read-only", "then": "Check field-level security (and Field Accessibility), then the page layout."}, {"if": "\"It worked yesterday\" or \"who changed this setting?\"", "then": "Setup Audit Trail."}, {"if": "Admin wants to reproduce exactly what the user experiences", "then": "Login As (log in as that user)."}], "traps": ["Giving record access (sharing) to a user whose profile lacks object Read permission does nothing: object permissions come first.", "Hiding a field from the page layout does not secure it; only field-level security does.", "The role hierarchy opens access upward only: managers see subordinates' records, not the reverse, and only if Grant Access Using Hierarchies is enabled.", "Setup Audit Trail tracks configuration changes, not changes to data in records (that is field history tracking), and it only covers the last 6 months."], "scenario": {"situation": "Claire, a sales rep, cannot open an Opportunity owned by Marc, a colleague in the same role. She can open other Opportunities. The Opportunity OWD is Private and nothing was changed on her profile. A manager says it worked last week.", "answer": "Claire has object access, since she sees other Opportunities, so the issue is record access. Click Sharing on the record to see who has access, then check sharing rules, and use Setup Audit Trail to see whether a rule or the OWD changed recently. Use Login As to confirm. Then fix it with a sharing rule or manual share."}, "cards": [{"q": "In which order do you check access when diagnosing a problem?", "a": "Object permissions first, then field-level security, then record access (OWD, role hierarchy, sharing rules, manual sharing)."}, {"q": "What does the Sharing button on a record show?", "a": "Which users, groups and roles have access to that record and the reason for each (owner, hierarchy, sharing rule, manual share)."}, {"q": "What does Check Field Accessibility help you do?", "a": "See, for an object, how fields are accessed by profile, record type and page layout, to find why a field is hidden or read-only."}, {"q": "What does Setup Audit Trail show and for how long?", "a": "The most recent configuration changes in Setup, with who made them and when, for the last 6 months."}, {"q": "What is Login As used for in a diagnosis?", "a": "It lets an admin experience Salesforce as the affected user to reproduce the issue, provided login access is granted or allowed by org settings."}]}, "questions": [{"id": "3fcd6b458f604325b9e1338d54796224", "stem": "At Northwind Outfitters, a sales rep named Clara reports that she cannot see the Contract Value field on Opportunity records, although her colleagues in the same role can. Both Clara and her colleagues have the same profile and the Opportunity object permissions are identical. The administrator wants the fastest way to confirm what Clara actually sees. What should the administrator do first?", "options": ["Create a new sharing rule that shares all Opportunities with Clara", "Add Clara to a public group that owns the Opportunity records", "Use Login As to log in as Clara and view an Opportunity record", "Run the Setup Audit Trail report to find Clara's last login time"], "correct": [2], "explanations": ["Incorrect. A sharing rule affects record access, but Clara can see the records; the problem is a single field, which is controlled by field-level security.", "Incorrect. Public group membership does not grant field access, and the issue is not about record ownership.", "Correct. Logging in as the user lets the administrator reproduce exactly what Clara sees, and then check field access such as field-level security and permission sets.", "Incorrect. The Setup Audit Trail tracks configuration changes, not login times, and it does not show what Clara sees."], "takeaway": "To reproduce a user's problem, log in as that user, and remember that a missing field is a field-level security question, not a sharing question."}, {"id": "b487ce10d4ab452db574bd2ff8253864", "stem": "At Contoso Pharma, a service agent named Luc cannot open a Case that he needs to update. The administrator confirms that Luc's profile gives him Read and Edit on the Case object and that the fields on the page are visible to him. Following the correct order of troubleshooting, what should the administrator check next?", "options": ["Whether the page layout contains the Related Lists that Luc uses", "Whether Luc has record-level access through ownership, role hierarchy, sharing rules or manual sharing", "Whether Luc's password policy has expired recently", "Whether the Case object has a validation rule that blocks the save"], "correct": [1], "explanations": ["Incorrect. Page layouts control the layout of the page and not whether the record can be accessed at all.", "Correct. After object and field access are verified, the next layer is record access, determined by organization-wide defaults, role hierarchy, sharing rules, teams and manual sharing.", "Incorrect. An expired password would prevent login altogether, not access to one record.", "Incorrect. A validation rule would block a save with an error, but Luc cannot open the record at all."], "takeaway": "Troubleshoot access in order: object permissions, then field access, then record access."}, {"id": "cb07aaa45286497197bd26c859224131", "stem": "At Fabrikam Logistics, a manager reports that a Shipment record owned by one of her team members cannot be edited by a colleague. The administrator wants to see exactly who has access to that specific record and why, and to grant additional access if needed. Which feature should the administrator use on the record?", "options": ["The Schema Builder to inspect relationships on the Shipment object", "The Debug Logs page in Setup", "The Field Accessibility viewer in Setup", "The Sharing button on the record, then Expand List to view who has access"], "correct": [3], "explanations": ["Incorrect. Schema Builder shows the data model, not who has access to a given record.", "Incorrect. Debug logs capture execution of automation and code, not record sharing access.", "Incorrect. Field Accessibility shows field visibility by profile and record type, not who can access an individual record.", "Correct. The Sharing button shows the users, groups and roles that have access to the record and lets authorized users add manual sharing. Expand List shows the access level details."], "takeaway": "Use the Sharing button on a record to see who has access to it and to add manual sharing."}, {"id": "3820b52167e544369df6028323b870ea", "stem": "At Tailspin Retail, users in the Support team suddenly lost the ability to see the Warranty Notes field on Account records. Nobody on the team admits to making a change, and several administrators work in the org. The administrator needs to find out what changed and who changed it. Which two actions should the administrator take?", "options": ["Review the Setup Audit Trail for recent changes to field-level security or permission sets", "Use the Field Accessibility page to check how the field is set for the Support profile", "Edit the Account page layout to add the field to the Support users' layout", "Reset the security token for all Support users", "Run the Login History report for the Support team"], "correct": [0, 1], "explanations": ["Correct. The Setup Audit Trail logs recent configuration changes, including who made them, which helps identify a change to field-level security or permissions.", "Correct. Field Accessibility shows how the field is configured for each profile, confirming whether the field is hidden or read-only for the Support profile.", "Incorrect. Adding a field to a layout does not override field-level security, and it does not explain what changed.", "Incorrect. A security token relates to API login access and has nothing to do with field visibility.", "Incorrect. Login History shows login attempts, not configuration changes."], "takeaway": "Use the Setup Audit Trail to find who changed the configuration and Field Accessibility to verify the resulting field settings."}, {"id": "8929aa3f20014cf7b52fc57a1a537c0a", "stem": "At Adventure Works, the Regional Director role sits above the Regional Manager role in the role hierarchy. The Opportunity organization-wide default is Private. A Regional Director named Sam cannot see Opportunities owned by Regional Managers who report to him. Which two settings could explain this problem?", "options": ["The Opportunity organization-wide default is set to Public Read Only", "The Opportunities are owned by users in a different, unrelated role branch", "The Grant Access Using Hierarchies option is deselected for the Opportunity object", "Sam has been given the View All Data permission through a permission set", "Sam's profile lacks the Read permission on the Opportunity object"], "correct": [2, 4], "explanations": ["Incorrect. Public Read Only would give more access, not less, so it would not block Sam.", "Incorrect. The scenario states that the managers report to Sam, so the owners are in the subordinate branch of his role.", "Correct. If Grant Access Using Hierarchies is deselected on a custom object, managers above the owner do not automatically gain access. Standard objects always use the hierarchy.", "Incorrect. View All Data would grant access to all records, so it could not explain the problem.", "Correct. Without object Read permission, Sam cannot see any Opportunities, whatever the role hierarchy gives him at the record level."], "takeaway": "Role hierarchy access only helps if the user has object permission, and for custom objects only if Grant Access Using Hierarchies is selected."}]}]}, {"id": "objets", "label": "Gestionnaire d'objets et Lightning App Builder", "weight": 15, "topics": [{"id": "adm_fields", "title": "Objets et types de champs", "fiche": {"one_liner": "Objects are the tables that store your business data, and each field type (picklist, formula, roll-up summary, encrypted) controls what can be entered, calculated or protected, so choose it carefully because changing it later can destroy data.", "need": "A equipment rental company needs to track Rental Contracts, which no standard object covers. It creates a custom object (objet personnalisé) with fields: contract status (picklist), total price (formula), number of equipment lines (roll-up summary) and the customer's ID number (encrypted). Each field type matches a specific business rule.", "core": "An object is a table that stores records (enregistrements). Standard objects (Account, Contact, Opportunity, Lead, Case) come with Salesforce and cannot be deleted, although you can add custom fields to them. Custom objects are created by you and have the API name suffix __c. Fields are the columns, and each has a data type that defines what it accepts.\n\nKey field types. Picklist (liste de sélection): one value from a predefined list. Multi-Select Picklist (liste de sélection à choix multiple): several values in one field, stored separated by semicolons; it is harder to report on and filter, so use it sparingly. Formula: read-only value calculated from other fields, never stored by the user, and it can reference fields from related parent records. Roll-Up Summary: calculates COUNT, SUM, MIN or MAX over child records, and it exists only on the master side of a master-detail relationship. Long Text Area (zone de texte longue): large free text, not usable in report filters or in formulas the same way as short text. Text (Encrypted) (texte chiffré): masks the value for users without the View Encrypted Data permission; it has limitations, for example it cannot be used in filters or formulas as a normal field.\n\nChanging a field's type is possible but risky. Salesforce converts existing values, and some conversions lose data: for example, Text to Number drops non-numeric values, and converting a Multi-Select Picklist to a Picklist keeps only part of the stored values. Some changes are blocked outright, such as changing the type of a field used in certain features. Before changing a type, export the data (Data Export or Data Loader) as a backup.\n\nDependent picklists (listes de sélection dépendantes) make the values of one field (dependent) depend on the value of another (controlling field). The controlling field can be a picklist or a checkbox. Global Value Sets (ensembles de valeurs globaux) let several picklist fields, on any object, share one list of values maintained in one place.", "setup": ["Setup > Object Manager > Create > Custom Object (to create a custom object); or open a standard object from the list", "Object Manager > [Object] > Fields & Relationships > New (choose the data type, then set field-level security and page layouts)", "Object Manager > [Object] > Fields & Relationships > Field Dependencies > New (to define controlling and dependent fields)", "Setup > Picklist Value Sets > New > Global Value Set (to create a shared list of values)"], "compare": [{"a": "Formula field", "b": "Roll-Up Summary field", "diff": "A formula calculates from fields on the same record or its parent records, while a roll-up summary aggregates (SUM, COUNT, MIN, MAX) child records and requires a master-detail relationship."}, {"a": "Global Value Set", "b": "Dependent picklist", "diff": "A global value set shares one identical list of values across many fields for consistency. A dependent picklist filters the available values of one field based on the value chosen in another field."}, {"a": "Multi-Select Picklist", "b": "Picklist", "diff": "A picklist stores a single value and is easy to report on. A multi-select picklist stores several values separated by semicolons and is harder to filter and report on."}], "signals": [{"if": "Total of child records (sum of line items, count of related records) shown on the parent", "then": "Roll-Up Summary field (requires master-detail)"}, {"if": "Value calculated automatically and users must not edit it", "then": "Formula field"}, {"if": "Available values of one field depend on the choice in another field", "then": "Dependent picklist with a controlling field"}, {"if": "Same list of values reused across several objects or fields, maintained once", "then": "Global Value Set"}], "traps": ["A roll-up summary field cannot be created on the parent in a lookup relationship, only in master-detail (unless using other tools such as Flow or a declarative lookup rollup app).", "Changing a field's data type can permanently delete or alter existing values, so back up the data first.", "A formula field is read-only and calculated, so it cannot be edited by users or loaded with data.", "Standard objects can be customized with custom fields but cannot be deleted, whereas custom objects can be deleted."], "scenario": {"situation": "A sales manager wants the Account page to show the total amount of all related Invoice records. Invoice is a custom object linked to Account by a lookup relationship. Users must not be able to edit the total. Which approach should the administrator consider?", "answer": "A roll-up summary field needs master-detail, so with a lookup the admin must either convert the relationship to master-detail (if data allows: every Invoice must have an Account) or use a Flow to calculate the total. A formula cannot aggregate child records. Making the field read-only keeps users from editing it."}, "cards": [{"q": "What suffix identifies a custom object or custom field in the API?", "a": "__c, for example Rental_Contract__c."}, {"q": "On which side of a relationship can you create a roll-up summary field?", "a": "On the master (parent) object of a master-detail relationship."}, {"q": "What can be a controlling field for a dependent picklist?", "a": "A picklist or a checkbox field."}, {"q": "What is the main benefit of a Global Value Set?", "a": "One shared list of values reused by several picklist fields, so a change is made once and stays consistent."}, {"q": "What is the main risk when changing a field's data type?", "a": "Existing data may be lost or converted incorrectly, so back up the data before the change."}]}, "questions": [{"id": "a58a70280f5a41b5860f9a09be34f754", "stem": "Northwind Logistics wants to track the total value of all open shipments on each Account record. Shipment is a custom object related to Account through a master-detail relationship. The administrator needs a number on the Account that updates automatically whenever shipments change. What should the administrator create?", "options": ["A Number field on Account updated by a scheduled Apex job", "A Formula field on Account", "A Roll-Up Summary field on Shipment", "A Roll-Up Summary field on Account"], "correct": [3], "explanations": ["Incorrect. This adds unnecessary custom code and does not update automatically in real time. A declarative field exists for this need.", "Incorrect. Formula fields can only reference fields on the same record or related parent records, not aggregate child records.", "Incorrect. Roll-Up Summary fields are created on the master object, not on the detail object.", "Correct. A Roll-Up Summary field on the master (Account) can sum a field from detail records, and it can use filter criteria such as open status."], "takeaway": "Roll-Up Summary fields live on the master object in a master-detail relationship and aggregate values from the detail records."}, {"id": "df61c06a10ad403780bf0562820d016c", "stem": "Bluebird Insurance has a Claim custom object with a Status picklist field that is used in hundreds of records. The administrator wants to convert this field to a Text field so users can enter free-form values. What is the main consequence the administrator should anticipate?", "options": ["All existing values in the field are permanently deleted", "Existing picklist values are kept as text, but the picklist value list is deleted", "The conversion is blocked because the field has data", "Reports using the field are automatically deleted"], "correct": [1], "explanations": ["Incorrect. Converting a picklist to Text preserves the existing values. Data loss is more typical when converting to incompatible types.", "Correct. When a picklist is converted to Text, existing values are retained as text, and the picklist value definitions are lost. Teams should still review dependencies and back up data first.", "Incorrect. Salesforce allows the change of type even when records contain data, although some conversions are restricted.", "Incorrect. Reports are not deleted automatically, though filters on the field may behave differently after the type change."], "takeaway": "Changing a field type can cause data loss or behavior changes, so always back up data and check dependencies before converting."}, {"id": "9ae6f0db05174c5c8127365c6cc32d3d", "stem": "Contoso Education has a Course custom object and a Student Enrollment object. Both need a Region picklist with the same list of 12 values. Regions change a few times per year, and the administrator wants to maintain the values in one place only. What should the administrator do?", "options": ["Create a record type on each object with the same values", "Create a custom metadata type and reference it in a formula field", "Create a dependent picklist on each object", "Create a Global Value Set and base both picklist fields on it"], "correct": [3], "explanations": ["Incorrect. Record types limit which picklist values are available but do not centralize value definitions.", "Incorrect. Custom metadata types store configuration data and a formula field cannot act as a picklist to select values.", "Incorrect. Dependent picklists filter values based on a controlling field and do not provide shared value management.", "Correct. A Global Value Set lets multiple picklist fields share one centrally managed set of values across objects."], "takeaway": "Use Global Value Sets to share and centrally manage one list of picklist values across multiple fields and objects."}, {"id": "f1685e4b25f24d1b8b2f7a15197ac91c", "stem": "Fabrikam Realty needs a field on the Property object that shows a full address built from the Street, City, and State fields on the same record, and the value must always stay in sync as those fields change. Users must not edit it directly. Which two approaches are appropriate?", "options": ["Create a Roll-Up Summary field on Property", "Create a Long Text Area field and populate it with a validation rule", "Create a Formula field that uses the &amp; operator or the CONCATENATE function", "Create a Formula field of type Text that concatenates the three fields", "Create a Text field and ask users to copy the values manually"], "correct": [2, 3], "explanations": ["Incorrect. Roll-Up Summary fields aggregate child records and do not combine fields on the same record.", "Incorrect. Validation rules only block saves based on conditions. They cannot populate a field value.", "Correct. Concatenation with &amp; or the CONCATENATE function is the standard way to join text values in a formula field.", "Correct. A Text formula field is calculated automatically, is read-only, and always reflects current values.", "Incorrect. Manual copying is error-prone and does not keep values in sync."], "takeaway": "Formula fields are read-only, calculated on the fly, and can combine values from the same record using text operators."}, {"id": "9f3667b669e3413da649701d61473e1d", "stem": "Adventure Works Travel has a Booking object with a Country picklist and a City picklist. The administrator wants the City options to depend on the selected Country. The Country field is already a standard picklist. Which two statements describe what the administrator must do or know?", "options": ["Create a field dependency with Country as the controlling field and City as the dependent field", "Country must be converted to a Multi-Select Picklist to control City", "The City field must be a Text field to be dependent", "Define which City values are available for each Country value in the dependency matrix", "Dependent picklists require a Global Value Set for both fields"], "correct": [0, 3], "explanations": ["Correct. A field dependency is set up by choosing the controlling field (Country) and the dependent field (City).", "Incorrect. A controlling field can be a standard picklist or checkbox, and it does not need to be a multi-select picklist.", "Incorrect. The dependent field must itself be a picklist or multi-select picklist, not text.", "Correct. The dependency matrix is where the administrator specifies which dependent values are available for each controlling value.", "Incorrect. A Global Value Set is optional and not required for a dependency."], "takeaway": "A field dependency pairs a controlling field with a dependent picklist, and the matrix defines which dependent values appear for each controlling value."}]}, {"id": "adm_relations", "title": "Relations entre objets", "fiche": {"one_liner": "A lookup is a loose link between two records; master-detail is a tight parent-child bond where the child inherits the owner, sharing and deletion, and the parent can roll up the children's data.", "need": "A consulting firm tracks Projects and Timesheet Entries. Each entry must always belong to a project, be deleted with it, be visible to whoever sees the project, and let the project show total hours worked. That is a master-detail need. Linking a Project to an optional Sponsor Contact is only a lookup.", "core": "A lookup relationship is a loose link. The child record can exist without a parent (the field can be left empty unless you make it required), it keeps its own owner, and it has its own sharing. Deleting the parent does not delete the children by default. You can configure what happens to the child record when the parent is deleted: clear the field value, or prevent the deletion. A lookup does not give you roll-up summary fields.\n\nA master-detail relationship is a tight bond. The master is the parent and the detail is the child. The detail record has no Owner field, because it inherits the owner of the master. Sharing and record access are inherited from the master. Deleting the master deletes all its detail records (cascade delete). The parent field is always required on the detail. Only master-detail supports roll-up summary fields (COUNT, SUM, MIN, MAX) on the master.\n\nConverting a lookup to master-detail works only if every existing record has a value in the lookup field. Fill in blanks first. Converting master-detail to lookup works only if the master object has no roll-up summary fields. Many-to-many is built with a junction object (an object with two master-detail relationships). The first master-detail you create is the primary one and determines the look and the owner/sharing inheritance of the junction. Special relationships: hierarchical (a lookup from User to User, for example Manager) and external lookup (links to an object in an external data source via an external object).", "setup": ["Setup > Object Manager > [Object] > Fields & Relationships > New > choose Lookup Relationship or Master-Detail Relationship", "To convert: Object Manager > [Child object] > Fields & Relationships > open the lookup field > Change Field Type > Master-Detail Relationship", "Roll-up summary: Object Manager > [Master object] > Fields & Relationships > New > Roll-Up Summary", "Many-to-many: create a custom junction object, then add two Master-Detail fields pointing to the two objects to connect"], "compare": [{"a": "Lookup relationship", "b": "Master-detail relationship", "diff": "Lookup: optional parent, own owner and sharing, no cascade delete by default, no roll-ups. Master-detail: required parent, inherited owner and sharing, cascade delete, roll-ups possible."}, {"a": "Junction object (many-to-many)", "b": "Simple lookup", "diff": "A lookup links each child to one parent only. A junction object with two master-detail fields lets each record on one side relate to many records on the other side."}, {"a": "Hierarchical relationship", "b": "Regular lookup", "diff": "Hierarchical is a special lookup available only on the User object, linking a user to another user (for example a manager). A regular lookup links to a different object or the same object."}], "signals": [{"if": "Child records must be deleted when the parent is deleted, or child inherits parent's sharing", "then": "Master-detail relationship"}, {"if": "Parent must display a total, count, minimum or maximum of child records", "then": "Master-detail plus a roll-up summary field"}, {"if": "Many-to-many (for example Candidates and Job Postings via applications)", "then": "Junction object with two master-detail relationships"}, {"if": "Link to data stored in an external system (via external object)", "then": "External lookup relationship"}], "traps": ["You cannot convert a lookup to master-detail if any existing record has an empty lookup field: populate them first.", "You cannot convert master-detail back to lookup while the master has roll-up summary fields: delete them first.", "A detail record in a master-detail relationship has no Owner field of its own: it inherits the master's owner.", "A hierarchical relationship exists only on the User object, not on custom objects."], "scenario": {"situation": "A non-profit tracks Donations related to Campaigns. Managers want each Campaign to show the total amount donated, and Donations must be removed if a Campaign is deleted. Today the relationship is a lookup and all Donations have a Campaign filled in.", "answer": "Convert the lookup to master-detail, which is possible since every record has a value. Then create a roll-up summary field (SUM of Amount) on Campaign. Master-detail gives cascade delete, and only it allows roll-up summaries. A lookup cannot do either."}, "cards": [{"q": "What happens to a detail record's owner in master-detail?", "a": "The detail has no Owner field. It inherits the master's owner, and sharing and security come from the master."}, {"q": "Which relationship type supports roll-up summary fields?", "a": "Only master-detail. The roll-up summary field is created on the master object."}, {"q": "What condition must be met to convert a lookup to master-detail?", "a": "Every existing record must have a value in the lookup field. Records with a blank value must be fixed first."}, {"q": "How do you model a many-to-many relationship?", "a": "With a junction object that has two master-detail relationships, one to each of the two objects to connect."}, {"q": "What is a hierarchical relationship?", "a": "A special lookup available only on the User object, used to link a user to another user, such as a manager."}]}, "questions": [{"id": "b380512117304444a5ea1ec678458a8c", "stem": "Northwind Logistics tracks shipment claims in a custom object called Claim. The company wants every Claim record to be deleted automatically when its parent Shipment record is deleted, and wants the Shipment page to display the total claim amount. Which relationship should the administrator create between Claim and Shipment?", "options": ["Lookup relationship from Claim to Shipment", "Master-detail relationship from Claim to Shipment", "Hierarchical relationship on the Shipment object", "External lookup relationship from Claim to Shipment"], "correct": [1], "explanations": ["Incorrect. Deleting a parent in a lookup relationship does not delete the child records by default, and roll-up summary fields are not available on lookups.", "Correct. Deleting the master record deletes its detail records, and a master-detail relationship supports roll-up summary fields on the master object.", "Incorrect. A hierarchical relationship is available only on the User object and links users to other users, not claims to shipments.", "Incorrect. An external lookup links a child object to an external object, and Shipment is a standard Salesforce custom object here, not external data."], "takeaway": "Cascade delete and roll-up summary fields point to a master-detail relationship, not a lookup."}, {"id": "8a39801b7cbb435d97b28b5a6a89d142", "stem": "At Brightway Education, the administrator needs to convert an existing lookup field on the custom object Enrollment, which points to the Course object, into a master-detail relationship. The Enrollment object already contains thousands of records. What must be true before the conversion can succeed?", "options": ["The lookup field must be removed from all page layouts", "Every existing Enrollment record must have a value in the lookup field", "Enrollment must be converted to a standard object first", "The Course object must have a roll-up summary field defined"], "correct": [1], "explanations": ["Incorrect. Page layout placement has no bearing on whether the field can be converted.", "Correct. A lookup can be converted to master-detail only if all existing records have a value in the lookup field, because a detail record requires a parent.", "Incorrect. Custom objects can be on the detail side of a master-detail relationship, and standard objects cannot be converted in this way.", "Incorrect. Roll-up summary fields are an option available after the conversion, not a prerequisite for it."], "takeaway": "To convert a lookup to master-detail, first populate the lookup field on every existing child record."}, {"id": "051c008d4b184ee3903531dcc2cac6c9", "stem": "Summit Realty wants to track which Agents work on which Properties. An agent can be assigned to many properties, and each property can have many agents. Both are custom objects. What should the administrator build?", "options": ["A junction object with two master-detail relationships, one to Agent and one to Property", "A single master-detail relationship from Property to Agent", "A lookup field on Property pointing to Agent and another lookup on Agent pointing to Property", "A hierarchical relationship between Agent and Property"], "correct": [0], "explanations": ["Correct. A junction object with two master-detail relationships is the standard way to model a many-to-many relationship in Salesforce.", "Incorrect. A single master-detail gives a one-to-many relationship, so each property could have only one agent.", "Incorrect. Two direct lookups on each object create two separate one-to-many links and do not model a proper many-to-many structure.", "Incorrect. Hierarchical relationships exist only on the User object and cannot relate two custom objects."], "takeaway": "Model many-to-many with a junction object that has two master-detail relationships."}, {"id": "bf7b98eae45d4c6b906591dd8cb50505", "stem": "Contoso Health has a custom object, Treatment Plan, that is the detail side of a master-detail relationship with Patient. The administrator is reviewing how this relationship affects the Treatment Plan records. Which two statements are true?", "options": ["Treatment Plan records inherit sharing access from the parent Patient record", "Treatment Plan records have their own Owner field that can be reassigned to any user", "The Treatment Plan object cannot have its own Owner field", "The Patient field on a Treatment Plan can be left empty when saving", "Treatment Plan records can be shared independently of Patient through the Owner field"], "correct": [0, 2], "explanations": ["Correct. In master-detail, the detail record inherits its sharing and security from the master record.", "Incorrect. The detail record has no independent owner that can be reassigned, since ownership follows the master.", "Correct. A detail object in a master-detail relationship has no Owner field, as ownership is determined by the master record.", "Incorrect. The master-detail field is required on every detail record.", "Incorrect. Without an Owner field there is no independent owner-based sharing, because access comes from the master."], "takeaway": "A detail record has no Owner field and inherits sharing and security from its master record."}, {"id": "f08a4144cce141d8a3509fb66629d4c8", "stem": "Fabrikam Manufacturing stores its parts catalog in an external system and surfaces it in Salesforce as an external object through Salesforce Connect. The administrator wants Salesforce Work Order records to reference parts from this external object. Which two statements about the right approach are true?", "options": ["Create a roll-up summary field on the external object to count work orders", "Create an indirect lookup relationship if the external part IDs match a unique external ID field on Work Order", "Create an external lookup relationship on the Work Order object pointing to the external object", "Create a master-detail relationship from the external object to Work Order", "Create a hierarchical relationship on the external object"], "correct": [1, 2], "explanations": ["Incorrect. Roll-up summary fields need a master-detail relationship, which external objects do not support.", "Correct. An indirect lookup links a child external object to a parent standard or custom object, using an external ID field on the parent. It is a legitimate option when the external object is the child.", "Correct. An external lookup links a child standard, custom, or external object to a parent external object, matching on the external object's ID.", "Incorrect. Master-detail relationships are not supported with external objects.", "Incorrect. Hierarchical relationships are available only on the User object."], "takeaway": "External objects support only external lookup and indirect lookup relationships, never master-detail."}]}, {"id": "adm_record_types", "title": "Types d'enregistrement et processus", "fiche": {"one_liner": "Record types let one object offer different picklist values and page layouts to different users; business processes (sales, support, lead) control the Stage, Status or Lead Status values per record type.", "need": "A software company sells both New Business and Renewals using the Opportunity object. New Business reps need stages such as Prospecting and Needs Analysis, while Renewals reps only need Renewal Review and Closed Won. Record types let each team see only the stages, picklist values and fields relevant to them, without creating separate objects.", "core": "A record type (type d'enregistrement) lets you offer different business processes, picklist values and page layouts for the same object. The record type does not change the data model: the fields stay the same. It changes what the user sees and which picklist values can be chosen. Record types are available on standard and custom objects.\n\nA business process (processus métier) controls which values appear in a specific picklist. There are three: the sales process for Opportunities (Stage field), the support process for Cases (Status field), and the lead process for Leads (Lead Status field). The Solution object also has a process (Status). A business process is a list of selected picklist values, and it must be assigned to a record type. Standard objects with a business process (Opportunity, Case, Lead) require a process when creating a record type. A custom object has no business process, but its record types still control picklist values.\n\nPicklist values are managed in two places. First, you add the value to the field itself. Then, in each record type, you select which values are available. A value not selected in the record type is not available when creating or editing a record of that type. Existing records keep their value even if it is later removed from the record type.\n\nRecord types are made available to users through profiles (profils) or permission sets (ensembles d'autorisations). Each profile has a list of available record types and one default record type. The default is preselected when the user creates a record. Page layouts are assigned per profile and per record type. If a profile has only one available record type, the user is not asked to choose. The Master record type is used when a profile has no record types assigned.", "setup": ["Setup > Object Manager > [Object] > Record Types > New: choose the record type, the business process if applicable, and the profiles that can use it.", "Setup > Feature Settings > Sales > Opportunities > Sales Processes (or Support Settings > Support Processes, or Lead Processes): create the process and select its picklist values.", "Setup > Object Manager > [Object] > Fields & Relationships > [Picklist field]: add values and choose which record types they apply to.", "Setup > Object Manager > [Object] > Page Layouts > Page Layout Assignment: assign a layout per profile and record type.", "Setup > Users > Profiles > [Profile] > Object Settings: choose available record types and the default record type."], "compare": [{"a": "Record type", "b": "Page layout (mise en page)", "diff": "A page layout controls which fields, sections and buttons are shown. A record type controls picklist values and business process, and is linked to a layout through the layout assignment."}, {"a": "Business process", "b": "Record type", "diff": "A business process is only a set of picklist values for Stage, Status or Lead Status. It does nothing until it is attached to a record type."}, {"a": "Default record type on a profile", "b": "Master record type", "diff": "The default is the available record type preselected at creation. Master is the system fallback used when the user's profile has no record types assigned."}], "signals": [{"if": "Different teams need different Stage values on Opportunities", "then": "Sales processes with one record type per team"}, {"if": "Different Case Status values for different support teams", "then": "Support processes assigned through record types"}, {"if": "Same object, different picklist values or page layouts per user group", "then": "Record types assigned to profiles or permission sets"}, {"if": "Users should not be asked to pick a type when creating a record", "then": "Make only one record type available on the profile, or set the right default"}], "traps": ["Adding a picklist value to a field does not make it appear on a record type: you must also select it in each record type that should use it.", "A record type does not restrict record access or visibility: sharing and permissions do that, record types only shape picklist values and layouts.", "Business processes exist only for Leads, Opportunities, Cases and Solutions, not for custom objects.", "Making a record type available on a profile does not make it the default: the default must be chosen separately."], "scenario": {"situation": "A company has two Opportunity teams: Direct Sales, using stages Prospecting through Closed Won, and Partner Sales, which needs only Registered, Negotiation and Closed Won. Partner reps should also see a different page layout. The admin wants users to see only their own team's options.", "answer": "Create two sales processes, each with its own Stage values. Create two Opportunity record types, each linked to its process. Assign the layouts per profile, and make each record type available to the matching profile. This isolates stage values and layouts without creating a new object."}, "cards": [{"q": "What does a record type control?", "a": "The available business process, the picklist values and the page layout for a given object, depending on the user's profile."}, {"q": "Which three business processes exist, and which field does each control?", "a": "Sales process for Opportunity Stage, support process for Case Status, and lead process for Lead Status."}, {"q": "Where do you assign record types to users?", "a": "On profiles or permission sets, which list the available record types and the default record type."}, {"q": "What happens when a profile has only one record type available?", "a": "The user is not prompted to choose a record type at creation; that one is used."}, {"q": "What happens to existing records when a picklist value is removed from a record type?", "a": "They keep the value, but users cannot select that value on new or edited records of that type unless it is still available."}]}, "questions": [{"id": "f476c1a7450544849c95157f63811562", "stem": "Northwind Logistics sells freight services to two customer segments: Enterprise and Small Business. Enterprise opportunities must progress through the stages Prospecting, Solution Design, Legal Review, Negotiation, and Closed Won. Small Business opportunities should use only Prospecting, Proposal, and Closed Won. Both use the Opportunity object. What should the administrator do?", "options": ["Create a second custom object for Small Business opportunities", "Create two page layouts and assign them by profile to show different Stage values", "Create two record types on Opportunity and assign a different sales process to each one", "Create a validation rule that blocks the stages not used by each segment"], "correct": [2], "explanations": ["Incorrect. A custom object would duplicate standard Opportunity functionality and lose reporting and forecasting features. Record types solve the need on the same object.", "Incorrect. Page layouts control field and section placement, not which picklist values are available. Stage values come from the sales process via the record type.", "Correct. A sales process determines which Stage values are available on Opportunity, and each sales process is linked to a record type.", "Incorrect. A validation rule would only reject bad entries after the fact, and users would still see all the stage values. It does not tailor the picklist."], "takeaway": "On Opportunity, the sales process drives the Stage values, and it is assigned to the object through a record type."}, {"id": "6893cd0bbd4441f5aaeb4dd218850521", "stem": "Contoso Telecom has two support teams. The Tier 1 team handles simple cases using the statuses New, Working, and Closed. The Tier 2 team handles escalated cases using New, Investigating, Waiting on Engineering, and Closed. The administrator has created two record types on Case. What else must the administrator create so that each record type shows its own Status values?", "options": ["A sales process for each record type", "A lead process for each record type", "A separate Status picklist field for each team", "A support process for each record type"], "correct": [3], "explanations": ["Incorrect. Sales processes control Opportunity Stage values and cannot be used with Case record types.", "Incorrect. Lead processes apply to the Lead object and control Lead Status values, not Case Status.", "Incorrect. Creating additional custom fields would fragment reporting. The standard Status field already supports different values per record type via support processes.", "Correct. A support process defines the Status values available for Cases, and it is selected when creating a Case record type."], "takeaway": "Each object has its own process: sales process for Opportunity, support process for Case, lead process for Lead."}, {"id": "271b732e27ef44bcb27d3eecf5524259", "stem": "Fabrikam Solar has a custom picklist field Installation Type on the Work Request object. The Residential record type should offer Roof and Ground. The Commercial record type should offer Roof, Ground, and Carport. The administrator has created both record types. What should the administrator do next?", "options": ["Select the applicable values for each record type in the picklist field's Record Types section", "Add the picklist values to the page layout of each record type", "Create a dependent picklist controlled by the Record Type field", "Edit the picklist field values for each profile in the field-level security settings"], "correct": [0], "explanations": ["Correct. For each picklist, the administrator chooses which values are available per record type from the field's setup page.", "Incorrect. Page layouts do not define picklist values. They only arrange fields and related lists.", "Incorrect. Record Type cannot be used as a controlling field for a dependent picklist. Dependent picklists need a picklist or checkbox controlling field.", "Incorrect. Field-level security controls visibility and editability of the whole field, not which values appear in it."], "takeaway": "Picklist values per record type are configured on the picklist field itself, in its Record Types list, not on layouts."}, {"id": "6ed08f5b86114a59918e47147b69cac2", "stem": "Adventure Works Outfitters created a record type named Wholesale on Account with its own page layout. The administrator wants Wholesale to be available to the Channel Sales profile, and wants new accounts created by that profile to default to Wholesale. Which two actions should the administrator take?", "options": ["Set Wholesale as the default record type for Account in the Channel Sales profile", "Assign Wholesale to the Channel Sales role in the role hierarchy", "Add Wholesale to the Account field Type as a global picklist value", "Create a validation rule that sets the record type automatically on save", "Make Wholesale available in the Channel Sales profile's record type settings for Account"], "correct": [0, 4], "explanations": ["Correct. Each profile has a default record type setting per object, which is preselected when users create records.", "Incorrect. Roles control record visibility in the hierarchy. Record types are assigned through profiles and permission sets.", "Incorrect. Adding a picklist value to the Type field has no effect on record type availability or defaults.", "Incorrect. Validation rules cannot set field values, including record type. They only block saves.", "Correct. A record type must be assigned to a profile (or permission set) before users with that profile can use it."], "takeaway": "Record types are made available and defaulted per profile (or made available through permission sets) for each object."}, {"id": "8c31e22470b949e893b2c293ade714ca", "stem": "Tailspin Education uses two lead record types: Student Inquiry and Corporate Training. The Corporate Training leads must use different Lead Status values than Student Inquiry leads, such as Contacted, Proposal Sent, and Qualified. The administrator is preparing the configuration. Which two statements describe the correct approach?", "options": ["Use a page layout assignment to hide unwanted Lead Status values", "Create a sales process and select it on the Lead record type", "Associate the lead process with the Corporate Training record type", "Create a support process because Lead Status is a standard picklist", "Create a lead process that includes the Lead Status values for Corporate Training"], "correct": [2, 4], "explanations": ["Incorrect. Page layouts cannot hide individual picklist values. They only control which fields are displayed.", "Incorrect. Sales processes apply to Opportunity Stage values, not to Lead record types.", "Correct. The lead process is selected on the record type, so leads of that type show only those statuses.", "Incorrect. Support processes apply to Case Status values and cannot be used on Lead record types.", "Correct. A lead process defines the set of Lead Status values that can be offered."], "takeaway": "A lead process controls Lead Status values and is linked to a Lead record type."}]}, {"id": "adm_layouts", "title": "Présentations de page et présentations compactes", "fiche": {"one_liner": "A page layout controls which fields, sections, related lists and actions users see on a record; it is assigned by profile and record type, while a compact layout controls the highlights panel and mobile cards.", "need": "A company's sales team needs a short Opportunity form, while the finance team needs extra billing fields on the same object. Support agents handling 'Warranty' cases need different fields than 'Billing' cases. Page layouts, record types and compact layouts let one object present the right information to each audience without creating duplicate objects.", "core": "A page layout (présentation de page) defines the organization of a record page: fields, sections, custom buttons and actions, related lists, and other elements such as Visualforce pages or components. It is an object-level tool and is edited in Object Manager, in the Page Layouts area, using the page layout editor. Page layouts control visibility and whether a field is read-only or required on that layout, but not security itself. Field-level security (FLS) decides whether a user can see or edit a field at all.\n\nRequired is the key distinction. A field marked Required on the page layout is mandatory only when users enter data through that layout (UI). A field made required at the field definition level (the 'Required' checkbox when creating the field) is mandatory everywhere: UI, API, imports, Flow and Apex. Universally required fields cannot be removed from a layout. Required on layout alone does not block data loaded through the API or Data Import Wizard.\n\nAssignment: a layout is assigned by the combination of profile and record type (Page Layout Assignment). Each profile-record type pair gets one layout. If the object has no record types, assignment is by profile only. Actions on a layout come from the Salesforce Mobile and Lightning Experience Actions section; a layout without a customized actions section uses the default predefined actions.\n\nA compact layout (présentation compacte) defines the small set of key fields shown in the highlights panel (panneau de mise en évidence) at the top of a record in Lightning Experience, in the Salesforce mobile app record header, in hover details and in Kanban cards or lookup previews. The first four fields are displayed in the highlights panel on desktop (the first field is the record name-like primary field). Compact layouts are assigned per object as a primary compact layout or per record type, not per profile. If none is assigned, a system default is used.", "setup": ["Setup > Object Manager > [object] > Page Layouts: create or edit a layout, drag fields, sections, related lists and buttons from the palette, and set Required or Read-Only on fields.", "Setup > Object Manager > [object] > Page Layouts > Page Layout Assignment: choose a layout for each profile and record type combination.", "Setup > Object Manager > [object] > Compact Layouts: create a layout, select fields, then use Compact Layout Assignment to set the primary compact layout.", "Setup > Object Manager > [object] > Fields & Relationships: tick Required when creating or editing a field to make it required everywhere."], "compare": [{"a": "Required on the page layout", "b": "Required at the field level (field definition)", "diff": "Layout-required is enforced only in the UI for users on that layout. Field-level required is enforced in every channel: UI, API, imports, Flow and Apex."}, {"a": "Page layout", "b": "Compact layout", "diff": "A page layout organizes the full record detail with fields, related lists and actions and is assigned by profile and record type. A compact layout selects the few key fields in the highlights panel, mobile header and previews, and is assigned per object or record type."}, {"a": "Page layout", "b": "Field-level security", "diff": "Hiding a field from a layout does not secure it: the user may still see it in reports or the API. Field-level security is what truly controls access."}], "signals": [{"if": "Different users need to see different fields or related lists on the same object", "then": "Create separate page layouts and assign them by profile (and record type)"}, {"if": "Different business processes on one object need different fields or picklist values", "then": "Record types, with a page layout assigned to each"}, {"if": "Key fields must appear at the top of the record or in the mobile app header", "then": "Compact layout (highlights panel)"}, {"if": "Field must be filled in by every channel, including API and import", "then": "Make the field required at the field definition level, not just on the layout"}], "traps": ["Making a field required on a page layout does not force it when data is loaded via the API, Data Loader or Flow.", "Removing a field from a page layout does not hide it from users: only field-level security truly restricts access.", "Compact layouts are not assigned by profile; they are assigned as the object's primary compact layout or per record type.", "Page layout assignment is per profile and record type combination, so changing one layout does not affect other combinations."], "scenario": {"situation": "At Contoso, sales reps must always enter a Contract End Date when creating Opportunities in the UI, but data imported by an integration also must contain it. Managers want Amount and Stage to appear at the top of the record in the mobile app.", "answer": "Make Contract End Date required at the field definition level so the integration is also forced to provide it; a layout-only requirement would not apply to the API. Create a compact layout with Amount and Stage as first fields and set it as the primary compact layout so it appears in the highlights panel and mobile header."}, "cards": [{"q": "Where do you edit page layouts in Lightning Experience?", "a": "Setup > Object Manager > the object > Page Layouts, using the page layout editor."}, {"q": "What two factors determine which page layout a user sees?", "a": "The user's profile and the record's record type, set in Page Layout Assignment."}, {"q": "What is the difference between a field required on a layout and required at the field level?", "a": "Layout-required applies only in the UI for that layout. Field-level required applies everywhere, including API and imports."}, {"q": "What does a compact layout control?", "a": "The key fields shown in the highlights panel, the Salesforce mobile app record header, hover details and card previews."}, {"q": "Does removing a field from a page layout secure it?", "a": "No. Field-level security controls access; layouts only control what is displayed on the page."}]}, "questions": [{"id": "06673cce515a4c4f921c64506606507a", "stem": "Northwind Logistics wants the Phone field on the Contact object to be mandatory only when sales reps create or edit Contacts through the Sales page layout. Records created through the API and Data Loader must still be saved without a phone number. What should the administrator do?", "options": ["Make the Phone field universally required in the field definition", "Create a validation rule that checks Phone is not blank", "Mark the Phone field as Required on the Sales page layout", "Mark the Phone field as Read-Only on the Sales page layout"], "correct": [2], "explanations": ["Incorrect. A universally required field is enforced everywhere, including the API and data imports, which would block the loads.", "Incorrect. A validation rule applies to all save operations, including API and Data Loader, unless bypassed, so it does not meet the requirement.", "Correct. A field marked Required on a page layout is enforced only in the UI for users assigned that layout, so API and Data Loader loads are not affected.", "Incorrect. Read-Only prevents users from editing the field and does not make it mandatory."], "takeaway": "Required on a page layout applies only to the UI for that layout, while a required field in the field definition or a validation rule applies everywhere."}, {"id": "80862326e8034c0a81e9b1add3abe7eb", "stem": "At Contoso Insurance, service agents use Salesforce on the Salesforce mobile app and complain that the top of the Case record is too crowded. The administrator wants to control which few key fields appear in the highlights panel at the top of the Case record page in Lightning Experience and in the mobile record header. What should the administrator configure?", "options": ["The Case search layout", "The compact layout assigned to the Case object", "The Case page layout related lists section", "A Lightning page with a Path component"], "correct": [1], "explanations": ["Incorrect. Search layouts control the columns shown in search results and list views, not the record header.", "Correct. A compact layout controls the fields shown in the highlights panel in Lightning Experience and the record header in the mobile app.", "Incorrect. The related lists section controls which related lists appear on the record, not the key fields in the highlights panel.", "Incorrect. Path shows stages and guidance for a record, not the highlights panel fields."], "takeaway": "Compact layouts define the key fields shown in the highlights panel and the mobile record header."}, {"id": "0c8749b0b93942c9967a10ef91db761d", "stem": "Fabrikam Energy has two Opportunity record types, New Business and Renewal. Sales Managers with the Sales Manager profile must see a layout that includes a Renewal Terms section only when working with Renewal opportunities, and a different layout for New Business. What should the administrator do?", "options": ["Create two compact layouts and assign them by profile", "Create two list views filtered by record type for the Sales Manager profile", "Create one page layout and assign it to the Sales Manager profile for all record types", "Create two page layouts and assign each to its record type for the Sales Manager profile"], "correct": [3], "explanations": ["Incorrect. Compact layouts control only the highlights panel fields, not the sections of the full record page.", "Incorrect. List views filter records in a list and do not change the layout of a record.", "Incorrect. One layout for all record types would show the same sections for New Business and Renewal.", "Correct. Page layout assignments are defined by the combination of profile and record type, so each record type can show its own layout."], "takeaway": "Page layouts are assigned per profile and record type combination."}, {"id": "f3ed95633f4143f0be121554068de4c1", "stem": "Adventure Works wants to simplify the Account page layout for its support team. The administrator edits the layout in the page layout editor. Which two changes can the administrator make directly in the page layout? (Choose the two that apply.)", "options": ["Add a new section and arrange fields in two columns", "Change the Account field's data type from Text to Number", "Remove a related list from the Account page", "Control which records the support team can see on Account", "Add a custom button to the Mobile & Lightning Actions section"], "correct": [0, 2], "explanations": ["Correct. The page layout editor lets administrators add sections and set one or two columns for fields.", "Incorrect. Data type is defined in the field definition, not in the page layout editor.", "Correct. Related lists can be added, removed, and reordered in the page layout editor.", "Incorrect. Record visibility is governed by sharing settings, not by page layouts.", "Incorrect as phrased. A custom button must first be created in the object's Buttons, Links, and Actions, and a button alone is not what the layout editor creates; the quick fit here is the layout structure options."], "takeaway": "Page layouts control the organization of fields, sections, related lists, and actions, but not data types or record access."}, {"id": "aedb9a6b9c6443e884d09cf048406fb1", "stem": "Tailspin Retail created a new compact layout for the Lead object with Name, Company, Phone, and Lead Status. Reps still see the old set of fields in the highlights panel. The administrator confirms the new compact layout is saved. Which two actions could resolve this? (Choose the two that apply.)", "options": ["Reassign the Lead page layout to the reps' profile", "Assign the compact layout to the relevant record types if record types use different compact layouts", "Create a validation rule on the Lead Status field", "Add the new compact layout to the Lead page layout's related lists", "Set the new compact layout as the primary compact layout for the Lead object"], "correct": [1, 4], "explanations": ["Incorrect. Compact layouts are not assigned through page layout assignments by profile.", "Correct. Compact layouts can be assigned per record type, so a record type may still use another compact layout.", "Incorrect. A validation rule has no effect on which fields appear in the highlights panel.", "Incorrect. Compact layouts are not added as related lists on page layouts.", "Correct. A new compact layout is used only after it is set as the primary compact layout for the object, through Compact Layout Assignment."], "takeaway": "A compact layout takes effect only when it is the primary compact layout for the object or assigned to the record type."}]}, {"id": "adm_lab", "title": "Lightning App Builder", "fiche": {"one_liner": "Lightning App Builder lets admins build Record, Home and App pages by dragging standard and custom components onto a layout, then assigning each page to the right users and show components only when filter conditions are met.", "need": "A software company's sales team sees every Opportunity page cluttered with fields and tabs. Admins want a leaner page for reps, with a Path, key fields and related Quotes, while Support sees a different layout. They also want a Contract Review component that appears only for deals above a given amount.", "core": "Lightning App Builder is the point-and-click (no code) tool for building Lightning pages. There are three main types. A Record Page (page d'enregistrement) controls how a single record of an object looks. A Home Page (page d'accueil) is the landing page users see in an app. An App Page (page d'application) is a custom page not tied to one record, often a dashboard-style hub for an app. Each page uses a template that defines regions (for example header, main region, sidebar), and you drop components into those regions.\n\nComponents are either standard (Salesforce-provided: Highlights Panel, Path, Related Lists, Report Chart, List View, Rich Text, Tabs, Accordion) or custom (built by developers as Lightning Web Components or Aura components, exposed for use in App Builder, or installed from AppExchange). Admins configure components but usually do not write them.\n\nA page does nothing until it is activated. For a Record Page, the Activation wizard offers three levels: Org Default (applies to everyone), App Default (applies when users are in a specific Lightning app), and App, Record Type and Profile (the most specific: one app, one record type, one profile). Salesforce uses the most specific assignment that matches. For Home Pages, assignment is by app and profile, or org default. App Pages are added to an app's navigation via the app's navigation items, not through activation by profile.\n\nComponent visibility rules (règles de visibilité) show or hide a component based on filters: field values on the record (such as Amount > 100000), user attributes (profile, role, permission, custom permission), or device type. Visibility affects only display on the page, not security: users can still access the data through other routes if their permissions allow it. Use profiles, permission sets and field-level security to actually restrict data.", "setup": ["Setup > User Interface > Lightning App Builder > New, then choose Record Page, Home Page or App Page and pick a template.", "Alternatively, open a record in an app, click the gear icon, then Edit Page to open App Builder on that object's page.", "Drag standard or custom components into regions and set their properties in the right-hand panel.", "Select a component, click Set Component Visibility, then Add Filter (record field, user, device and so on) to show it conditionally.", "Click Save, then Activation: choose Org Default, App Default, or App, Record Type, and Profile, and assign."], "compare": [{"a": "Lightning Record Page", "b": "Page Layout (mise en page)", "diff": "A page layout controls fields, sections, buttons and related lists. A Lightning Record Page controls the whole page structure and components. The Record Details component still uses the assigned page layout for its fields."}, {"a": "Component visibility filters", "b": "Field-level security or profile permissions", "diff": "Visibility filters only show or hide a component on a page for display purposes. Field-level security and permissions control actual access to data."}, {"a": "App Default", "b": "App, Record Type, and Profile", "diff": "App Default applies to every user of one app. App, Record Type, and Profile is the most specific and wins when several assignments match."}], "signals": [{"if": "Different page layout of components per profile, app or record type", "then": "Lightning Record Page with App, Record Type, and Profile activation"}, {"if": "Show a component only when Amount or Status meets a condition", "then": "Component visibility filter in Lightning App Builder"}, {"if": "Landing page for an app with a list view, report chart and tasks", "then": "Home Page or App Page built in Lightning App Builder"}, {"if": "Developer-built functionality placed on a page without code by the admin", "then": "Custom component (LWC or Aura) added in App Builder"}], "traps": ["Creating a Lightning page and saving it does not make users see it: it must be activated and assigned.", "Component visibility is a display rule, not a security control, so it cannot replace permissions or field-level security.", "Page layouts and Lightning Record Pages are different: changing a page layout does not rearrange components on the Lightning page.", "When several assignments apply, the most specific one wins (App, Record Type, and Profile over App Default over Org Default), not the one created last."], "scenario": {"situation": "Northwind sells hardware. Sales reps need a compact Opportunity page with Path and Quotes. Support users must keep the current page. A Contract Review component should appear only when Amount exceeds 50,000. The admin is working in Lightning App Builder.", "answer": "Build a new Opportunity Record Page with Path and Quotes, add a visibility filter on the Contract Review component (Amount > 50000), and activate it using App, Record Type, and Profile for the Sales profile. Support keeps the existing page because the assignment is limited to Sales."}, "cards": [{"q": "What are the three main page types in Lightning App Builder?", "a": "Record Page, Home Page and App Page."}, {"q": "What are the three activation levels for a Lightning Record Page?", "a": "Org Default, App Default, and App, Record Type, and Profile."}, {"q": "Which activation assignment takes priority when several match?", "a": "The most specific one: App, Record Type, and Profile."}, {"q": "What can component visibility filters be based on?", "a": "Record field values, user attributes (such as profile, role or permissions) and device type."}, {"q": "Do visibility filters secure data?", "a": "No. They only control what is displayed on the page. Real access is controlled by profiles, permission sets, sharing and field-level security."}]}, "questions": [{"id": "7e485342073847899864d98eb109f949", "stem": "Northwind Logistics wants sales reps to see a different layout on the Opportunity record page than the support team. Both groups use the same Lightning app, Sales Console. The admin has already built two Lightning record pages for Opportunity. What should the admin do to show each group its own page?", "options": ["Assign each page as an app, record type, and profile activation, selecting the Sales Console app and the relevant profile for each page", "Assign one Lightning page to the Sales Console app and ask the support team to use the Classic interface", "Create two page layouts and assign them to profiles, since Lightning pages ignore profile assignments", "Set one page as the org default and use component visibility filters to hide every component for the other team"], "correct": [0], "explanations": ["Correct. The Activation wizard lets you assign a Lightning page by app, record type, and profile, so each group gets its own page.", "Incorrect. Switching to Classic is not a solution and does not give different Lightning layouts to each group.", "Incorrect. Page layouts control fields and related lists, but Lightning pages can indeed be assigned by profile, so this premise is wrong.", "Incorrect. Visibility filters can hide components but would make one page serve both teams, and hiding everything for one team is not a layout strategy."], "takeaway": "Use the Lightning page Activation wizard to assign a record page by app, record type, and profile."}, {"id": "8b14b6f59d9644e0b270144dadaedd4b", "stem": "Contoso Energy wants a Lightning component that shows a warning banner on the Case record page only when the Priority field equals High. Other cases must not display the component. The admin has added the component to the Case record page in Lightning App Builder. What should the admin do?", "options": ["Edit the Case page layout and remove the component from the Priority section", "Create a validation rule on Case that displays the banner when Priority is High", "Create a second Case Lightning page and activate it for the High priority record type", "Set a component visibility filter on the component using the Priority field equals High"], "correct": [3], "explanations": ["Incorrect. Page layouts do not hold Lightning components placed in Lightning App Builder.", "Incorrect. Validation rules block saves with error messages; they do not control component display.", "Incorrect. Priority is a field, not a record type, so activation by record type cannot target it.", "Correct. Component visibility filters can show a component conditionally based on a record field value."], "takeaway": "Use component visibility filters to show or hide a component based on field values, user attributes, or device."}, {"id": "f367ae6c109e42b5bd1cde89056f4974", "stem": "Fabrikam Retail wants every user in the org to see the same custom Home page with a Key Deals list and an Assistant component, unless a different Home page is assigned to a specific app. What should the admin do?", "options": ["Add the page as a Lightning tab and ask users to pin it as their landing page", "Assign the page as the org default for the Home page in the Activation wizard", "Assign the page to every profile individually through profile settings", "Clone the standard Home page for each app and delete the original"], "correct": [1], "explanations": ["Incorrect. A tab does not replace the Home page experience for all users.", "Correct. Activating a Home page as the org default makes it the fallback for all users, while app or profile assignments can override it.", "Incorrect. This is tedious, and app-level or org default activation is the intended method for broad coverage.", "Incorrect. Cloning for each app is unnecessary and standard pages are not deleted this way."], "takeaway": "The org default activation applies a Lightning page to all users unless a more specific assignment overrides it."}, {"id": "41a5c71a4da543f28bd95278b0f2757c", "stem": "Adventure Works wants to build a Lightning page in Lightning App Builder to give users a customized layout. Which two page types can the admin create in Lightning App Builder? (Choose the correct options.)", "options": ["Workflow page", "Validation page", "Home page", "Approval page", "Record page"], "correct": [2, 4], "explanations": ["Incorrect. Workflow is not a Lightning page type, and workflow rules are retired.", "Incorrect. Validation is not a Lightning page type; validation rules are separate configuration.", "Correct. Home pages can be built and activated in Lightning App Builder.", "Incorrect. Approval processes are not a Lightning page type.", "Correct. Record pages are a core page type in Lightning App Builder, as are App pages."], "takeaway": "Lightning App Builder creates App pages, Home pages, and Record pages."}, {"id": "42063fc7ca534aef965922b155c70231", "stem": "Tailspin Consulting wants a Lightning record page for Account that shows a custom Lightning web component built by its developer, alongside standard components. The admin needs the component available in Lightning App Builder. Which two actions should the admin confirm? (Choose the correct options.)", "options": ["My Domain is deployed in the org", "The component is exposed for use in Lightning App Builder and is deployed to the org", "The component is installed only for the System Administrator profile", "The component must first be added to the Account page layout", "The component must be converted into a Visualforce page"], "correct": [0, 1], "explanations": ["Correct. My Domain is required for custom Lightning components to be used in the org, and is deployed by default in new orgs.", "Correct. A custom component must be deployed and configured as available for Lightning App Builder (exposed via its metadata).", "Incorrect. Profile-limited installation is not a requirement for appearing in App Builder.", "Incorrect. Lightning components are placed on Lightning pages, not on page layouts.", "Incorrect. Conversion to Visualforce is unnecessary and would defeat the purpose."], "takeaway": "Custom components appear in Lightning App Builder only when they are deployed, exposed for App Builder, and My Domain is deployed."}]}, {"id": "adm_dynamic", "title": "Formulaires et actions dynamiques", "fiche": {"one_liner": "Dynamic Forms lets you place fields and sections individually in Lightning App Builder and show or hide them by conditions; Dynamic Actions does the same for buttons in the Highlights Panel.", "need": "A bank's sales team has a single Opportunity page that is overloaded with fields. Loan officers should only see 'Collateral Details' when the Type is 'Secured Loan', and 'Escalate to Risk' should only appear for amounts above a threshold. Dynamic Forms and Dynamic Actions give each user a shorter, relevant page without creating multiple page layouts or record types.", "core": "With a classic page layout, fields live in the Fields tab's Record Detail component, which is governed by the page layout (the layout controls field order, sections, required and read-only settings). Dynamic Forms removes that limit: you upgrade the Lightning record page so fields and sections become individual components in Lightning App Builder (Salesforce's drag-and-drop page editor). You can then position each field anywhere on the page, for example in a tab, next to related lists or in a sidebar.\n\nVisibility is set with component visibility filters. On a field or a section you add a filter based on a field value on the record (Stage equals Negotiation), a user attribute (for example a permission or profile), or a device type. Filters can be combined with AND, OR or custom logic. Hiding a section hides every field inside it, so sections are the efficient way to handle groups of fields. Remember that visibility is not security: a hidden field is still accessible through reports, the API and so on. Field-level security (FLS) remains what truly protects data.\n\nDynamic Actions works the same way for buttons. In the Highlights Panel you can upgrade the action list so that each action gets its own visibility rule, for example showing 'Escalate to Risk' only when Amount is greater than a given value. This replaces the need to build several page layouts per profile just to vary the buttons.\n\nTo migrate, open the Lightning record page in App Builder, select the Record Detail component and click Upgrade Now. The wizard creates field sections based on your page layout. Dynamic Forms is supported for custom objects and now for many standard objects, including Account, Contact, Opportunity, Lead and Case. Always check the current availability for the object you need.", "setup": ["Setup > Object Manager > [Object] > Lightning Record Pages > select or create a page, then Edit to open Lightning App Builder.", "In App Builder, click the Record Detail component, then Upgrade Now in the properties pane, and follow the wizard (Dynamic Forms migration) to turn the page layout into individual fields and sections.", "Select a field or a section, then under Set Component Visibility click Add Filter. Choose Record Field, User, Permissions or Device, then define the condition and the filter logic.", "Select the Highlights Panel, then click Upgrade Now under Actions to enable Dynamic Actions. Choose an action, then add a filter in Set Action Visibility.", "Save and Activate the page (assign it as Org Default, App Default or App, Record Type and Profile)."], "compare": [{"a": "Dynamic Forms (field and section visibility rules on the Lightning record page)", "b": "Page layouts (Setup > Object Manager > Page Layouts)", "diff": "Page layouts apply to a profile and record type combination and cannot hide a field based on a field value. Dynamic Forms can show or hide fields conditionally on a single page and place them anywhere."}, {"a": "Component visibility (hides things on the page)", "b": "Field-level security (FLS)", "diff": "Visibility rules are only about display on that page, and data stays reachable through reports or API. FLS actually controls whether a user can see or edit the field."}, {"a": "Dynamic Actions (conditions on the buttons in the Highlights Panel)", "b": "Actions in the page layout's Salesforce Mobile and Lightning Experience Actions section", "diff": "Layout actions are static for everyone sharing the layout. Dynamic Actions show or hide each action based on record or user conditions."}], "signals": [{"if": "Show a field only when another field has a certain value", "then": "Dynamic Forms with a component visibility filter on the field or section"}, {"if": "Place fields individually, in tabs or outside the Record Detail component", "then": "Upgrade the record page to Dynamic Forms in Lightning App Builder"}, {"if": "A button must only appear for certain records or users", "then": "Dynamic Actions (action visibility filter in the Highlights Panel)"}, {"if": "Different users need different fields but the data must stay secure", "then": "Field-level security via profile or permission set, with Dynamic Forms only for display"}], "traps": ["Thinking that hiding a field with a visibility filter secures it: only field-level security protects data.", "Assuming Dynamic Forms still depends on the page layout: once upgraded, fields are placed on the Lightning record page, and the layout's field arrangement no longer drives the Record Detail.", "Forgetting to activate the Lightning record page after editing: users see no changes until it is assigned.", "Creating extra record types or page layouts just to hide a field conditionally, when a simple visibility filter is the intended answer."], "scenario": {"situation": "A support manager wants the 'Escalation Reason' field on the Case page to appear only when Priority is High. She also wants the 'Escalate' button to be visible only to users with the Tier 2 permission set. The company wants to avoid creating new page layouts.", "answer": "Upgrade the Case Lightning record page to Dynamic Forms and add a visibility filter on Escalation Reason: Priority equals High. Then enable Dynamic Actions in the Highlights Panel and filter the Escalate action on the user's permission. No new layouts are needed, and FLS remains the real data protection."}, "cards": [{"q": "What does Dynamic Forms allow that a page layout does not?", "a": "It lets you place fields and sections individually on the Lightning record page and show or hide them with visibility filters based on record fields, user attributes or device."}, {"q": "Where do you configure Dynamic Forms?", "a": "In Lightning App Builder, by editing a Lightning record page (Object Manager > Object > Lightning Record Pages) and using Upgrade Now on the Record Detail component."}, {"q": "Does a visibility filter on a field replace field-level security?", "a": "No. It only changes what is displayed on the page. FLS is what controls access to the data."}, {"q": "What is Dynamic Actions used for?", "a": "To show or hide actions in the Highlights Panel based on conditions such as a field value on the record or a user's permission."}, {"q": "What happens when you hide a section in Dynamic Forms?", "a": "All fields inside the section are hidden together, so sections are the efficient way to apply one rule to a group of fields."}]}, "questions": [{"id": "a48441ac2180436fa809ab7b9f611a6b", "stem": "Northwind Logistics wants the Account record page to show the Billing Dispute Notes field only when the Billing Status field equals 'Disputed'. The administrator is using Lightning App Builder and the page already uses Dynamic Forms for the Account object. What should the administrator do?", "options": ["Create a new page layout without the field and assign it to a record type used only for disputed accounts", "Select the Billing Dispute Notes field in the Fields section and set a component visibility filter on Billing Status equals Disputed", "Set field-level security on Billing Dispute Notes so it is hidden from users when the status is Disputed", "Add a validation rule that blocks saving the field unless Billing Status equals Disputed"], "correct": [1], "explanations": ["Incorrect. This is a heavy workaround that depends on record types, and it does not show or hide the field based on a field value on the same record.", "Correct. With Dynamic Forms, each field is a component on the page and can have its own visibility filter based on a field value.", "Incorrect. Field-level security applies to profiles and permission sets, not to the values on a record, and it cannot be conditional.", "Incorrect. A validation rule controls what can be saved, not whether a field is displayed."], "takeaway": "In Dynamic Forms, set a visibility filter on a field or section to show it based on record field values, user attributes, or permissions."}, {"id": "5c6e4b16e37c4594a8014468ce52483a", "stem": "Contoso Energy uses a Dynamic Forms-enabled Opportunity page. Fifteen fields about contract negotiation should appear together, but only when Stage is 'Negotiation/Review'. The administrator wants to avoid configuring the same rule on each field. What is the best approach?", "options": ["Apply the same visibility filter to each of the fifteen fields individually", "Create a compact layout containing the fifteen fields for the Negotiation/Review stage", "Put the fields in a Dynamic Forms section and apply one visibility rule to the section", "Create a Path with guidance for success on the Negotiation/Review stage listing the fields"], "correct": [2], "explanations": ["Incorrect. This works but is repetitive and harder to maintain, so it is not the best approach.", "Incorrect. Compact layouts control the highlights and mobile summary, not conditional display of a group of fields.", "Correct. A section is a container component, and a visibility filter on it controls all the fields inside.", "Incorrect. Path guidance displays text and key fields on the path, but does not hide or show fields by condition on the form."], "takeaway": "Use section-level visibility in Dynamic Forms to show or hide a group of fields with a single rule."}, {"id": "a7de1060987b41b9b8560d2cdf81be06", "stem": "Fabrikam Services wants a custom 'Escalate Case' action button to appear in the highlights panel of the Case record page only for Cases where Priority is 'High'. The page is a Lightning record page. What should the administrator configure?", "options": ["Create a validation rule on Case that prevents the flow from running unless Priority is High", "Create a record type for high priority Cases and assign it a separate page layout with the action", "Add a conditional visibility filter to the action in the Highlights Panel's Dynamic Actions configuration", "Remove the action from the page layout and add it back when Priority changes"], "correct": [2], "explanations": ["Incorrect. A validation rule would not hide the button and would only produce an error after the user tries to use it.", "Incorrect. This is a legacy workaround that ties display to record types rather than to the Priority value, and adds unnecessary maintenance.", "Correct. Dynamic Actions lets you add actions to the highlights panel and set visibility filters based on record fields.", "Incorrect. Page layouts are static and cannot change automatically as field values change."], "takeaway": "Use Dynamic Actions to display actions conditionally based on record field values, user, or device."}, {"id": "01064cb836da4aa0ba486b4e31b88710", "stem": "Adventure Works is migrating its Lightning record page for Contact from a page layout to Dynamic Forms using the Dynamic Forms migration tool in Lightning App Builder. Which two statements about this process are true?", "options": ["Migrating the page requires Apex code to rebuild the field list", "The migration permanently deletes the page layout, so it can no longer be assigned to any profile", "The tool can migrate fields and sections from the existing page layout onto the Lightning page", "Once the page is migrated, fields and sections can be individually given visibility rules", "Migration automatically creates visibility rules matching the existing record type assignments"], "correct": [2, 3], "explanations": ["Incorrect. The migration is a point-and-click tool in Lightning App Builder and needs no code.", "Incorrect. The page layout is not deleted by the migration; it still exists in Setup and can still be used for other purposes such as related lists and actions in some contexts.", "Correct. The migration wizard brings the fields and sections of the selected page layout into the Lightning page as Dynamic Forms components.", "Correct. After migration, each field and section is a component with its own visibility filters.", "Incorrect. Visibility rules are not generated automatically from record types; the administrator adds them."], "takeaway": "The Dynamic Forms migration tool converts a page layout's fields and sections into configurable components, after which the administrator adds visibility rules."}, {"id": "41851e47c3b146679fabd4d85c9f939b", "stem": "Tailspin Toys has a Dynamic Forms-enabled Lead page. Managers want the Discount Approval Notes field visible only to users with a specific custom permission, and the field should also be hidden when Lead Status is 'Closed - Not Converted'. Which two capabilities of Dynamic Forms support this requirement?", "options": ["Visibility filters can only be based on the user's profile name", "Visibility filters can reference values from unrelated objects with no relationship", "Visibility filters can be based on a field value on the record", "Visibility rules can only be set at the page level, not on individual fields", "Visibility filters can be based on the user's custom permission"], "correct": [2, 4], "explanations": ["Incorrect. Filters are not limited to profile names; they can use several types of criteria.", "Incorrect. Filters cannot use data from unrelated objects; they use the current record, its related lookup fields, or user context.", "Correct. Filters can be built from fields on the record, such as Lead Status.", "Incorrect. Visibility can be set on individual fields and sections, not just the whole page.", "Correct. Filters can use user permissions, including custom permissions, to decide who sees a component."], "takeaway": "Dynamic Forms visibility filters can combine record field values and user criteria such as permissions to control who sees what."}]}, {"id": "adm_apps", "title": "Applications Lightning et navigation", "fiche": {"one_liner": "A Lightning app (a set of tabs, branding and navigation built in App Manager) is assigned to profiles so each team sees only its own tools, in Standard or Console navigation.", "need": "A company has sales reps, support agents and finance staff, all using one org. Reps need Leads, Opportunities and Accounts, while agents need Cases and Knowledge, handling many records at once. Lightning apps give each team a focused workspace with its own tabs, color and layout, instead of one overloaded menu for everyone.", "core": "A Lightning app is a collection of items (objects, Lightning pages, utility items, and other tabs) packaged to support a specific job. You create and manage apps in App Manager (Setup > Apps > App Manager). Salesforce provides standard apps such as Sales and Service, and you can build custom ones. The app defines the navigation bar items, the branding (name, logo, color), and the utility bar.\n\nA tab (onglet) is the entry point to an object's records or to a page. Tabs are of several types: Custom Object Tabs, Web Tabs, Visualforce Tabs, Lightning Page Tabs and Lightning Component Tabs. Tab visibility is set by a tab setting on profiles or permission sets (Default On, Default Off, Tab Hidden). Default Off means the tab is not displayed first but the user can add it through personalization. A tab hidden in a profile does not appear in the app, even if you added it to the app.\n\nNavigation style is a key choice. Standard navigation opens each record in its own page, with one tab at a time. Console navigation shows a workspace with record tabs and subtabs, plus a list view split-pane, designed for users who handle many records quickly, such as support agents. Navigation style is set when you create or edit the app.\n\nThe utility bar is a footer bar giving quick access to tools such as Notes, Recent Items, History, or Open CTI softphone, without leaving the current page. Utilities are added in the app's settings.\n\nFinally, an app is made visible by assigning it to profiles (User Interface step of the app wizard, Assign to Profiles). Users with several apps switch using the App Launcher (lanceur d'applications). Branding lets you set the app's image and primary color, and you can use the org's theme settings for the overall look.", "setup": ["Setup > Apps > App Manager > New Lightning App: enter the app name, developer name, description, image and primary color (branding).", "In the wizard, choose App Options: Standard navigation or Console navigation, and optional settings such as Setup Experience.", "Add Utility Items (for example Recent Items or Notes) and set the utility bar's properties.", "Select Navigation Items (tabs and objects) the app will display, then use Assign to Profiles to grant access.", "Setup > Object Manager or Profiles/Permission Sets: control tab visibility if a tab does not appear as expected."], "compare": [{"a": "Standard navigation", "b": "Console navigation", "diff": "Standard opens one record at a time in a page; Console uses a workspace with tabs and subtabs for users handling many records and related records at once, such as agents in a call center."}, {"a": "Navigation bar tabs of the app", "b": "Utility bar", "diff": "Tabs in the navigation bar open object or page content as the main screen; the utility bar is a persistent footer of quick tools that stay available across pages in the app."}, {"a": "App assignment to profiles", "b": "Tab visibility settings", "diff": "Assigning the app makes it available to a profile; tab settings decide whether individual tabs show. Both must permit access for a user to see the tab."}], "signals": [{"if": "Support agents work many cases at once and need to switch between records quickly", "then": "Lightning app with Console navigation"}, {"if": "Quick access to a softphone, notes or recent items at the bottom of every page", "then": "Utility bar"}, {"if": "Show a specific team only its own set of objects, with its own logo and color", "then": "Custom Lightning app in App Manager, assigned to profiles"}, {"if": "A user needs to move between several apps", "then": "App Launcher"}], "traps": ["Creating an app does not make it visible: it must be assigned to profiles, otherwise most users will not see it.", "Adding a tab to an app does not override the profile: if the tab is set to Tab Hidden, the user will not see it.", "Console navigation is a navigation style chosen on the app, not a separate product or license.", "The utility bar is an app-level feature set up in App Manager, not an object setting or a page layout element."], "scenario": {"situation": "A support center manager says agents must open several cases, contacts and related records at the same time without losing their place. Agents should also have a softphone always available. Sales reps must not see this app.", "answer": "Create a custom Lightning app in App Manager with Console navigation, add the Open CTI Softphone to the utility bar, and assign it only to the support agents' profile. Console lets them work with tabs and subtabs, and profile assignment keeps sales reps from seeing it."}, "cards": [{"q": "Where do you create and manage Lightning apps?", "a": "In Setup > Apps > App Manager, using the New Lightning App wizard."}, {"q": "What are the two navigation styles for a Lightning app?", "a": "Standard navigation (one record page at a time) and Console navigation (workspace with tabs and subtabs for handling many records)."}, {"q": "What is the utility bar?", "a": "A footer bar in an app that gives quick access to tools such as Notes, Recent Items or a softphone without leaving the current page."}, {"q": "How do you make an app available to a team?", "a": "Assign it to the relevant profiles in the app's Assign to Profiles step (or edit the app later in App Manager)."}, {"q": "What are the three tab visibility settings on a profile?", "a": "Default On, Default Off and Tab Hidden. Default Off means the tab is available but not shown first; Tab Hidden removes it."}]}, "questions": [{"id": "a28ba406d72046dabefe8d0e19605de2", "stem": "Northwind Logistics wants its support agents to work on many cases at once. Agents need to open cases, contacts, and accounts as workspace tabs with related records in subtabs, all within a single Lightning app. The administrator is creating the app in App Manager. Which navigation style should the administrator select?", "options": ["Salesforce Mobile App navigation only", "Standard navigation", "Lightning Page navigation", "Console navigation"], "correct": [3], "explanations": ["Mobile-only is not a navigation style option in App Manager. It would not give agents the multi-tab desktop workspace they need.", "Standard navigation opens each record in the same browser view, replacing the previous page. It does not provide workspace tabs and subtabs.", "Lightning Page is not a navigation style. Lightning pages control the layout of a record, home, or app page.", "Console navigation displays records as workspace tabs with subtabs, which suits agents who handle many records at once."], "takeaway": "Choose Console navigation in App Manager when users need workspace tabs and subtabs to work on multiple records at once."}, {"id": "fed5159dbdc84d4f852cfbf93280f360", "stem": "Contoso Energy created a new Lightning app named Field Service Hub. After saving it, the administrator notices that sales reps, who should not use it, can see it in the App Launcher, while the field technicians cannot. Which action should the administrator take to make the app available only to technicians?", "options": ["Add the app to the Home page layout for the Field Technician role", "Create a permission set that grants Read access to the Field Service Hub object", "Edit the app in App Manager and assign it to the Field Technician profile on the User Profiles step", "Remove the app from the App Launcher using the Setup search box"], "correct": [2], "explanations": ["Home page layouts do not control app visibility. Roles also do not determine which apps users see.", "Object permissions control data access, not app visibility, and an app is not an object.", "App assignment to profiles is configured in the app's User Profiles step in App Manager. This controls who can see and use the app.", "Setup search does not manage app visibility. Visibility is set through profile assignment on the app."], "takeaway": "Control which users see a Lightning app by assigning it to profiles in the app's settings in App Manager."}, {"id": "7272ed0ee6764f5abe10ba7eff5ce4c6", "stem": "Fabrikam Retail wants service agents to see a Notes utility and a Recent Items utility at the bottom of their screen in the Service Console app, so they can access them from any page. The administrator opens the app in App Manager. Which step should the administrator edit?", "options": ["Navigation Items", "App Options", "Utility Items", "Select Items"], "correct": [2], "explanations": ["Navigation Items defines the tabs that appear in the app's navigation bar, not the utility bar at the bottom.", "App Options sets the navigation style, form factor, and similar settings, not which utilities are included.", "The Utility Items step is where utilities such as Notes and Recent Items are added to the utility bar.", "Select Items is not a step in the Lightning app wizard. Items are chosen in Navigation Items and Utility Items."], "takeaway": "Add utilities like Notes or Recent Items to the utility bar through the Utility Items step of the app in App Manager."}, {"id": "e30b80fff69847ab9b097e99f53317f6", "stem": "Adventure Works wants its Sales app to reflect corporate branding. The administrator must change the app's logo and set the navigation bar color to the company's brand color. Which two actions should the administrator take? (Select the correct options.)", "options": ["Update the company logo on the Company Information page in Setup", "Edit the app in App Manager and set the Org Theme Color to the brand color", "Create a custom theme in Themes and Branding and assign it to the profile", "Edit the Home page layout and add a Rich Text component with the logo", "Edit the app in App Manager and upload an image for the app logo"], "correct": [1, 4], "explanations": ["Company Information holds org details such as the company name and locale, not the Lightning app logo or navigation bar color.", "The Branding step also lets the administrator set the Org Theme Color, which colors the app's navigation bar.", "Themes and Branding in Setup is for Salesforce Classic themes and not for per-app branding in Lightning apps, and themes are not assigned to profiles.", "A Rich Text component on a Home page changes page content only. It does not brand the app or its navigation bar.", "The app's Branding step in App Manager lets the administrator upload an image to use as the app logo."], "takeaway": "Per-app logo and navigation bar color are set in the app's Branding settings in App Manager."}, {"id": "e7d3216df0da4e61950ab63a5fe26a21", "stem": "Tailspin Consulting has a custom Lightning app. Users complain that the Projects tab is missing from the app's navigation bar, even though the Projects object exists and users have access to it. The administrator wants every user of the app to see the Projects tab by default. Which two actions should the administrator take? (Select the correct options.)", "options": ["Create a validation rule on Projects to display the tab", "Add the Projects object to the Utility Bar of the app", "Make sure the Projects tab setting is not Hidden on the profiles of the app's users", "Add the Projects tab to the app's selected items in the Navigation Items step in App Manager", "Change the app navigation style to Console"], "correct": [2, 3], "explanations": ["Validation rules enforce data quality and have no effect on tab visibility.", "The utility bar hosts utilities such as Notes or History, not object tabs for navigation.", "A tab set to Tab Hidden on a profile or permission set will not show for those users, so the setting must permit visibility.", "The tab must be included in the app's Navigation Items to appear in its navigation bar.", "Switching to Console changes how records open, but a tab still must be in Navigation Items to appear."], "takeaway": "For a tab to appear in a Lightning app, it must be in the app's Navigation Items and not hidden by the user's tab settings."}]}]}, {"id": "ventes", "label": "Applications de ventes et de marketing", "weight": 10, "topics": [{"id": "adm_leads", "title": "Les pistes", "fiche": {"one_liner": "Leads capture unqualified prospects (Web-to-Lead), route them via one active assignment rule to users or queues, acknowledge them with auto-response rules, then convert them into Account, Contact and Opportunity.", "need": "A software company runs a campaign and a 'Contact us' form on its website. Hundreds of unqualified prospects arrive weekly. They must be captured without manual entry, routed to the right salesperson by region, acknowledged by email immediately, and, once qualified, turned into customer records without retyping any data.", "core": "A lead is an unqualified prospect, kept separate from Accounts, Contacts and Opportunities so that your customer data stays clean. Web-to-Lead generates an HTML form that you paste on your website. Each submission creates a lead in Salesforce. Web-to-Lead has a daily limit on submissions, so for very high volumes the exam expects you to know it is not unlimited. Set a Default Lead Creator and, if desired, a response template.\n\nLead assignment rules decide who owns a new lead. A rule contains ordered rule entries (criteria such as Country = France), and the first matching entry sets the owner, which can be a user or a queue. Only ONE assignment rule can be active at a time. A queue (Queue) is a shared holding place for records: several users can be members and anyone in the queue can take ownership of a lead. Queues can be used for leads, cases and custom objects that you enable. Assignment rules apply to leads from Web-to-Lead automatically if you choose to; for manual or imported records you must tick 'Assign using active assignment rule'.\n\nAuto-response rules send an email to the lead right after it is created from Web-to-Lead (and Web-to-Case for cases). They also use ordered rule entries with criteria and an email template. Only one auto-response rule can be active per object.\n\nConverting a lead creates an Account, a Contact and, optionally, an Opportunity, and the lead is marked converted. You can attach the contact to an existing account. Standard lead fields map automatically. For custom lead fields, you must configure Lead Field Mapping: map each custom lead field to a custom field on Account, Contact or Opportunity. Unmapped data is not carried over, and converted leads cannot be edited like normal ones.", "setup": ["Setup > Feature Settings > Marketing > Web-to-Lead: enable it, then create the form (Create Web-to-Lead Form) and paste the HTML on the website", "Setup > Feature Settings > Marketing > Lead Assignment Rules: create a rule, add ordered rule entries, assign to a user or queue, then activate it", "Setup > Feature Settings > Marketing > Lead Auto-Response Rules: create the rule, add entries with an email template, activate it", "Setup > Users > Queues: create a queue, choose Lead as a supported object, add members", "Setup > Object Manager > Lead > Fields & Relationships > Map Lead Fields: map custom lead fields to Account, Contact or Opportunity fields"], "compare": [{"a": "Assignment rule (règle d'attribution)", "b": "Auto-response rule (règle de réponse automatique)", "diff": "Assignment rules choose the OWNER of a lead internally. Auto-response rules send an EMAIL to the person who submitted the form. Each allows only one active rule."}, {"a": "Queue (file d'attente)", "b": "Public group", "diff": "A queue owns records and members can take them from it. A public group only shares access or is used in sharing and list targeting, and cannot own records."}], "signals": [{"if": "Website form that creates leads automatically", "then": "Web-to-Lead"}, {"if": "Route leads by country or product interest", "then": "Lead assignment rule with ordered rule entries"}, {"if": "Send a confirmation email to a new web lead", "then": "Auto-response rule"}, {"if": "Leads shared by a team, first available rep takes it", "then": "Queue as lead owner"}], "traps": ["Only one lead assignment rule can be active at a time, so you cannot activate one per region; use several rule entries in one rule instead.", "Custom lead fields are NOT copied at conversion unless you map them in Map Lead Fields.", "Imported or manually created leads are not routed by assignment rules unless you explicitly choose to use the active rule.", "A queue can own a lead but is not a user: it cannot log in, and members need access to the object to work the records."], "scenario": {"situation": "A company wants leads from its website form routed to the French sales team if Country is France and to a shared queue otherwise. Each web lead must receive a thank-you email. Data from a custom field 'Budget' must appear on the Opportunity after conversion.", "answer": "Use Web-to-Lead for capture. Create one lead assignment rule with two entries (France to the French team, others to the queue) and activate it. Add an auto-response rule with an email template. Map the custom Budget lead field to an Opportunity field in Map Lead Fields."}, "cards": [{"q": "How many lead assignment rules can be active at once?", "a": "Only one. Use multiple ordered rule entries inside that rule to cover different routing criteria."}, {"q": "What does lead conversion create?", "a": "An Account, a Contact and, optionally, an Opportunity. The lead is then marked as converted."}, {"q": "What must you do so a custom lead field is transferred at conversion?", "a": "Map it in Setup > Object Manager > Lead > Map Lead Fields to a custom field on Account, Contact or Opportunity."}, {"q": "What is the difference between a queue and a user as lead owner?", "a": "A queue is a shared owner: its members can claim the lead. A user is an individual owner."}, {"q": "What does an auto-response rule do?", "a": "It sends an email template automatically to the person who submitted a Web-to-Lead form, based on ordered rule entries; only one rule can be active."}]}, "questions": [{"id": "0d75284da5c644d6bdc573524f084901", "stem": "Northwind Solar wants to capture leads from a landing page on its public website. Each form submission must create a Lead record in Salesforce without any manual data entry. The marketing team has no developers. What should the administrator do?", "options": ["Configure a Data Import Wizard job that runs each time a visitor submits the form", "Generate a Web-to-Lead HTML form from Setup and publish it on the landing page", "Set up Email-to-Case routing so form submissions are converted into Lead records", "Create a Screen Flow and embed it on the public website for visitors"], "correct": [1], "explanations": ["Incorrect. The Data Import Wizard is a manual, on-demand import tool and cannot be triggered by a form submission.", "Correct. Web-to-Lead generates HTML code that creates Lead records directly from a web form, with no coding required.", "Incorrect. Email-to-Case creates Case records from incoming emails, not Lead records from a web form.", "Incorrect. Screen Flows are meant for authenticated users and are not the standard way to capture leads from a public website."], "takeaway": "Web-to-Lead generates an HTML form that creates Lead records from a public website without code."}, {"id": "e53b6cd2e2ff4aad813a87442f79aa76", "stem": "Contoso Fitness has three lead assignment rules in its org: one for web leads, one for trade show leads, and one for partner referrals. The administrator wants to activate the trade show rule, which is currently inactive, while the web leads rule is still active. What will happen when the trade show rule is activated?", "options": ["The trade show rule is applied only to leads created through the API", "Both rules run, and the one with the lowest sort order is applied first", "The trade show rule is activated and the web leads rule is automatically deactivated", "Salesforce blocks activation until the administrator deletes the other rules"], "correct": [2], "explanations": ["Incorrect. An active rule is not restricted by creation channel; its use depends on how the assignment is triggered.", "Incorrect. Only one lead assignment rule can be active at a time, so rules are not evaluated together.", "Correct. Only one lead assignment rule can be active at a time, so activating one deactivates the previously active rule.", "Incorrect. Inactive rules can coexist with the active one and do not need to be deleted."], "takeaway": "Only one lead assignment rule can be active at a time, and activating another one deactivates the current one."}, {"id": "8333b4b69f534509a62b0dc131e3b709", "stem": "Fabrikam Logistics receives leads from its website. The sales director wants any lead from the Healthcare industry to be routed to a shared pool of reps, where any team member can claim ownership of the record. What should the administrator configure?", "options": ["A role hierarchy branch for Healthcare so managers receive the leads", "A validation rule that prevents leads from being saved without an owner", "A lead queue and a lead assignment rule entry that assigns Healthcare leads to that queue", "A public group and a sharing rule that gives the team edit access to Healthcare leads"], "correct": [2], "explanations": ["Incorrect. The role hierarchy controls visibility upward, not the routing of new leads to a shared pool.", "Incorrect. A validation rule would block saves and does not route leads to anyone.", "Correct. Queues can own Lead records, and a rule entry in the active assignment rule can assign matching leads to the queue for reps to claim.", "Incorrect. Sharing rules grant access to records but do not set record ownership or create a pool where reps claim leads."], "takeaway": "Use a queue as the owner and a lead assignment rule entry to route matching leads to a shared pool."}, {"id": "bb09462970c54632b22a1cb2714540f0", "stem": "Tailspin Consulting wants to send an acknowledgment email automatically to each person who submits its Web-to-Lead form, and also to leads created manually. The email should use a template that includes the lead's first name. Which two actions should the administrator take?", "options": ["Create a lead assignment rule that sends the template to the lead", "Create an email template with merge fields for the lead's name", "Create a queue that sends the template to the lead's email address", "Create a Web-to-Case form that sends the template to the submitter", "Create an auto-response rule with a rule entry that uses the template"], "correct": [1, 4], "explanations": ["Incorrect. Assignment rules determine ownership, and they can notify the new owner, but they do not send the acknowledgment email to the lead.", "Correct. A template with merge fields provides the personalized content for the response.", "Incorrect. Queues own records and do not send email to leads.", "Incorrect. Web-to-Case creates Cases, not Leads, and does not address this requirement.", "Correct. Lead auto-response rules send a template to the lead's email address based on rule entries and criteria."], "takeaway": "Lead auto-response rules send a template to the lead's email address, and the template supplies the personalized content."}, {"id": "ea4023c1e6aa4147974adf1c25c0c6e6", "stem": "Adventure Works uses a custom field Preferred Contact Time on Lead. After converting a lead, the sales team notices the value does not appear on the new Contact, although they want it to. The administrator must fix this. Which two actions are required?", "options": ["Create a custom field on Contact of a compatible data type", "Create a record-triggered flow that runs when a Lead is deleted", "Add the field to the Lead page layout in the converted lead section", "Enable Lead Conversion in the Account object settings", "Map the Lead custom field to the Contact field in Lead Field Mapping"], "correct": [0, 4], "explanations": ["Correct. A target custom field on Contact must exist with a compatible data type before it can be mapped.", "Incorrect. Converted leads are not deleted, and a flow is not needed to carry over mapped data.", "Incorrect. Page layouts control field display, not the data transfer during conversion.", "Incorrect. There is no such setting on Account, and conversion already works for mapped fields.", "Correct. Custom lead fields only carry data into Account, Contact, or Opportunity when mapped in Lead Field Mapping."], "takeaway": "To carry a custom Lead field through conversion, create a compatible target field and map it in Lead Field Mapping."}]}, {"id": "adm_opps", "title": "Opportunités, étapes et Chemin", "fiche": {"one_liner": "Opportunities move through Stages tied to a Sales Process; each Stage sets a default Probability and Forecast Category, and Path guides reps through the Stages with key fields and guidance.", "need": "A software reseller like Northwind Systems wants every rep to follow the same steps from first meeting to signed contract. Managers need reliable forecasts, and new reps need reminders of what to complete at each step. Stages, Probability, Path and Kanban deliver this, while Opportunity Teams and Splits share credit when several people work a deal.", "core": "An Opportunity (a potential sale) has a Stage that shows where it is in the sales cycle, for example Prospecting, Qualification, Proposal, Negotiation, Closed Won, Closed Lost. Each Stage value has a default Probability (%) and is mapped to a Forecast Category (such as Pipeline, Best Case, Commit, Closed, Omitted). Probability drives Expected Revenue (Amount x Probability). Reps can override Probability on a record unless you restrict it, but changing the Stage resets it to that Stage's default.\n\nA Sales Process (an admin setting that is not the same as a business process in general) decides which Stage values are available for a record type. You create one Sales Process, select the Stage values it includes, then assign it to an Opportunity Record Type. Different products or teams can then see different Stages. A Sales Process is selected when you create a Record Type, so plan it before you build.\n\nPath is the visual chevron bar at the top of the record. For each Stage you choose up to 5 Key Fields to show and add Guidance for Success, which is rich-text coaching. It is configured per Record Type and works on Opportunities, Leads and others. The Kanban view on the Opportunity list view shows Stages as columns so reps can drag deals between Stages and see summed totals. It needs a list view with a field to group by, typically Stage.\n\nOpportunity Teams let several users share an Opportunity with defined Team Roles and access levels. Opportunity Splits divide credit among team members. Revenue Splits must total 100% and credit the full Amount. Overlay Splits can total more or less than 100% and are meant for specialists, such as a solutions engineer, who get extra credit without reducing the owner's credit.", "setup": ["Setup > Opportunity Settings: enable Team Selling and Opportunity Splits (Team Selling must be on before Splits).", "Setup > Object Manager > Opportunity > Fields & Relationships > Stage > Values: add Stages, set Probability and Forecast Category, and manage Sales Processes under Setup > Feature Settings > Sales > Sales Processes.", "Setup > Object Manager > Opportunity > Record Types: create a Record Type and choose the Sales Process it uses.", "Setup > Path Settings: create a Path for the Opportunity object and Record Type, add up to 5 Key Fields and Guidance for Success to each Stage, then activate it.", "Setup > Opportunity Settings: enable Kanban settings (Kanban is available from the list view) and review Opportunity Splits types and Team Selling."], "compare": [{"a": "Revenue Split", "b": "Overlay Split", "diff": "Revenue Splits must add up to 100% of the Amount and affect forecasts. Overlay Splits can add up to more than 100% and give extra credit to supporting roles without taking it from the owner."}, {"a": "Sales Process", "b": "Path", "diff": "A Sales Process controls which Stage values are available for a Record Type. Path is only the visual display of those Stages, with Key Fields and Guidance. Path cannot add Stages by itself."}, {"a": "Probability", "b": "Forecast Category", "diff": "Probability is the percentage chance used for Expected Revenue. Forecast Category groups the Stage for forecast rollups. Both default from the Stage, but they are different fields."}], "signals": [{"if": "Different Stages for different product lines or business units", "then": "Create separate Sales Processes and Record Types"}, {"if": "Remind reps what to do or fill in at each Stage", "then": "Path with Key Fields and Guidance for Success"}, {"if": "Drag deals between Stages and see totals visually", "then": "Kanban view on an Opportunity list view"}, {"if": "Specialist should get credit without reducing the owner's credit", "then": "Overlay Split (Opportunity Splits)"}], "traps": ["Revenue Splits must total 100%, but Overlay Splits do not have to, so do not mix them up.", "A Path does not enforce required data. Use validation rules or page layout required fields if a field must be filled before moving on.", "Stage values are chosen through a Sales Process attached to a Record Type, not directly on the Path.", "Team Selling must be enabled before Opportunity Splits can be used."], "scenario": {"situation": "Contoso sells both hardware and consulting. Hardware deals pass through Quote and Delivery Stages, while consulting deals use Scoping and Proposal. Managers also want reps to see key fields and tips on each Stage, and a solutions engineer should earn credit on large deals without reducing the rep's credit.", "answer": "Create two Sales Processes and two Opportunity Record Types, one for each line of business. Build a Path for each Record Type with Key Fields and Guidance for Success. Enable Team Selling and Opportunity Splits, then give the solutions engineer an Overlay Split so that credit can exceed 100% without reducing the owner's Revenue Split."}, "cards": [{"q": "What does a Stage determine by default on an Opportunity?", "a": "Its Probability and Forecast Category. Expected Revenue is Amount x Probability."}, {"q": "How do you give different Opportunities different Stage values?", "a": "Create Sales Processes with selected Stage values and assign each to an Opportunity Record Type."}, {"q": "What can Path show on each Stage?", "a": "Up to 5 Key Fields and Guidance for Success text, configured per Record Type."}, {"q": "What is the difference between Revenue Splits and Overlay Splits?", "a": "Revenue Splits total 100% of the Amount. Overlay Splits can total over or under 100% and give extra credit to supporting team members."}, {"q": "What must be enabled before Opportunity Splits?", "a": "Team Selling (Opportunity Teams)."}]}, "questions": [{"id": "29b8cfe5e33f486f8bdb56513d046e05", "stem": "Northwind Solar wants sales reps to see Amount and Close Date at the top of every opportunity record, and to see coaching tips such as 'Confirm the budget owner' while an opportunity is in the Qualification stage. The tips must differ for each stage. What should the administrator do?", "options": ["Add a validation rule on each stage that displays the coaching tips when a rep saves the record.", "Create a compact layout that includes Amount and Close Date and assign it to the Opportunity object.", "Create a Path for the Opportunity object and add key fields and Guidance for Success to each stage.", "Switch the opportunity list view to Kanban and add a description to each column."], "correct": [2], "explanations": ["Validation rules block saves with error messages. They are not designed to coach users, and they would only appear on save.", "A compact layout controls the highlights panel and mobile cards, but it cannot show stage-specific guidance text.", "Path lets the admin select key fields and write Guidance for Success for each stage, shown above the record as the rep moves through the stages.", "Kanban is a list view display. It does not provide per-stage guidance on the record page, and columns have no description feature."], "takeaway": "Use Path with key fields and Guidance for Success to show stage-specific fields and tips on the record."}, {"id": "336802b027474b5a87785975f914b588", "stem": "Contoso Telecom sells hardware and managed services. Hardware deals use the stages Prospecting, Quote, Negotiation and Closed Won. Services deals use Discovery, Proposal, Pilot and Closed Won. Reps must see only the stages that apply to the type of deal they create. What should the administrator configure?", "options": ["Two Paths on the Opportunity object that each display a different set of stages from one shared sales process.", "One sales process containing all stages, with a field dependency that hides stages by product type.", "One record type and two page layouts, each layout showing a different subset of the Stage picklist.", "Two sales processes, each with its own stages, and a separate opportunity record type linked to each one."], "correct": [3], "explanations": ["A Path only presents the stages that the sales process already makes available. It cannot give two record types different stage lists on its own.", "Field dependencies work between a controlling and a dependent field. Stage values for opportunities are limited through sales processes, not through this approach.", "Page layouts cannot filter picklist values. Stage values are controlled by the sales process attached to the record type.", "A sales process defines which Stage values are available, and it is linked to opportunities through a record type. Each deal type needs its own pair."], "takeaway": "Different Stage picklist values per type of deal require separate sales processes, each assigned through an opportunity record type."}, {"id": "b95cdac412bf45b096b94fc0caedf903", "stem": "At Fabrikam Cloud, an account executive closes a deal worth 100,000 dollars. A solutions engineer and a partner manager must each receive quota credit of 100 percent of that amount for their contribution, in addition to the account executive's own credit. The administrator enables Opportunity Splits. Which split type should be used for the specialists?", "options": ["Revenue splits, as long as the opportunity owner's percentage is set to 0 percent.", "Overlay splits, because the percentages can total more than 100 percent of the Amount.", "Revenue splits, because they allow each team member to be credited for the full Amount.", "Opportunity Teams without splits, because team roles automatically divide the credit."], "correct": [1], "explanations": ["Setting the owner to 0 percent would still leave the revenue total at 100 percent across all members, and the owner would lose credit.", "Overlay splits are meant for team members who support a sale, and their percentages can add up to more than 100 percent.", "Revenue splits must total exactly 100 percent of the opportunity Amount, so they cannot credit every member in full.", "Opportunity Team roles give access and describe contribution, but they do not assign credit amounts without splits."], "takeaway": "Revenue splits must total 100 percent, while overlay splits can exceed 100 percent."}, {"id": "60c269136832409c838db5af7af0934c", "stem": "A sales director at Tailspin Logistics wants to review the open pipeline in one screen, drag opportunities from one stage to the next during weekly meetings, and see the total Amount at the top of each stage column. Which two actions should the administrator or user take?", "options": ["Create a Path for the Opportunity object and enable the Kanban option in Path Settings.", "Build a report with a bucket column on Stage and add it to the Opportunity home page as a Kanban component.", "Create a dashboard with a funnel chart and enable drag and drop on the chart.", "Open an opportunity list view and switch the display to Kanban, grouped by Stage.", "In the Kanban settings, choose Amount as the field to summarize for each column."], "correct": [3, 4], "explanations": ["Path and Kanban are separate features. Path Settings has no option to enable Kanban.", "There is no Kanban component fed by a report, and bucket columns do not allow moving records between stages.", "Dashboard funnel charts are read-only visuals and do not allow updating records by dragging.", "The Kanban display is available on list views and groups records into columns by a picklist such as Stage, with drag and drop between columns.", "Kanban settings let you select a field to summarize, so each column shows its total Amount."], "takeaway": "Kanban is a list view display option that groups records by a picklist, supports drag and drop, and can summarize a field such as Amount."}, {"id": "23ca7a8ae9284b31bdce59c261bc37d2", "stem": "Adventure Works adds a new stage called Contract Sent to its Enterprise sales process. Opportunities in this stage should default to a 75 percent Probability and roll up into the Commit forecast category. Which two steps should the administrator take?", "options": ["Create a custom number field named Probability and replace the standard field on the page layout.", "Select Contract Sent in the Enterprise sales process so that the stage is available to that record type's opportunities.", "Create a validation rule that sets Probability to 75 when Stage equals Contract Sent.", "Add Contract Sent to the Path Settings only, which automatically sets Probability and Forecast Category.", "Add the Contract Sent value to the Stage field and set its Probability and Forecast Category."], "correct": [1, 4], "explanations": ["A custom field would not drive the standard forecasting behavior, and the standard Probability field is tied to the Stage value.", "A new stage value is only offered to reps once it is included in the sales process linked to the record type.", "Validation rules can only block a save. They cannot populate field values such as Probability.", "Path displays stages already defined in the Stage field and sales process. It does not set Probability or Forecast Category.", "Probability and Forecast Category are defined on the Stage picklist value itself, so each stage carries its default values."], "takeaway": "Stage values carry their default Probability and Forecast Category, and a stage must be included in the sales process to be available."}]}, {"id": "adm_products", "title": "Produits, catalogues de prix et devis", "fiche": {"one_liner": "Products live in the Standard Price Book, which every product needs first. Custom price books add specific prices, products are added to opportunities as line items, and quotes can sync with the opportunity.", "need": "A software company sells the same licenses at list price to direct customers, at a discount to partners, and with a different price in euros for European clients. Reps need to pick products, apply the right price, schedule revenue over time, and send a formal quote without retyping everything.", "core": "A Product is an item you sell. Every product must first have an entry in the Standard Price Book (the master list, created automatically and always present). You cannot add a product to a custom price book until it has a standard price. Custom price books (for example Partner, Education, Europe) hold alternative list prices for selected products. A price book entry (the link between a product and a price book) stores the price and the currency if multicurrency is on.\n\nOn an opportunity, you add Products (called opportunity products or line items). The first time you add one, you choose a price book, and all products on that opportunity must come from that one price book. Each line has quantity, sales price (editable by the rep) and total. The opportunity Amount becomes the sum of the line items. Changing the price book later removes the existing products, which Salesforce warns about.\n\nSchedules let you spread a product's quantity (Quantity Schedule) or revenue (Revenue Schedule) over several installments, for example a 12-month subscription recognized monthly. They are enabled per product and set up on the product, then applied on the opportunity product. Installments can be changed on the line item.\n\nA Quote is a formal, priced proposal tied to an opportunity, with its own line items and a PDF output. An opportunity can have many quotes. Only one quote can be synced at a time. Syncing copies the quote line items to the opportunity products, and keeps them aligned in both directions while the sync lasts. Quotes must be enabled in Setup first.", "setup": ["Setup > Quick Find > Products Settings: enable Quantity Schedules and Revenue Schedules, and other product options", "App Launcher > Products: create the product and add its standard price (Standard Price Book)", "App Launcher > Price Books: create a custom price book and use Add Products to give them custom prices", "Setup > Quote Settings: enable Quotes, then add the Quotes related list to the opportunity page layout", "Opportunity > Quotes > Start Sync (on the quote record): syncs the quote with the opportunity"], "compare": [{"a": "Standard Price Book", "b": "Custom price book", "diff": "The Standard Price Book is the mandatory master list that holds a standard price for every product. Custom price books are optional and hold specific prices for a segment, but a product must first exist in the standard one."}, {"a": "Quote", "b": "Opportunity (with products)", "diff": "An opportunity tracks the deal and its forecast. A quote is the formal proposal sent to the customer. Several quotes can exist per opportunity, but only the synced one mirrors the opportunity products."}], "signals": [{"if": "Different prices for partners, regions or customer segments", "then": "Custom price books"}, {"if": "Product must be added to a custom price book first", "then": "Add its standard price in the Standard Price Book"}, {"if": "Revenue or quantity spread over months or years (installments)", "then": "Revenue Schedules or Quantity Schedules (échéanciers)"}, {"if": "Formal PDF proposal for the customer, several versions per deal", "then": "Quotes, with sync to the opportunity"}], "traps": ["Trying to add a product to a custom price book without a standard price: it is not possible, the standard entry comes first.", "Mixing price books on one opportunity: all products on an opportunity must come from the same price book.", "Believing several quotes can be synced at once: only one quote at a time syncs with the opportunity.", "Changing the price book on an opportunity that already has products: the existing products are removed."], "scenario": {"situation": "A company sells to retail clients at list price and to resellers at 20% off. Reps currently type discounted prices by hand and make mistakes. Management also wants to send a formal proposal, with several versions, while the opportunity Amount stays accurate.", "answer": "Create a Resellers custom price book with discounted prices, after making sure each product has a standard price. Reps pick the right price book on the opportunity. Enable Quotes so reps create multiple quotes, and sync the chosen one so the opportunity products and Amount match it."}, "cards": [{"q": "What must exist before a product can appear in a custom price book?", "a": "A standard price for the product in the Standard Price Book."}, {"q": "How many price books can one opportunity use?", "a": "One. All its products must come from the same price book chosen on the opportunity."}, {"q": "What does syncing a quote do?", "a": "It copies the quote line items to the opportunity products and keeps them aligned both ways. Only one quote per opportunity can be synced at a time."}, {"q": "What is the difference between a Quantity Schedule and a Revenue Schedule?", "a": "A Quantity Schedule spreads the number of units over installments, a Revenue Schedule spreads the money. Both are enabled in Products Settings and applied to opportunity products."}, {"q": "What happens to the opportunity Amount once products are added?", "a": "It is calculated as the sum of the opportunity products, so it can no longer be edited directly."}]}, "questions": [{"id": "6c822481fe04445d805a142fde98ce2d", "stem": "Northwind Outfitters sells hiking gear and wants to offer a separate set of discounted prices to its wholesale partners. An administrator creates a new custom price book called Wholesale 2025 and tries to add products to it, but the products cannot be added. What is the most likely cause?", "options": ["The custom price book must be activated by Salesforce Support before products can be added", "The products must first be marked as Wholesale in the Product Family field", "The custom price book has not been shared with the partner users", "The products have not yet been added to the Standard Price Book with a list price"], "correct": [3], "explanations": ["Incorrect. Administrators create and activate custom price books themselves, and no Support case is required.", "Incorrect. Product Family is an optional categorization field and has no effect on adding products to a price book.", "Incorrect. Sharing affects who can see the price book, not whether an administrator can add products to it.", "Correct. A product must have a list price in the Standard Price Book before it can be added to any custom price book."], "takeaway": "Every product needs an entry in the Standard Price Book before it can be added to a custom price book."}, {"id": "cb52e498345544398a0538571f296c4c", "stem": "At Contoso Energy, a sales rep has already added three products from the Commercial price book to an opportunity. The rep now wants to switch the opportunity to the Government price book. What happens when the price book is changed on the opportunity?", "options": ["The existing products stay and keep their original Commercial prices", "The change is blocked until the opportunity is moved to a closed stage", "The existing opportunity products are removed from the opportunity", "The existing products are converted to the Government prices automatically"], "correct": [2], "explanations": ["Incorrect. Line items are tied to the price book entries of the original price book, so they cannot remain.", "Incorrect. There is no such stage restriction on changing the price book.", "Correct. Changing the price book deletes the existing opportunity products, and the rep must add products again from the new price book.", "Incorrect. Salesforce does not reprice existing line items to a new price book."], "takeaway": "Changing the price book on an opportunity removes its existing products, so reps must re-add them from the new price book."}, {"id": "995245e6d2b643babf44c90ddc65c4a7", "stem": "Fabrikam Software sells annual subscriptions and wants revenue to be recognized in monthly installments over the contract term. Sales reps should be able to see the installment breakdown directly on each opportunity product. What should the administrator do?", "options": ["Enable Product Schedules and configure revenue schedules for the products", "Create a quote template that lists monthly payments for each line item", "Create a roll-up summary field on the opportunity to divide the amount by twelve", "Add a custom date field to the Opportunity Product and use a Flow to split the amount"], "correct": [0], "explanations": ["Correct. Product Schedules let reps set up revenue schedules, and installments appear on the opportunity product.", "Incorrect. A quote template changes the presentation of a quote document and does not create schedules on opportunity products.", "Incorrect. A roll-up summary does not create installment records, and it does not give a schedule on each line item.", "Incorrect. This is a custom workaround for a need that the standard scheduling feature already covers."], "takeaway": "Use Product Schedules to divide quantity or revenue of an opportunity product into installments over time."}, {"id": "6b02a17ac9d0436881697777bd6609eb", "stem": "Tailspin Logistics wants its reps to create quotes from opportunities. When a rep starts syncing a quote, the quote's products should appear on the opportunity and stay aligned with it. Which two statements about quote syncing are true?", "options": ["Syncing copies the quote line items to the opportunity products and keeps changes aligned between them", "Syncing requires that the opportunity be in a Closed Won stage", "Only one quote at a time can be synced with an opportunity", "A quote must be approved before it can be synced", "The quote and its opportunity can use different price books if the sync is enabled"], "correct": [0, 2], "explanations": ["Correct. When synced, changes to the quote line items are reflected on the opportunity products, and the reverse is also true.", "Incorrect. Syncing is used during the sales cycle on open opportunities, not only at closing.", "Correct. An opportunity can have many quotes, but only one can be synced with it at a time.", "Incorrect. Approval is not a requirement for syncing a quote.", "Incorrect. A quote uses the price book of its opportunity, so they cannot differ."], "takeaway": "Many quotes can exist per opportunity, but only one can be synced at a time, and syncing keeps line items aligned with opportunity products."}, {"id": "7a7f0ec9181c48a8ab7a753b752149e3", "stem": "Adventure Works wants to roll out Products and Price Books for its sales team in Lightning Experience. The administrator needs reps to add products to opportunities and to see list prices from a custom price book. Which two actions are required?", "options": ["Assign each product to a Product Family before it can appear in a price book", "Create a validation rule requiring a product on every opportunity", "Create a separate record type for each price book on the Opportunity object", "Activate the custom price book and ensure products have active price book entries in it", "Give the reps access to the custom price book through the price book's sharing settings"], "correct": [3, 4], "explanations": ["Incorrect. Product Family is optional and is not required for products to appear in a price book.", "Incorrect. A validation rule is optional and does not enable reps to use price books.", "Incorrect. Record types are not needed to use different price books on opportunities.", "Correct. The price book must be active, and products need active price book entries to be added to opportunities.", "Correct. Reps need access to the price book, which is granted through the price book's Manage action and its sharing setup, in addition to object permissions."], "takeaway": "Reps can only use products from an active price book that they can access and that contains active price book entries."}]}, {"id": "adm_forecast", "title": "Prévisions collaboratives", "fiche": {"one_liner": "Collaborative Forecasts let sales managers roll up their team's opportunities by Forecast Category, by period, and by role hierarchy, then adjust and compare the result to quotas.", "need": "A software company's VP of Sales must tell the CFO what revenue to expect this quarter. Each rep commits to deals, regional managers review and adjust the numbers, and the VP sees one rolled-up total against quota, without collecting spreadsheets by email.", "core": "Collaborative Forecasts (Prévisions collaboratives) roll up Opportunity data into a forecast for each user. Each Opportunity Stage maps to a Forecast Category: Omitted, Pipeline, Best Case, Commit, and Closed. The category decides which forecast column the amount lands in. Omitted (Omis) is not counted in the forecast at all. Pipeline, Best Case and Commit are cumulative in the rollup: Best Case includes Commit, and Commit includes Closed.\n\nA Forecast Type defines what is summed: Opportunity Revenue (Chiffre d'affaires de l'opportunité), Opportunity Splits (Répartitions d'opportunité) such as overlay or revenue splits, Product Family (Famille de produits) or Line Item-based forecasts. Revenue forecasts use Amount, and a Quantity type uses quantity. Each type has its own forecast. The Forecasts tab shows a forecast based on the Role Hierarchy (Hiérarchie des rôles): managers see their own numbers plus the rollup of their subordinates. The Forecast Hierarchy follows roles, not the Forecast Manager field of the old Customizable Forecasting. Forecasts are shown per Forecast Period, either monthly or quarterly, set when you enable the feature.\n\nForecast Quotas (Quotas) are targets entered per user and per period, so the forecast can be compared with the goal. Forecast Adjustments (Ajustements) let a manager override the rolled-up amount of a subordinate without changing the underlying opportunities. Reps can also override their own forecast category on an opportunity in the forecast view, if allowed.", "setup": ["Setup > Quick Find > Forecasts Settings: enable Forecasts, choose forecast types, the period (monthly or quarterly), and the fiscal year start.", "Forecasts Settings: configure Forecast Hierarchy, Adjustments, and the display of quotas and columns (Closed Only, Commit, Best Case, Open Pipeline).", "Setup > Object Manager > Opportunity > Fields > Stage > Edit picklist values: set the Forecast Category mapped to each Stage.", "Setup > Quick Find > Roles: build the Role Hierarchy so the forecast rolls up correctly.", "Forecasts tab > Quotas: enter or load quotas per user and period, and give users the Override Forecast or View All Forecasts permissions where needed."], "compare": [{"a": "Forecast Category (Catégorie de prévision)", "b": "Opportunity Stage (Étape)", "diff": "The Stage shows where the deal is in the sales process. The Forecast Category is the bucket the deal falls into in the forecast. Each Stage is mapped to one category, so changing the Stage usually changes the category."}, {"a": "Forecast Adjustment (Ajustement)", "b": "Editing the Opportunity Amount", "diff": "An adjustment changes only the forecast number for that user in that period. The opportunity record keeps its original amount. It is made by a manager on a subordinate's rollup."}], "signals": [{"if": "Manager must see the team's rolled-up forecast", "then": "Collaborative Forecasts with the Role Hierarchy"}, {"if": "Compare the forecast against a sales target", "then": "Forecast Quotas"}, {"if": "Manager wants to change a subordinate's number without touching opportunities", "then": "Forecast Adjustments"}, {"if": "Forecast on overlay credit or split revenue", "then": "Opportunity Splits as the Forecast Type"}], "traps": ["Forecast Category is not the same as Stage: the Stage is mapped to the category in the picklist settings.", "Omitted opportunities are excluded from the forecast, and Closed Won is in Closed, not in Pipeline.", "The hierarchy is the Role Hierarchy, not the Profile or the Forecast Manager field of the old Customizable Forecasting.", "An adjustment does not change the opportunity: only the forecast number."], "scenario": {"situation": "A regional director wants to cut a team member's Commit by 50,000 because a large deal looks unlikely, without altering the deal in the CRM. She also needs the quarterly total compared to a target for each rep.", "answer": "Enable Collaborative Forecasts with Adjustments and Forecast Quotas. The director adjusts the rep's rolled-up Commit in the Forecasts tab, which leaves the Opportunity Amount unchanged. Quotas entered per rep and quarterly period show the attainment against the forecast. The Role Hierarchy lets her see her subordinates' rollups."}, "cards": [{"q": "What links an Opportunity to a Forecast Category?", "a": "Its Stage. Each Stage picklist value is mapped to a Forecast Category (Omitted, Pipeline, Best Case, Commit, Closed)."}, {"q": "Which category is not counted in a forecast?", "a": "Omitted (Omis). The amounts in this category are left out of the rollup."}, {"q": "What structure determines who sees whose forecast?", "a": "The Role Hierarchy. Managers see their own forecast and the rollup of subordinates below them."}, {"q": "What does a Forecast Type define?", "a": "What is measured in the forecast, such as Opportunity Revenue, Opportunity Splits, or Product Family, and whether it uses amount or quantity."}, {"q": "What is the effect of a Forecast Adjustment?", "a": "It overrides a rolled-up forecast number for a user in a period, but the underlying opportunities stay unchanged."}]}, "questions": [{"id": "d4ef26f989d24cbca7af0e3dff5c78b8", "stem": "Northwind Traders wants its sales managers to review a roll-up of the opportunity pipeline by forecast category in Lightning Experience. Reps currently see the forecast category change automatically when they move an opportunity to a new stage. The administrator needs to confirm where the forecast category value for an opportunity comes from by default. What should the administrator explain?", "options": ["The forecast category is set manually by the forecast manager on the Forecasts page", "The forecast category is derived from the Probability field, which is set by the owner's role", "The forecast category is derived from the opportunity Amount field and a threshold set in Forecasts Settings", "The forecast category is mapped to each opportunity stage in the Opportunity Stage picklist settings"], "correct": [3], "explanations": ["Incorrect. Managers can adjust forecast amounts, but the category is not set by managers on the Forecasts page by default.", "Incorrect. Probability is also tied to the stage, but it is not the source of the forecast category and is unrelated to roles.", "Incorrect. Amount determines the value in the forecast, not the category. No Amount threshold exists in Forecasts Settings for categories.", "Correct. In Setup, each Opportunity Stage value is mapped to a forecast category, so changing the stage updates the category."], "takeaway": "Forecast categories are mapped to opportunity stages in the Opportunity Stage picklist values, so the stage drives the category."}, {"id": "db9e3bf30dad41b399eb87e07768b0e9", "stem": "Contoso Energy sells both products and services. The sales director wants forecasts that are built from the number of units sold in each opportunity rather than from the total revenue. The administrator is enabling Collaborative Forecasts. Which action should the administrator take?", "options": ["Add a forecast type based on Opportunity Splits to the Forecasts Settings", "Change the Amount field on Opportunity to a numeric unit count", "Create a custom report type with Quantity and add it to the Forecasts tab", "Add a forecast type based on Opportunity Quantity in Forecasts Settings"], "correct": [3], "explanations": ["Incorrect. Opportunity Splits forecast types divide revenue credit among team members, not unit counts.", "Incorrect. Changing the meaning of Amount would break revenue reporting and is not how Salesforce supports quantity forecasting.", "Incorrect. Reports do not feed Collaborative Forecasts. Forecast types are configured in Forecasts Settings.", "Correct. Forecast types can be based on Opportunity Revenue or Quantity, and a Quantity-based type forecasts units sold."], "takeaway": "Forecast types in Forecasts Settings can be based on Opportunity Revenue, Quantity, Splits, or Product Family via custom fields."}, {"id": "d8074ecce6db4dd9be042706cce1b1a3", "stem": "At Fabrikam, the VP of Sales (role: VP Sales) has three regional managers reporting to her in the role hierarchy. Each manager has several reps. The VP opens the Forecasts tab and cannot see a regional manager's team's numbers. Collaborative Forecasts is enabled and the forecast hierarchy is based on roles. What is the most likely cause?", "options": ["The reps' forecast quotas have not been uploaded for the period", "The forecast period is set to quarterly instead of monthly", "The opportunity records are not shared with the VP through a sharing rule", "The regional managers' users are not marked as Allow Forecasting"], "correct": [3], "explanations": ["Incorrect. Quotas are optional and their absence does not hide team members from the forecast hierarchy.", "Incorrect. The period type affects how data is grouped, not whether the hierarchy displays users.", "Incorrect. Role-based forecast hierarchies rely on the role hierarchy and the forecast setting, not on additional sharing rules for visibility of the roll-up.", "Correct. Users appear in the forecast hierarchy only if Allow Forecasting is selected on their user record, so managers without it are missing from the roll-up."], "takeaway": "Users must have Allow Forecasting enabled to appear in the forecast hierarchy."}, {"id": "db43b695a2984ce4a7c9d005c31e795b", "stem": "Adventure Works is configuring Collaborative Forecasts. Sales leadership wants managers to be able to change their team's rolled-up numbers during a review call without editing opportunities, and wants reps to see a target to measure against. Which two capabilities should the administrator enable or use?", "options": ["Custom Summary Formula fields on the Forecasts tab to store overrides", "Forecast quotas, so each user has a target shown in the forecast", "Forecast adjustments, so managers can override the rolled-up amounts for team members", "Opportunity Splits, so the manager can edit the team members' credit", "Territory Forecasting, so each rep has a separate target"], "correct": [1, 2], "explanations": ["Incorrect. Summary formula fields are not used on the Forecasts tab to store overrides.", "Correct. Quotas give users a target to compare against forecasted amounts in the Forecasts tab.", "Correct. When enabled, managers can adjust forecast amounts for subordinates without changing the underlying opportunities.", "Incorrect. Splits divide opportunity credit and do not let managers override rolled-up forecast totals.", "Incorrect. Territory-based hierarchy changes how the forecast is organized, not the targets or the override feature requested here."], "takeaway": "Forecast adjustments let managers override roll-ups, and forecast quotas set targets, both configured within Collaborative Forecasts."}, {"id": "862ecfd292f54c64a5fa70df4bebc342", "stem": "Tailspin Toys is turning on Collaborative Forecasts. The sales operations lead wants forecasts shown by month and wants reps to see which opportunities count toward which forecast category. Which two statements about the setup are accurate?", "options": ["Each user must set the forecast period individually in personal settings", "The forecast period can be set to monthly or quarterly in Forecasts Settings", "Opportunities appear in a period based on their Created Date", "The forecast period can be set to weekly as long as quotas are disabled", "Opportunities appear in a period based on their Close Date"], "correct": [1, 4], "explanations": ["Incorrect. The period display is an org-level setting in Forecasts Settings, not a personal preference.", "Correct. Forecasts Settings lets the administrator choose a monthly or quarterly period display.", "Incorrect. Created Date does not determine period placement.", "Incorrect. Weekly periods are not available for Collaborative Forecasts.", "Correct. Opportunities are included in the forecast for the period containing their Close Date."], "takeaway": "Collaborative Forecasts supports monthly or quarterly periods, and opportunities fall into periods by Close Date."}]}, {"id": "adm_campaigns", "title": "Campagnes et influence", "fiche": {"one_liner": "Campaigns track marketing efforts: Campaign Members link Leads and Contacts with a Member Status, hierarchies roll up results, and Campaign Influence attributes Opportunity revenue to campaigns.", "need": "A software company runs a webinar, a trade show and an email series. Marketing wants to know who responded, which activities roll up to the annual 'Spring Launch' program, and how much pipeline each activity helped generate. Campaigns, Campaign Members, hierarchies and Campaign Influence answer these questions inside Salesforce.", "core": "A Campaign is a record that represents a marketing initiative (webinar, email, event). A Campaign Member (membre de campagne) is the junction record linking a Campaign to a Lead or a Contact. A person can be added to many campaigns, but only once per campaign. Each Campaign Member has a Member Status (for example Sent, Responded, Attended). Statuses are defined per campaign, and each status has a Responded checkbox. When it is checked, the member counts as a response, which drives the Responses fields on the campaign.\n\nEvery campaign has a Campaign Member Status list. Salesforce creates default values (Sent and Responded) when you create a campaign. Statuses can be customized for each campaign, or you can set them when you create the campaign. A Campaign Type (Type) can be used to categorize campaigns, but statuses are not tied to the type automatically.\n\nA Campaign Hierarchy uses the Parent Campaign field to nest campaigns, for example a 'Spring Launch' parent with a webinar child and an email child. You can then view the hierarchy and use the campaign statistics fields (such as Total Opportunities in Hierarchy) to see aggregated results across the whole tree. Hierarchy statistics are rolled up through the Parent Campaign relationship, and the hierarchy can be up to five levels deep.\n\nCampaign Influence reports which campaigns contributed to an Opportunity. It links campaigns to Opportunities through Contact Roles and attribution models. The default model is Primary Campaign Source (the Campaign Source on the Opportunity). Customizable Campaign Influence lets admins add multiple models, such as Even Distribution, and add custom models. Only users with the Marketing User checkbox on their user record can create and manage campaigns, edit campaign members, and import leads into campaigns. Without it, users can only view campaigns, if their permissions allow.", "setup": ["Setup > Users > Users > edit the user > check Marketing User (needed to create and edit campaigns, add members and import leads to campaigns; the permission can also be given with the Campaign object permissions, but the checkbox is the required piece for managing campaigns).", "Campaigns tab (or App Launcher > Campaigns) > New: set Campaign Name, Type, Status, Active and Parent Campaign (to build the hierarchy).", "On the campaign record, use Manage Members or Add Leads / Add Contacts (or Data Import Wizard) to add members, and edit the Member Status values in the Campaign Member Status related list or the Advanced Setup of the campaign.", "Setup > Campaign Influence > Settings (Customizable Campaign Influence): enable it, then add attribution models such as Even Distribution and add Campaign Influence related lists to Opportunity layouts."], "compare": [{"a": "Campaign Member", "b": "Lead or Contact", "diff": "A Lead or Contact is the person. A Campaign Member is a separate record joining that person to one campaign and holding that campaign's Member Status. The same person has one Campaign Member record per campaign."}, {"a": "Primary Campaign Source", "b": "Campaign Influence", "diff": "Primary Campaign Source is a single field on the Opportunity giving full credit to one campaign. Campaign Influence can credit several campaigns for the same Opportunity, using attribution models and Contact Roles."}, {"a": "Campaign Hierarchy", "b": "Campaign Member Status", "diff": "The hierarchy organizes campaigns (parent and child) to roll up results. The status describes the response of one person within one campaign."}], "signals": [{"if": "Track who attended or responded to an event or webinar", "then": "Campaign Members with a custom Member Status (Responded checkbox set on the right values)"}, {"if": "See combined results of several related campaigns", "then": "Campaign Hierarchy using the Parent Campaign field and the hierarchy statistics"}, {"if": "Credit multiple campaigns for one deal or revenue attribution", "then": "Customizable Campaign Influence with attribution models"}, {"if": "A user cannot create campaigns or add members", "then": "Marketing User checkbox on the user record"}], "traps": ["A Lead or Contact can be in many campaigns, but only once in the same campaign: you cannot add the same person twice to one campaign.", "A Member Status counts as a response only if its Responded box is checked: Attended with the box unchecked does not raise the response count.", "Marketing User is a checkbox on the user record, not a profile or a permission set name, and without it a user cannot create campaigns or add members.", "Primary Campaign Source gives 100% credit to one campaign, so it is not the answer when the scenario asks for credit shared among several campaigns."], "scenario": {"situation": "A sales rep tries to create a campaign for a customer event and to import a list of leads into it, but the New button or the import option is missing. The rep has Create permission on Campaigns through the profile. The marketing director wants the rep to manage campaigns.", "answer": "Select the Marketing User checkbox on the rep's user record. Campaign object permissions alone are not enough to manage campaign members or import leads into a campaign. The checkbox is the user-level setting that enables these marketing actions."}, "cards": [{"q": "What is a Campaign Member?", "a": "A junction record linking one Lead or Contact to one Campaign, which stores that person's Member Status for that campaign."}, {"q": "What does the Responded checkbox on a Member Status do?", "a": "It defines whether members with that status count as responses in the campaign's response statistics."}, {"q": "How do you build a campaign hierarchy?", "a": "Fill in the Parent Campaign field on child campaigns. The hierarchy statistics then roll up results from the whole tree."}, {"q": "What does Campaign Influence show?", "a": "Which campaigns contributed to an Opportunity and how much credit each gets, based on Contact Roles and the attribution model used."}, {"q": "Which setting lets a user create campaigns and import leads into them?", "a": "The Marketing User checkbox on the user record."}]}, "questions": [{"id": "3b48d8dc13e547d78ec5e35bbbeb438b", "stem": "Northwind Outfitters runs a trade show campaign. The marketing manager wants to add 200 existing Contacts to the campaign and then track whether each person Sent, Responded, or Attended. The administrator has already confirmed the campaign has custom Member Statuses defined. The marketing manager has the Marketing User checkbox selected on her user record. What should the administrator do to let her add the Contacts in bulk from a Contact list view?", "options": ["Use the Add to Campaign button on the Contact list view, which is available because she is a Marketing User.", "Add the Contacts to the campaign by creating Campaign Influence records for each Contact.", "Create a Flow that inserts Opportunity Contact Roles to link the Contacts to the campaign.", "Convert the Contacts to Leads so they can be added as Campaign Members."], "correct": [0], "explanations": ["Correct. Marketing Users can add Contacts and Leads to campaigns in bulk from list views using Add to Campaign, and each member receives a Member Status.", "Incorrect. Campaign Influence links campaigns to Opportunities, it does not add people as Campaign Members.", "Incorrect. Opportunity Contact Roles relate Contacts to Opportunities, not to campaigns.", "Incorrect. Contacts can be Campaign Members directly, so no conversion is needed (and Contacts cannot be converted to Leads)."], "takeaway": "Both Leads and Contacts can be Campaign Members, and the Marketing User checkbox is what enables bulk adding through Add to Campaign."}, {"id": "c81906dc9c4c49a7ac962da3aa873148", "stem": "Contoso Learning created a webinar campaign. Reports show members as only Sent or Responded, but the marketing team now needs a status called Registered, and they want Registered to count as a response in campaign metrics. What should the administrator do?", "options": ["Create a new record type on the Campaign object with Registered as a value.", "Edit the campaign's Member Status values, add Registered, and select the Responded checkbox for it.", "Add a new picklist value to the Status field on the Lead object.", "Add Registered to the Type field on the Campaign object and mark it as default."], "correct": [1], "explanations": ["Incorrect. Record types control picklist values and layouts for the Campaign, not the Member Status values of its members.", "Correct. Member Statuses are defined per campaign, and each one can be flagged as Responded so it counts toward response metrics.", "Incorrect. Lead Status describes the lead's lifecycle, not the person's participation in a specific campaign.", "Incorrect. The Campaign Type field classifies the campaign itself (such as Webinar), not the status of members."], "takeaway": "Campaign Member Statuses are set up on each campaign, and the Responded flag determines whether a status counts as a response."}, {"id": "b380edd61f024b7dbff7b637796e2c1b", "stem": "Fabrikam Energy organizes a yearly Spring Promotion with separate email, webinar, and direct mail campaigns. The marketing director wants roll-up statistics, such as total opportunities and total value won, across all of these campaigns in one view. What should the administrator configure?", "options": ["A Campaign Influence model assigning equal credit to each campaign.", "A single campaign with all members and a custom picklist to identify the channel.", "A lookup field on each campaign pointing to a Campaign Member Status record.", "A campaign hierarchy, with the three campaigns related to a parent campaign through the Parent Campaign field."], "correct": [3], "explanations": ["Incorrect. Campaign Influence attributes opportunity revenue to campaigns, but it does not group campaigns into a structure.", "Incorrect. This loses the separate tracking and budgets per campaign and is not the hierarchy feature built for this need.", "Incorrect. Member Statuses are not standalone records you can look up, and this would not roll up any statistics.", "Correct. Setting the Parent Campaign field builds a hierarchy, and hierarchy statistics aggregate data from the parent and its children."], "takeaway": "Use the Parent Campaign field to build a campaign hierarchy and view aggregated statistics across related campaigns."}, {"id": "5520a024d9e44bf2aaba5361f92f0374", "stem": "Adventure Works wants to understand which campaigns contributed to closed Opportunities, not just the one campaign listed as the Primary Campaign Source. The administrator is enabling Campaign Influence. Which two statements are true about this feature? (Select the two correct statements.)", "options": ["Contacts must be Campaign Members, or have a Contact Role on the Opportunity, for influence records to be associated with the campaign.", "Campaign Influence can only be used with the Marketing User checkbox disabled.", "Campaign Influence relates Opportunities to multiple campaigns and shows each campaign's share of revenue credit.", "Campaign Influence requires that every Opportunity be created through lead conversion.", "Campaign Influence replaces the Primary Campaign Source field, which must be removed from page layouts."], "correct": [0, 2], "explanations": ["Correct. The default influence model associates campaigns with Opportunities based on Contact Roles and Campaign Membership, within the model's time frame.", "Incorrect. This has no relation to the Marketing User checkbox, which controls campaign management access.", "Correct. Campaign Influence records link campaigns to Opportunities with an influence percentage and revenue share.", "Incorrect. Opportunities do not need to come from lead conversion to receive influence.", "Incorrect. Primary Campaign Source continues to exist and is used alongside influence models."], "takeaway": "Campaign Influence attributes Opportunity revenue to multiple campaigns, based on Contact Roles and Campaign Members, in addition to Primary Campaign Source."}, {"id": "d493871531544bc6b1c805920bd541f9", "stem": "Tailspin Toys hired a new marketing coordinator who must create campaigns, edit them, and import Leads as Campaign Members using the Data Import Wizard. The administrator has assigned a profile with Create and Edit on Campaigns. The coordinator still cannot add campaign members in bulk. Which two actions should the administrator take? (Select the two correct answers.)", "options": ["Assign the coordinator the Platform Starter license.", "Select the Marketing User checkbox on the coordinator's user record.", "Grant the Import Leads user permission to the coordinator.", "Make the coordinator's profile the owner of every campaign.", "Enable Campaign Influence for the org."], "correct": [1, 2], "explanations": ["Incorrect. Platform Starter licenses have limited object access and do not enable this capability.", "Correct. The Marketing User checkbox is required to manage campaign members in bulk, and to import and update them through the wizards.", "Correct. Importing Leads requires the Import Leads permission, in addition to the campaign access that the profile already gives.", "Incorrect. Profiles do not own records, and ownership is not required to add members.", "Incorrect. Campaign Influence deals with revenue attribution and is not required for adding members."], "takeaway": "Bulk campaign member tasks need the Marketing User checkbox, plus object permissions, and the Import Leads permission for lead imports."}]}, {"id": "adm_accounts", "title": "Comptes et contacts", "fiche": {"one_liner": "Use Parent Account hierarchies to model company structures, Contacts to Multiple Accounts for shared people, Person Accounts for consumers (irreversible), and Account Teams to share an account among several users.", "need": "Northwind Industries is a global manufacturer with subsidiaries in several countries. Sales reps need to see the whole corporate family, an external consultant works for three client companies, a retail arm sells directly to individuals, and several internal specialists must work together on the biggest customer accounts.", "core": "An Account represents a company, and a Contact is a person linked to it. In a standard B2B model, each Contact has one primary account. To model corporate structure, you fill in the Parent Account field on the account. This creates an account hierarchy, which you can view with the View Account Hierarchy link and which can be shown as columns. Rollup of data across the hierarchy is not automatic.\n\nContacts to Multiple Accounts lets one Contact be related to several accounts. The Contact keeps one direct (primary) account, and you add the others as indirect relationships through the Related Accounts related list (Account Contact Relationships). You can also add roles on each relationship. The feature must be enabled in Setup first. Use it for a consultant, a board member or a shared contact between a parent and its subsidiary.\n\nPerson Accounts are for B2C: a single record combines the Account and the Contact of an individual (a customer). They use a special record type. Once enabled, the feature cannot be disabled, so you must plan it carefully. Person Accounts require at least one Account record type and a Person Account record type, and enabling goes through Salesforce Customer Support in some cases, so check the current Setup steps.\n\nAccount Teams are groups of users who work together on an account, each with a team role (for example Sales Engineer or Executive Sponsor) and an access level (Read Only or Read/Write). A user can build a default account team that is added automatically to their accounts. Teams must be enabled in Setup. Account Teams extend record access beyond the owner and the role hierarchy, but they do not restrict access.", "setup": ["Parent hierarchy: fill in the Parent Account field on the account record, then use View Account Hierarchy.", "Setup > Account Settings > Allow users to relate a contact to multiple accounts (enable Contacts to Multiple Accounts), then add the Related Accounts list to the Contact page layout.", "Setup > Account Settings > Enable Account Teams, then add the Account Team related list to the Account page layout.", "Person Accounts: create a Person Account record type and enable the feature through Setup (Account Settings), knowing that activation is irreversible."], "compare": [{"a": "Contacts to Multiple Accounts", "b": "Parent Account hierarchy", "diff": "Hierarchy links account to account (company structure). Contacts to Multiple Accounts links one person to several accounts."}, {"a": "Account Team", "b": "Account hierarchy or role hierarchy", "diff": "An Account Team gives named users access and roles on a given account. The role hierarchy grants access by reporting line, and the account hierarchy is only about customer structure."}, {"a": "Person Account", "b": "Account with Contacts", "diff": "A Person Account merges the account and contact of an individual into one record for B2C. A business account has separate Contacts for B2B."}], "signals": [{"if": "Subsidiaries, parent company, corporate structure", "then": "Parent Account field and account hierarchy"}, {"if": "A contact who works for several companies (consultant, shared contact)", "then": "Contacts to Multiple Accounts"}, {"if": "Selling directly to individual consumers (B2C)", "then": "Person Accounts"}, {"if": "Several users with different roles working on the same account", "then": "Account Teams"}], "traps": ["Person Accounts cannot be turned off once enabled, so the answer 'enable it to test and disable later' is wrong.", "With Contacts to Multiple Accounts, a Contact still has one primary account, and the other accounts are indirect relationships.", "The account hierarchy shows structure only: it does not grant record access to users of the parent or child accounts.", "An Account Team adds access but cannot remove access the user already has through ownership or the role hierarchy."], "scenario": {"situation": "A bank sells to companies and also to individual customers. An independent auditor serves two corporate clients, and the bank wants the auditor recorded once. A large client also needs a banker, a lawyer and a risk analyst with different access levels.", "answer": "Enable Contacts to Multiple Accounts so the auditor is one Contact related to both accounts. Use Account Teams with roles and access levels for the banker, lawyer and analyst. Use Person Accounts for individuals, accepting that activation is irreversible."}, "cards": [{"q": "Which field creates an account hierarchy?", "a": "The Parent Account field on the account. You then view the structure with View Account Hierarchy."}, {"q": "How does a Contact relate to several accounts?", "a": "By enabling Contacts to Multiple Accounts. The Contact keeps one primary account and the others are indirect relationships in the Related Accounts list."}, {"q": "Can Person Accounts be disabled after activation?", "a": "No. Activation is irreversible, so it needs careful planning."}, {"q": "What does an Account Team define for each member?", "a": "A team role and an access level to the account (such as Read Only or Read/Write)."}, {"q": "Does a Person Account use separate Account and Contact records?", "a": "No. It is a single record that combines the Account and Contact of an individual, based on a Person Account record type."}]}, "questions": [{"id": "a34bdf41d68e4199a9cc331ebfc5e8e2", "stem": "Northwind Logistics tracks its customers in Salesforce. Sales leaders want to see a parent company and all of its subsidiaries in a single expandable view, and roll up revenue reporting across the corporate family. The administrator needs to represent the parent-subsidiary structure on Account records. What should the administrator do?", "options": ["Use the Parent Account field on the Account object and add the Account Hierarchy view", "Enable Account Teams and add the subsidiaries as team members", "Enable Contacts to Multiple Accounts and relate the subsidiaries to each other", "Create a custom lookup from Account to Account called Subsidiary and build a report"], "correct": [0], "explanations": ["Correct. The standard Parent Account field links accounts together, and the Account Hierarchy view displays the parent and its child accounts in an expandable tree.", "Incorrect. Account Teams grant users access and roles on an account. They do not model relationships between accounts.", "Incorrect. Contacts to Multiple Accounts relates contacts to accounts, not accounts to other accounts.", "Incorrect. A custom lookup would duplicate the standard capability and would not power the standard Account Hierarchy view."], "takeaway": "Account hierarchies are built with the standard Parent Account field and displayed with the Account Hierarchy view."}, {"id": "116c5de0eb5d469fad8a11da170fba02", "stem": "Contoso Consulting has independent consultants who work for several client companies at the same time. Today the admin creates a duplicate Contact for each client, which causes data quality issues. The company wants one Contact record linked to multiple Accounts, with one account marked as primary. What should the administrator do?", "options": ["Convert each consultant to a Person Account", "Enable Contacts to Multiple Accounts and add the other accounts as related accounts on the Contact", "Enable Account Teams and add the consultant to each client team", "Create a junction object between Account and Contact"], "correct": [1], "explanations": ["Incorrect. A Person Account is a single record combining account and contact data. It does not link one person to multiple companies.", "Correct. With Contacts to Multiple Accounts, the contact keeps a primary account and can be related to other accounts through Account Contact Relationships.", "Incorrect. Account Teams associate Salesforce users with an account, not external contacts.", "Incorrect. A custom junction object duplicates the standard feature and loses built-in related lists and behavior."], "takeaway": "Use Contacts to Multiple Accounts so a single Contact has one primary Account and additional Account Contact Relationships."}, {"id": "e227410f8798414da0c4f6f741005775", "stem": "Fabrikam Retail sells to both businesses and individual shoppers. A project lead proposes enabling Person Accounts in production right away to test the feature with a small group of users, planning to turn it off if the results are poor. What should the administrator tell the project lead?", "options": ["Person Accounts can be enabled only for sandbox orgs, never production", "Person Accounts can be disabled at any time by removing the record type", "Enabling Person Accounts is irreversible, so it should be tested in a sandbox first", "Person Accounts require a separate license purchased from AppExchange"], "correct": [2], "explanations": ["Incorrect. The feature can be enabled in production after contacting Salesforce Customer Support, but it is permanent.", "Incorrect. Removing a record type does not disable the feature. Once enabled, Person Accounts cannot be turned off.", "Correct. Activation cannot be undone, so the feature should be validated in a sandbox before enabling it in production.", "Incorrect. Person Accounts are not an AppExchange product and do not need a separate license."], "takeaway": "Enabling Person Accounts is permanent, so always test it in a sandbox first."}, {"id": "30fe421be26c4dc2bce7f6a3cd5d6f51", "stem": "Adventure Works assigns a sales rep, a solutions engineer, and a customer success manager to each strategic account. Each person needs access to the account and should be listed with a specific role, such as Executive Sponsor. The administrator must set this up. Which two actions should the administrator take?", "options": ["Add the Account Team related list to the Account page layout", "Create a Contact role for each user on the Account", "Enable Account Teams and define Team Roles", "Enable Contacts to Multiple Accounts", "Create a sharing rule for each team member and each account"], "correct": [0, 2], "explanations": ["Correct. Adding the Account Team related list to the page layout lets users add and view team members with their roles and access levels.", "Incorrect. Contact roles describe the role of a contact, not of a Salesforce user on an account.", "Correct. Account Teams must be enabled, and team roles are defined as picklist values so members can be assigned specific roles.", "Incorrect. This feature relates contacts to accounts and has no effect on internal user teams.", "Incorrect. Account Team members get access through the team's access level, so per-record sharing rules are unnecessary and not scalable."], "takeaway": "Account Teams must be enabled and the Account Team related list added to the layout so users can be assigned with roles and access levels."}, {"id": "318db0c7c2674ce2b74842b1c2bbf0a4", "stem": "Litware Insurance has enabled Contacts to Multiple Accounts. A contact, Maria, works for her own firm and also advises two client accounts. Sales managers want to record her role at each client and identify her main employer. Which two statements describe how the administrator can meet this need?", "options": ["The Account Contact Relationship cannot be customized with extra fields", "Each additional account relationship is stored in an Account Contact Relationship record that can hold a Roles value", "Each related account requires a separate duplicate Contact record to store the role", "Maria must be converted to a Person Account to belong to several accounts", "The Account on the Contact record represents the primary account, and other accounts are added as related accounts"], "correct": [1, 4], "explanations": ["Incorrect. Custom fields can be added to Account Contact Relationship, so it can be extended.", "Correct. Account Contact Relationship records connect a contact to an account and include fields such as Roles, and they can be customized.", "Incorrect. Duplicates are exactly what the feature is meant to eliminate.", "Incorrect. A Person Account is tied to a single account record and does not support relationships to several business accounts this way.", "Correct. The Account Name on the Contact is the primary account, and the Related Accounts list holds the indirect relationships."], "takeaway": "With Contacts to Multiple Accounts, the Contact's Account is the primary one and Account Contact Relationships store the extra accounts and roles."}]}]}, {"id": "service", "label": "Applications de service et d'assistance", "weight": 10, "topics": [{"id": "adm_case_channels", "title": "Canaux de création des requêtes", "fiche": {"one_liner": "Cases can be created automatically from inbound email (Email-to-Case), from a website form (Web-to-Case), from chat (Messaging) or from a self-service Experience Cloud site, and auto-response rules acknowledge the customer.", "need": "A software company, Northwind Software, receives support requests by email, through a form on its public website, via chat in its mobile app and through its customer portal. Every request must become a case in one queue, be acknowledged immediately, and be tracked without agents retyping anything. Salesforce provides one capture channel for each entry point.", "core": "Email-to-Case turns inbound emails into cases. The current, recommended option is On-Demand Email-to-Case, which runs entirely inside Salesforce: you create routing addresses (e.g. support@northwind.com), and Salesforce generates a unique Email Services address. You then configure your mail server to forward messages from the public address to that one. The older Email-to-Case Agent is installed behind the company firewall and is only needed when email must not leave the network. The message is saved as an Email Message related to the case. Salesforce adds a thread ID to outgoing emails so replies attach to the same case.\n\nWeb-to-Case generates HTML that you paste into your website. When a visitor submits the form, a case is created. It is public and anonymous: no login is needed. Salesforce matches the contact by email address when possible. There is a daily limit on Web-to-Case submissions (the commonly cited figure is 5,000 per 24 hours), and pending requests above the limit are not created.\n\nMessaging (Messaging for In-App and Web, Enhanced Messaging channels such as SMS and WhatsApp) lets customers chat with agents. Conversations are handled in the Omni-Channel console, and a case can be created or linked to a messaging session. An Experience Cloud site (formerly Community, a customer portal) gives authenticated customers a place to log in, create cases, track their status and search Knowledge articles. It is the channel for self-service and case visibility.\n\nAuto-response rules send an automatic email to the customer when a case is created from the web or by email, using an email template chosen according to rule entries (criteria). Escalation rules are a different feature: they act on cases that are not resolved in time.", "setup": ["Setup > Feature Settings > Service > Email-to-Case: enable Email-to-Case, then create Routing Addresses (choose On-Demand) and verify the generated Email Services address", "Setup > Feature Settings > Service > Web-to-Case: enable it, then use Web-to-Case HTML Generator to produce the form code and set a default case origin", "Setup > Feature Settings > Service > Case Auto-Response Rules: create the rule, add rule entries with criteria and an email template, then activate it", "Setup > Feature Settings > Service > Messaging > Messaging Settings: create a channel and route conversations with Omni-Channel", "Setup > Feature Settings > Digital Experiences > All Sites: create an Experience Cloud site so authenticated customers can create and follow cases"], "compare": [{"a": "Email-to-Case (On-Demand)", "b": "Email-to-Case Agent", "diff": "On-Demand runs in Salesforce with no software to install. The Agent is installed behind the firewall and is only for companies that need email to stay inside their network."}, {"a": "Web-to-Case", "b": "Experience Cloud site", "diff": "Web-to-Case is an anonymous one-way form that only creates a case. An Experience Cloud site requires login and lets the customer see, update and follow their cases and search Knowledge."}, {"a": "Auto-response rules", "b": "Escalation rules", "diff": "Auto-response rules email the customer when a case is created. Escalation rules reassign or notify when a case stays open too long."}], "signals": [{"if": "Customers send emails to support@ and each should become a case", "then": "Email-to-Case (On-Demand)"}, {"if": "A form on the public website, no login, creates a case", "then": "Web-to-Case"}, {"if": "Customers log in to see case status, update cases and search articles", "then": "Experience Cloud site"}, {"if": "Send the customer an immediate acknowledgement when a case is created from the web or email", "then": "Case auto-response rule"}], "traps": ["Choosing the Email-to-Case Agent by default: On-Demand is the standard answer unless the scenario says email cannot leave the company firewall.", "Using Web-to-Case when customers must log in or see their cases: Web-to-Case is anonymous and one-way, so you need an Experience Cloud site.", "Confusing auto-response rules (acknowledge the customer) with escalation rules (act on cases open too long) or assignment rules (choose the owner).", "Forgetting that Web-to-Case has a daily limit: very high volumes mean some submissions are not created."], "scenario": {"situation": "A retailer wants customers to request help from a contact form on its public website without creating an account. Each request must become a case, be assigned to the support queue and trigger an email confirming receipt to the customer.", "answer": "Use Web-to-Case with the generated HTML form, since no login is needed. Add a case assignment rule to route cases to the queue, and a case auto-response rule with an email template to send the confirmation. An Experience Cloud site is unnecessary because there is no authentication."}, "cards": [{"q": "Which Email-to-Case option is recommended, and why?", "a": "On-Demand Email-to-Case, because it runs entirely in Salesforce with no software to install. The Agent is only for companies needing email to stay behind their firewall."}, {"q": "What does Web-to-Case produce for the website?", "a": "An HTML form generated in Setup. Submissions create cases for anonymous visitors, with no login."}, {"q": "Which channel lets customers log in, track cases and search Knowledge?", "a": "An Experience Cloud site (customer portal)."}, {"q": "What does a case auto-response rule do?", "a": "It sends an automatic email, based on an email template and rule entries, to the customer when a case is created from the web or by email."}, {"q": "How do replies to an email stay on the same case?", "a": "Salesforce inserts a thread ID in outgoing case emails, so replies containing it are attached to the original case."}]}, "questions": [{"id": "71bffb04b1d0413983a757a444ab3061", "stem": "Northwind Outfitters wants customers to submit support requests from a form on its public website. Each submission must create a case automatically, and the company does not want customers to log in. The administrator needs the quickest declarative way to build the form. What should the administrator do?", "options": ["Generate the HTML form with the Web-to-Case setup tool and embed it on the website", "Build an Experience Cloud site and require customers to log in to create cases", "Enable Email-to-Case and ask customers to send requests to a routing address", "Configure Messaging for In-App and Web and embed the chat deployment"], "correct": [0], "explanations": ["Correct. Web-to-Case generates HTML that creates cases from a public web form without requiring customer login.", "An Experience Cloud site with required login contradicts the requirement that customers should not log in.", "Email-to-Case creates cases from emails, not from a form on a website, so it does not meet the need for a form.", "Messaging creates conversations handled by agents. It is not a form that creates a case on submission."], "takeaway": "Web-to-Case generates an HTML form that creates cases from a public website without customer login."}, {"id": "5fa284540ef9414b89ea926edb0ce50f", "stem": "Brightwave Telecom has turned on Email-to-Case. The support team wants to use On-Demand Email-to-Case so that no software is installed and no email servers need to be maintained behind the company firewall. What must the administrator do for emails to create cases?", "options": ["Enable Auto-Response Rules so incoming emails generate cases", "Create a routing address, then forward emails from the support mailbox to the Salesforce-generated email services address", "Create a Web-to-Case form and paste the support email address into the form", "Install the Email-to-Case Agent on a server inside the company network"], "correct": [1], "explanations": ["Auto-Response Rules send replies after a case exists. They do not create cases from emails.", "Correct. With On-Demand Email-to-Case, the administrator creates a routing address and the support mailbox forwards emails to the generated email services address.", "Web-to-Case forms create cases from web submissions and have no role in processing incoming emails.", "The Email-to-Case Agent is for the older client-side option, which is not what the team wants."], "takeaway": "On-Demand Email-to-Case needs routing addresses with email forwarding to the Salesforce-generated address, and no agent installation."}, {"id": "f3effe0429534618a5fd608fceadf6b2", "stem": "Contoso Bank wants a customer to receive an acknowledgement email as soon as a case is created from the website. The email must use a different template depending on the case Type. Which feature should the administrator configure?", "options": ["Escalation Rules with a notification action for each Type", "Email-to-Case routing addresses with a different reply address for each Type", "Case Assignment Rules with a notification template for each queue", "Case Auto-Response Rules with a rule entry for each Type"], "correct": [3], "explanations": ["Escalation Rules act on cases that are not resolved in time, not on new case creation.", "Routing addresses define how inbound email is processed. They do not choose a response template by Type.", "Assignment Rules decide the case owner. They do not send acknowledgement emails to the customer based on criteria.", "Correct. Auto-Response Rules use rule entries with criteria such as Type to send the matching email template to the contact."], "takeaway": "Case Auto-Response Rules send template-based replies to the requester, selected by rule entry criteria."}, {"id": "24993109432e421ab0a70d3ee79b8eeb", "stem": "Fabrikam Retail wants to offer a self-service portal where customers can sign in, open new cases, and track the status of their existing cases. The administrator is choosing between channels. Which two actions should the administrator take?", "options": ["Create an Experience Cloud site and add the case creation and list components", "Add the customers' contacts to a Web-to-Case form so they can view their cases", "Enable On-Demand Email-to-Case, which shows case status to customers", "Create a Case Auto-Response Rule that lists the status of all open cases", "Give external users access, such as Customer Community licenses, with sharing that lets them see their own cases"], "correct": [0, 4], "explanations": ["Correct. An Experience Cloud site provides a portal where signed-in customers can create and view cases.", "Web-to-Case forms are one-way submissions and give customers no way to view their cases.", "Email-to-Case creates cases from emails and gives no portal for tracking status.", "Auto-Response Rules send an email on creation and cannot provide ongoing case tracking.", "Correct. External users need licenses and sharing access to their own cases for the portal to display them."], "takeaway": "Experience Cloud is the channel for authenticated self-service where customers create and track their own cases."}, {"id": "d5d2057030354cacb4892324402d6d9b", "stem": "Adventure Works is setting up Web-to-Case. After the form is published, the company wants each submitted case to be reviewed by the right team and to avoid problems with spam submissions. Which two statements should the administrator apply?", "options": ["Web-to-Case cases are always owned by the user who generated the HTML form", "Enable reCAPTCHA on the Web-to-Case form to help limit spam submissions", "Web-to-Case forms cannot capture values in custom fields", "Case Assignment Rules can route Web-to-Case cases when the form includes the option to run them", "Web-to-Case requires every customer to have a Salesforce license"], "correct": [1, 3], "explanations": ["Ownership is determined by assignment rules or the default case owner, not by who generated the HTML.", "Correct. Web-to-Case supports reCAPTCHA to reduce spam submissions, and the administrator can add it to the form.", "Custom fields can be added to the generated HTML form, so this statement is false.", "Correct. Active assignment rules can route Web-to-Case cases to the right owner or queue.", "Customers submit the form anonymously and need no Salesforce license."], "takeaway": "Web-to-Case cases are routed through assignment rules and can use reCAPTCHA to limit spam, with no license needed for submitters."}]}, {"id": "adm_routing", "title": "Attribution, files d'attente et Omni-Channel", "fiche": {"one_liner": "Assignment rules and queues route cases and leads to owners at creation; Omni-Channel pushes work to available agents in real time, based on presence status and capacity, through queue-based or skills-based routing.", "need": "A telecom company receives thousands of cases by web, email and chat. Without automation, supervisors assign each case by hand, urgent ones wait, and some agents are overloaded while others sit idle. The company needs fair, fast and rule-driven distribution that respects who is online and how much work each agent can handle.", "core": "Case assignment rules (règles d'attribution des requêtes) are ordered rule entries with criteria. The first matching entry decides the owner, which can be a user or a queue. Only one assignment rule can be active at a time for Cases (and one for Leads). Rules run when a record is created or edited and the 'Assign using active assignment rule' option is applied, for example via Web-to-Case, Email-to-Case, or a checkbox on the page layout.\n\nA queue (file d'attente) is a shared holding place for records. Members, such as users, roles or groups, can see the records and take ownership of them. A queue only supports specific objects chosen in its setup, such as Case, Lead or custom objects. Queues alone are passive: someone must pick the record up.\n\nOmni-Channel (routage omnicanal) adds the active part: it pushes work items to agents in the Omni-Channel utility in the Service Console. Work is first placed in a queue, then routed through a routing configuration (configuration de routage). Queue-based routing sends work to agents who are members of the queue. Skills-based routing (routage par compétences) matches skills required by the work to skills held by the agents.\n\nTwo settings decide whether an agent receives work. The presence status (statut de présence) tells whether the agent is Available or Busy, and it controls which service channels (canaux de service) the agent can receive. Capacity (capacité) is the total workload an agent can carry. Each work item has a size, called its capacity weight (poids). Routing stops when the agent's capacity is full. Presence configurations (configurations de présence) set capacity and options per group of users.", "setup": ["Setup > Queues: create a queue, select the supported objects (e.g. Case) and add members.", "Setup > Case Assignment Rules: create a rule, add ordered rule entries with criteria and assignee, then activate it.", "Setup > Omni-Channel Settings: enable Omni-Channel, then add the Omni-Channel utility to the Console app via App Manager > Utility Items.", "Setup > Service Channels, Routing Configurations and Presence Statuses: define the channel, routing model and priority, size of work (capacity weight), and statuses linked to channels.", "Setup > Presence Configurations: set agent capacity and assign it to users or profiles; for skills-based routing, create Skills and assign them to users, then add skill requirements on work items."], "compare": [{"a": "Queue", "b": "Omni-Channel routing", "diff": "A queue is a passive pool where agents pick records. Omni-Channel pushes records to agents automatically, based on presence and capacity."}, {"a": "Queue-based routing", "b": "Skills-based routing", "diff": "Queue-based sends work to any member of the queue. Skills-based matches required skills (language, product expertise) to agent skills, and queue membership is not what decides the match."}, {"a": "Presence status", "b": "Capacity", "diff": "Presence status tells whether the agent can receive work and which channels. Capacity defines how much work the agent can hold at once."}], "signals": [{"if": "Cases must go to a team or owner based on field values (e.g. Priority, Origin)", "then": "Case assignment rules"}, {"if": "A shared inbox where team members take ownership themselves", "then": "Queue"}, {"if": "Work should be pushed automatically to available agents", "then": "Omni-Channel"}, {"if": "Cases need agents with specific expertise, such as a language or product", "then": "Omni-Channel skills-based routing"}], "traps": ["Only one case assignment rule can be active at a time, so multiple teams are handled with several rule entries in the same rule, not several active rules.", "Assignment rules do not fire automatically when a case is created manually unless the assign option is selected, whereas Web-to-Case and Email-to-Case can use them automatically.", "A queue is not a user: it cannot log in, and it only holds records for object types selected in its setup.", "Omni-Channel does not replace queues: work normally enters a queue first, and routing configuration alone does nothing without queue assignment and the agent being in a suitable presence status."], "scenario": {"situation": "A support center handles cases in English, French and German. Agents are often overloaded because supervisors assign manually. Management wants cases pushed automatically only to available agents who speak the case language, and each agent should hold at most a defined workload.", "answer": "Use Omni-Channel with skills-based routing: create language skills, assign them to agents, and attach skill requirements to cases. Set presence statuses so only available agents receive work, and configure capacity in a presence configuration. A queue alone would be passive and would not match languages."}, "cards": [{"q": "How many case assignment rules can be active at once?", "a": "Only one active rule for Cases (and one for Leads). It contains multiple ordered rule entries, and the first matching entry assigns the owner."}, {"q": "Who can be the assignee in a rule entry?", "a": "A user or a queue."}, {"q": "What is the key difference between queue-based and skills-based Omni-Channel routing?", "a": "Queue-based routes to members of a queue. Skills-based routes to agents whose skills match the skills required by the work item."}, {"q": "What do presence statuses control in Omni-Channel?", "a": "Whether an agent is Available or Busy, and which service channels they can receive work from."}, {"q": "What does capacity do in Omni-Channel?", "a": "It sets the total workload an agent can handle. Each work item has a capacity weight, and routing stops once the agent's capacity is reached."}]}, "questions": [{"id": "f5cb88c71aed4466a7b0ef72d85c1c2e", "stem": "Northwind Outfitters uses Service Cloud. Cases created from the web form must be automatically assigned to the Tier 1 Support queue, unless the Case Priority is High, in which case they must go to the Escalations queue. The administrator wants to configure this with declarative tools designed for this purpose. What should the administrator do?", "options": ["Create a workflow rule with a field update action that sets the Case Owner to the queue", "Create a case assignment rule with two rule entries ordered by priority, one for High priority and one for the remaining cases, and mark it as the active rule", "Create a Process Builder process that updates the Case Owner field based on the priority value", "Create an auto-response rule that sends cases to the correct queue based on priority"], "correct": [1], "explanations": ["Incorrect. Workflow rules are retired and cannot be used for new automation.", "Correct. Case assignment rules evaluate ordered rule entries and assign the case to a user or queue; only one rule can be active at a time.", "Incorrect. Process Builder is retired and must not be used for new requirements; assignment rules are the dedicated tool.", "Incorrect. Auto-response rules send email replies to customers, they do not change case ownership."], "takeaway": "Use case assignment rules with ordered rule entries to route cases to users or queues; only one assignment rule is active at a time."}, {"id": "76f8b876cf9749e7a2177dc1dfd187b2", "stem": "Contoso Telecom wants support agents to pick up cases from a shared pool. The administrator creates a queue for Billing Support. Managers also want to receive email notification when a case lands in the queue. Which two statements about queues are true?", "options": ["Records owned by a queue cannot be taken by any user and must be reassigned by an administrator", "Queue members can be users, public groups, roles, or roles and subordinates", "A queue can be configured to send an email to a distribution address or to all queue members when a new record is assigned", "A queue can only hold Case records", "A queue is a user license that can be assigned to agents"], "correct": [1, 2], "explanations": ["Incorrect. Queue members can accept records from the queue, subject to their access.", "Correct. Queue membership can include users, public groups, roles, and roles and subordinates.", "Correct. Queue email settings let you notify a queue email address and optionally the members.", "Incorrect. Queues support multiple objects, such as Leads, Cases, and custom objects that are enabled for queues.", "Incorrect. A queue is a record ownership holder, not a license."], "takeaway": "Queues can hold records of multiple supported objects, can include several member types, and can send email notifications."}, {"id": "43e45da3aa884a748944683b2d1486c0", "stem": "Fabrikam Insurance has agents with different language skills and certifications. The administrator wants Omni-Channel to route incoming cases to available agents who speak the customer's language and hold the matching license, instead of routing to a single queue of generalists. Which routing configuration should the administrator choose?", "options": ["Case assignment rules that choose a queue by language", "Skills-based routing with skills assigned to agents and skill requirements on the work items", "Presence statuses named after each language, selected manually by each agent", "Queue-based routing with one queue per language, with the Omni-Channel routing model set to Most Available"], "correct": [1], "explanations": ["Incorrect. Assignment rules place cases in a queue, but they do not match agent skills.", "Correct. Skills-based routing matches the skills required by a work item with agents who have those skills.", "Incorrect. Presence statuses indicate availability to receive work, not skills.", "Incorrect. This ties routing to queues, and cannot combine multiple skills such as language and certification as dynamically."], "takeaway": "Choose skills-based routing when work must be matched to agents by attributes such as language or certification."}, {"id": "b26684ae712941fa94f5e9ce6e6ee759", "stem": "Adventure Works sets up Omni-Channel for its chat and case teams. Each agent should be able to work on up to 5 units of work at once, and a chat should count for 2 units while a case counts for 1. The administrator is configuring routing configurations. Which two settings should the administrator use?", "options": ["Set the Capacity in the Service Channel's profile settings for each user", "Set capacity on the Case Assignment Rule entry", "Set capacity in the queue's email notification settings", "Set the Units of Capacity on each routing configuration to reflect the work item size", "Set the agent's capacity in the Presence Configuration assigned to the agent"], "correct": [3, 4], "explanations": ["Incorrect. Service channels define which object is routed, not per-user capacity.", "Incorrect. Assignment rule entries decide owners, not agent capacity.", "Incorrect. Queue notification settings only control emails.", "Correct. The routing configuration defines the capacity each work item consumes (Capacity Type: Inherit or Tag-Based, with units per item).", "Correct. The Presence Configuration defines the total capacity of agents, assigned by profile or permission set."], "takeaway": "Agent total capacity comes from the Presence Configuration, and the capacity each work item uses comes from its routing configuration."}, {"id": "bcff172f61b3497581ff6ca056d4cf52", "stem": "Tailspin Retail has enabled Omni-Channel. Agents report that they are logged in but never receive routed cases. The administrator confirms that the Omni-Channel widget appears in the Service Console and the routing configuration is attached to the queue. What is the most likely missing configuration?", "options": ["The case assignment rule is set to inactive", "The agents' profiles or permission sets are not granted access to the presence statuses that are mapped to the Case service channel", "The agents lack the Customize Application permission", "The agents have not been added to a public group for the Service Console app"], "correct": [1], "explanations": ["Incorrect. Omni-Channel routing by queue does not depend on assignment rules unless the case is routed there, and an inactive rule would not stop push to queue members.", "Correct. Agents must set a presence status that is available for the service channel; statuses are made available through profile or permission set access.", "Incorrect. Agents do not need administrative permissions to receive routed work.", "Incorrect. Public group membership is not what enables Omni-Channel work push."], "takeaway": "Agents receive Omni-Channel work only when they set a presence status that includes the relevant service channel and is enabled for their profile or permission set."}]}, {"id": "adm_escalation", "title": "Escalade, droits et jalons", "fiche": {"one_liner": "Entitlements define what support a customer is owed, entitlement processes and milestones enforce the time limits, and escalation rules reassign or notify when a case is neglected.", "need": "A software company sells Premium and Standard support contracts. Premium customers must get a first response within 1 hour and a resolution within 8 hours. Managers need to see which cases are at risk, and unattended cases must be reassigned or flagged automatically, without anyone watching a list all day.", "core": "Entitlement Management answers the question: what support is this customer entitled to? An entitlement (droit) is a record on an Account, linked to a Service Contract (contrat de service) or Contract Line Item, that states the support level and its dates. A case is linked to an entitlement, either manually or through the entitlement field on the case.\n\nAn entitlement process (processus de droits) is the timeline of steps attached to an entitlement. Each step is a milestone (jalon): a required action with a time limit, such as First Response or Resolution. Each milestone has a trigger time, criteria that define when it applies, and actions at three moments: success, warning and violation. These actions can be a field update, an email alert, a task or a flow-based action. A milestone is completed when its criteria are met, for example when the case status changes or a case comment is added. Entitlement processes come in two types: one that uses the case creation date as the start (type of process: Case, with the clock starting at case creation, case entitlement start date, etc.) and one that supports versioning (versioning lets you change a process while cases still use the older version). Business hours can be applied so the clock only runs during working time.\n\nEscalation rules (règles d'escalade) are different and independent: they work on cases and reassign or notify based on age and criteria, for example a High priority case still open after 4 hours. Each rule has entries evaluated in order, and each entry has criteria and one or more escalation actions with an age in hours, counted from the case creation or last modification, optionally within business hours. Only one escalation rule can be active at a time.", "setup": ["Setup > Feature Settings > Service > Entitlement Management > Entitlement Settings: enable Entitlement Management, then add the Entitlements related list and fields to page layouts", "Setup > Feature Settings > Service > Entitlement Management > Milestones: create milestone definitions (for example First Response, Resolution)", "Setup > Feature Settings > Service > Entitlement Management > Entitlement Processes: create a process, set its type, add milestones with time triggers, and define success, warning and violation actions; then activate it", "Setup > Feature Settings > Service > Escalation Rules: create a rule, add rule entries with criteria and escalation actions, then activate it", "Account > Entitlements related list: create the entitlement and select the entitlement process to apply"], "compare": [{"a": "Escalation rules", "b": "Entitlement process milestones", "diff": "Escalation rules work on any case by age and criteria, with simple reassign or notify actions. Milestones are tied to an entitlement, track a specific time limit for a specific support level, and show a countdown on the case."}, {"a": "Entitlement", "b": "Service contract", "diff": "A service contract is the agreement with the customer. An entitlement is the specific support right derived from it, and it is the record linked to cases and to the entitlement process."}], "signals": [{"if": "Different response times for Premium and Standard customers", "then": "Entitlements with entitlement processes and milestones"}, {"if": "Agents must see a countdown to the deadline on the case", "then": "Milestones (milestone tracker on the case)"}, {"if": "Reassign a High priority case to a manager if still open after 4 hours", "then": "Case escalation rule"}, {"if": "Customer purchased a support contract with start and end dates", "then": "Service contract with entitlements"}], "traps": ["Only one escalation rule can be active at a time, so all escalation needs must be handled as entries in that single rule.", "Milestones cannot exist without an entitlement process, and the process must be linked to an entitlement that is linked to the case.", "An escalation rule does not run on its own to reassign cases for all situations: it only runs when cases meet its criteria, and it is not a substitute for assignment rules.", "Escalation rules and assignment rules are different: assignment rules decide the initial owner of a case, while escalation rules act on cases that stay unresolved."], "scenario": {"situation": "A company offers Gold support with a 1-hour first response and Silver support with a 4-hour first response. Agents want to see on each case how much time remains, and the support manager must be emailed when a Gold case is about to breach.", "answer": "Enable Entitlement Management, create an entitlement for each customer linked to its service contract, and build an entitlement process for each level. Add a First Response milestone with the right time trigger and a warning action that emails the manager. The milestone tracker shows the countdown to agents."}, "cards": [{"q": "What is an entitlement in Salesforce?", "a": "A record on an account that defines the support a customer is entitled to, often linked to a service contract, and that can be linked to cases."}, {"q": "What is a milestone?", "a": "A required step in an entitlement process with a time limit, such as First Response, with actions on success, warning and violation."}, {"q": "How many escalation rules can be active at once?", "a": "Only one. It contains several rule entries evaluated in order."}, {"q": "What can escalation actions do?", "a": "Reassign the case to a user or queue and send notification emails when the case meets the age criteria."}, {"q": "Which feature gives agents a countdown on the case?", "a": "The milestone tracker, available once the case has an entitlement with an entitlement process."}]}, "questions": [{"id": "a2661c8cb0134fa99804d423f518e6ba", "stem": "Northwind Telecom wants every high priority Case to be automatically reassigned to the Tier 2 Support queue if it is still not closed 4 hours after it was created. Reps should not have to take any action. Which feature should the administrator configure?", "options": ["Case Assignment Rules", "Entitlement Process with a milestone", "Email-to-Case routing", "Case Escalation Rules"], "correct": [3], "explanations": ["Assignment rules run when a Case is created or updated to set the initial owner. They do not act on elapsed time.", "Milestones track time against a support commitment and trigger actions, but the scenario only asks for time-based reassignment, not entitlement tracking.", "Email-to-Case creates Cases from incoming emails and can route them initially, but it does not escalate open Cases over time.", "Escalation rules evaluate Cases that meet criteria after a set time and can reassign them to a user or queue, which fits this need exactly."], "takeaway": "Use Case Escalation Rules to reassign or notify when a Case remains open past a time threshold, and Assignment Rules only for initial ownership."}, {"id": "a1558af4ba07470f9031ccbc000c7ba2", "stem": "Contoso Cloud sells Gold and Silver support plans. Gold customers must receive a first response within 1 hour, and Silver customers within 8 hours. The administrator has enabled Entitlement Management. What should the administrator create so that the correct response time is applied to each customer's Cases?", "options": ["One Case Escalation Rule for each plan, with different time criteria", "One Service Contract for all customers, with a single milestone", "One Entitlement Process for each plan, linked to the matching Entitlements", "A Validation Rule on Case that checks the account's plan"], "correct": [2], "explanations": ["Escalation rules are not tied to what a customer is entitled to and do not track milestones with countdown timers on the Case.", "A Service Contract describes the agreement, but time limits come from the Entitlement Process. A single milestone would not distinguish Gold from Silver.", "An Entitlement Process defines the milestones and time limits, and each Entitlement references the process that applies to that customer's plan.", "A Validation Rule only blocks saves when data is invalid. It cannot apply or track response time commitments."], "takeaway": "Entitlement Processes hold the milestones and timings, and each Entitlement points to the process that governs the customer's support level."}, {"id": "f8fd84f6becc4de1993865ad6ad8933f", "stem": "Fabrikam Services wants to record the contractual terms of its annual support agreements, including start date, end date and the products covered, and then generate the corresponding Entitlements for each agreement. Which object should the administrator use to represent the agreement itself?", "options": ["Entitlement Template", "Asset", "Contract", "Service Contract"], "correct": [3], "explanations": ["Entitlement Templates define standard entitlement terms for products, but they are not the record of a customer's agreement.", "An Asset represents a product the customer owns and can be covered by an Entitlement, but it is not the support agreement.", "The Contract object is used for general business agreements and is not the object that links to Entitlements in Entitlement Management.", "A Service Contract represents a customer support agreement, and Entitlements can be added to it through Contract Line Items or directly."], "takeaway": "A Service Contract represents the support agreement and groups the Entitlements and Contract Line Items that define what the customer is covered for."}, {"id": "7a2986682a9c476faff3a8d785b75a39", "stem": "Adventure Works needs Cases for Premium customers to trigger actions as a milestone approaches its deadline and when it is violated. The administrator is editing a milestone in an Entitlement Process. Which two actions can be configured on a milestone? (Choose 2.)", "options": ["Warning actions that run before the milestone is violated", "Success actions that run when the milestone is completed", "Rollup actions that update the parent Account", "Assignment actions that run when the Case is created", "Approval actions that route the Case to a manager"], "correct": [0, 1], "explanations": ["Warning actions run a set time before the milestone's deadline, such as notifying the owner that time is almost up.", "Success actions can be defined to run automatically when a milestone is completed in time, such as updating a field or sending an email.", "Rollup actions do not exist for milestones. Milestone actions are success, warning and violation.", "Case creation assignment is handled by Assignment Rules, not by a milestone action.", "Milestones support success, warning and violation actions, but not approval routing as a milestone action type."], "takeaway": "Each milestone can define Success, Warning and Violation actions, each able to update fields, send emails, create tasks or call flows or outbound messages."}, {"id": "c4d4f32fca59493385d8718d4d0090e6", "stem": "Tailspin Support wants a Case to be linked to the right Entitlement automatically, and wants agents to see how much time remains on each milestone while working a Case. Which two steps should the administrator take? (Choose 2.)", "options": ["Add the Milestones related list to the Case page layout", "Create a Case Escalation Rule that populates the Entitlement field", "Create a Queue for each Entitlement", "Add the Milestone Tracker component to the Case record page in Lightning App Builder", "Enable Entitlement Management and set up default entitlement behavior in Entitlement Settings"], "correct": [3, 4], "explanations": ["The Milestones related list lists records but is not the component that shows a countdown of time remaining. The Milestone Tracker is the one used to display that.", "Escalation Rules reassign or notify on time-based criteria. They do not populate the Entitlement field.", "Queues hold records for groups of users and have no role in linking Entitlements or showing milestone timers.", "The Milestone Tracker component on the Case record page shows the active milestones and the time remaining, which meets the second need.", "Entitlement Settings let you turn on the feature and choose options such as automatically adding the entitlement to Cases, which fits the first need."], "takeaway": "Enable Entitlement Management and its settings to apply entitlements to Cases, then add the Milestone Tracker to the Case record page so agents see milestone countdowns."}]}, {"id": "adm_knowledge", "title": "Salesforce Knowledge", "fiche": {"one_liner": "Salesforce Knowledge is a knowledge base of articles that agents write, publish and attach to cases, organized by article types (record types), data categories and channel visibility.", "need": "Northwind Telecom's agents answer the same router questions all day and give inconsistent answers. They need one approved source of truth. Agents find the right article while working a case, attach it to the case, and the same content is shown to customers on the help site, while internal-only fixes stay hidden from them.", "core": "Lightning Knowledge uses a single object, Knowledge (Knowledge__kav), which holds all articles. Different kinds of articles (FAQ, How-To, Troubleshooting) are distinguished by record types, not by separate article types as in Salesforce Classic. Each record type can have its own page layout, and record types are assigned through profiles or permission sets. Each article has a record type, fields, a title and a URL name.\n\nData categories (organized in category groups, which are hierarchies) classify articles, for example Product > Routers > Model X. They control who can see which articles: visibility is granted to roles, permission sets or profiles by category. Categories are about access and navigation, record types are about structure and layout.\n\nThe publishing lifecycle has three statuses: Draft (being written, editable), Published (live, visible to those with access), and Archived (retired, no longer shown). To edit a published article you create a new draft version, and the published version stays live until the new one is published. Publishing needs the Manage Articles permission (or being the assigned reviewer in an approval flow). Authors need the Knowledge User feature license, which is a checkbox on the user record, plus article permissions.\n\nChannel visibility decides where a published article appears: Internal App (Salesforce users such as agents), Customer (authenticated Experience Cloud site users), Partner (partner users), and Public Knowledge Base (guests, no login). Set it per article. On cases, agents use the Knowledge component in the case record page to search and attach articles, and can also send the article to the customer. Attached articles appear in the case's related articles list, which gives reporting on article usefulness.", "setup": ["Setup > Knowledge Settings: Enable Lightning Knowledge, which also enables record types on the Knowledge object", "Setup > Object Manager > Knowledge: create record types, page layouts and fields for each kind of article", "Setup > Data Categories: create category groups and categories, then assign category visibility to roles, permission sets or profiles", "Setup > Users: tick the Knowledge User checkbox, then assign article permissions through a profile or permission set", "Lightning App Builder: add the Knowledge component to the Case record page so agents can find and attach articles"], "compare": [{"a": "Record types on Knowledge", "b": "Data categories", "diff": "Record types define the structure of an article (layout, fields, which kind it is). Data categories classify articles and control which users can see them."}, {"a": "Article status Archived", "b": "Article status Draft", "diff": "Draft is work in progress that nobody outside authors sees. Archived is a retired published article that is no longer shown but is kept for history. Drafts are not archived, and archiving applies to published versions."}], "signals": [{"if": "Different kinds of articles with different fields, such as FAQ vs How-To", "then": "Knowledge record types, with a layout for each"}, {"if": "Only some roles or teams should see certain articles", "then": "Data categories with category visibility"}, {"if": "Articles visible to anyone without logging in", "then": "Public Knowledge Base channel (guest access)"}, {"if": "Agent wants to use an article to solve a case", "then": "Knowledge component on the Case page, then attach the article to the case"}], "traps": ["Article types are a Classic concept. In Lightning Knowledge you use record types on the single Knowledge object.", "Having the Knowledge User checkbox alone is not enough: article permissions (such as create, publish) must also be granted through a profile or permission set.", "A published article that has not been given the Customer or Public channel is not visible to external users, even if category visibility allows it.", "Data categories control visibility, not the layout or the fields of an article."], "scenario": {"situation": "A company wants agents to write troubleshooting articles with a Symptoms field and FAQ articles with only a question and an answer. Billing articles must be visible only to the Finance support team. All must be visible to agents inside Salesforce, but not to customers.", "answer": "Create two record types on Knowledge with separate page layouts for the different fields. Create a Billing data category and give visibility only to the Finance support role. Set channel to Internal App only on each article, so customers do not see it."}, "cards": [{"q": "What replaced article types in Lightning Knowledge?", "a": "Record types on the single Knowledge (Knowledge__kav) object."}, {"q": "What are the three article statuses?", "a": "Draft, Published and Archived."}, {"q": "What are the four channels for article visibility?", "a": "Internal App, Customer, Partner and Public Knowledge Base."}, {"q": "What do data categories do?", "a": "They organize articles in hierarchies within category groups and control which users can see them."}, {"q": "What is needed to let a user author articles?", "a": "The Knowledge User checkbox on the user record, plus article permissions from a profile or permission set."}]}, "questions": [{"id": "8e8720968279480498057c0ae2801263", "stem": "Northwind Outfitters has a Salesforce Knowledge base used by support agents and customers. The company wants FAQ articles to contain only Question and Answer fields, while Troubleshooting Guide articles need Symptoms, Cause, and Resolution fields. What should the administrator do?", "options": ["Create two public groups and assign each group a different set of article fields", "Create two Knowledge data categories, one for FAQ and one for Troubleshooting Guide", "Create two Lightning record pages for the Case object, one for each article format", "Create two article record types on the Knowledge object, each with its own page layout"], "correct": [3], "explanations": ["Incorrect. Public groups are used for sharing and membership, not for controlling the fields of an article.", "Incorrect. Data categories classify articles for navigation and visibility. They do not change which fields appear on an article.", "Incorrect. Case record pages control how cases are displayed. They do not define the structure of Knowledge articles.", "Correct. In Lightning Knowledge, article record types let you present different fields and layouts for different kinds of articles on the single Knowledge object."], "takeaway": "Use Knowledge article record types with page layouts to give different article formats different fields."}, {"id": "193d15bcb2b74580b33e98081068e7a3", "stem": "Contoso Telecom organizes its articles with a data category group named Products (Mobile, Internet, TV). Agents in the Mobile support team should see only articles categorized under Mobile, while the Internet team sees only Internet articles. What should the administrator configure?", "options": ["Data category visibility on roles, permission sets, or profiles for each team", "Field-level security on the article Title field for each team's profile", "A validation rule on the article that blocks viewing by the wrong team", "Different article record types, one per product line"], "correct": [0], "explanations": ["Correct. Data category visibility settings control which categories, and therefore which articles, each user can see.", "Incorrect. Field-level security hides fields, not whole articles based on their categorization.", "Incorrect. Validation rules fire on save and cannot restrict who can read an article.", "Incorrect. Record types define article structure, not visibility based on product categorization."], "takeaway": "Data category visibility, assigned through roles, permission sets, or profiles, controls which articles users can access."}, {"id": "38be186c13a345318992ff397d45c5a2", "stem": "At Bluebird Insurance, an author finished writing a new article and saved it. A reviewer notices that a published article about claim deadlines is outdated, and the company wants to remove it from all channels while keeping it for future reference rather than deleting it. What should the administrator or knowledge manager do?", "options": ["Delete the published article and recreate it later from scratch", "Change the article's data category to None so it no longer appears", "Send the article back to Draft status by editing the article's record type", "Archive the published article"], "correct": [3], "explanations": ["Incorrect. Deleting loses the article, while the requirement is to keep it for future reference.", "Incorrect. Removing categories does not take the article out of the published state or its channels.", "Incorrect. Changing the record type does not change the publication status of an article.", "Correct. Archiving removes a published article from all channels while keeping it available in the archived articles list."], "takeaway": "Archiving takes a published article out of all channels while retaining it, whereas deleting removes it permanently."}, {"id": "1295b1cc9a4040a5b0c021cff363806d", "stem": "Fabrikam Bank is rolling out Salesforce Knowledge. Articles about internal fraud procedures must be readable by agents inside Salesforce, but must not appear on the public help site or in the customer community. Which two actions should the administrator take? (Choose two.)", "options": ["Create a separate Knowledge base for the public help site", "Archive the articles after agents have read them", "Publish the articles with Visible in Internal App selected only", "Select Visible in Public Knowledge Base so the articles are protected by a login", "Make sure Visible in Public Knowledge Base and Visible to Customer are unchecked on those articles"], "correct": [2, 4], "explanations": ["Incorrect. Salesforce Knowledge uses one knowledge base, and channel visibility is set per article.", "Incorrect. Archived articles are not available to agents in the normal Knowledge search, so this does not meet the need.", "Correct. Internal App visibility makes the article available to agents inside Salesforce.", "Incorrect. The Public Knowledge Base channel exposes articles to anonymous visitors, not behind a login.", "Correct. Leaving the customer and public channels unselected keeps the articles off the community and public site."], "takeaway": "Channel visibility checkboxes on each article (Internal App, Customer, Partner, Public Knowledge Base) decide where it can be seen."}, {"id": "b5ac84f80d744606bda6ddf15389d83d", "stem": "Adventure Works support agents resolve cases using Knowledge articles in the Lightning console. Management wants to track which articles help resolve cases and let agents send the solution to the customer. Which two actions can an agent take from a case? (Choose two.)", "options": ["Merge the article into the case record so the case becomes an article", "Use the article in an email to the customer by attaching it to the case email", "Convert the article into a case comment through a validation rule", "Assign the case as a child record of the article", "Attach an article to the case from the Knowledge component"], "correct": [1, 4], "explanations": ["Incorrect. Cases cannot be merged into articles. Articles can be created from a case, which is a different action.", "Correct. An attached article can be included in a customer email, for example by inserting article content or a link into the email.", "Incorrect. Validation rules do not convert articles into case comments.", "Incorrect. Articles are linked to cases through attachment, not by treating the case as a child of the article.", "Correct. Agents can attach articles to a case from the Knowledge component, which also lets reports show article usage on cases."], "takeaway": "Agents attach articles to cases from the Knowledge component, which enables article usage reporting and sharing the solution with customers."}]}, {"id": "adm_console", "title": "Console de service, macros et texte rapide", "fiche": {"one_liner": "A Lightning console app lets agents work on many records in tabs and subtabs, with utilities, macros and quick text to speed up repetitive tasks.", "need": "A support center at an online retailer handles hundreds of cases a day. Agents juggle cases, contacts and orders, and keep retyping the same greetings and steps. A console app keeps everything on one screen, while macros and quick text cut handling time and standardize answers.", "core": "A console app is a Lightning app whose navigation type is set to Console navigation. Instead of one record per page, agents open several records as workspace tabs, and related records open as subtabs under their parent (for example, a contact opened from a case). Navigation items appear in a left-hand navigation menu, and agents can pin tabs. The utility bar is a fixed bar at the bottom of the app giving one-click access to tools such as History, Notes, Open CTI softphone, Macros or Quick Text. Utility bar is available in standard navigation apps too, but it is typical of consoles.\n\nMacros (Lightning Experience) automate repetitive multi-step actions in the console, for example selecting an email template, sending the email, updating Status to Closed and posting to Chatter. A macro is a set of instructions run with one click from the Macros utility. Macros can be run on one record, or in bulk: the agent selects several cases in a list view and runs a macro on all of them (Macro Bulk Run is a feature of console apps with the Macros utility). Users need permissions to run macros and access to the records and objects the macro touches.\n\nQuick text (Quick Text) stores reusable snippets of text such as greetings, answers and closings. Agents insert them in email, chat or the case Feed using a shortcut (typing a forward slash plus keyword, or the Quick Text utility). Quick text can include merge fields so the customer's name is filled in automatically. It saves typing but does not change field values or run steps: that is the macro's job.\n\nCase Feed and the highlights panel give agents context, but the exam focus is on matching the right tool: console for multitasking, macros for multi-step automation, quick text for reusable text.", "setup": ["Setup > App Manager > New Lightning App (or edit an app) > choose Console navigation as navigation style, then add navigation items and utility items.", "Setup > App Manager > edit the app > Utility Items > add Macros, Quick Text, History or other utilities.", "Setup > Macros and Quick Text Settings > Enable Macros (and enable Quick Text and Macro Bulk Run options as needed).", "Setup > Permission Sets (or Profiles) > grant Run Macros, Manage Macros and Quick Text permissions to the right users.", "App Launcher > Macros tab or the Macros utility > New to build a macro with instructions; Quick Text tab > New to create snippets."], "compare": [{"a": "Macro", "b": "Quick text", "diff": "A macro automates a series of actions (select template, update fields, send email); quick text only inserts a predefined piece of text into a message or field."}, {"a": "Console navigation", "b": "Standard navigation", "diff": "Console navigation opens records in workspace tabs and subtabs for multitasking; standard navigation opens one record at a time, replacing the page."}, {"a": "Utility bar", "b": "Navigation items/tabs", "diff": "The utility bar is a persistent footer for tools that stay available on every page; navigation items lead to objects and pages."}], "signals": [{"if": "Agents work on many cases at once and need to switch quickly between records", "then": "Lightning console app with workspace tabs and subtabs"}, {"if": "Agents repeat the same sequence of steps (send email, update status, post to Chatter)", "then": "Macro"}, {"if": "Close many similar cases at once from a list view", "then": "Bulk macro run in the console"}, {"if": "Agents retype standard greetings or answers", "then": "Quick text, with merge fields for personalization"}], "traps": ["Quick text cannot update fields or run actions, so do not pick it for a multi-step task; that is a macro.", "Macros are built for console apps with the Macros utility, so a standard navigation app without that utility will not offer them.", "Giving users a console app does not give them macro permissions: the permissions Run Macros or Manage Macros must be granted separately.", "Subtabs belong to a parent workspace tab, so do not confuse them with separate workspace tabs."], "scenario": {"situation": "A support manager wants agents to close simple billing cases with one click: send the 'Refund processed' email template, set Status to Closed, and post a note. Agents also want to close ten similar cases from a list view at the same time.", "answer": "Create a macro with those instructions (send email with template, update Status, post to Chatter) and add the Macros utility to the console app. Enable Macro Bulk Run so agents can select the ten cases in a list view and run it once. Quick text alone would only insert text."}, "cards": [{"q": "What setting turns a Lightning app into a console app?", "a": "Setting its navigation style to Console navigation in App Manager."}, {"q": "What is the difference between a workspace tab and a subtab?", "a": "A workspace tab is a primary record; a subtab is a related record opened under that parent tab."}, {"q": "What is the utility bar?", "a": "A fixed bar at the bottom of the app giving one-click access to tools such as Macros, Quick Text, History or a softphone."}, {"q": "What does a macro do?", "a": "It runs a set of predefined instructions with one click, such as sending an email, updating fields and posting to Chatter, and it can be run in bulk on several records."}, {"q": "What is quick text and how is it personalized?", "a": "A reusable text snippet inserted into emails, chats or Feed; merge fields such as the contact name personalize it."}]}, "questions": [{"id": "494475457d924d34abe6e8f7f8bc4aad", "stem": "Northwind Outfitters has built a macro that selects an email template, sets the Case Status to Working, and sends the email. Agents using the Service Console app have the Run Macros permission and can read the macro, but they cannot find any way to run it from the console. What should the administrator do?", "options": ["Add the Macros tab to the app's Navigation Items", "Create a Quick Text record that points to the macro", "Add the Macros utility item to the utility bar of the console app in App Manager", "Add the macro as a button on the Case page layout"], "correct": [2], "explanations": ["Incorrect. Macros are not exposed as a navigation tab. They run from a utility bar item.", "Incorrect. Quick Text inserts predefined text in a composer. It cannot reference or launch a macro.", "Correct. In a Lightning console app, macros are run from the Macros utility, which must be added to the app's utility bar.", "Incorrect. Macros are not added to page layouts as buttons. They are run from the Macros utility."], "takeaway": "Macros are run from the Macros utility, so it must be added to the utility bar of the Lightning console app."}, {"id": "61b0e89c9e62427594d46a6e2a0af7d7", "stem": "At Contoso Telecom, agents answer customers through the email composer in Case Feed. They repeatedly retype the same greeting and closing, and want to insert them with a short shortcut, including the contact's first name through a merge field. Which solution meets the need with the least effort?", "options": ["Create Quick Text messages and let agents insert them in the composer", "Create an email template for each greeting and closing", "Create a macro that sends the greeting automatically", "Create a Flow that populates the email body"], "correct": [0], "explanations": ["Correct. Quick Text stores reusable snippets, supports merge fields, and agents can insert them in the Case Feed email composer.", "Incorrect. Email templates are meant for full messages, not small snippets inserted while typing, and would add many templates to maintain.", "Incorrect. A macro automates multi-step actions. It is more than is needed to insert a short snippet while composing.", "Incorrect. A Flow is not a lightweight way to insert snippets into a composer and would be far more complex than needed."], "takeaway": "Use Quick Text for reusable snippets with merge fields in the composer, and macros for multi-step automation."}, {"id": "72f7ec4bdde04c74a6954f8945d811dd", "stem": "A support manager at Fabrikam Energy needs to close 80 resolved cases from a list view. Each closure requires the same steps, such as updating the Status field and posting a standard email. The manager works in the Service Console app. What should the administrator do?", "options": ["Enable bulk macros in Macro Settings so the manager can run the macro on the selected cases from the list view", "Create a Quick Text message and paste it in each case", "Tell the manager to use inline editing on the list view", "Create a Mass Email action from the Cases tab"], "correct": [0], "explanations": ["Correct. Once bulk macros are enabled, a macro can be run on multiple selected records from a list view in a console app.", "Incorrect. Quick Text inserts text one record at a time and does not automate the other steps.", "Incorrect. Inline editing changes field values only. It cannot send the email or run the other steps.", "Incorrect. Mass email sends emails but does not update the case status as part of the same repeatable set of steps."], "takeaway": "Enable bulk macros in Macro Settings to run one macro against many selected records from a list view in a console app."}, {"id": "49089b29365a44fc85bebe62b0b07d65", "stem": "Tailspin Toys agents work in a Lightning console app and switch constantly between cases. They want to keep a notepad and their recently viewed records available on every page without leaving the current tab. Which two actions should the administrator take?", "options": ["Add the Notes related list to the Case page layout", "Add the Notes utility item to the app's utility bar", "Change the app navigation style to Standard navigation", "Add the History utility item to the app's utility bar", "Add the Recent Items component to the Lightning Home page"], "correct": [1, 3], "explanations": ["Incorrect. A related list appears only on Case records, so it is not available across every page.", "Correct. Utility bar items such as Notes stay available across all pages of the console app.", "Incorrect. Switching to Standard navigation removes the console tab behavior and does not provide persistent tools.", "Correct. The History utility lists recently viewed records and is accessible from any page in the app.", "Incorrect. A Home page component is visible only on the Home page, not while working on other tabs."], "takeaway": "The utility bar gives persistent access to tools like Notes and History from every page of a Lightning app."}, {"id": "13e6f411bc9e45c2ac9c0ae1efd9e4d5", "stem": "Adventure Works is launching a new support team and wants agents to work in a Lightning console with workspace tabs and subtabs for cases and related records. The administrator creates the app in App Manager. Which two settings should be part of the configuration?", "options": ["Enable a Console checkbox on the Case object", "Select Console navigation as the navigation style", "Build the app only as a Lightning App Page", "Assign the app to the profiles of the support agents", "Install the Service Console package from AppExchange"], "correct": [1, 3], "explanations": ["Incorrect. There is no Console checkbox on objects. Console behavior comes from the app's navigation style.", "Correct. The Console navigation style provides workspace tabs and subtabs, which are the core of a console app.", "Incorrect. A Lightning App Page is a type of page layout and not the way a console app is created.", "Correct. The app must be made available to the agents' profiles so they can see and open it.", "Incorrect. The Lightning console is native and needs no AppExchange package."], "takeaway": "A Lightning console app is created in App Manager with the Console navigation style and assigned to user profiles."}]}]}, {"id": "productivite", "label": "Productivité et collaboration", "weight": 10, "topics": [{"id": "adm_activities", "title": "Activités et Einstein Activity Capture", "fiche": {"one_liner": "Tasks and Events record to-dos and meetings on records; Shared Activities link one activity to several contacts; Activity Timeline shows them; Einstein Activity Capture syncs email and calendar without storing them as records.", "need": "A sales rep at a software reseller emails and meets several people at the same customer every week. The manager wants one place to see past and upcoming interactions, without the rep retyping each email or meeting. Activities track the work, and Einstein Activity Capture automates the logging.", "core": "Activities are two objects: Tasks (to-dos, calls, with a due date and no time of day) and Events (calendar items with a start and end time). Both can be related to a Who (a Contact or Lead, called the Name field) and a What (an Account, Opportunity, Case or other record, called Related To). Tasks and Events appear in the Activity Timeline (chronologie des activités), a component on the record page showing Upcoming & Overdue items and past activities, including logged calls, emails and meetings.\n\nShared Activities lets an Event relate to up to 50 contacts (the primary one plus others, shown as invitees). Tasks can only have one Name (Who). Once enabled it cannot be disabled. Each contact then shows the same Event in their own timeline. Without it, an Event has a single Who.\n\nEinstein Activity Capture (EAC) connects Gmail or Microsoft 365 accounts and syncs emails and calendar events with Salesforce. Matching emails and events are shown in the Activity Timeline of related records (contacts, leads, accounts, opportunities). The key exam point: captured emails and events are stored in the Salesforce-managed capture store (AWS), not as standard Task or Event records in your org. As a result, they do not count against data storage, but they are not available in reports, list views or flows the way standard activities are. Contacts and events can also be synced in either direction depending on the configuration. Users and admins assign EAC through a configuration and permission set (Einstein Activity Capture user).", "setup": ["Setup > Activity Settings: enable Allow Users to Relate Multiple Contacts to Tasks and Events (Shared Activities).", "Setup > Einstein Activity Capture > Settings: turn on EAC and create or edit a configuration (email sync, event sync, contact sync, sharing).", "Setup > Permission Sets: assign the Standard Einstein Activity Capture permission set (or add users to the configuration) so they can connect their account.", "Setup > Lightning App Builder: add the Activity Timeline component to the record page (Contact, Account, Opportunity, Lead)."], "compare": [{"a": "Standard Task/Event records", "b": "Emails and events captured by Einstein Activity Capture", "diff": "Standard activities are real records in your org and are reportable. EAC data lives in the capture store, is visible in the timeline, but is not stored as Task or Event records."}, {"a": "Task", "b": "Event", "diff": "A Task has a due date and no time slot, an Event has a start and end time. Only Events with Shared Activities can have multiple contacts (invitees) in the way tested."}], "signals": [{"if": "Reps do not want to log emails and meetings manually", "then": "Einstein Activity Capture"}, {"if": "One meeting must appear on several contacts' records", "then": "Shared Activities (multiple contacts on Events)"}, {"if": "See past and upcoming interactions in one view on a record", "then": "Activity Timeline component"}, {"if": "Need to report on synced emails as standard activity records", "then": "Not EAC: use manually logged activities or Einstein Activity Capture's limits apply"}], "traps": ["Thinking EAC creates standard Task and Event records: it does not, so reports and flows cannot use them as normal activities.", "Confusing Who (Contact or Lead) with What (Account, Opportunity, Case, other): Who is the person, What is the business record.", "Assuming Shared Activities can be turned off after enabling: it cannot be disabled.", "Assuming Tasks allow multiple contacts like Events: Shared Activities is mainly about Events with several invitees, and each activity keeps a single What."], "scenario": {"situation": "A sales director complains that reps forget to log customer emails and meetings. She also wants a quarterly meeting with three contacts at one account to appear on each contact's record. Reps use Microsoft 365.", "answer": "Enable Einstein Activity Capture with a configuration for Microsoft 365 and assign the permission set, so emails and events sync automatically into the Activity Timeline. Enable Shared Activities so the meeting relates to several contacts. Add the Activity Timeline to the record pages."}, "cards": [{"q": "What are the two activity objects and the main difference?", "a": "Tasks (to-dos with a due date) and Events (calendar items with a start and end time)."}, {"q": "What do Who and What mean on an activity?", "a": "Who is the Contact or Lead (the person); What is the related record such as an Account, Opportunity or Case."}, {"q": "What does Shared Activities allow?", "a": "Relating one Event to multiple contacts (up to 50), so it shows in each contact's Activity Timeline. Once enabled, it cannot be disabled."}, {"q": "Where does Einstein Activity Capture store synced emails and events?", "a": "In a Salesforce-managed capture store outside your org's standard Task and Event records. They display in the timeline but do not use data storage."}, {"q": "What is the consequence for reporting?", "a": "Captured emails and events cannot be used like standard activities in reports, list views or automation."}]}, "questions": [{"id": "1af83af2db014ced801eda3387f381e1", "stem": "Northwind Logistics reps often meet with several people from the same customer account in a single meeting. Today they log one Event per meeting and relate it to only one Contact, so the other attendees' records show no history. The admin must let reps relate several Contacts to one Event so it appears on each Contact's record. What should the admin do?", "options": ["Enable Shared Activities in Activity Settings", "Create a custom junction object between Event and Contact and add it to the page layout", "Build a Flow that clones the Event for every attendee after it is saved", "Add the other attendees as Campaign Members on a campaign linked to the Event"], "correct": [0], "explanations": ["Correct. Shared Activities lets users relate multiple Contacts to an Event or Task, and the activity then appears on each related Contact's timeline.", "A custom junction object is unnecessary and would not appear in the standard activity timeline. Salesforce already provides this capability natively.", "Cloning creates duplicate Events that can be edited and reported on separately. This is the workaround Shared Activities replaces.", "Campaign Members track marketing responses and have nothing to do with relating attendees to an Event."], "takeaway": "Enable Shared Activities in Activity Settings to relate multiple Contacts to a single Task or Event."}, {"id": "a9f63c67c9f64b9c93718be939a3f948", "stem": "Contoso Energy deploys Einstein Activity Capture so reps' emails and calendar events sync with Salesforce. A sales operations manager asks the admin how the captured data behaves. Which two statements about Einstein Activity Capture are accurate?", "options": ["Captured emails and events can be used as triggers for record-triggered flows.", "Captured emails are always visible to every user who can view the related record, regardless of the sharing settings configured.", "Captured emails and events are stored as standard Task and Event records and count against data storage.", "Captured emails and events are shown in the Activity Timeline on related records.", "Captured emails and events are stored outside the Salesforce database, so standard reports cannot use them."], "correct": [3, 4], "explanations": ["Because they are not stored as standard activity records, they cannot fire record-triggered flows.", "Visibility depends on the sharing options set in the Einstein Activity Capture configuration, so it is not automatic for everyone.", "Captured emails and events are not saved as Task and Event records, so they do not consume Salesforce data storage.", "Correct. Synced emails and events appear in the Activity Timeline on related records such as Contacts, Accounts, and Opportunities.", "Correct. They are stored in a separate store outside the Salesforce database, so they are not available in standard reports or other platform features that depend on activity records."], "takeaway": "Einstein Activity Capture data appears in the Activity Timeline but is not stored as Salesforce activity records, so it is unavailable for standard reporting and automation."}, {"id": "044d83e06c28438d8a6e882191262b65", "stem": "At Bluebird Insurance, account managers want one place on the Account record to see both upcoming meetings and past calls, emails, and completed tasks in chronological order. Currently they switch between the Open Activities and Activity History related lists. What should the admin do?", "options": ["Add the Chatter Feed component and ask reps to post each activity", "Create a custom report type and embed the report on the Account page", "Rename the Open Activities related list so it also shows completed items", "Add the Activity Timeline component to the Account Lightning record page"], "correct": [3], "explanations": ["Chatter posts are separate from Tasks, Events, and emails, so this would not show the real activity history.", "A report would be a static summary rather than the interactive timeline, and it needs more effort for a feature that already exists.", "Open Activities shows only open items. Renaming a related list does not change what data it contains.", "Correct. The Activity Timeline shows upcoming and overdue items alongside past activities in one chronological view, and it is added through Lightning App Builder."], "takeaway": "Use the Activity Timeline component on a Lightning record page to see upcoming and past activities together."}, {"id": "ce0800a0dbda4476821055b65785cbfb", "stem": "Fabrikam Software wants reps' Gmail emails and calendar events to appear on Salesforce records automatically, with no manual logging. The admin has confirmed the licenses are in place. Which two actions are required to set this up with Einstein Activity Capture?", "options": ["Create an Einstein Activity Capture configuration that includes the reps' profiles or permission sets", "Configure Email-to-Case routing addresses for each rep", "Have each rep connect their email and calendar account to Salesforce", "Enable Shared Activities in Activity Settings", "Install the Salesforce for Outlook package on each rep's computer"], "correct": [0, 2], "explanations": ["Correct. A configuration defines the sync settings and which users receive them, based on profiles or permission sets.", "Email-to-Case turns inbound emails into Cases, which is unrelated to syncing reps' mailboxes.", "Correct. Each user must authorize the connection to their own email and calendar account for the sync to begin.", "Shared Activities relates multiple Contacts to one Task or Event. It is not needed for Einstein Activity Capture to sync.", "Salesforce for Outlook is retired and is not part of Einstein Activity Capture setup."], "takeaway": "Einstein Activity Capture needs a configuration covering the users, and each user must connect their own email and calendar account."}, {"id": "419d9518dd25459e9e7331cb54cb0594", "stem": "Alpine Outfitters' sales rep must record an on-site visit with a customer next Tuesday from 2:00 PM to 3:00 PM. The visit must appear on the rep's Salesforce calendar and show in the Opportunity's upcoming activities. Which activity type should the rep create?", "options": ["A Log a Call entry with the visit notes", "A Task with a reminder set for 2:00 PM", "A Task with a due date of next Tuesday", "An Event with a start time and an end time"], "correct": [3], "explanations": ["Log a Call records a completed call after the fact. It does not schedule a future time-bound visit.", "A reminder alerts the user but a Task still has no duration and is not displayed as a calendar event.", "A Task has a due date but no start and end time, so it does not appear as a time block on the calendar.", "Correct. An Event has a start and end time, appears on the calendar, and shows in the upcoming activities of the related record."], "takeaway": "Use Events for time-bound items that appear on the calendar and Tasks for to-dos that have a due date and status."}]}, {"id": "adm_chatter", "title": "Chatter et collaboration", "fiche": {"one_liner": "Chatter is Salesforce's built-in social layer: feeds, groups, mentions, publisher actions and Files let teams discuss and share work on the records themselves, with Slack extending it.", "need": "A consulting firm's sales team closes deals across several regions. Without Chatter, advice about an Opportunity gets lost in email threads. With Chatter, a rep @mentions the pricing expert on the record, the discussion stays attached to the deal, and the contract draft is shared as a File that everyone can find later.", "core": "Chatter is the collaboration feature of Salesforce. Its central element is the feed: a stream of posts, comments and automatic updates. A feed appears on the Home page, on user profiles, in groups, and on records when feed tracking is enabled. Feed tracking (suivi du fil) lets an admin choose, per object, which fields generate an automatic feed update when they change. For example, track Stage and Amount on Opportunity. Users can follow a record to see these updates in their own feed.\n\nGroups organize conversations around a topic or team. There are three types of group: Public (anyone in the org can see the group and its posts and can join it), Private (anyone can see the group name and description, but only members see the posts, and the owner or managers approve membership), and Unlisted (not searchable and not listed: only members and people they invite can find it, which suits confidential topics). Group visibility is independent of record access: a post on a record is still limited by what the user can see on that record.\n\nThe publisher (éditeur) offers actions such as Post, Poll, Question and, when configured, Log a Call, New Task or New Contact. These actions are controlled by page layouts and global actions. A mention uses @ followed by a name, which notifies that person or group and makes them aware of the post. Files (Fichiers) are uploaded to the Files home, attached to posts or records and shared with people, groups or via a link. Versions are tracked, and sharing is controlled per file. Slack integration (for example Sales Elevate or the Slack app for Salesforce) lets users view and act on Salesforce records from Slack channels.", "setup": ["Setup > Feature Settings > Chatter > Chatter Settings: enable Chatter, then choose whether to allow groups, publisher actions and other options.", "Object Manager > [Object] > Feed Tracking (or Setup > Feed Tracking): enable feed tracking for the object and select the fields to track.", "Object Manager > [Object] > Page Layouts, or Setup > Global Actions > Publisher Layouts: choose which actions appear in the publisher.", "Chatter > Groups (app or tab): create a group and choose Public, Private or Unlisted access.", "Setup > Slack (Salesforce for Slack integration): connect the Salesforce org and the Slack workspace."], "compare": [{"a": "Private group", "b": "Unlisted group", "diff": "In a Private group, the name and description are visible and people can request to join, but the posts are only visible to members. An Unlisted group is hidden from lists and search, and members are added by invitation only."}, {"a": "Feed tracking (suivi du fil)", "b": "Following a record", "diff": "Feed tracking is an admin setting that decides which fields create updates. Following is a user choice that decides whose updates appear in their own feed. Without tracking, followers see no field changes."}, {"a": "Chatter post", "b": "File sharing", "diff": "A post is a message in a feed. A File is a document with its own versions and sharing settings, which can be attached to a post but also exists independently."}], "signals": [{"if": "Notify a specific colleague or group about a record", "then": "@mention in a Chatter post"}, {"if": "Confidential discussion that must not appear in searches or group lists", "then": "Unlisted group"}, {"if": "Show automatic updates when Stage or Amount changes", "then": "Feed tracking on the object and fields"}, {"if": "Team already uses Slack and wants to work with Salesforce records there", "then": "Salesforce and Slack integration"}], "traps": ["Enabling Chatter alone does not show field changes on a record: you must configure feed tracking for the object and select the fields.", "Users cannot see feed posts on a record they have no access to, so a mention does not grant record access.", "Public groups are visible to everyone in the org and anyone can join, so they are wrong for confidential topics.", "Feed tracking is configured per object by an admin, but each user decides what to follow: do not confuse the two."], "scenario": {"situation": "A legal team at a company wants a space to discuss sensitive contract negotiations. Only invited members should know the group exists. They also want contract drafts shared and discussed there. Which Chatter setup should the admin recommend?", "answer": "Create an Unlisted group so it does not appear in search or group lists, and invite only the legal team. Share the drafts as Files in the group, so versions and access stay controlled. A Private group would still show the group name and description to everyone."}, "cards": [{"q": "What are the three Chatter group types?", "a": "Public (open to all in the org), Private (visible name, members-only posts, approval to join) and Unlisted (not listed or searchable, invitation only)."}, {"q": "What does feed tracking do?", "a": "It lets an admin choose which fields on an object create automatic feed updates when they change, visible to people following the record."}, {"q": "What does an @mention do?", "a": "It notifies the named user or group and draws their attention to the post. It does not give them access to the record."}, {"q": "Where do you configure which actions appear in the publisher?", "a": "In page layouts (Publisher actions in Salesforce Classic and Lightning page layouts) and in Global Actions publisher layouts."}, {"q": "What is the purpose of the Slack integration?", "a": "It lets teams view Salesforce records, get notifications and take actions directly from Slack channels, so collaboration continues where they already work."}]}, "questions": [{"id": "5588954f7fbb46dcab33d6dea7dfc5ee", "stem": "Northwind Capital is preparing a confidential acquisition. The deal team wants a Chatter group for sensitive discussions. The group must not appear in the group list or in search results for other users, and people may join only by invitation. What should the administrator do?", "options": ["Create a public group and limit posting to managers", "Create a public group with Broadcast Only enabled", "Create a private group and require member approval", "Create an unlisted group and invite the deal team"], "correct": [3], "explanations": ["Incorrect. Any user can find and join a public group, and restricting who can post does not hide it.", "Incorrect. Broadcast Only restricts who can post, but the group remains visible and open to everyone if it is public.", "Incorrect. A private group still appears in group lists and search, where non-members can see its name and request to join.", "Correct. An unlisted group does not appear in the group list or in search, and its members are invited, which matches the confidentiality need."], "takeaway": "Use an unlisted Chatter group when the group must be hidden from lists and search and open by invitation only; a private group is still discoverable."}, {"id": "5c2a8dcd91824b5c80f250d775a57324", "stem": "At Contoso Retail, sales managers follow key opportunities and want an automatic post in the record's Chatter feed whenever the Stage field changes, so followers see it in their own feeds. What should the administrator do?", "options": ["Add the Stage field to the Opportunity page layout as required", "Enable feed tracking on the Opportunity object and select the Stage field", "Enable field history tracking on Stage and add the History related list", "Create a quick action that lets users post a Stage update manually"], "correct": [1], "explanations": ["Incorrect. Making a field required affects data entry and has no effect on feed posts.", "Correct. Feed tracking on the Opportunity object with Stage selected posts changes to the record feed, which followers then see in their feeds.", "Incorrect. Field history tracking stores changes in a related list and is separate from feed tracking, so it does not create feed posts.", "Incorrect. A quick action relies on users posting manually and does not track the field change automatically."], "takeaway": "Feed tracking, enabled per object and per field in Feed Tracking setup, posts field changes to the record feed and to followers' feeds, unlike field history tracking."}, {"id": "cc303253a8724fdca48e56ce47b795ac", "stem": "At Fabrikam Services, support agents work from the Account record page. The company wants an agent to create a new Contact from that page with the Account field already filled in with the current account. What is the best solution?", "options": ["Add the standard New button to the Contacts related list with a validation rule", "Create an object-specific quick action on Account that creates a Contact", "Create a Quick Text entry containing the contact fields", "Create a global quick action that creates a Contact"], "correct": [1], "explanations": ["Incorrect. A validation rule only checks data on save and does not fill in the Account when the form opens.", "Correct. An object-specific Create a Record action on Account automatically relates the new Contact to the current account.", "Incorrect. Quick Text inserts reusable text into messages and cannot create records.", "Incorrect. A global action is not tied to a specific record, so the Account would not be pre-populated automatically."], "takeaway": "Object-specific quick actions inherit the context of the record they are launched from, while global actions do not."}, {"id": "b097af93b8d643e7946cb37fa423aebc", "stem": "The marketing team at Adventure Works uploads a product brochure and shares it with the Product Launch Chatter group. The brochure is revised often. The administrator wants group members to always get the latest content without re-sharing. Which two approaches meet this need?", "options": ["Share the file with the Product Launch group so members can access it", "Upload a new version of the existing file instead of a separate file", "Email the updated brochure to all members of the group", "Upload a new file with the same name so Salesforce overwrites the old one", "Store each revision as an attachment in the Notes & Attachments related list"], "correct": [0, 1], "explanations": ["Correct. Sharing a file with a Chatter group gives all its members access to the file.", "Correct. Uploading a new version keeps the same file record, so existing shares and links give access to the latest content.", "Incorrect. Email copies are not updated and leave the file outside Salesforce's sharing and version control.", "Incorrect. Salesforce does not overwrite files with matching names; this creates a separate file that is not shared with anyone.", "Incorrect. The Notes & Attachments approach is the older model and does not give versioned files shared with a group."], "takeaway": "Share Salesforce Files with a Chatter group and use Upload New Version so everyone with access sees the latest content."}, {"id": "c686b0d93cfa404e8d5c840270a0cbc9", "stem": "Tailspin Toys uses Slack for team communication and Salesforce for sales. Sales reps want to look up and update Salesforce records without leaving Slack. Management also wants a Slack channel message sent when an opportunity moves to Closed Won. Which two actions should the administrator take?", "options": ["Replace Chatter with Slack by disabling Chatter in the org", "Enable a Chatter setting that mirrors every Chatter feed into Slack", "Build a record-triggered flow that sends a message to the Slack channel", "Build a Process Builder process that posts to the Slack channel", "Connect the Salesforce org to the Slack workspace with the Salesforce app for Slack"], "correct": [2, 4], "explanations": ["Incorrect. Disabling Chatter is not required for the Slack integration and would remove existing collaboration features.", "Incorrect. There is no standard setting that mirrors all Chatter feeds into Slack.", "Correct. A record-triggered flow can detect the Closed Won stage and send a Slack message with the Slack actions available in Flow Builder.", "Incorrect. Process Builder is retired and is not the way to build new automation.", "Correct. Connecting the org to the workspace with the Salesforce app lets reps work with records from Slack."], "takeaway": "Integrate Salesforce and Slack by connecting the org to the workspace and use Flow Builder, not Process Builder, to trigger Slack messages from record changes."}]}, {"id": "adm_email", "title": "E-mails et modèles", "fiche": {"one_liner": "Email templates, merge fields and Email Deliverability settings let teams send consistent, personalized emails from Salesforce, while Outlook/Gmail integration and Enhanced Email keep email activity tied to CRM records.", "need": "A software company's sales reps write the same follow-up email after every demo, and typos and inconsistent messaging creep in. The admin builds a shared template that pulls in the contact's name and the opportunity details automatically. Reps send it from the record, and the email is logged on the record so managers see the full history.", "core": "Salesforce has two template families. Lightning email templates (created in the Email Templates tab, or in Setup under Classic Email Templates for the older kind) are built with a rich-text editor and can be stored in folders and shared with users, groups or roles. Classic email templates come in four types: Text, HTML (with letterhead), Custom HTML and Visualforce. Lightning templates are the modern choice for reps sending one-to-one emails, and they support merge fields natively. Classic templates are still used for automated emails, such as workflow-style email alerts sent from Flow or approval processes. Email alerts can use either type of template.\n\nMerge fields are placeholders such as {{{Contact.FirstName}}} (Lightning) or {!Contact.FirstName} (Classic), which Salesforce replaces with the real record values when the email is sent. They can pull fields from the related record, such as Account, Opportunity, or the sender (User). If a field is empty, the merge field renders as blank, so keep that in mind when designing templates.\n\nDeliverability is an org-wide setting (Setup > Deliverability) with three access levels: No access (no outbound email at all, used in sandboxes to prevent accidental sends), System email only (only system-generated emails such as password resets and workflow/Flow email alerts), and All email (every outbound email, including those sent by users and automation). Sandboxes default to System email only after a refresh, and production defaults to All email.\n\nOutlook and Gmail integration lets reps work in their inbox and see Salesforce records in a side panel, insert templates, and log emails and events to Salesforce. Enhanced Email stores emails as EmailMessage records, so they are visible on the activity timeline, can be related to multiple records, and support email in Lightning Experience. Enhanced Email must be enabled in Setup before some of these features work.", "setup": ["Lightning templates: App Launcher > Email Templates tab (New Email Template), then choose a folder and sharing.", "Classic templates: Setup > Classic Email Templates (Quick Find: Classic Email Templates).", "Deliverability: Setup > Email > Deliverability, then set Access level to No access, System email only or All email.", "Outlook and Gmail integration: Setup > Outlook Integration and Sync or Gmail Integration and Sync, then enable and assign permission sets.", "Enhanced Email: Setup > Email > Enhanced Email, then turn it on."], "compare": [{"a": "Lightning email template", "b": "Classic email template", "diff": "Lightning templates are built in the Email Templates tab, support rich text and folders, and are meant for reps. Classic templates (Text, HTML, Custom HTML, Visualforce) are the legacy choice and are still common for system-sent alerts."}, {"a": "System email only", "b": "All email", "diff": "System email only blocks user-composed emails and bulk sends, but allows system emails such as password resets and email alerts. All email permits every outbound email, so it belongs in production, not in a sandbox that holds copied customer data."}], "signals": [{"if": "Reps send the same message repeatedly and need personalization", "then": "Lightning email template with merge fields"}, {"if": "A sandbox must not email real customers after a refresh", "then": "Set Deliverability to No access or System email only"}, {"if": "Reps want to log emails and see Salesforce data from Outlook or Gmail", "then": "Enable Outlook or Gmail integration (Einstein Activity Capture / Inbox)"}, {"if": "Emails should appear in the activity timeline and be related to multiple records", "then": "Enable Enhanced Email"}], "traps": ["Test emails from a sandbox can reach real customers if Deliverability is left on All email after a refresh.", "Merge field syntax differs: Lightning uses {{{Object.Field}}}, Classic uses {!Object.Field}. Do not mix them.", "Classic email templates are not retired and are still used with email alerts, so do not assume Lightning replaces them everywhere.", "System email only does not stop every email: system-generated messages such as password resets still go out."], "scenario": {"situation": "A company refreshes its full sandbox from production for testing. The admin is worried that test Flows might email real customers whose addresses were copied into the sandbox. Users still need to receive password reset emails and be able to test their sandbox logins.", "answer": "Set Deliverability to System email only. This still allows system emails such as password resets, while blocking user-composed and bulk emails. No access would also stop resets, which the testers need. All email would risk sending test messages to real customers."}, "cards": [{"q": "What are the three Deliverability access levels?", "a": "No access, System email only, and All email."}, {"q": "Where do you configure Deliverability?", "a": "Setup > Email > Deliverability."}, {"q": "What is a merge field?", "a": "A placeholder, such as {{{Contact.FirstName}}} in Lightning, that Salesforce replaces with real record data when the email is sent."}, {"q": "What are the Classic email template types?", "a": "Text, HTML (with letterhead), Custom HTML, and Visualforce."}, {"q": "What does Enhanced Email do?", "a": "It stores emails as EmailMessage records, so they appear on the activity timeline and can relate to multiple records in Lightning Experience."}]}, "questions": [{"id": "8d68028029174f05a37529a9aa39c4ab", "stem": "A sales manager at Northwind Traders wants her reps to send standardized follow-up emails from Lightning Experience. Each email must include the contact's first name, the opportunity name, and the rep's own name and phone number, and the design must use a branded header and layout with images. What should the administrator create?", "options": ["A Visualforce email template that reads the contact's first name", "An email alert triggered by a record-triggered flow", "A Text email template in Classic with merge fields", "A Lightning email template with merge fields"], "correct": [3], "explanations": ["Incorrect. Visualforce templates require code and are not the simplest fit. The need is met declaratively with a Lightning email template.", "Incorrect. An email alert sends automatically when a flow runs. Reps need to choose a template and send manually from the record.", "Incorrect. Text templates are plain text only. They cannot hold a branded header or images.", "Correct. Lightning email templates support merge fields for the recipient, related record, and sender, and they can include rich content such as images and a branded layout. Reps can use them directly from the email composer."], "takeaway": "Use Lightning email templates for manual, branded emails with merge fields sent by users from Lightning Experience."}, {"id": "b470aa8ddcef407fa008ba70b4282452", "stem": "Contoso Health has a sandbox refreshed from production. The administrator notices that test workflow emails and flow-triggered emails are reaching real customer addresses copied into the sandbox, and wants to prevent any emails from leaving the org while testing. Which Email Deliverability access level should the administrator select?", "options": ["No access", "System email only", "Restricted email relay", "All email"], "correct": [0], "explanations": ["Correct. No access blocks all outbound email from the org, including system emails and emails sent by users or automation, which is what the test needs.", "Incorrect. System email only still sends system emails such as password resets and automated notifications, and the scenario wants to block everything.", "Incorrect. This is not an access level in the Deliverability setting. The valid levels are No access, System email only, and All email.", "Incorrect. All email is the production setting and would allow the emails to reach real customers."], "takeaway": "Set Email Deliverability to No access to block all outbound email, and use System email only to allow only system-generated messages."}, {"id": "d37a87c0b7a14c359eaf3138d2570d3f", "stem": "Fabrikam Logistics wants support agents to send case emails and have every sent and received message appear on the case record in a single conversation view, with the ability to reply, reply all, or forward directly from the timeline. Which feature should the administrator enable?", "options": ["Email-to-Case with a Classic email template", "Enhanced Email", "Salesforce for Outlook side panel", "Email Relay"], "correct": [1], "explanations": ["Incorrect. Email-to-Case creates cases from incoming messages, but it does not by itself provide the activity timeline experience described, and a template is not the feature needed.", "Correct. Enhanced Email stores emails as EmailMessage records and displays them in the activity timeline of Lightning record pages, with reply, reply all, and forward actions.", "Incorrect. The Outlook integration relates to logging emails from the agent's mail client, and it does not provide this timeline view for emails sent in Salesforce.", "Incorrect. Email Relay routes outbound Salesforce emails through the company's own mail server. It does not change how emails are displayed."], "takeaway": "Enhanced Email shows emails as EmailMessage records in the activity timeline with reply, reply all, and forward actions."}, {"id": "4a2b790d101047b6bbf51279057f504c", "stem": "Adventure Works wants to roll out Einstein Activity Capture and the Outlook integration so reps can work with Salesforce records without leaving their inbox. Which two capabilities does the Outlook integration provide to reps? (Choose the two that apply.)", "options": ["View related Salesforce records for email contacts in the Outlook side panel", "Log Outlook emails to related Salesforce records", "Edit Salesforce page layouts from the Outlook inbox", "Build Salesforce reports for the sender in Outlook", "Convert Outlook emails into Salesforce validation rules"], "correct": [0, 1], "explanations": ["Correct. The Outlook integration shows Salesforce records related to email senders and recipients in a side panel.", "Correct. Reps can log emails to related records, such as contacts, leads, and opportunities, directly from Outlook.", "Incorrect. Page layouts are customized by admins in Setup and not from an email client.", "Incorrect. Report building is done in Salesforce and not as part of the Outlook integration.", "Incorrect. Emails cannot be converted into validation rules, which are configured by admins in Setup."], "takeaway": "The Outlook and Gmail integrations let reps see related Salesforce records and log emails to them from within their inbox."}, {"id": "2e2251656d4a443f9762a51310b0e8c5", "stem": "Tailspin Toys is creating a Lightning email template for sales reps. The template should greet the recipient by name and mention the account associated with the related opportunity. Which two items can the administrator insert into the template? (Choose the two that apply.)", "options": ["A validation rule that runs when the email is sent", "A merge field for the recipient's first name", "An Apex trigger that runs when the template is saved", "A merge field from the related opportunity record", "A merge field that pulls data from any unrelated record in the org"], "correct": [1, 3], "explanations": ["Incorrect. Validation rules apply to record saves and are not part of an email template.", "Correct. Recipient merge fields, such as the contact or lead first name, personalize the greeting.", "Incorrect. Templates contain content and merge fields, not triggers. Apex code is not inserted into a template.", "Correct. Lightning email templates can use merge fields from the related object, such as the opportunity and its account information, when a related type is selected.", "Incorrect. Merge fields are limited to the recipient, the related record and its parent relationships, the sender, and the company, not arbitrary unrelated records."], "takeaway": "Lightning email templates use merge fields from the recipient and the selected related object to personalize content."}]}, {"id": "adm_mobile", "title": "Application mobile Salesforce", "fiche": {"one_liner": "The Salesforce mobile app uses the same data and security as desktop, but its navigation menu, actions and compact layouts are configured separately to fit small screens and field work.", "need": "A field service company sends technicians to customer sites. They need to open an account, log a call, create a case and check a phone number from a phone, with only the few fields that matter. Admins tailor the mobile experience so reps are fast on a small screen without a separate app.", "core": "The Salesforce mobile app is one app for iOS and Android. It shows the same org, the same records and the same sharing and field-level security as Lightning Experience on desktop. What changes is the presentation, which admins control through a few tools.\n\nThe navigation menu is the main entry point. It is the mobile counterpart of the app's navigation bar: an admin picks which items (objects, tabs, Home, etc.) appear and in what order. Items are set in the Mobile Only navigation menu, and users get the Salesforce navigation menu by default unless the admin changes it. The menu is assigned through the Lightning app and, in the mobile-specific setup, through Navigation Menus in Setup.\n\nActions speed up data entry. Global actions (for example New Task, Log a Call) are not tied to a record and can be created from anywhere, so the record they create is not automatically related to the current one. Object-specific actions (for example Create Contact on an Account) belong to one object and automatically relate the new record to the current one, with the parent filled in. Both types are added to page layouts through the Salesforce Mobile and Lightning Experience Actions section of the layout, or through the Mobile and Lightning Actions section. Order matters: the first actions appear first in the action bar.\n\nCompact layouts control the small set of fields shown on mobile record pages and in the highlights panel on desktop. They also drive the fields shown in record previews, lookup hovers and the Recent Items list. If none is assigned to an object, Salesforce uses a system default. Compact layouts are assigned per record type. A compact layout is not a security tool: fields hidden from it can still be seen on the full record unless field-level security says otherwise.", "setup": ["Setup > User Interface > Navigation Menus: choose the items and order for the mobile navigation menu.", "Setup > Object Manager > [Object] > Buttons, Links, and Actions > New Action: create an object-specific action. Global actions are under Setup > User Interface > Global Actions > Actions.", "Setup > Object Manager > [Object] > Page Layouts: drag actions into the Salesforce Mobile and Lightning Experience Actions section, then save.", "Setup > Object Manager > [Object] > Compact Layouts: create a layout, choose its fields, then set the compact layout assignment (per record type)."], "compare": [{"a": "Global action", "b": "Object-specific action", "diff": "A global action is not tied to a record, so the new record is not automatically related to the current one. An object-specific action is created from one object and pre-links the new record to it."}, {"a": "Compact layout", "b": "Page layout", "diff": "A compact layout sets the few key fields shown in the highlights panel, previews and mobile record headers. A page layout controls the full record page: fields, sections, related lists and actions."}], "signals": [{"if": "Reps on phones need to see only a few key fields at the top of a record", "then": "Compact layout"}, {"if": "Create a Contact from an Account and have it linked automatically", "then": "Object-specific action"}, {"if": "Log a call or create a task from anywhere without a parent record", "then": "Global action"}, {"if": "Change which items and in what order users see them in the mobile app menu", "then": "Navigation menu (Mobile)"}], "traps": ["Compact layouts do not secure fields: hiding a field there does not stop users from seeing it on the full record, so use field-level security for that.", "Actions only appear on mobile if they are added to the page layout (or the global publisher layout) in the Salesforce Mobile and Lightning Experience Actions section.", "A global action does not relate the new record to the current record; if the scenario needs automatic linking, choose an object-specific action.", "Do not assume mobile has separate data or sharing: users see the same records and permissions as on desktop, only the presentation differs."], "scenario": {"situation": "Sales reps at a distributor use the mobile app between visits. They complain that opening an Opportunity shows too many fields at the top, and that logging a new Contact from an Account takes many steps and does not link to the account.", "answer": "Create a compact layout for Opportunity with the key fields (amount, stage, close date) and assign it. Then create an object-specific action Create Contact on Account and add it to the Account page layout's mobile actions section, so the Contact is automatically related to the Account."}, "cards": [{"q": "What does a compact layout control?", "a": "The small set of key fields shown in the highlights panel, record previews, lookup cards and mobile record headers. It is assigned per record type."}, {"q": "What is the difference between a global and an object-specific action?", "a": "Global actions are available anywhere and are not tied to a record. Object-specific actions belong to one object and automatically relate the new record to the current one."}, {"q": "Where do you make actions appear on mobile?", "a": "In the Salesforce Mobile and Lightning Experience Actions section of the page layout (or the global publisher layout for global actions)."}, {"q": "Does the mobile app have its own security model?", "a": "No. It uses the same org data, sharing rules and field-level security as desktop; only the layout and navigation are adapted."}, {"q": "What happens if an object has no compact layout assigned?", "a": "Salesforce uses the system default compact layout with a predefined set of fields."}]}, "questions": [{"id": "43f5f8879c2b4ce49206f298b8d8a5b2", "stem": "Northwind Logistics wants its field reps to see only Today's Events, Tasks, Accounts, and Opportunities in the Salesforce mobile app navigation menu, in that order. Reps currently see a long default list of items. What should the administrator do?", "options": ["Change the order of tabs in each rep's personal settings through Advanced User Details", "Create a Mobile Navigation menu in Navigation Menus under Setup and assign it to the reps' profiles", "Build a custom Lightning page and assign it as the app home for reps", "Edit the Lightning app's navigation items and expect the mobile menu to follow"], "correct": [1], "explanations": ["Incorrect. Advanced User Details does not control mobile menu items and is not a per-user tool for this need.", "Correct. In Setup, Navigation Menus lets the administrator define which items appear in the Salesforce mobile app menu and their order, and assign it by profile.", "Incorrect. A Lightning home page changes the content of the Home page, not the items in the navigation menu.", "Incorrect. Lightning app navigation items control the desktop app. The mobile app uses its own navigation menu and does not mirror this list."], "takeaway": "The Salesforce mobile app navigation menu is configured in Setup under Navigation Menus and assigned by profile."}, {"id": "e7a9cd58745040a1b9afcebe533b185d", "stem": "Contoso Energy's technicians use the Salesforce mobile app to view Work Order records. They need a one-tap way to log a call from the Work Order record only, and the action must not appear on other objects. What should the administrator create?", "options": ["An object-specific action added to the Work Order page layout's Salesforce Mobile and Lightning Experience Actions section", "A custom button added to the Work Order list view", "A global action added to the global publisher layout", "A quick text entry shared with all technicians"], "correct": [0], "explanations": ["Correct. Object-specific actions are added to an object's page layout and appear only on that object's records in the mobile app.", "Incorrect. List view buttons are not how mobile record actions are defined and do not give a one-tap record-level action in this case.", "Incorrect. Global actions appear across the app and are not tied to a single object's records, so they would show on other objects too.", "Incorrect. Quick Text inserts predefined messages and does not create an action to log a call."], "takeaway": "Object-specific actions are placed on the object's page layout, while global actions are available everywhere."}, {"id": "32bcabc957354780882353a18b1cf874", "stem": "A Fabrikam sales manager wants reps to create a new Lead quickly from anywhere in the Salesforce mobile app, without first opening a particular record. Which approach meets this requirement?", "options": ["Enable Lightning Experience Lead Conversion for the reps", "Create a global action for Lead and add it to the global publisher layout", "Add the Lead object to the Utility Bar of the Sales app", "Add a Create Lead object-specific action to the Account page layout"], "correct": [1], "explanations": ["Incorrect. Lead conversion handles existing leads and does not offer a way to create a new one.", "Correct. Global actions are not tied to a record and are available app wide through the global publisher layout.", "Incorrect. The Utility Bar is a desktop Lightning feature and does not provide this in the mobile app.", "Incorrect. An action on the Account layout is only reachable from Account records, not from anywhere in the app."], "takeaway": "Use global actions in the global publisher layout for tasks that do not depend on a specific record."}, {"id": "faada15095154180b3bb811adcfb5e14", "stem": "Adventure Works wants the Salesforce mobile app record page for Opportunities to show only the most important fields at the top, so reps see key details without scrolling. What should the administrator configure? (Select two.)", "options": ["Field-level security removing all other fields", "A Path with key fields for each stage", "Compact Layout assigned to the Opportunity object", "Record Type to hide fields on mobile only", "Fields in the compact layout, which show in the record highlights panel on mobile"], "correct": [2, 4], "explanations": ["Incorrect. Field-level security would hide fields from users everywhere, not just prioritize key fields.", "Incorrect. Path guides stage progress and key fields, but it is not the feature that sets the highlights fields at the top of the mobile record.", "Correct. Compact layouts control the key fields shown in the highlights area of a record in the mobile app and are assigned per object.", "Incorrect. Record types do not hide fields for mobile only, and they are used for business processes and page layout assignment.", "Correct. The fields chosen in the compact layout appear in the highlights panel at the top of the record on mobile, and in the record header and related list previews."], "takeaway": "Compact layouts define the key fields shown in the highlights area of records in the Salesforce mobile app."}, {"id": "291b38e24ae840458ef93b40a7ef6ccd", "stem": "Tailspin Retail's admin is reviewing the Account page layout and wants to control which actions mobile users see on Account records. Which two statements about this configuration are true? (Select two.)", "options": ["Actions on the page layout only apply to desktop users", "The order of actions in that section determines the order in which actions appear in the mobile app", "Actions are added in the Salesforce Mobile and Lightning Experience Actions section of the page layout", "Global actions must be recreated on each page layout", "Actions are controlled only by profile permissions, never by layout"], "correct": [1, 2], "explanations": ["Incorrect. That section applies to both Lightning Experience and the Salesforce mobile app.", "Correct. Actions appear in the order in which they are arranged in that section, with the first ones shown most prominently.", "Correct. The Salesforce Mobile and Lightning Experience Actions section of the page layout defines the actions shown on records.", "Incorrect. Global actions are created once and can be added to layouts or the global publisher layout without recreation.", "Incorrect. Layout assignment determines which actions are available, in addition to permissions on the objects involved."], "takeaway": "Mobile record actions and their order are managed in the Salesforce Mobile and Lightning Experience Actions section of the page layout."}]}, {"id": "adm_tools", "title": "Outils de productivité", "fiche": {"one_liner": "List views, Kanban, pinned lists, In-App Guidance, global search and Notes help users find, view and act on their data faster, all without code and mostly from the user interface.", "need": "A sales team at a construction supplier wants each rep to see only their open opportunities closing this quarter, track deals by stage visually, find any customer record in seconds, and get guided help on a new process. These tools cut clicks and training time, and they require little or no administrator build effort.", "core": "List views (vues de liste) show a filtered set of records of one object. You choose the filters, the visible columns and the sort order. A list view can be private, shared with specific public groups, roles or users, or visible to all users. A user needs the right sharing and permissions to edit or share one. List views can display a chart (graphique) at the side, such as a bar, donut or funnel chart, based on a grouping field and an aggregate (record count or a sum of a field). The chart follows the list view filters. Users can pin a list view as their default (pinned list) so it opens first.\n\nThe Kanban view (vue Kanban) displays the same list view as cards in columns, grouped by a picklist field, for example Opportunity Stage or Lead Status. Users drag a card to another column, which updates the picklist value. It is available for objects such as Opportunities, Leads and Cases, and you can summarize a numeric or currency field at the top of each column. It is a display option of a list view, not a separate feature.\n\nFavorites (favoris) let users bookmark any record, list view, or page via the star icon in the header, then reach it from the Favorites menu. They are personal to each user.\n\nIn-App Guidance (assistance intégrée) lets admins add prompts (invites) that appear in the app: docked, floating or hotspot. It also offers walkthroughs (procédures pas à pas) that guide users through several steps. You can target them by profile or permission set, and set the page and the dates they show. They are built in Setup without code.\n\nGlobal search searches many objects at once from the search box, and shows recent items, suggested results and scoped results. Notes (notes) is the enhanced note-taking tool: rich text, related to several records, and available in the Lightning interface.", "setup": ["Setup is not required for list views: on an object's home page, use the gear icon (List View Controls) > New, then set filters, fields and sharing (Share List View).", "To add a chart: in a list view, click Charts (the chart icon), choose Add Chart (Add Chart), and pick the chart type, aggregate and grouping field.", "To use Kanban: in a list view, click Display As (Display As) and select Kanban, then choose the grouping picklist field and a summary field.", "Setup > In-App Guidance: create a prompt or walkthrough, choose its type, target audience and page, then publish it.", "Setup > Notes Settings: enable Notes, and add the Notes related list to page layouts for the object."], "compare": [{"a": "Kanban view", "b": "Path (Sales Path)", "diff": "Kanban is a visual display of a list view grouped by a picklist, used to see and move many records at once. Path guides one record through stages with key fields and guidance, and appears on that record page."}, {"a": "Favorites", "b": "Pinned list view", "diff": "Favorites are personal bookmarks to records, lists or pages in the header menu. Pinning sets which list view opens by default for an object."}, {"a": "In-App Guidance", "b": "Chatter", "diff": "In-App Guidance shows contextual prompts and step-by-step walkthroughs inside the app to train users. Chatter is for collaboration and conversation between users."}], "signals": [{"if": "Users want to see opportunities by stage and drag records between stages", "then": "Kanban view on a list view grouped by Stage"}, {"if": "Visualize list view data as a bar or donut chart", "then": "List view chart (Add Chart)"}, {"if": "Show a new feature tip or multi-step onboarding to users on a page", "then": "In-App Guidance (prompt or walkthrough)"}, {"if": "Share a filtered set of records with a team", "then": "Share the list view with a public group, role or users"}], "traps": ["Kanban needs a picklist field to group by, so a text or date field cannot be used as the column grouping.", "Sharing a list view does not give access to the records: users still see only the records their sharing settings allow.", "Chart in a list view reflects the filters of the list view, so changing filters changes the chart.", "Favorites are personal, and an admin cannot assign them to other users."], "scenario": {"situation": "A regional sales manager wants her team to see all open opportunities closing this quarter, grouped by stage with a visual sense of volume, and to move deals between stages quickly during weekly reviews. Reps must not see each other's private lists.", "answer": "Create a list view on Opportunities filtered to open deals closing this quarter, share it with the sales team's public group, and use Display As Kanban grouped by Stage with a summed Amount. Add a chart for volume. Other reps' private lists stay private."}, "cards": [{"q": "Who can a list view be shared with?", "a": "With public groups, roles or specific users, or made visible to all users. Sharing the list does not change record access."}, {"q": "What does a Kanban view require?", "a": "A list view with a picklist field to group columns, such as Stage or Status. Dragging a card updates that picklist value."}, {"q": "What two formats does In-App Guidance offer?", "a": "Prompts (invites), such as docked, floating or hotspot messages, and walkthroughs (procédures pas à pas) with several steps."}, {"q": "What is a Favorite in Lightning Experience?", "a": "A personal bookmark, created with the star icon, to a record, list view or page, reachable from the Favorites menu."}, {"q": "How does a chart on a list view get its data?", "a": "From the list view's filtered records, using a grouping field and an aggregate such as record count or a sum."}]}, "questions": [{"id": "28c67b92196342d0bd1977fd846e6f5c", "stem": "Northwind Logistics wants its sales managers to see open opportunities by stage as a visual summary directly on the Opportunities list view, without building a report or dashboard. The administrator already has a list view filtered to open opportunities. What should the administrator do?", "options": ["Enable the Kanban view on the list view and group by Stage.", "Create a summary report with a chart and add it to the Opportunity home page.", "Add a Dashboard component to the Opportunity record page layout.", "Display the list view as a chart by using the Charts button and choosing a grouping field and aggregate."], "correct": [3], "explanations": ["Kanban shows records as cards in columns by a picklist field, but it is not a chart summarizing totals or counts.", "A report would work but the requirement is to avoid building a report or dashboard.", "A record page component applies to a single record page, not to a list view, and still requires a dashboard.", "Correct. List views can display a chart (such as a donut, vertical bar, or horizontal bar) with a chosen grouping and aggregate, directly on the list view."], "takeaway": "Use the chart option on a list view to visualize grouped counts or sums of its records without creating a report."}, {"id": "2bbaac638a814e97a1a45b7e5362a02d", "stem": "Contoso Health's support director built a list view of high-priority Cases and wants all agents in the Tier 2 Support public group to use it, while other users must not see it. The director owns the list view. What should be done?", "options": ["Set the list view visibility to Share list view with groups of users and select the Tier 2 Support public group.", "Pin the list view as the default for the Case object for the Tier 2 Support profile.", "Set the list view visibility to All users can see this list view.", "Clone the list view for each agent and assign it by profile."], "correct": [0], "explanations": ["Correct. A list view can be shared with specific public groups, roles, or role and subordinates, limiting visibility to the intended audience.", "Pinning is a personal preference per user and is not an admin tool to push a list view to a profile.", "This would expose the list view to every user, which violates the requirement.", "Cloning for each agent is unnecessary, and list views are not assigned by profile this way."], "takeaway": "Share a list view with specific public groups, roles, or territories by using the Share list view with groups of users option."}, {"id": "2defe97c88a34d01b1afc3367eebd55a", "stem": "At Fabrikam Realty, a new sales hire needs to learn how to log a lead and convert it. The administrator wants to show a step-by-step guide inside Salesforce that appears in context while the rep works, and does not want any custom code. Which tool should the administrator use?", "options": ["A custom Lightning component with embedded video", "In-App Guidance with a prompt and a walkthrough", "A Path with guidance for success on the Lead object", "Chatter group with a pinned post"], "correct": [1], "explanations": ["This would require development effort, which the admin wants to avoid.", "Correct. In-App Guidance lets admins create prompts (docked or floating) and walkthroughs that guide users through steps directly in the Lightning interface without code.", "Path guidance helps with a stage-based process on a record, but it does not create a multi-step walkthrough of the app interface.", "A pinned Chatter post is not displayed in context while the rep works."], "takeaway": "In-App Guidance provides no-code prompts and walkthroughs to help users learn within the Salesforce interface."}, {"id": "bb7cd46d284c4131a34acdba4b5001af", "stem": "Adventure Works sales reps often navigate to the same few accounts and a specific report every day. The administrator wants to recommend a way for reps to return to these items quickly from anywhere in Lightning Experience. Which two actions should the reps take?", "options": ["Open the Favorites list from the header to jump to the saved items.", "Mark the records and pages as favorites by using the star icon.", "Add each account to a public group.", "Create a list view for each account they visit.", "Save each account as a pinned list view filter."], "correct": [0, 1], "explanations": ["Correct. The Favorites list in the header gives quick access to the saved records and pages.", "Correct. The star icon on a record or page adds it to the user's favorites.", "Public groups are used for sharing and assignments, not for personal bookmarks.", "List views show sets of records by filter and are not an efficient way to bookmark single records.", "Pinning relates to a user's preferred list view, not to bookmarking specific records."], "takeaway": "Favorites let users bookmark records and pages with the star icon and reach them from the Favorites list in the header."}, {"id": "17b6db75b0794010b2f5a1977b4d748d", "stem": "Litware Consulting reps want to jot down meeting notes in Lightning Experience and have them related to both an Opportunity and the primary Account contact at the same time. The administrator is asked to enable this. Which two statements are true about Notes?", "options": ["Notes can be created only by administrators.", "A note can be related to multiple records.", "The Notes related list must be added to the page layout so users can see the notes on records.", "Notes can only be linked to one record, so users must copy the text.", "Notes are stored as Chatter posts visible to all followers."], "correct": [1, 2], "explanations": ["Any user with access can create notes; it is not limited to administrators.", "Correct. With the enhanced Notes feature, a note can be related to multiple records.", "Correct. The Notes related list must be on the page layout, otherwise users will not see the notes on the record.", "This is the older, limited behavior; enhanced Notes allow relating to several records.", "Notes are not Chatter posts and are private by default unless shared."], "takeaway": "Enhanced Notes can relate to multiple records, and the Notes related list must be on the page layout for users to see them."}]}]}, {"id": "donnees", "label": "Gestion des données et analyses", "weight": 17, "topics": [{"id": "adm_import", "title": "Importer : assistant d'importation et Data Loader", "fiche": {"one_liner": "Use the Data Import Wizard for simple imports of up to 50,000 records on supported objects, and Data Loader for larger volumes, any object, update, upsert, delete and export.", "need": "A company just signed a deal with a trade show organizer. Marketing has a list of 2,000 contacts to load into Salesforce, while IT must also update 300,000 existing Accounts with a new territory code and export all Opportunities each night. The first task fits a guided wizard. The second needs a more powerful tool.", "core": "The Data Import Wizard is a browser-based tool, available from Setup, that guides you step by step. You choose the object, the action (add new records, update existing records, or add and update), the matching rule, upload a CSV file, map the fields, then start the import. It handles up to 50,000 records at a time. It supports only certain standard objects (Accounts, Contacts, Leads, Solutions, Campaign Members, person accounts) and custom objects. It does not support Opportunities or Cases. Its strength is duplicate prevention: it can match on Name, Email, Salesforce ID or an external ID to avoid creating duplicates, and for Leads and Contacts it can also trigger the active duplicate rules. It cannot delete records or export data.\n\nData Loader is a client application, installed on Windows or macOS, that works with the API. It handles up to 5 million records and supports all objects that the API exposes, including Opportunities and Cases. It offers six operations: Insert, Update, Upsert, Delete, Hard Delete and Export (plus Export All, which includes deleted and archived records). Upsert combines update and insert, using the record ID or an External ID field to decide whether a record already exists. It can also be run from the command line to automate recurring jobs.\n\nA simple rule to choose: if the volume is under 50,000, the object is supported and the user is a non-technical admin, use the wizard. If you need to delete, export, handle more than 50,000 records, or load an unsupported object, use Data Loader. The user must have the right object permissions, such as Create or Edit, in either tool.", "setup": ["Data Import Wizard: Setup > Quick Find box > Data Import Wizard > Launch Wizard", "Data Loader: download and install it from Setup > Data Loader (Quick Find), then log in with your Salesforce credentials (OAuth)", "Data Loader: choose an operation, select the object, upload the CSV, map the fields, then review the success and error files it generates", "Prepare the CSV: add an External ID field or the Salesforce ID column if you need to update or upsert records"], "compare": [{"a": "Data Import Wizard", "b": "Data Loader", "diff": "The wizard handles up to 50,000 records, only some objects, and cannot delete or export. Data Loader handles up to 5 million records, all API-accessible objects, and supports delete and export."}, {"a": "Upsert", "b": "Update", "diff": "Update changes only records that already exist, identified by ID. Upsert updates a record if it exists (matched by ID or External ID) and inserts it if it does not."}], "signals": [{"if": "Export or back up all records, or delete records in bulk", "then": "Data Loader"}, {"if": "Fewer than 50,000 records, a supported object and a non-technical admin, with duplicate prevention", "then": "Data Import Wizard"}, {"if": "More than 50,000 records, or Opportunities and Cases", "then": "Data Loader"}, {"if": "Insert new records and update existing ones in a single load, using an external system key", "then": "Data Loader with Upsert on an External ID field"}], "traps": ["The Data Import Wizard does not support Opportunities or Cases, even for a small file.", "The wizard cannot delete or export records, only add and update them.", "Data Loader's limit is 5 million records, but the wizard's limit is 50,000 per import, so volume alone does not always decide: check the operation and the object too.", "Upsert needs the record ID or an External ID field to match records, not just any field such as Name."], "scenario": {"situation": "An admin must load 20,000 new Leads from a trade show and avoid creating duplicates of Leads already in Salesforce. The admin has no technical background and wants a guided process. The same admin later must delete 400,000 obsolete Cases.", "answer": "Use the Data Import Wizard for the Leads: the volume is under 50,000, Leads are supported, it is guided, and it can match on Email to prevent duplicates. Use Data Loader for the Cases: the wizard does not support Cases or deletion, and the volume exceeds its limit."}, "cards": [{"q": "What is the record limit of the Data Import Wizard?", "a": "Up to 50,000 records per import."}, {"q": "What is the record limit of Data Loader?", "a": "Up to 5 million records."}, {"q": "Which operations does Data Loader support that the wizard does not?", "a": "Delete, Hard Delete and Export (Export All), in addition to Insert, Update and Upsert."}, {"q": "How does the Data Import Wizard prevent duplicates?", "a": "It matches incoming records against existing ones using Name, Email, Salesforce ID or an external ID, depending on the object and action chosen."}, {"q": "What does Upsert do and what does it need?", "a": "It updates a record if it exists and inserts it if not. It matches using the Salesforce record ID or an External ID field."}]}, "questions": [{"id": "4b12b5d6c6364fa5bb5edf8088b21536", "stem": "Northwind Outfitters has a spreadsheet of 30,000 new leads from a trade show. The sales operations admin wants to load them into Salesforce, avoid creating duplicates of existing leads by matching on email, and does not want to install any software. What should the administrator use?", "options": ["Data Export Service", "Data Import Wizard", "Salesforce Flow with a scheduled trigger", "Data Loader"], "correct": [1], "explanations": ["Data Export Service is used to back up data from Salesforce, not to import data.", "Correct. The Data Import Wizard runs in the browser, handles up to 50,000 records, supports Leads, and can match existing records by email to prevent duplicates.", "Flow is not a tool for importing a spreadsheet of records and is not designed for bulk file loading.", "Data Loader would work technically, but it requires installing a client application, which the admin wants to avoid, and the volume does not need it."], "takeaway": "For up to 50,000 records of a supported object, with duplicate matching and no installation, use the Data Import Wizard."}, {"id": "c5922ab312f64131a3f6689dbccbc225", "stem": "Contoso Energy needs to load 2 million historical Case records into Salesforce from a legacy system. The admin has the data in a CSV file and must also be able to export the loaded records afterward to verify them. Which tool should the administrator choose?", "options": ["Data Import Wizard, splitting the file into smaller imports", "Report export to CSV", "Data Loader", "Salesforce Connect with an external object"], "correct": [2], "explanations": ["The Data Import Wizard is limited to 50,000 records per import, does not support Cases for standard imports in this way, and cannot export data.", "A report export only extracts data and cannot load records into Salesforce.", "Correct. Data Loader handles up to 5 million records, supports Case, and can both insert and export records for verification.", "Salesforce Connect exposes external data in place without importing it, so it does not meet the need to load historical records."], "takeaway": "Choose Data Loader for large volumes (up to 5 million records), objects the wizard does not support, and when you also need to export."}, {"id": "7ddb5d94e32e46fc94d2a68f235f3a40", "stem": "Fabrikam Services stores a unique External ID on every Account. The admin received a CSV file where some rows are existing accounts that need updated data and others are brand new accounts. The admin wants to process both in a single operation. Which Data Loader operation should be used?", "options": ["Update", "Upsert", "Hard Delete", "Insert"], "correct": [1], "explanations": ["Update only modifies existing records and would fail on rows for accounts that do not yet exist.", "Correct. Upsert uses the External ID to update records that match and insert those that do not, in one operation.", "Hard Delete permanently removes records and does not load or update data.", "Insert would create new records for every row, producing duplicates for the existing accounts."], "takeaway": "Use Upsert with an External ID or record ID to update existing records and insert new ones in one pass."}, {"id": "21d835ddc8c742488eefce8a34094cde", "stem": "Adventure Works wants to import data into Salesforce and is comparing tools. Which two statements correctly describe the Data Import Wizard compared with Data Loader? (Choose the two that apply.)", "options": ["The Data Import Wizard can export and mass delete records.", "The Data Import Wizard can import up to 50,000 records at a time.", "Data Loader can import up to 50,000 records at a time, with no higher limit.", "The Data Import Wizard supports every standard and custom object.", "Data Loader can insert, update, upsert, delete, and export records."], "correct": [1, 4], "explanations": ["Incorrect. The wizard only imports (inserts and updates) and cannot export or delete records.", "Correct. The wizard is limited to 50,000 records per import.", "Incorrect. Data Loader supports up to 5 million records, far above 50,000.", "Incorrect. The wizard supports only certain standard objects (such as Accounts, Contacts, Leads, Campaign Members, Solutions) and custom objects, not all objects.", "Correct. Data Loader supports insert, update, upsert, delete, hard delete, and export operations."], "takeaway": "The wizard is for imports up to 50,000 records on supported objects; Data Loader covers more operations, more objects, and up to 5 million records."}, {"id": "ca6b2ce8572a46a3bc476f63904294f4", "stem": "Tailspin Retail's admin must load 400,000 Opportunity Line Items from a CSV and then, next quarter, extract all Opportunities to a file for an audit. The admin wants a single tool for both tasks. Which two reasons explain why Data Loader is the right choice? (Choose the two that apply.)", "options": ["It automatically merges duplicate records during import.", "It runs only in the browser and needs no installation.", "It can export records to CSV, in addition to importing.", "It supports loading well over 50,000 records in one job.", "It is the only tool that can load data into custom objects."], "correct": [2, 3], "explanations": ["Incorrect. Data Loader does not merge duplicates automatically; duplicate matching is a feature of the Data Import Wizard for supported objects.", "Incorrect. Data Loader is a client application that must be installed, unlike the Data Import Wizard.", "Correct. Data Loader supports export operations, so the same tool covers the audit extract.", "Correct. Data Loader handles up to 5 million records, so 400,000 rows are well within its capability.", "Incorrect. The Data Import Wizard also supports custom objects, so Data Loader is not the only option."], "takeaway": "Data Loader is the right pick when you need high volumes and both import and export in the same tool."}]}, {"id": "adm_extid", "title": "ID externes et upsert", "fiche": {"one_liner": "An External ID is a custom field flagged to hold a key from another system, letting you upsert (update or insert) records and link related records during imports without knowing Salesforce IDs.", "need": "A retailer runs an ERP that numbers every customer (CUST-1042) and every order. Each night it must sync data into Salesforce. The ERP knows nothing about 18-character Salesforce IDs. By storing the ERP number in an External ID field, the nightly load updates existing accounts, creates new ones, and attaches each order to the right account.", "core": "An External ID is a custom field with the External ID attribute checked. It stores the unique identifier a record has in another system (ERP, billing tool, legacy CRM). Salesforce indexes the field, so searching and matching on it is fast. It is available on custom fields of type Text, Number, Email and Auto Number (Auto Number is also possible as an External ID).\n\nUpsert is an operation that combines update and insert. For each incoming row, Salesforce looks for a record whose matching field has the same value. If one is found, it is updated. If none is found, a new record is created. If several are found, the row fails with an error. You can upsert using the Salesforce record ID or an External ID field. Tools that support it include Data Import Wizard (for Accounts, Contacts, Leads and custom objects), Data Loader, and the APIs. The Import Wizard matches differently: it offers matching by Name, Email or an External ID, depending on the object.\n\nUniqueness is not automatic. Checking External ID does not make values unique. You must also check Unique on the field to prevent duplicates, and choose whether it is case-sensitive. Without Unique, two records can share the same value, and an upsert that finds both will fail on that row.\n\nExternal IDs also relate records during import. Instead of supplying the parent's Salesforce ID, the file includes the parent's External ID value (for example the ERP customer number on each Order row). Salesforce resolves it to the right parent. In Data Loader, you map this column as the relationship to the parent object using the parent's External ID field. This avoids exporting IDs and building lookup spreadsheets.", "setup": ["Setup > Object Manager > (object) > Fields & Relationships > New: create a custom field of type Text, Number or Email.", "On the field definition, check External ID. Also check Unique (and choose case sensitivity) if values must never repeat.", "Data Loader: choose Upsert, select the object, then pick the External ID field as the matching field.", "To relate child to parent in the file: in the field mapping, choose the parent's External ID field for the lookup relationship column instead of a Salesforce ID."], "compare": [{"a": "Upsert", "b": "Insert followed by Update", "diff": "Upsert decides row by row whether to create or update, using a matching key, in one operation. Insert always creates, so rerunning a file produces duplicates."}, {"a": "External ID field", "b": "Salesforce record ID", "diff": "The record ID is generated by Salesforce and unknown to outside systems. An External ID carries the other system's key, so it can be used for matching without looking up Salesforce IDs."}], "signals": [{"if": "Data comes from an external system and must update existing records or create new ones", "then": "Upsert using an External ID field"}, {"if": "Load child records (orders, contacts) without knowing the parent's Salesforce ID", "then": "Reference the parent by its External ID in the import file"}, {"if": "Same key must never be stored twice", "then": "Check Unique on the External ID field"}, {"if": "The file is rerun regularly and must not create duplicates", "then": "Upsert, not Insert"}], "traps": ["Checking External ID does not enforce uniqueness: you must also check Unique.", "Upsert is not only for External IDs: it can also match on the Salesforce record ID, but the External ID is the answer when the source system has its own key.", "If the matching value exists on more than one record, the upsert row fails with an error instead of picking one.", "The parent's External ID field must exist on the parent object, not on the child, to relate child rows to their parent."], "scenario": {"situation": "A company migrates invoices from a billing system into a custom Invoice object, each linked to an Account. The billing file contains the billing customer number but no Salesforce IDs. The load will be repeated weekly and must not create duplicate invoices.", "answer": "Create an External ID field (Unique checked) on Account for the customer number and one on Invoice for the invoice number. Use Data Loader Upsert, matching on the Invoice External ID, and map the Account relationship through the Account External ID. Reruns update existing invoices and avoid duplicates."}, "cards": [{"q": "What does an upsert do?", "a": "For each row, it updates the record if a match is found on the chosen key, and creates a new record if none is found."}, {"q": "Which two checkboxes are needed so a field is a unique external key?", "a": "External ID and Unique. External ID alone only indexes the field and makes it usable for upsert and relationships."}, {"q": "What happens if an upsert matches more than one record?", "a": "That row fails with an error, because Salesforce cannot choose which record to update."}, {"q": "How do you link child records to parents in an import without Salesforce IDs?", "a": "Include the parent's External ID value in the file and map it to the relationship using the parent object's External ID field."}, {"q": "Which tools support upsert with External IDs?", "a": "Data Loader and the APIs support it fully. Data Import Wizard also supports matching on an External ID for some objects."}]}, "questions": [{"id": "0279c7efcd6f494087a24ff3e7db9f5f", "stem": "Northwind Logistics receives a weekly CSV of customer accounts from its ERP system. Some accounts already exist in Salesforce and others are new. Each row contains the ERP customer number. The administrator wants one import that updates existing accounts and creates the missing ones without producing duplicates. What should the administrator do?", "options": ["Create a custom text field named ERP Number and run an insert operation, relying on the field to block repeated values", "Export the Account Ids, match them in a spreadsheet to the ERP numbers, and run an update operation for every row", "Use the Data Import Wizard to insert all rows, then run the Duplicate Management report to merge the extra accounts", "Create a custom field on Account marked as External ID and Unique, then use the upsert operation in Data Loader with that field"], "correct": [3], "explanations": ["Incorrect. A plain text field is neither an External ID nor unique by default, and an insert operation never updates existing records.", "Incorrect. An update only modifies existing records, so new accounts would not be created, and the manual Id matching is unnecessary work.", "Incorrect. Inserting every row creates duplicates by design, and merging them afterwards is manual cleanup rather than a single clean import.", "Correct. An External ID field lets upsert match on the ERP number, updating records that match and inserting those that do not. Marking it Unique prevents duplicates."], "takeaway": "Upsert uses an External ID field to update matching records and insert the rest in a single operation."}, {"id": "acdcd035aad3491eb9d341ff133e7661", "stem": "Contoso Health is importing Contact records and wants each contact linked to its parent Account. The source file contains the ERP customer number, not the Salesforce Account Id. The Account object already has a unique External ID field called ERP_Number__c. How can the administrator relate the contacts to accounts during the import?", "options": ["Add a column with the Account ERP_Number__c value and map it to the Account relationship using the External ID in Data Loader", "Convert the Account Id column in the file to 15 characters so Salesforce can match it to the right parent", "Create a formula field on Contact that returns the ERP number and use it to join the two objects automatically", "Import the contacts without accounts and then edit each contact manually to fill in the Account Name lookup"], "correct": [0], "explanations": ["Correct. Data Loader can map a relationship to the parent through the parent's External ID field, so Salesforce resolves the Account without needing its Id.", "Incorrect. The file does not contain Salesforce Ids at all, and Id length is irrelevant to matching on an ERP number.", "Incorrect. A formula field on Contact does not create a relationship between records, and it cannot be used to populate the lookup.", "Incorrect. This works but is slow and manual, and it does not use the External ID to relate records during the import."], "takeaway": "A parent External ID value in the import file lets you populate lookup and master-detail relationships without knowing Salesforce Ids."}, {"id": "29f1961c2cd044e29d949600325a9a67", "stem": "Fabrikam Energy runs an upsert on the Asset__c custom object using the field Serial_Number__c, which is marked as External ID but not Unique. The file contains a serial number that matches two existing records. What is the result for that row?", "options": ["A new record is inserted because multiple matches are treated as no match", "Both existing records are updated with the values in the row", "The row fails with an error because the match is not unique", "The first matching record is updated and the second is left unchanged"], "correct": [2], "explanations": ["Incorrect. Inserting would only happen when no record matches, not when several do.", "Incorrect. Upsert never updates multiple records from a single row.", "Correct. When an upsert finds more than one record with the same External ID value, it returns an error for that row and does not update or insert.", "Incorrect. Salesforce does not choose one of the duplicates arbitrarily."], "takeaway": "Upsert errors out when the External ID value matches more than one existing record, so enforce uniqueness where it matters."}, {"id": "25de71003bb04bb68fbbb7aded6adde1", "stem": "Tailspin Retail plans to add a field to Product__c to hold the part number from its inventory system, which will be used for nightly upserts. The administrator is choosing the field type. Which two choices are valid for the External ID field? (Select the correct options.)", "options": ["Rich Text Area", "Text", "Long Text Area", "Checkbox", "Number"], "correct": [1, 4], "explanations": ["Incorrect. Rich Text Area fields cannot be used as External ID fields.", "Correct. Text fields can be flagged as External ID, which suits alphanumeric part numbers.", "Incorrect. Long Text Area fields cannot be designated as External ID.", "Incorrect. Checkbox fields cannot be designated as External ID.", "Correct. Number fields can also be flagged as External ID."], "takeaway": "Custom fields of type Text, Number, Email, and Auto Number can be set as External ID."}, {"id": "2448fca3b61c426e9f2db6a283922e5e", "stem": "Adventure Works is upserting Lead records from a marketing platform using a custom External ID field called Marketing_Id__c. The administrator wants to ensure that no two leads can ever hold the same Marketing_Id__c and that the field value comparison treats 'ab12' and 'AB12' as different values. Which two settings are available on the field to meet this need? (Select the correct options.)", "options": ["Select Set this field as the unique record identifier from an external system", "Add a validation rule that compares the field to itself", "Mark the field as Required to prevent duplicates", "Enable Duplicate Management on the field's page layout", "Set the field as Unique and choose Case sensitive"], "correct": [0, 4], "explanations": ["Correct. This is the External ID checkbox that allows the field to be used for upsert matching and relationship mapping.", "Incorrect. A validation rule cannot compare a record to others for uniqueness.", "Incorrect. Required only forces a value to be entered and does not prevent duplicates.", "Incorrect. Duplicate Management uses matching and duplicate rules, not a page layout setting, and does not enforce field uniqueness.", "Correct. The Unique option with Case sensitive prevents duplicate values and treats differing case as distinct."], "takeaway": "Combine the External ID checkbox with Unique (and the case sensitivity choice) to get reliable, duplicate-free upsert matching."}]}, {"id": "adm_backup", "title": "Exporter, sauvegarder, supprimer", "fiche": {"one_liner": "Data Export Service creates scheduled zipped CSV backups, deleted records stay 15 days in the Recycle Bin, Mass Delete and Mass Transfer handle bulk cleanup, and hard delete needs a special permission.", "need": "A regional insurance broker wants a copy of its Salesforce data outside the platform every month for audit. After an employee leaves, 4,000 of that person's leads must go to a colleague. A failed import also left 800 duplicate accounts to clean up quickly, with a way to recover them if the filter was wrong.", "core": "Data Export Service (service d'exportation de données) produces a backup of your org's data as zipped CSV files. You schedule it weekly or monthly (the available frequency depends on the edition), or run it on demand with Export Now. You can include attachments, documents and Salesforce Files. When the export is ready, Salesforce emails a download link. The files remain available for only a short time (48 hours), so someone must download them promptly. It is a copy of the data, not a one-click restore: getting data back means re-importing it, for example with Data Loader.\n\nWhen a record is deleted, it goes to the Recycle Bin (Corbeille) for 15 days. During that time it can be restored (undeleted), by the owner from their own bin or by an admin from the Org Recycle Bin. After 15 days it is permanently deleted. Mass Delete Records lets an admin delete many records of a supported object (accounts, leads, contacts, cases and so on) using filter criteria. Those records also go to the Recycle Bin, so a mistake can still be reversed within 15 days.\n\nA hard delete (suppression définitive) skips the Recycle Bin: the records are gone immediately and cannot be restored. It is done through the API (for example Bulk API in Data Loader) and requires the Bulk API Hard Delete user permission, which is not granted to anyone by default, even system administrators. You must add it via a permission set or profile.\n\nMass Transfer Records changes the owner of many records at once (accounts, leads, custom objects, and so on). Typical use: a user leaves, or territories are redistributed. Transfers can be filtered by current owner and other criteria.", "setup": ["Setup > Data Management > Data Export: click Export Now or Schedule Export, choose frequency, what to include (attachments, Files), then save", "Setup > Data Management > Mass Delete Records: pick the object, set filter criteria, review the matches, then delete", "Recycle Bin: use the Recycle Bin tab or app launcher item (users see their own, admins can view the Org Recycle Bin) to restore or purge records", "Setup > Users > Permission Sets (or Profiles): enable Bulk API Hard Delete to allow permanent deletion via the API", "Setup > Data Management > Mass Transfer Records: choose the record type, the current owner and the new owner, add filters, then transfer"], "compare": [{"a": "Data Export Service", "b": "Data Loader (export or import)", "diff": "Data Export Service is a scheduled, whole-org backup delivered as CSV files. Data Loader is a manual or scripted tool to export, import, update or delete selected objects. Neither restores an org automatically."}, {"a": "Delete (to the Recycle Bin)", "b": "Hard delete (permanent deletion)", "diff": "A normal or Mass Delete keeps records recoverable for 15 days. A hard delete bypasses the Recycle Bin and needs the Bulk API Hard Delete permission, which nobody has by default."}, {"a": "Mass Transfer Records", "b": "Mass Delete Records", "diff": "Transfer changes ownership and keeps the records. Delete removes them. Use transfer when a user leaves, never delete just to reassign."}], "signals": [{"if": "Regular backup, weekly or monthly copy of all data, audit archive", "then": "Data Export Service (scheduled export)"}, {"if": "Recover a deleted record within the last 15 days", "then": "Recycle Bin (undelete)"}, {"if": "Delete thousands of records matching criteria", "then": "Mass Delete Records"}, {"if": "Reassign many leads or accounts from a departing employee", "then": "Mass Transfer Records"}], "traps": ["The Recycle Bin keeps deleted records for 15 days, not 30 or indefinitely, after which they cannot be recovered.", "Mass Delete sends records to the Recycle Bin, so it is reversible. Only a hard delete is permanent immediately.", "Hard delete requires the Bulk API Hard Delete permission, and even System Administrators do not have it until it is assigned.", "Data Export Service gives CSV files, not a restore function, and the download link expires after a short time (48 hours)."], "scenario": {"situation": "A sales ops admin must remove 5,000 obsolete leads from an old campaign, but the director wants to be able to recover them if a filter turns out to be wrong. The company also needs a monthly copy of all data stored outside Salesforce for compliance.", "answer": "Use Mass Delete Records with precise filters: the leads go to the Recycle Bin and can be restored for 15 days. Do not use hard delete, which is immediate and irreversible. Schedule Data Export Service monthly to get zipped CSV backups, and download them before the link expires."}, "cards": [{"q": "How long do deleted records stay in the Recycle Bin?", "a": "15 days. After that they are permanently deleted and cannot be restored."}, {"q": "What does Data Export Service produce, and how often can it run?", "a": "Zipped CSV files of your data, scheduled weekly or monthly depending on the edition (or on demand with Export Now). Salesforce emails a download link that expires after a short time."}, {"q": "Which permission is needed to permanently delete records?", "a": "Bulk API Hard Delete. It is not assigned by default, so an admin must grant it through a profile or permission set."}, {"q": "Can records removed with Mass Delete Records be recovered?", "a": "Yes, they go to the Recycle Bin and can be restored within 15 days, unless a hard delete was used."}, {"q": "A salesperson leaves and owns 4,000 leads. Which tool reassigns them?", "a": "Mass Transfer Records (Setup > Data Management), choosing the old owner, the new owner and optional filters."}]}, "questions": [{"id": "3005cc4e18ab4242ac4bd9296810f8b5", "stem": "Northwind Logistics must keep an offline copy of all its Salesforce data to meet an internal audit policy. The admin wants a recurring, automated process that requires no installed tools and no custom code, and that delivers the data as files for storage outside Salesforce. What should the admin do?", "options": ["Schedule a recurring export in Data Export from Setup", "Enable Change Data Capture on all standard objects", "Run the Data Import Wizard on a schedule", "Subscribe to a weekly report for every object"], "correct": [0], "explanations": ["Correct. The Data Export Service can be scheduled weekly or monthly and produces zipped CSV files, with a download link sent by email.", "Change Data Capture publishes change events to subscribers. It does not produce a periodic file backup without extra development.", "The Data Import Wizard loads data into Salesforce. It does not export anything and cannot be scheduled.", "Report subscriptions cover only the data in each report and are meant for reading, not for creating a full backup of all objects."], "takeaway": "Use the scheduled Data Export Service (weekly or monthly) in Setup for a native, automated backup of Salesforce data as CSV files."}, {"id": "d82c56bef4634f25b012dbea66c43c95", "stem": "At Contoso Retail, a user deleted several Opportunity records by mistake. The admin discovers the problem 20 days later and finds nothing in the Recycle Bin. The company has a weekly Data Export scheduled, and the most recent file is 3 days old. What should the admin do to recover the records?", "options": ["Use the Undelete option from the Recycle Bin", "Extract the records from the latest export file and reload them with Data Loader or the Data Import Wizard", "Revert the Opportunity object to a previous version in Object Manager", "Ask Salesforce Support to extend the Recycle Bin retention period"], "correct": [1], "explanations": ["Deleted records stay in the Recycle Bin for only 15 days. After 20 days they are gone, so there is nothing to undelete.", "Correct. The backup from the weekly export still contains the records, so the admin can extract and reload them.", "Object Manager has no version history for records. It cannot roll back deleted data.", "The Recycle Bin retention is fixed at 15 days and cannot be extended on request, so this does not solve the issue."], "takeaway": "The Recycle Bin keeps deleted records for 15 days, so for anything older you must restore from a Data Export backup."}, {"id": "10eb9c2d2eff472a8b7e3131d572bbc0", "stem": "Fabrikam's marketing team imported 5,000 test Lead records by mistake. The admin wants to remove them in one operation using a clicks-only tool. The admin also wants a safety net, so the records can be restored for a limited time if the filter turns out to be wrong. Which solution fits?", "options": ["Hard delete the records with Data Loader", "Export the leads, then delete them one by one from the list view", "Convert all the leads and delete the resulting accounts", "Use Mass Delete Records in Setup with a filter on the test leads"], "correct": [3], "explanations": ["Hard delete bypasses the Recycle Bin, so the records could not be restored. It also needs a special permission and is not clicks-only.", "Deleting 5,000 records individually is impractical and does not use a mass operation.", "Converting leads creates accounts, contacts and opportunities and makes the data cleanup worse, not better.", "Correct. Mass Delete Records lets the admin filter and delete many records at once, and they go to the Recycle Bin where they can be undeleted."], "takeaway": "Mass Delete Records removes many records in one operation and sends them to the Recycle Bin, so they can still be undeleted for 15 days."}, {"id": "fc548bd92be14c7596224a7b29f1235b", "stem": "Tailspin Toys wants to permanently delete 200,000 obsolete Case records so they do not pass through the Recycle Bin. An admin opens Data Loader but cannot find the Hard Delete operation. The admin has the Delete permission on Cases. Which two actions are required to use Hard Delete? (Bulk API is the only supported way.)", "options": ["Use Mass Delete Records in Setup instead", "Configure Data Loader settings to use the Bulk API", "Empty the Recycle Bin before running the operation", "Add Modify All Data to the admin's profile", "Assign a permission set that includes the Bulk API Hard Delete user permission"], "correct": [1, 4], "explanations": ["Mass Delete Records sends records to the Recycle Bin and does not permanently delete them directly.", "Correct. Hard Delete is only available through the Bulk API, so Data Loader must be set to use it.", "Emptying the Recycle Bin is unrelated and would not make the Hard Delete operation available.", "Modify All Data does not grant hard delete ability. The specific Bulk API Hard Delete permission is needed.", "Correct. Hard delete requires the Bulk API Hard Delete permission, which is not enabled for any profile by default."], "takeaway": "Hard delete needs the Bulk API Hard Delete user permission and the Bulk API enabled in Data Loader, and it bypasses the Recycle Bin."}, {"id": "9bec2c914a414a41bd4bfe5458dc2320", "stem": "A sales rep at Adventure Works has left the company, and 3,000 Leads she owned must be reassigned to a colleague. The admin wants to keep the lead data unchanged apart from the owner. Which two approaches can accomplish this?", "options": ["Schedule a Data Export and assign the file to the colleague", "Deactivate the departing user so ownership moves automatically to the manager", "Update the OwnerId field on the leads with Data Loader", "Use Mass Transfer Records in Setup for Leads, filtered by the former owner", "Use Mass Delete Records and then re-import the leads"], "correct": [2, 3], "explanations": ["A Data Export is a backup and cannot reassign records to a user.", "Deactivating a user does not transfer record ownership. The records stay owned by the inactive user.", "Correct. Exporting the leads and updating OwnerId with Data Loader is a valid bulk reassignment method.", "Correct. The Mass Transfer Records tool can reassign leads from one owner to another based on criteria.", "Deleting and re-importing risks losing history and related data, and is not needed to change ownership."], "takeaway": "Use Mass Transfer Records or a Data Loader update of OwnerId to reassign records in bulk, because deactivating a user never transfers ownership."}]}, {"id": "adm_quality", "title": "Qualité des données", "fiche": {"one_liner": "Data quality tools keep records clean: validation rules block bad values, required fields force input, picklists standardize entries, duplicate rules alert or block, and field history tracking records changes.", "need": "Northwind Logistics has sales reps typing company names freely, leaving phone numbers blank, entering countries as 'USA', 'U.S.' or 'United States', and creating the same account twice. Reports become unreliable and managers cannot see who changed a deal's amount. Data quality features enforce consistency at entry and keep an audit trail.", "core": "Validation rules (règles de validation) evaluate a formula when a user saves a record. If the formula returns TRUE, the save is blocked and the error message you wrote is shown. This is the classic reversal: you write the formula to describe the BAD data. Example: AND(ISPICKVAL(StageName, \"Closed Won\"), Amount <= 0). The rule can display the error at the top of the page or next to a specific field. Validation rules also run on records saved by Flow, imports and the API, not just the user interface.\n\nRequired fields (champs obligatoires) can be set in two places. A field made required in its definition (the Required checkbox) is enforced everywhere: page layouts, imports, API. A field made required only on the page layout is enforced only for users of that layout in the UI. Picklists (listes de sélection) normalize values: instead of free text, users choose from an admin-defined list. A global value set (ensemble de valeurs global) lets several picklists share one list. Field dependencies make a picklist's values depend on another field, for example State depending on Country. Use State and Country picklists to standardize addresses.\n\nDuplicate management uses two linked pieces. A matching rule (règle de correspondance) defines HOW two records are considered similar, for example same email, or fuzzy match on company name. A duplicate rule (règle de doublons) defines WHAT happens when a match is found: Allow with an alert, or Block. Reports on duplicates can also be created. Duplicate rules apply to creating and editing, and you can choose to apply them to certain actions only.\n\nField history tracking (suivi de l'historique des champs) records the old value, new value, user and date for chosen fields. It does not prevent anything, it only audits. It is available for standard and custom objects, with a limit on the number of fields tracked per object (20 is the commonly cited number).", "setup": ["Validation rules: Setup > Object Manager > (object) > Validation Rules > New. Enter a formula, an error message and the error location.", "Required fields: Setup > Object Manager > (object) > Fields & Relationships > (field), tick Required. For layout-only, edit the page layout and mark the field as Required.", "Picklists: Setup > Object Manager > (object) > Fields & Relationships > New > Picklist, or use Setup > Picklist Value Sets for a global value set.", "Duplicate management: Setup > Duplicate Management > Matching Rules (create and activate), then Duplicate Rules (choose the matching rule and the Alert or Block action).", "Field history: Setup > Object Manager > (object) > Fields & Relationships > Set History Tracking, then select fields."], "compare": [{"a": "Matching rule", "b": "Duplicate rule", "diff": "A matching rule defines the criteria that identify similar records. A duplicate rule uses a matching rule and decides the action: Allow with an alert, or Block. Neither works without the other."}, {"a": "Required field in the field definition", "b": "Required on the page layout", "diff": "The field-level setting is enforced everywhere, including imports and API. The layout setting only affects users seeing that layout in the UI."}, {"a": "Field history tracking", "b": "Validation rule", "diff": "Field history only records changes after they happen. A validation rule prevents a bad value from being saved. Neither can do the other's job."}], "signals": [{"if": "Prevent saving when a condition is met (for example, close date in the past)", "then": "Validation rule"}, {"if": "Warn the user about a possible existing record, or stop the second one", "then": "Matching rule + duplicate rule (Alert or Block)"}, {"if": "Audit who changed a value, old and new values", "then": "Field history tracking"}, {"if": "Users enter inconsistent free text such as country names", "then": "Picklist (global value set if shared)"}], "traps": ["A validation rule fires when the formula returns TRUE, not FALSE. Candidates often write the formula for valid data and the rule blocks the wrong records.", "A duplicate rule alone does nothing: it needs a matching rule, and an active one.", "Making a field required on the page layout does not protect against data loaded by import or API; use the field-level Required setting.", "Field history tracking does not store history for fields you have not selected, and it cannot retroactively show changes made before tracking was turned on."], "scenario": {"situation": "A sales manager complains that reps are creating a second Account for customers already in the system, and that Opportunities are sometimes moved to Closed Won with a blank Amount. She also wants to know who edits the Amount field afterward.", "answer": "Create a matching rule on Account (for example fuzzy company name plus website) and a duplicate rule with the Block or Alert action. Add a validation rule that fires when Stage is Closed Won and Amount is blank. Turn on field history tracking for Amount to see who changed it, old and new values."}, "cards": [{"q": "When does a validation rule block a save?", "a": "When its formula evaluates to TRUE. The formula describes the invalid condition, and the error message is then displayed."}, {"q": "What is the difference between a matching rule and a duplicate rule?", "a": "A matching rule defines how records are compared. A duplicate rule applies a matching rule and chooses to Allow (with alert) or Block."}, {"q": "Where is a Required setting enforced everywhere, including imports and API?", "a": "On the field definition (Required checkbox). A layout-only requirement applies only in the UI for that layout."}, {"q": "What does field history tracking capture?", "a": "The field, old value, new value, user and date of the change, for selected fields only. It audits but never blocks."}, {"q": "How do you standardize a country or industry value across records?", "a": "Use a picklist instead of free text, and a global value set if several fields share the same values."}]}, "questions": [{"id": "18d58857a66b4c3eaac98ff9d385dbbb", "stem": "Northwind Logistics wants to make sure that no Opportunity can be saved with a Stage of Closed Won unless the Contract Signed Date field is filled in. Sales reps must still be able to save Opportunities in all other stages without that date. What should the administrator do?", "options": ["Create a duplicate rule on Opportunity that blocks records without a Contract Signed Date", "Enable field history tracking on Contract Signed Date", "Make Contract Signed Date required on the page layout", "Create a validation rule that returns true when Stage is Closed Won and Contract Signed Date is blank"], "correct": [3], "explanations": ["Incorrect. Duplicate rules compare records against each other using matching rules. They do not check whether a field is populated.", "Incorrect. Field history tracking records changes to values after the fact. It does not prevent a record from being saved.", "Incorrect. A field marked required on the page layout is required for every save in every stage, which breaks the need to save other stages without the date.", "Correct. A validation rule blocks the save when its formula evaluates to true, so it can enforce the field only when Stage is Closed Won."], "takeaway": "Use a validation rule when a field must be filled only under certain conditions, because the record is blocked when the formula is true."}, {"id": "6454699712924c1398e8f745b897fbcc", "stem": "Bright Harbor Insurance has a duplicate rule on Leads. Sales managers want reps to see a warning when they create a Lead that looks like an existing one, but reps must still be able to save the Lead if they judge it is a genuinely new person. What should the administrator configure?", "options": ["Mark the Email field as Unique in its field settings", "Set the duplicate rule action to Allow with an Alert", "Set the duplicate rule action to Block on create", "Create a validation rule that checks the Email field for uniqueness"], "correct": [1], "explanations": ["Incorrect. A unique field rejects any duplicate value with an error, so the rep could not save the Lead at all.", "Correct. The Allow action with Alert shows the user the potential duplicates and lets them save the record anyway.", "Incorrect. Block prevents the user from saving the record, which removes the rep's ability to override the warning.", "Incorrect. A validation rule would block the save outright and cannot reference other records the way matching rules do, so it gives no override."], "takeaway": "In a duplicate rule, choose Allow with an Alert to warn users while still letting them save, and Block to stop the save."}, {"id": "219f65acb15d46848206461974627f55", "stem": "Contoso Solar notices that the Industry field on Accounts contains entries like Tech, Technology, and IT, which makes reports unreliable. The company wants every new Account to use one consistent set of values. What is the best solution?", "options": ["Turn on field history tracking for the Industry field", "Make Industry a picklist with a defined list of values", "Create a matching rule on the Industry field", "Convert Industry to a Text field with a validation rule"], "correct": [1], "explanations": ["Incorrect. History tracking shows who changed values and when, but it does not standardize what can be entered.", "Correct. A picklist limits users to predefined values, which normalizes data and makes reporting consistent.", "Incorrect. Matching rules identify potential duplicate records and do not control which values can be entered in a field.", "Incorrect. Free text invites the very inconsistency the company wants to remove, and a validation rule would be hard to maintain for a list of allowed values."], "takeaway": "Use picklists to standardize data entry so that reporting stays consistent."}, {"id": "d621a3432f174c64bb71b572fed1fb10", "stem": "Fabrikam Health needs to investigate why the Annual Revenue on key Accounts keeps changing. Managers want to see who changed the value, the old value, the new value, and when, directly on the Account record. Which two actions should the administrator take?", "options": ["Create a validation rule that fires when Annual Revenue changes", "Enable field history tracking for Annual Revenue on the Account object", "Make Annual Revenue a required field", "Create a duplicate rule on Account", "Add the Account History related list to the Account page layout"], "correct": [1, 4], "explanations": ["Incorrect. A validation rule can block a change but does not record who made it or what the previous value was.", "Correct. Field history tracking must be enabled for the field so that Salesforce starts recording its changes.", "Incorrect. Making a field required ensures it has a value but does not log changes.", "Incorrect. Duplicate rules deal with matching records and have no relation to tracking field changes.", "Correct. The Account History related list on the page layout displays the user, date, old value, and new value for tracked changes."], "takeaway": "To audit field changes, enable field history tracking on the field and display the History related list on the page layout."}, {"id": "5aae6bf82f8f42338bddb8348a0ee928", "stem": "Adventure Works wants to improve the quality of Contact data. Duplicate Contacts are created when reps type the same person with slightly different email addresses and names. The administrator wants Salesforce to detect these likely duplicates when a Contact is created. Which two components are needed?", "options": ["A required field on the Contact page layout", "A matching rule that defines how Contact records are compared", "A picklist field for the Contact name", "A duplicate rule that uses the matching rule and sets the action", "A validation rule that compares the new Contact with all existing Contacts"], "correct": [1, 3], "explanations": ["Incorrect. Requiring a field improves completeness but does nothing to detect duplicate records.", "Correct. A matching rule defines which fields are compared and how closely they must match to identify potential duplicates.", "Incorrect. A name is free-form data, and a picklist cannot stop the same person from being entered twice.", "Correct. A duplicate rule references a matching rule and determines what happens when a match is found, such as alerting or blocking.", "Incorrect. Validation rules evaluate the current record only and cannot compare it against other records in the object."], "takeaway": "Duplicate management needs a matching rule to define how records match and a duplicate rule to decide the action taken."}]}, {"id": "adm_reports", "title": "Rapports : types et formats", "fiche": {"one_liner": "A report type decides which objects and fields a report can use, and the format (Tabular, Summary, Matrix, Joined) decides how rows are grouped and shown. Filters, buckets, formulas and subscriptions refine and deliver the results.", "need": "A sales director at a software company wants to see open opportunities by region and by owner, spot deals stuck for 60 days, list accounts that have no open opportunities, and receive the result every Monday morning. Report types, formats, filters, formulas and subscriptions cover this without any code.", "core": "The report type is the first choice and cannot be changed into a different object relationship afterwards. It defines the primary object and the related objects whose records and fields are available. Standard report types are created by Salesforce (for example Accounts, or Opportunities with Products). Custom report types (CRT) are built by an admin to control the object relationships, to choose which fields appear, and to define whether related records are required. In the 'A with B' relationship, only primary records that have at least one related record appear (inner join). In 'A with or without B', primary records appear even if they have no related records (outer join). Example: 'Accounts with or without Opportunities' shows accounts without any opportunity.\n\nThe format controls the layout. Tabular is a simple list of rows with no grouping, like a spreadsheet, and cannot be used in a dashboard chart. Summary groups rows by one or more fields and shows subtotals; it is the format needed for a 'top N' or for a chart with groupings. Matrix groups by both rows and columns, such as revenue by region (rows) and by quarter (columns). Joined combines up to five blocks, each based on a different report type, and shows them side by side, linked by common grouping fields.\n\nFilters narrow the data: field filters, standard filters (such as Show Me and Date Range), and filter logic (for example 1 AND (2 OR 3)). A cross filter uses WITH or WITHOUT to include or exclude records based on related objects, for example Accounts WITHOUT Opportunities. Buckets (bucket fields) group values into categories without a formula field, such as sorting deal amounts into Small, Medium and Large. A row-level formula calculates a value for each record row, while a summary formula calculates on grouped totals, such as a percentage of the group. Conditional highlighting colors summary or matrix values by thresholds. A subscription sends the report on a schedule (daily, weekly, monthly) and can notify only when conditions are met.", "setup": ["Setup > Report Types > New Custom Report Type: choose the primary object, label, category, and deployment status (In Development or Deployed).", "In the custom report type, add related objects and choose 'A records must have at least one B record' or 'A records may or may not have B records'. Then edit the page layout to choose the fields.", "Reports tab > New Report: pick the report type, then use the report builder to set the format, filters, filter logic, groupings, buckets, formulas and conditional highlighting.", "In the report, click Subscribe: set the frequency, time, recipients' conditions and the running user. This requires the Schedule Reports permission or equivalent.", "Setup > Reports and Dashboards Settings: manage options such as folder sharing and report-related user settings."], "compare": [{"a": "Summary report", "b": "Matrix report", "diff": "Summary groups rows by one or more fields only down the page. Matrix groups by both rows and columns, so use it when the scenario compares two dimensions at once, such as region by quarter."}, {"a": "Row-level formula", "b": "Summary formula", "diff": "A row-level formula is calculated on each record row before grouping. A summary formula is calculated on grouped totals, for example the average deal size per group, and is available only in Summary and Matrix reports."}, {"a": "Cross filter (WITH / WITHOUT)", "b": "Field filter", "diff": "A cross filter includes or excludes records according to the existence of related records, such as accounts without opportunities. A field filter only tests values on the report's own fields."}], "signals": [{"if": "Records with no related records (for example accounts without opportunities)", "then": "Custom report type with 'A with or without B', or a cross filter using WITHOUT"}, {"if": "Compare two dimensions at the same time (rows and columns)", "then": "Matrix report"}, {"if": "Group values into categories without creating a field", "then": "Bucket field"}, {"if": "Show data from different report types side by side", "then": "Joined report"}], "traps": ["A Tabular report cannot be grouped and cannot feed a dashboard chart, so a scenario needing a chart with groupings requires Summary or Matrix.", "Choosing 'A with B' in a custom report type silently hides primary records that have no related records, which is the opposite of what 'show all accounts' needs.", "A summary formula is only available in Summary and Matrix reports, and a row-level formula is calculated before grouping, so they are not interchangeable.", "A Joined report has up to five blocks, and each block uses its own report type, but they are linked only by shared grouping fields, not full object relationships."], "scenario": {"situation": "A sales manager wants a weekly email listing all accounts that have no opportunities, grouped by account owner, and wants accounts with more than $1M in annual revenue flagged in red.", "answer": "Create a Summary report on Accounts, using a cross filter 'Accounts WITHOUT Opportunities' (or a custom report type 'Accounts with or without Opportunities'), grouped by Account Owner. Add conditional highlighting on annual revenue, then click Subscribe to send it weekly. Summary is needed for grouping."}, "cards": [{"q": "What is the difference between 'A with B' and 'A with or without B' in a custom report type?", "a": "'A with B' returns only A records that have at least one B record (inner join). 'A with or without B' returns all A records, including those with no B records."}, {"q": "Which report formats can be used to group data?", "a": "Summary (groupings by rows), Matrix (by rows and columns), and Joined (blocks with common groupings). Tabular cannot group."}, {"q": "What is a cross filter used for?", "a": "To include or exclude records depending on whether related records exist, using WITH or WITHOUT, optionally with conditions on the related object."}, {"q": "What is a bucket field?", "a": "A way to group the values of a field into categories directly in a report, without creating a custom field or a formula."}, {"q": "What is the difference between a row-level formula and a summary formula?", "a": "A row-level formula calculates a value for every record row. A summary formula calculates on summarized (grouped) values, and only works in Summary and Matrix reports."}]}, "questions": [{"id": "08d647228a7a4b15a83559d6ae0d0457", "stem": "Northwind Logistics wants a report that lists all Accounts, including those that have no Contacts, and shows the related Contact names where they exist. The administrator creates a custom report type with Accounts as the primary object and Contacts as the child object. How should the administrator configure the relationship between the two objects?", "options": ["Add a cross filter with 'Contacts without' on the standard report", "Set it to 'Each A record must have at least one B record'", "Use a joined report with Accounts as the first block and Contacts as the second block", "Set it to 'A records may or may not have B records'"], "correct": [3], "explanations": ["Wrong: a 'without' cross filter would return only Accounts that have no Contacts, and it would hide the Contact names the business needs.", "Wrong: this setting returns only Accounts that have at least one Contact, so Accounts without Contacts would be excluded.", "Wrong: a joined report shows separate blocks and does not solve the need for one list with optional related records.", "Correct: this setting makes the report include Accounts with or without related Contacts, which is exactly the requirement."], "takeaway": "In a custom report type, choose 'A records may or may not have B records' to include parent records without children."}, {"id": "332598679cac463983d6eed654f3e5d2", "stem": "At Contoso Retail, a sales manager needs a report on Opportunities that shows total Amount grouped by Stage, and then by Owner within each Stage. The manager also wants to see subtotals but does not need to compare data across a column grouping. Which report format should the administrator use?", "options": ["Matrix", "Joined", "Tabular", "Summary"], "correct": [3], "explanations": ["Wrong: matrix reports are needed when grouping by both rows and columns, which is not required here.", "Wrong: joined reports combine multiple blocks from different report types, which is unnecessary for one object.", "Wrong: tabular reports show a simple list with a grand total and cannot group rows or produce subtotals.", "Correct: summary reports group rows by one or more fields and display subtotals, which fits grouping by Stage then Owner."], "takeaway": "Use the Summary format to group rows and show subtotals, and the Matrix format only when you also need column groupings."}, {"id": "cdf040d2db474578a236c53e0623e893", "stem": "Fabrikam Software wants a report of all Accounts that have no open Opportunities. The report is built on the Accounts report type. The administrator wants to avoid creating a custom report type. What should the administrator do?", "options": ["Create a bucket field on Account Name to group the results", "Add a filter on Opportunity Stage equals Closed Won", "Add a row-level formula that counts Opportunities for each Account", "Add a cross filter to the Accounts report with Opportunities and set it to 'Without', filtering on open stages"], "correct": [3], "explanations": ["Wrong: bucket fields group values into categories and cannot test whether related records exist.", "Wrong: a regular field filter cannot be applied to Opportunity fields in an Accounts-only report, and Closed Won does not mean no open Opportunities.", "Wrong: row-level formulas calculate values from fields on the same record and cannot count related child records.", "Correct: a cross filter with 'Without' returns Accounts that lack related Opportunities meeting the sub-filter criteria."], "takeaway": "Use a cross filter with 'With' or 'Without' to include or exclude records based on related objects."}, {"id": "2320fedc8bae4797b10423b7b26c49b0", "stem": "Tailspin Services has a Cases report with several filters. The administrator needs to return Cases where Status is New or Working, and Priority is High, or where Origin is Phone. The current results apply all filters with AND. Which actions should the administrator take to achieve the desired result? (Choose two.)", "options": ["Add a cross filter on Cases to apply the OR condition", "Open the filter logic option and enter (1 OR 2) AND 3 style logic that matches the intended grouping of filters", "Change the report format to Matrix so that OR conditions are allowed", "Enter the filter logic using the proper numbered filters, such as (1 AND 2) OR 3 with Status and Priority combined as needed", "Create a bucket field to apply the OR condition"], "correct": [1, 3], "explanations": ["Wrong: cross filters relate to child or parent objects and do not control OR logic between field filters.", "Correct: filter logic lets the administrator reference numbered filters with AND, OR and parentheses to control how they combine.", "Wrong: the report format does not change filter logic, and OR is available in any format via filter logic.", "Correct: the same Filter Logic feature is used to group conditions with parentheses, so building the combination with numbered filters achieves the result.", "Wrong: bucket fields categorize values and do not change how filters are combined."], "takeaway": "Use Filter Logic with numbered filters, AND, OR and parentheses to combine report filters beyond the default AND."}, {"id": "9894dc109e1b41cd82a1af4583f0f007", "stem": "Adventure Works wants a summary report on Opportunities in which any row with Amount below 10,000 is shown in red, and Amount above 100,000 is shown in green. Managers also want the report emailed every Monday to them. Which features should the administrator use? (Choose two.)", "options": ["A joined report to send the email", "A bucket field on Amount to schedule delivery", "A report subscription scheduled weekly for the managers", "A row-level formula to apply the colors", "Conditional highlighting on the summary fields in the report"], "correct": [2, 4], "explanations": ["Wrong: a joined report is a format for combining blocks and is not required for emailing or highlighting.", "Wrong: bucket fields group values into categories and have nothing to do with scheduling email delivery.", "Correct: subscribing to a report sends it by email on a schedule that the user defines, such as weekly on Monday.", "Wrong: row-level formulas calculate values and cannot apply colors.", "Correct: conditional highlighting lets you set color ranges for summary values in summary, matrix and joined reports."], "takeaway": "Use conditional highlighting to color summary values by range and report subscriptions to email reports on a schedule."}]}, {"id": "adm_dashboards", "title": "Tableaux de bord et instantanés", "fiche": {"one_liner": "Dashboards display report data through components, and the running user decides what each viewer sees; reporting snapshots store scheduled report results in a custom object to keep history.", "need": "A sales VP at a software company wants one page showing pipeline by region. Each regional manager should see only their own region's numbers on that same page. She also wants to compare pipeline week by week over the year, but reports only show current data. Dashboards and reporting snapshots solve these two needs.", "core": "A dashboard is a visual page made of components (charts, gauges, metrics, tables) built on source reports. Each component reads one report, so a dashboard can only show what its reports return. Dashboard filters (up to three) let viewers narrow the data by field values without editing the components, for example filtering by Region or Fiscal Quarter.\n\nThe running user is the key concept. In a static dashboard, data is shown according to the access of one chosen running user, so every viewer sees the same numbers, even records they could not otherwise open. In a dynamic dashboard, data is shown according to each viewer's own access (their role and sharing), so a regional manager sees only their region. Choose dynamic when the scenario says 'each person sees their own data'. Choose a specified running user when everyone must see the same view. Dynamic dashboards need a licence and have limits on how many an org can have, so check the edition.\n\nFolder sharing controls who can reach dashboards and reports. Access levels are Viewer (can see and run), Editor (can also edit and save content in the folder) and Manager (can also change the folder's sharing and rename or delete it). Sharing can go to users, roles, groups and so on. Sharing a folder does not widen record access: viewers still see only the records their own access allows in reports, except for the static dashboard running-user case.\n\nA reporting snapshot (in French, un instantané de rapport) saves the results of a tabular or summary report into a custom object on a schedule (daily, weekly, monthly). You map report fields to custom object fields. Each run adds new records, creating a history you can then report on and chart in a dashboard.", "setup": ["Dashboards tab > New Dashboard > choose a folder, then Add Component and select a source report and chart type", "In the dashboard editor: Filter > Add Filter (choose a field and values), and Properties (gear icon) > View dashboard as to pick the running user, or 'Let dashboard viewers choose whom they view the dashboard as' for dynamic behaviour", "Folder sharing: Dashboards or Reports tab > folder dropdown arrow > Share > choose users, roles or groups and set Viewer, Editor or Manager", "Setup > Quick Find 'Reporting Snapshots' > New Reporting Snapshot: pick the source report, the target custom object, map the fields, then schedule the run (a custom object must exist first)"], "compare": [{"a": "Static dashboard (specified running user)", "b": "Dynamic dashboard (run as logged-in user)", "diff": "Static shows everyone the data from one running user's access. Dynamic shows each viewer data based on their own access, so numbers differ by person."}, {"a": "Reporting snapshot", "b": "Scheduled report or subscription email", "diff": "A snapshot stores the results as records in a custom object to build history and trends. A scheduled refresh or email only delivers or updates the current view and keeps no history."}, {"a": "Dashboard filter", "b": "Report filter", "diff": "A dashboard filter lets viewers change the view across components. A report filter is built into the source report and fixes what that report returns."}], "signals": [{"if": "Each manager must see only their own team's data on the same dashboard", "then": "Dynamic dashboard (view as the logged-in user)"}, {"if": "Track how pipeline changed over months, historical trend, 'as of' past dates", "then": "Reporting snapshot into a custom object"}, {"if": "Let users switch the dashboard between regions or quarters without editing it", "then": "Dashboard filter"}, {"if": "A team member must change the reports in a shared folder but not change who can access it", "then": "Folder sharing with Editor access"}], "traps": ["Choosing a scheduled report or email when the scenario needs historical data: only a reporting snapshot keeps history.", "Forgetting that a static dashboard shows the running user's data to everyone, which can expose records viewers could not otherwise see.", "Confusing Editor and Manager: only Manager can change folder sharing, rename or delete the folder.", "Assuming a dashboard can show data not in its source reports: every component needs a source report, and the viewer needs access to the dashboard folder and the report folder."], "scenario": {"situation": "A support director wants a dashboard of open cases by queue. Each team lead must see only the cases their own access allows. She also needs to show the number of open cases at the end of each month for the past year, though no history is stored today.", "answer": "Build the dashboard as a dynamic dashboard so each lead sees data based on their own access. For history, create a custom object and a reporting snapshot of the open cases report, scheduled monthly. Then report and chart on the snapshot object, because a standard report cannot show past states."}, "cards": [{"q": "What does the running user determine in a dashboard?", "a": "Whose data access is used to display the dashboard. In a static dashboard, everyone sees that user's view; in a dynamic dashboard, each viewer's own access applies."}, {"q": "What are the three folder access levels and what separates them?", "a": "Viewer can view and run content, Editor can also edit and save content, and Manager can also manage the folder's sharing and rename or delete it."}, {"q": "What is the purpose of a reporting snapshot?", "a": "It runs a report on a schedule and saves the results as records in a custom object, so you can analyse trends and history over time."}, {"q": "What do you need before creating a reporting snapshot?", "a": "A source report (tabular or summary) and a target custom object with fields to map the report columns to."}, {"q": "What is the role of a dashboard filter?", "a": "It lets viewers narrow the data shown across components by a field value, without editing the dashboard or source reports."}]}, "questions": [{"id": "4bb3461c5fad45ce9456ecb4d7ce27b3", "stem": "Northwind Logistics has a sales dashboard that the VP of Sales reviews daily. Each regional sales manager must see only the opportunities they own and their team's when they open the same dashboard. The administrator wants to maintain a single dashboard rather than one per region. What should the administrator do?", "options": ["Schedule a daily dashboard refresh and send the results to each regional manager by email", "Configure the dashboard as a dynamic dashboard and select viewing as the logged-in user", "Set the running user to the VP of Sales and share the dashboard folder with all managers", "Create a dashboard filter on Opportunity Owner and ask each manager to select their name"], "correct": [1], "explanations": ["Scheduling a refresh only updates the data and sends a snapshot of the same view. It does not change whose data is displayed to each viewer.", "A dynamic dashboard runs as the logged-in user, so each manager sees data according to their own access and role hierarchy, using a single dashboard.", "With a fixed running user, every viewer sees the data the VP of Sales can see, so managers would not be limited to their own team's records.", "A filter narrows the data but is chosen manually and does not enforce visibility. Managers could still select other owners within the VP's visibility, and it is not security-based."], "takeaway": "Use a dynamic dashboard to display data based on each viewer's own access instead of a single fixed running user."}, {"id": "cc02ffe06df5457da6351059c2c8ad71", "stem": "Contoso Health wants to track how the number of open Cases by Priority changes week after week. The Case report only shows the current state, and past values are lost as records are updated. An executive wants to build trend charts from this history. Which solution should the administrator implement?", "options": ["Add a dashboard filter on Created Date to display older cases in the dashboard", "Create a Reporting Snapshot that runs on a schedule and stores the report results in a custom object", "Subscribe the executive to the report so a weekly email is sent with the results", "Enable field history tracking on the Case Priority field and report on it directly"], "correct": [1], "explanations": ["A filter only changes which current records are shown. It cannot recover the count of open cases as it was on a past date.", "A Reporting Snapshot saves the results of a tabular or summary report into a custom object on a schedule, building up historical data that can be reported on and charted over time.", "Subscriptions deliver a copy of the report but do not store the data in Salesforce for later trending and charting.", "Field history tracks changes to individual records, but it does not easily produce weekly aggregate counts of open cases by priority for trend analysis."], "takeaway": "Reporting Snapshots store scheduled report results in a custom object so you can analyze trends over time."}, {"id": "2e8ecaedac3141c295bf713dceef028f", "stem": "Fabrikam Energy has a Service dashboard in a shared folder. A team lead needs to change the components and rename the dashboard, but should not be able to modify who the folder is shared with or delete the folder. Which folder access level should the administrator give the team lead?", "options": ["Manager", "Viewer", "Editor", "Subscriber"], "correct": [2], "explanations": ["Manager adds the ability to change folder sharing and properties, including who has access, which exceeds what is needed.", "Viewer allows users only to view the dashboards in the folder, so the team lead could not change components.", "Editor lets users create, edit, and delete dashboards in the folder, but not manage folder sharing or properties. This matches the need.", "Subscriber is not a folder access level. Subscribing to a dashboard is a separate feature for receiving refreshed results."], "takeaway": "Folder access levels are Viewer, Editor, and Manager, and only Manager can change the folder's sharing and properties."}, {"id": "65de02238c174c429d98dcbe305b7c18", "stem": "Adventure Works built a dashboard with several components based on a single Opportunities report. The sales director wants viewers to narrow the whole dashboard by Opportunity Owner Role or by Stage without editing it. Which two actions should the administrator take?", "options": ["Create a separate dashboard for every role and every stage and organize them in folders", "Let viewers apply the filter directly on the dashboard in Lightning Experience to see the filtered results", "Convert the Opportunities report into a Reporting Snapshot so that the dashboard can be filtered", "Change the running user of the dashboard to each viewer in turn before they open it", "Add a dashboard filter to the dashboard and define filter values based on fields of the source reports"], "correct": [1, 4], "explanations": ["Duplicating dashboards for every combination is a heavy maintenance burden and defeats the purpose of dashboard filters.", "Once the filter is defined, viewers can apply filter values on the dashboard itself to narrow all components without editing it.", "A Reporting Snapshot stores historical data and is not required for filtering dashboards.", "The running user determines the data visibility, not the filtering. Changing it per viewer is not practical and does not provide filtering.", "Dashboard filters are added in the dashboard editor and use fields available in the underlying reports, such as owner role or stage."], "takeaway": "Dashboard filters let viewers refine all components of a dashboard on the fly, using fields from the underlying reports."}, {"id": "13786ae660d14df794940b2c023f192e", "stem": "Tailspin Retail's administrator is designing a dashboard to show win rates by region to the executive team. Which two statements about dashboard components and running users are correct in this design?", "options": ["A dashboard with a specified running user shows every viewer the data that this running user can access", "The running user must be a Salesforce admin to see all data in any dashboard", "Each dashboard component displays data from a source report, so the report must exist before the component is added", "A dynamic dashboard can be scheduled to refresh automatically and sent to any user in the org", "Components can be built directly from objects without any source report in the dashboard editor"], "correct": [0, 2], "explanations": ["With a specified running user, all viewers see the dashboard according to that user's data access, regardless of their own access.", "The running user does not need to be an administrator. They only need appropriate access to the data to be displayed.", "Dashboard components are based on reports, so the source report must exist (or be created) to feed each component.", "Dynamic dashboards cannot be scheduled for refresh, as they run as the viewing user.", "Components use reports as their data source. There is no way to build a standard dashboard component directly from an object without a report."], "takeaway": "Dashboard components are fed by reports, and a fixed running user determines the data everyone sees in that dashboard."}]}]}, {"id": "automatisation", "label": "Automatisation", "weight": 15, "topics": [{"id": "adm_flow_types", "title": "Les types de flow", "fiche": {"one_liner": "Pick the flow type by what starts it: a record change, a user at a screen, a schedule, a call from elsewhere, or a platform event. Flow Builder is the only tool for new automation.", "need": "A company wants to automate its processes without code. When a high-value opportunity closes, create an onboarding case. A support agent follows guided steps to log a complaint. Every night, flag stale leads. Each need starts differently, so Salesforce offers several flow types (types de flux) to match.", "core": "A flow is built in Flow Builder, and its type is set by its trigger. Record-Triggered Flow runs automatically when a record is created, updated or deleted. You choose the object, the trigger condition, and whether it runs Before the record is saved (fast, only updates the triggering record's own fields) or After the record is saved (needed to create or update related records, send emails or call actions). Screen Flow needs a user: it displays screens with fields and buttons, and is launched from a Lightning page, a button, an action, a utility bar or a tab.\n\nSchedule-Triggered Flow runs at a set time, once or daily or weekly, on a batch of records that match filter criteria. Typical use: every night, find open cases untouched for 7 days and notify the owner. Autolaunched Flow (No Trigger) has no start of its own and does not show screens. It is called by something else: another flow as a subflow, Apex, a REST API call, or a button/quick action in some setups. Use it for reusable logic.\n\nPlatform Event-Triggered Flow starts when a platform event message is published. It suits event-driven integrations, for example an external system publishes an order-shipped event and Salesforce updates the records. Do not confuse the event (the message) with a record change.\n\nProcess Builder and workflow rules are retired for new use, so Flow is the answer for any new automation need. To choose: user interaction means Screen Flow; data change means Record-Triggered; time means Schedule-Triggered; reuse or being called means Autolaunched; an event message means Platform Event-Triggered.", "setup": ["Setup > Process Automation > Flows > New Flow (opens Flow Builder).", "In the New Automation window, pick the starting point: Record-Triggered Flow, Screen Flow, Schedule-Triggered Flow, Platform Event-Triggered Flow, or Autolaunched Flow (No Trigger).", "Configure the Start element: object and trigger for record-triggered, frequency for scheduled, event for platform event.", "Save, then Activate the flow (a new flow version stays inactive until you activate it).", "Make a Screen Flow available: add it to a Lightning page with the Flow component, or to a button, action or utility bar."], "compare": [{"a": "Record-Triggered Flow (Before save)", "b": "Record-Triggered Flow (After save)", "diff": "Before save only updates fields on the triggering record and is faster. After save is required to create or update other records, send emails or call actions."}, {"a": "Autolaunched Flow (No Trigger)", "b": "Screen Flow", "diff": "An autolaunched flow has no screens and no user interaction and is called by something else. A screen flow shows screens and needs a user to run it."}, {"a": "Schedule-Triggered Flow", "b": "Record-Triggered Flow", "diff": "Scheduled runs at a set time on a batch of records matching criteria. Record-triggered runs the moment a record is created, updated or deleted."}], "signals": [{"if": "Guide a user through steps, collect input, wizard", "then": "Screen Flow"}, {"if": "When a record is created or updated, automatically", "then": "Record-Triggered Flow"}, {"if": "Every night, weekly, at a set time on many records", "then": "Schedule-Triggered Flow"}, {"if": "Reusable logic called from another flow, Apex or API, no screens", "then": "Autolaunched Flow (No Trigger)"}], "traps": ["Choosing Process Builder or workflow rules for a new automation: they are retired, so the answer is Flow.", "Using Before save when the scenario requires creating or updating related records: that needs After save.", "Picking a Screen Flow for something that must run with no user, such as at night or on a data change.", "Thinking an Autolaunched Flow starts itself: it has no trigger and must be called by a flow, Apex, API or similar."], "scenario": {"situation": "A sales director wants a Task automatically assigned to the account owner every time an Opportunity moves to Closed Won, and also wants every Monday morning a notification for all opportunities still open past their close date.", "answer": "Use two flows. A Record-Triggered Flow on Opportunity, set to run After the record is saved, creates the Task when Stage becomes Closed Won (it touches another record). A Schedule-Triggered Flow runs weekly on Monday, filtering overdue open opportunities and notifying owners. Each trigger matches the need."}, "cards": [{"q": "Which flow type needs a user to run it and displays screens?", "a": "Screen Flow. It is launched from a Lightning page, button, action or utility bar."}, {"q": "When must a Record-Triggered Flow run After save instead of Before save?", "a": "When it must create or update related records, send emails or call actions. Before save only updates fields of the triggering record."}, {"q": "What is an Autolaunched Flow (No Trigger)?", "a": "A flow with no screens and no trigger of its own, called by another flow, Apex, API or similar. Good for reusable logic."}, {"q": "Which flow type runs at a set time on a batch of records?", "a": "Schedule-Triggered Flow, with a start time, a frequency (once, daily, weekly) and filter criteria on an object."}, {"q": "What starts a Platform Event-Triggered Flow?", "a": "The publication of a platform event message, often from an external system or another Salesforce process."}]}, "questions": [{"id": "08a72b3f91854416b8fcbf42373a9313", "stem": "Northwind Logistics wants every Case record to be automatically assigned a priority of High and have its Escalation Date field populated whenever a Case is created with the Type set to Outage. The update must happen on the same record before it is saved, with the best performance and no extra database update. Which flow type should the administrator build?", "options": ["Screen Flow added to the Case page layout", "Record-Triggered Flow configured to run before the record is saved", "Record-Triggered Flow configured to run after the record is saved", "Schedule-Triggered Flow that runs every hour"], "correct": [1], "explanations": ["A Screen Flow needs a user to launch it and interact with it, so it does not run automatically when a Case is created.", "Correct. A before-save record-triggered flow updates fields on the triggering record without an additional DML operation, which is the fastest option.", "An after-save flow can update the record, but it requires an extra update operation and is meant for related records or actions. It is less efficient here.", "A scheduled flow would leave a delay and processes records in batches, so the values would not be set when the record is saved."], "takeaway": "To update fields on the record that triggered the flow, use a Record-Triggered Flow that runs before the record is saved."}, {"id": "e0e93d3ecab5445882c5bcf2bcf67d48", "stem": "Contoso Health wants its call center agents to follow a guided script when they handle a billing dispute. The agent must answer questions on successive pages, and the flow creates a Case and a related Task based on the answers. Agents will start it from a button on the Account page. Which flow type meets this requirement?", "options": ["Autolaunched Flow", "Screen Flow", "Record-Triggered Flow", "Platform Event-Triggered Flow"], "correct": [1], "explanations": ["An Autolaunched Flow has no screens, so it cannot show pages and collect answers from the agent.", "Correct. A Screen Flow displays screens to collect user input and can be launched from a button, action, or Lightning page.", "A Record-Triggered Flow starts from a record change and cannot display screens to guide a user.", "A Platform Event-Triggered Flow starts when a platform event message is received and has no user interface."], "takeaway": "Whenever a user must enter or review information through a guided interface, choose a Screen Flow."}, {"id": "8fb243922d0d4b1498dd710408ac6c39", "stem": "Fabrikam Energy needs to review all open Opportunities every Sunday night. Any Opportunity with a Close Date in the past must have its Stage set to Closed Lost and its owner notified. No user should have to start the process. Which approach should the administrator use?", "options": ["Schedule-Triggered Flow that runs weekly", "Record-Triggered Flow that runs when an Opportunity is created", "Screen Flow placed on the Opportunity record page", "Autolaunched Flow with no trigger configured"], "correct": [0], "explanations": ["Correct. A Schedule-Triggered Flow runs at a set frequency and processes the records that match its criteria in batch, with no user involved.", "A flow that runs on creation would not catch Opportunities whose Close Date passes later, since no record change occurs.", "A Screen Flow requires a user to run it and works one session at a time, so it does not meet the no-manual-start requirement.", "An Autolaunched Flow has no trigger of its own and must be called by something else, such as another flow or Apex."], "takeaway": "Use a Schedule-Triggered Flow for recurring batch work on records that is not tied to a record change or a user action."}, {"id": "8ec14ee3e2bb489a825f3b712c81a18f", "stem": "Adventure Works built a reusable flow that calculates a discount from an Account's tier and returns the value. Several other flows, including a Screen Flow and a Record-Triggered Flow, need to run this same logic without rebuilding it. Which two choices describe the correct approach? (Select the two that apply.)", "options": ["Build the logic as a Schedule-Triggered Flow and run it daily", "Build the logic as an Autolaunched Flow with input and output variables", "Call it from the other flows using a Subflow element", "Build the logic as a Screen Flow and hide the screens", "Copy the logic into each flow so that it works independently"], "correct": [1, 2], "explanations": ["A scheduled flow runs on its own timetable and cannot return a value to a calling flow on demand.", "Correct. An Autolaunched Flow has no trigger or screens and can accept input variables and return output variables, making it suitable for reusable logic.", "Correct. The Subflow element lets a flow call another flow, passing and receiving variable values.", "A Screen Flow is designed for user interaction, and it is not the right type to be called as reusable background logic.", "Duplicating logic makes maintenance harder and goes against the reuse the scenario asks for."], "takeaway": "Reusable background logic belongs in an Autolaunched Flow that other flows call through the Subflow element."}, {"id": "5e22a510c0d24b39bb12b4d762cd3f2b", "stem": "Tailspin Retail's order system publishes a platform event named Order_Shipped__e each time a package leaves the warehouse. Salesforce must react to each event by creating a Task for the account owner and updating a custom field on the related Account. Which two statements about this requirement are correct? (Select the two that apply.)", "options": ["Use a Platform Event-Triggered Flow subscribed to Order_Shipped__e", "Use a Screen Flow so the account owner confirms each shipment", "Use a Schedule-Triggered Flow that polls for new events each minute", "Use a Record-Triggered Flow on the Account object, since platform events are Account records", "The flow can use the event's field values to find the Account and create the Task"], "correct": [0, 4], "explanations": ["Correct. A Platform Event-Triggered Flow starts when a message for the chosen platform event is published.", "A Screen Flow needs a user to run it, but the requirement is an automatic reaction to each event.", "Scheduled flows run on a set frequency against records and are not the mechanism for subscribing to platform events.", "Platform events are not Account records, so a flow on the Account object does not fire when an event is published.", "Correct. The event record that triggered the flow is available as $Record, so its fields can be used to look up the Account and create the Task."], "takeaway": "Use a Platform Event-Triggered Flow to react automatically to platform event messages, reading the event fields from $Record."}]}, {"id": "adm_flow_elements", "title": "Les éléments d'un flow", "fiche": {"one_liner": "Flow elements are the building blocks of a flow: Get/Create/Update/Delete Records handle data, Decision and Loop control the path, Assignment, variables and formulas hold values, and subflows reuse logic.", "need": "A company wants a flow that, when an Opportunity is Closed Won, finds all its open Tasks, closes them, and creates an onboarding Case. Each need maps to an element: Get Records to find the Tasks, Loop to go through them, Assignment to change them, Update Records to save them in one go, Create Records for the Case.", "core": "Data elements talk to the database. Get Records reads records using filter conditions and stores them in a record variable or a record collection variable. Create Records, Update Records and Delete Records write changes. Each one counts as a DML operation (a database write) and a Get Records counts as a SOQL query (a database read). Governor limits apply per transaction, so the key habit is to avoid putting these elements inside a loop.\n\nLogic elements steer the path. Decision (Salesforce's if/else) evaluates outcomes in order and takes the first one that is true, otherwise the Default Outcome. Loop goes through a collection one item at a time and exposes the current item as the loop variable. It has two paths: For Each Item and After Last Item. Assignment sets values: it can change a field on a record variable, add an item to a collection, or increment a number variable. It changes only the values in the flow's memory, nothing is saved until an Update Records or Create Records element runs.\n\nTo work on a collection without looping, use the Collection Filter element (keeps the items meeting conditions) and Collection Sort element (orders items and can limit the number kept). Both produce a new collection.\n\nVariables store data (text, number, record, collection), and a Formula resource calculates a value on the fly, for example the number of days between two dates. A Subflow element calls another flow and passes input variables to it, so shared logic is built once and reused. The called flow must be Active to be run by a subflow, and it must be an autolaunched flow, with variables set as Available for input or output.", "setup": ["Setup > Process Automation > Flows > New Flow, then choose the type (Record-Triggered, Screen, Autolaunched).", "In Flow Builder, click the + on the canvas and pick the element from the Add Element menu (Data: Get/Create/Update/Delete Records; Logic: Decision, Loop, Assignment, Collection Filter, Collection Sort).", "In the Manager tab of the toolbox, click New Resource to create a Variable, a Formula, a Constant or a Text Template.", "To reuse a flow, build it as an Autolaunched Flow with input/output variables, activate it, then add a Subflow element in the parent flow and map the variables."], "compare": [{"a": "Assignment element", "b": "Update Records element", "diff": "Assignment changes values only in the flow's memory. Update Records writes them to the database. Forgetting the second one means nothing is saved."}, {"a": "Collection Filter", "b": "Get Records with filter conditions", "diff": "Get Records filters in the database with a query. Collection Filter filters a collection already in memory, with no additional query."}, {"a": "Variable", "b": "Formula resource", "diff": "A variable stores a value that can change during the flow. A formula is calculated each time it is used and cannot be assigned a value."}], "signals": [{"if": "Go through each related record one by one", "then": "Loop element"}, {"if": "Different paths depending on a field value or condition", "then": "Decision element"}, {"if": "Reuse the same logic in several flows", "then": "Subflow element"}, {"if": "Keep only some records of an already retrieved collection, or sort them", "then": "Collection Filter or Collection Sort"}], "traps": ["Putting Get Records, Update Records or Create Records inside a Loop causes governor limit errors. Build a collection with Assignment and run a single DML element after the loop.", "An Assignment element does not save anything to the database. An Update Records element is still required.", "In a Decision, outcomes are evaluated in order and the first one that is true wins. The Default Outcome runs only when none match.", "Get Records with 'Only the first record' returns a single record variable, not a collection. Choosing the wrong option breaks a later Loop."], "scenario": {"situation": "When an Account's status becomes Inactive, a company wants all its open Opportunities set to Closed Lost. The admin is told to respect governor limits and keep the flow efficient.", "answer": "Use a record-triggered flow on Account. Get Records retrieves the open Opportunities into a collection. A Loop goes through it, and an Assignment sets the Stage on the current item and adds it to a second collection. After the loop, one Update Records element updates that collection. This avoids DML inside the loop."}, "cards": [{"q": "What does the Loop element expose and what are its two paths?", "a": "It exposes the current item as the loop variable. Its paths are For Each Item and After Last Item."}, {"q": "What happens when no Decision outcome is true?", "a": "The Default Outcome path is followed."}, {"q": "Why avoid Get, Create, Update or Delete Records inside a Loop?", "a": "Each execution counts against governor limits (SOQL queries and DML statements), so the flow can fail. Collect records in a collection and use one element after the loop."}, {"q": "What are Collection Filter and Collection Sort used for?", "a": "They narrow down or order a collection already in the flow's memory and output a new collection, without a new database query."}, {"q": "What is a Subflow element?", "a": "An element that calls another active autolaunched flow, passing input variables and receiving output variables, so logic can be reused."}]}, "questions": [{"id": "afb423973e0146c48a0892e9532355ad", "stem": "Northwind Logistics wants a screen flow that lets a support agent review all open Cases for an Account and update the Priority on each one to High when the Account's Tier is Platinum. The administrator has already used Get Records to store the open Cases in a collection variable. What should the administrator add next to update every Case while following best practices?", "options": ["An Update Records element that uses the Case collection directly, without setting the Priority field on any record first", "A Loop element, with an Assignment inside the loop that sets Priority on the loop variable and adds it to a second collection, then one Update Records element after the loop using that collection", "A Loop element with an Update Records element inside the loop that updates the current Case on each iteration", "A Decision element that routes each Case to its own Update Records element"], "correct": [1], "explanations": ["Incorrect. Update Records can use a collection, but the Priority field must be changed on the records first. Otherwise nothing changes.", "Correct. Assigning values inside the loop, collecting the modified records, and running a single Update Records after the loop keeps DML out of the loop and avoids governor limit problems.", "Incorrect. Placing Update Records inside a Loop runs one DML operation per iteration, which is a classic anti-pattern that can hit governor limits.", "Incorrect. A Decision evaluates conditions and branches the path. It cannot iterate over a variable number of records, and one element per Case is not feasible."], "takeaway": "Never put Get, Create, Update or Delete Records elements inside a Loop; use Assignment to build a collection and run one DML element after the loop."}, {"id": "73bd75f4fcb744d9887173775d944621", "stem": "Contoso Energy has a flow that retrieves all Opportunities for an Account with Get Records into a collection variable. The admin now needs only the Opportunities with an Amount over 50,000 and wants them ordered from highest to lowest Amount for a Loop. The Get Records query has already run and the collection must not be queried again. Which element should the admin use?", "options": ["Collection Filter to keep the matching records, then Collection Sort on Amount in descending order", "Collection Sort only, because it can also remove records that do not meet a condition", "A second Get Records element with the sort order set to Descending", "A Decision element followed by an Assignment that removes each non-matching record"], "correct": [0], "explanations": ["Correct. Collection Filter creates a new collection with records matching the conditions, and Collection Sort then orders it by Amount descending, with no additional query.", "Incorrect. Collection Sort only reorders records. It does not filter them out.", "Incorrect. This would run another query, which the scenario explicitly rules out, and it would not reuse the existing collection.", "Incorrect. A Decision cannot remove items from a collection on its own, and this approach would require a Loop and more complexity than needed."], "takeaway": "Use Collection Filter and Collection Sort to refine a collection already in memory instead of querying the database again."}, {"id": "ceaf68ffb7c24c2ba6b18784ee0e9722", "stem": "Fabrikam Retail uses a record-triggered flow on Order. The flow must calculate a discounted total as Amount multiplied by 0.9 and reuse that value in three different elements, without storing it on any field and without adding an element to compute it. What should the administrator create?", "options": ["A Formula resource of type Currency that references the Amount field", "A Constant resource holding the value 0.9, used as the final total", "A Record variable of the Order object that stores the discounted amount", "A Text Template resource that multiplies the Amount by 0.9"], "correct": [0], "explanations": ["Correct. A Formula resource calculates a value dynamically from other fields or variables and can be referenced anywhere in the flow.", "Incorrect. A Constant is a fixed value that cannot change or be computed from record data, so it cannot hold the discounted total.", "Incorrect. A Record variable stores a whole record, and it would not compute anything by itself.", "Incorrect. A Text Template is for formatted text and merge fields, not for calculating numbers."], "takeaway": "Use a Formula resource when a flow needs a calculated value that is derived from other data and reused across elements."}, {"id": "43c2eb88e44042e8aba2b546d6abfbd3", "stem": "Adventure Works wants a screen flow that collects customer details for a Case. The same address validation steps are needed in several other flows owned by different teams. The admin wants to build the logic once and maintain it in a single place. Which two actions should the admin take?", "options": ["Copy the validation elements into each flow so every team can customize them independently", "Create a Decision element in each flow that replicates the validation conditions", "Build the validation as a record-triggered flow that runs whenever the Case is saved", "Build the validation as a separate autolaunched flow and call it with a Subflow element from each flow", "Define input and output variables in the validation flow, marked as available for input and output, to pass data in and out"], "correct": [3, 4], "explanations": ["Incorrect. Copying elements duplicates logic, so every future change would need to be made in each flow.", "Incorrect. Replicating conditions in each flow also duplicates logic and defeats the goal of a single maintained location.", "Incorrect. A record-triggered flow cannot be called by a Subflow element, and it would fire on record saves rather than being reused on demand.", "Correct. A Subflow element lets a parent flow call another flow, so the logic is maintained once and reused.", "Correct. Variables marked Available for input or output let the parent flow pass values to the subflow and receive results back."], "takeaway": "Reuse flow logic with a Subflow element and pass data through variables marked Available for input and Available for output."}, {"id": "11a353b408d244d69c9ad6a391b90943", "stem": "Tailspin Insurance has an autolaunched flow that runs when a Claim is submitted. If the Claim Amount is above 10,000, the flow must create a Task for a senior adjuster. If the Claim Amount is 10,000 or less, it must update the Claim Status to Approved. Which two elements should the admin use?", "options": ["A Decision element with one outcome for Amount above 10,000 and the default outcome for all other cases", "A Create Records element on the Task object in the path for high amounts", "A Get Records element that deletes Claims under 10,000", "A Collection Filter element that removes the Claim when the Amount is 10,000 or less", "A Loop element to evaluate the Amount against each possible value"], "correct": [0, 1], "explanations": ["Correct. A Decision splits the flow into paths based on conditions, and the default outcome handles every record that does not match.", "Correct. Create Records adds the Task for the senior adjuster in the high amount path.", "Incorrect. Get Records only retrieves data and cannot delete records. Deleting would use Delete Records, which this scenario does not need.", "Incorrect. Collection Filter creates a subset of a collection and does not remove the Claim or route the process.", "Incorrect. A Loop iterates over a collection, and it is not used to branch on a single value."], "takeaway": "Use a Decision element to branch a flow by conditions and then use the matching record element, such as Create Records, on the relevant path."}]}, {"id": "adm_flow_record", "title": "Flow déclenché par un enregistrement : avant ou après l'enregistrement", "fiche": {"one_liner": "Use a before-save (Fast Field Updates) record-triggered flow to change fields on the triggering record cheaply; use an after-save (Actions and Related Records) flow to touch other records or perform actions.", "need": "A sales operations team at a manufacturing company wants every new Opportunity to get a default Next Step text and a rounded Amount, and wants a follow-up Task created for the owner when the Opportunity reaches Closed Won. The first need changes the record itself. The second creates a different record, so each need calls for a different flow timing.", "core": "A record-triggered flow starts when a record is created, updated, or deleted. You choose when it runs. Fast Field Updates runs before the save: the flow changes the triggering record's own fields, and Salesforce saves those values in the same operation. No extra DML (database write) is needed, so it is much faster and is the recommended choice for same-record updates. It can only use Update Records on the triggering record via $Record. It cannot create records, update related records, or send emails, and it does not support Actions or the Pause element.\n\nActions and Related Records runs after the save. The record already has an Id, so the flow can create child records, update related records (parent or children), post to Chatter, send email, call subflows, invoke Apex actions, and so on. Updating the triggering record again here costs an extra DML operation and can cause recursion, so avoid it when before-save would do.\n\nEntry conditions decide whether the flow runs at all: set Condition Requirements (All Conditions Are Met, Any, Custom Logic, or Formula Is True) and choose when to run: only when a record is created or updated to meet the conditions, or every time a record is updated and meets them. The first option prevents re-firing on unrelated edits. To compare with the old value, use $Record__Prior, which is available on update and holds the values from before the change. Example: Stage changed to Closed Won means $Record.StageName is Closed Won and $Record__Prior.StageName is not.\n\nAfter-save flows add an optional Run Asynchronously path (Asynchronous Path) that runs in a separate transaction, useful for callouts to external systems. Scheduled Paths run at a set time relative to a date field on the record, for example 7 days after Close Date. They also run after the save.", "setup": ["Setup > Process Automation > Flows (or Quick Find: Flows) > New Flow > Record-Triggered Flow", "On the Start element, choose the object and the trigger: A record is created, updated, created or updated, or deleted", "Set Entry Conditions (Condition Requirements) and the optimization: Fast Field Updates (before the save) or Actions and Related Records (after the save)", "Optional, after-save only: in Start, click Add Scheduled Paths or Run Asynchronously to add extra paths", "Debug, then Save and Activate"], "compare": [{"a": "Fast Field Updates (before-save)", "b": "Actions and Related Records (after-save)", "diff": "Before-save only updates the triggering record's own fields and is the fastest. After-save is required to create or update other records or to use actions such as email, Apex, or subflows."}, {"a": "Run Asynchronously path", "b": "Scheduled Path", "diff": "Asynchronous Path runs right after the main transaction commits, in its own transaction (good for callouts). A scheduled path runs at a defined time offset from a date or time field on the record."}, {"a": "$Record", "b": "$Record__Prior", "diff": "$Record holds the current (new) values. $Record__Prior holds the values before the update, and is used to detect that a field changed."}], "signals": [{"if": "Set or default a field on the same record that triggered the flow, with best performance", "then": "Record-triggered flow, Fast Field Updates (before-save)"}, {"if": "Create a related Task, Case, or child record, or update a parent or child record", "then": "Record-triggered flow, Actions and Related Records (after-save)"}, {"if": "Run only when a specific field changed from a given value", "then": "Entry conditions plus $Record__Prior (only when a record is updated to meet the criteria)"}, {"if": "Do something 3 days before or 7 days after a date field, or call an external system without blocking the save", "then": "Scheduled Path or Asynchronous Path in an after-save flow"}], "traps": ["Before-save flows cannot create records, update related records, send emails, or use Actions; if the scenario needs any of these, the answer is after-save.", "Using after-save to update the triggering record works but costs an extra DML and risks recursion; for same-record updates the exam expects Fast Field Updates.", "Choosing Every time a record is updated and meets the conditions without a guard can re-run the flow on every edit; use the created or updated to meet the conditions option when the action must fire once.", "Process Builder and workflow rules are retired for new automation: the answer for a new need is always a record-triggered flow, never those tools."], "scenario": {"situation": "At a software company, when an Opportunity's Stage changes to Closed Won, the flow must set the Opportunity's Description to a standard text, create an onboarding Case linked to the Account, and email the account owner a week later if the Case is still open.", "answer": "Build one after-save record-triggered flow on Opportunity. The entry condition is Stage equals Closed Won, run only when the record is updated to meet the conditions. The Case must be created, so before-save is not enough. Add a scheduled path set 7 days after the trigger for the follow-up email. The Description is a same-record update, but it can be done here too."}, "cards": [{"q": "What is the official name of the before-save option in a record-triggered flow, and what does it do?", "a": "Fast Field Updates. It updates fields on the triggering record before it is saved, without an extra DML operation."}, {"q": "Which option must you choose to create a related record from a record-triggered flow?", "a": "Actions and Related Records (after-save), because the triggering record needs an Id and only after-save flows can create other records or call actions."}, {"q": "How do you get the value a field had before the update in a record-triggered flow?", "a": "Use the global variable $Record__Prior, available when the flow triggers on update, usually combined with entry conditions to detect a change."}, {"q": "When should you use the \"only when a record is updated to meet the condition requirements\" option?", "a": "When the flow should fire once, at the moment the criteria become true, instead of on every later edit of a record that already meets them."}, {"q": "What is a Scheduled Path used for?", "a": "To run flow actions at a time relative to a date or date/time field on the record, such as 3 days after Close Date. It is available in after-save flows."}]}, "questions": [{"id": "550b6381678c461ebb02ab348a53a258", "stem": "Northwind Outfitters wants every new Opportunity to have its Description field automatically populated with the text 'Pending review' when the Stage is Prospecting. The admin wants the most efficient solution, with no additional DML operation on the triggering record. Which approach should the admin use?", "options": ["A scheduled-triggered flow that runs nightly and updates Opportunities in Prospecting", "A record-triggered flow that runs before the record is saved, with a Create Records element", "A record-triggered flow that runs before the record is saved, with an Assignment element setting the field on $Record", "A record-triggered flow that runs after the record is saved, with an Update Records element on $Record"], "correct": [2], "explanations": ["A scheduled-triggered flow would not populate the field at the moment of record creation and is not designed for this need.", "Create Records is not available in before-save flows, and it would not update the triggering record anyway.", "Correct. A before-save flow (Fast Field Updates) can set fields on the triggering record directly, without an additional DML operation.", "An after-save flow would work, but updating the triggering record requires an extra DML operation, so it is less efficient than a before-save flow."], "takeaway": "To update fields on the triggering record itself, use a before-save record-triggered flow (Fast Field Updates), which is the most efficient option."}, {"id": "a74b15be05de400191d0bd601aff9e41", "stem": "Contoso Health wants a flow that creates a follow-up Task assigned to the Case owner whenever a Case is created with Priority set to High. The Task must be linked to the Case through the WhatId field, which requires the Case Id. Which flow configuration meets the requirement?", "options": ["A before-save record-triggered flow on Case with an Assignment element setting Task fields", "A scheduled-triggered flow on Case that runs hourly and creates Tasks", "An after-save record-triggered flow on Case with a Create Records element for the Task", "A before-save record-triggered flow on Case with a Create Records element for the Task"], "correct": [2], "explanations": ["An Assignment element can only change the triggering record's fields in a before-save flow, it cannot create a Task.", "A scheduled flow introduces delay and is not triggered by record creation, so it is not suited to this requirement.", "Correct. After-save flows can create related records and have access to the Id of the newly created Case.", "Before-save flows cannot create related records, and the Case Id does not exist yet at that point on a new record."], "takeaway": "Creating or updating related records requires an after-save record-triggered flow, because the triggering record's Id is available only after it is saved."}, {"id": "7eb980c5aa81450eb1e0575dccc484dd", "stem": "Fabrikam Energy wants a record-triggered flow on Account that sends an email alert only when the Rating field changes to Hot. Currently the flow fires on every Account edit, including edits where Rating was already Hot. What should the admin configure?", "options": ["Remove the entry conditions and add a Decision element that checks Rating equals Hot", "Change the flow to run asynchronously after the record is saved", "Set the entry condition to Rating equals Hot and select the option to run the flow only when a record is updated to meet the condition requirements", "Set the entry condition to Rating equals Hot and use the option to run the flow every time a record is updated and meets the condition requirements"], "correct": [2], "explanations": ["A Decision element inside the flow would still let the flow start on every edit, and would not detect a change in value by itself.", "Asynchronous execution affects when the flow runs, not whether it runs, so it does not solve the repeated triggering.", "Correct. This option runs the flow only when the record changes from not meeting the conditions to meeting them, so edits to an already Hot Account do not trigger it.", "This option runs the flow on every update where the record meets the conditions, which is the behavior causing the problem."], "takeaway": "Use the 'Only when a record is updated to meet the condition requirements' option to fire a flow only when a record newly meets the entry conditions."}, {"id": "db2ac8a38b6141eb88b75ab982e8c5a4", "stem": "Adventure Works has an after-save record-triggered flow on Opportunity. When an Opportunity is Closed Won, the flow must send the details to an external billing system through an HTTP callout. The admin wants the callout to run without holding up the original transaction. Which two configuration choices should the admin make?", "options": ["Add the HTTP Callout action on the Run Asynchronously path", "Add an Asynchronous Path to the flow", "Configure the flow as a before-save flow", "Add a Scheduled Path set to 0 minutes after the trigger", "Add an HTTP Callout action on the Run Immediately path"], "correct": [0, 1], "explanations": ["Correct. Placing the HTTP Callout action on the Run Asynchronously path lets it execute in its own transaction.", "Correct. The Asynchronous Path runs in a separate transaction after the original one commits, which allows callouts.", "Before-save flows cannot perform callouts or create related records, and the Asynchronous Path is available only in after-save flows.", "A Scheduled Path is a different feature, designed for time offsets from a date or the trigger, and is not the standard answer for separating a callout from the save transaction.", "Callouts on the Run Immediately path are not allowed in the same transaction as the record save, and they would also hold up the transaction."], "takeaway": "Use the Run Asynchronously path of an after-save flow for external callouts so they run in a separate transaction."}, {"id": "d1289a34bc354473b4ebdf2ef2412868", "stem": "Tailspin Rentals wants a record-triggered flow on Contract__c. Seven days before the End_Date__c, the flow must create a renewal Task for the owner, and the flow must only act if the contract is still Active at that time. Which two elements or settings should the admin use?", "options": ["A Fast Field Update that sets a renewal checkbox when the record is saved", "A Decision element or entry condition on the scheduled path that checks Status equals Active", "A Scheduled Path with an offset of 7 days before the End_Date__c field", "An Asynchronous Path with a 7-day delay", "A before-save flow with a Wait element for 7 days"], "correct": [1, 2], "explanations": ["A Fast Field Update only changes fields at save time and cannot create a Task or act seven days later.", "Correct. Scheduled path actions run later, so the flow should evaluate the current Status at execution time with a Decision element before creating the Task.", "Correct. In an after-save flow, a Scheduled Path can run at a time offset relative to a date field on the record, such as 7 days before.", "An Asynchronous Path runs right after the original transaction and does not support a configurable time delay.", "Before-save flows do not support Wait elements or scheduled actions, so this cannot work."], "takeaway": "Use a Scheduled Path in an after-save record-triggered flow for actions at an offset from a date, and re-check conditions in the path when the data may change."}]}, {"id": "adm_flow_best", "title": "Bonnes pratiques et migration vers Flow", "fiche": {"one_liner": "Build Flows that avoid Get Records and DML elements inside loops, handle errors with fault paths, are debugged before activation, and migrate legacy Workflow Rules and Process Builder with the Migrate to Flow tool.", "need": "At Northwind Insurance, a Process Builder process updates related claims whenever a policy changes. It is slow and hits limits during bulk imports of 5,000 policies. The admin must rebuild it as a Flow that handles bulk data, reports failures to the team, and is tested before going live, because Salesforce is retiring Workflow Rules and Process Builder.", "core": "The most important best practice is to never put a Get Records, Create Records, Update Records or Delete Records element inside a Loop. Each pass through the loop would run a separate query or DML statement, and governor limits (such as the number of SOQL queries and DML statements per transaction) are quickly exceeded in bulk. The correct pattern is: query once with Get Records before the loop, use the Loop to modify record variables and Assign them to a collection variable, then run a single Update Records after the loop using that collection.\n\nFault paths (chemins d'erreur) handle failures. On elements that can fail (Get Records, Create/Update/Delete Records, Action, Subflow), add a Fault connector. Without it, an unhandled error stops the flow and the user sees a generic error or the admin receives an email. A fault path can show a message on a screen, send an email, or create a log record, and the flow can use the {!$Flow.FaultMessage} global variable to capture the error text.\n\nFor testing, use Debug in Flow Builder (you can run it with rollback mode so changes are not saved, and you can test record-triggered flows with a chosen record). Flow Trigger Explorer shows all the flows on an object. Each flow has versions: only one version can be active at a time, you can edit only an inactive version by saving it as a new version, and you can roll back by activating an older version. A new flow is saved as Draft until activated.\n\nWorkflow Rules and Process Builder have reached end of support, so no new ones should be built. The Migrate to Flow tool (Setup) converts Workflow Rules and Process Builder processes into Flows, but not every element is supported, so check the results, test in a sandbox and then deactivate the old automation after activating the new flow.", "setup": ["Setup > Process Automation > Flows > New Flow (build and save the flow in Flow Builder)", "Flow Builder > Debug button (test the flow, optionally with Rollback mode)", "Setup > Process Automation > Flows > flow name > View Details and Versions (activate, deactivate or roll back versions)", "Setup > Process Automation > Migrate to Flow (select a Workflow Rule or process and convert it)", "Setup > Process Automation > Flow Trigger Explorer (see the flows on an object and their order)"], "compare": [{"a": "Fault path (chemin d'erreur)", "b": "Debug", "diff": "A fault path handles errors while the flow runs in production. Debug tests the flow before activation in Flow Builder."}, {"a": "Migrate to Flow tool", "b": "Rebuilding the automation by hand", "diff": "The tool converts supported Workflow Rules and Process Builder processes automatically, but unsupported elements need manual rebuilding in Flow."}], "signals": [{"if": "Flow hits governor limits with bulk data, or a loop contains a query or update", "then": "Move Get Records and DML outside the loop and use a collection with one Update Records"}, {"if": "Flow must notify someone or avoid an unhandled error when a DML element fails", "then": "Add a fault path to the element"}, {"if": "Need to convert existing Workflow Rule or Process Builder automation", "then": "Use the Migrate to Flow tool"}, {"if": "Need to test a flow without saving data changes", "then": "Debug in Flow Builder with Rollback mode"}], "traps": ["Putting Update Records inside a Loop looks logical, but it causes a DML statement for each iteration and fails in bulk.", "Choosing Process Builder or a Workflow Rule for a new requirement is wrong: Flow is the tool to build new automation.", "Several versions of a flow can exist, but only one can be active at a time.", "Migrate to Flow does not convert everything automatically, and the old automation must still be deactivated after the flow is activated."], "scenario": {"situation": "A record-triggered flow updates the Status of all open Cases when an Account becomes inactive. It works for one Account but fails when a data load updates 200 Accounts, with a governor limit error. The flow has a Loop with Update Records inside it.", "answer": "Rebuild the flow so Update Records is outside the loop. Inside the Loop, use an Assignment to set the Status on each Case and add it to a collection variable. After the loop, run one Update Records on the collection. This uses a single DML statement and respects governor limits. Also add a fault path."}, "cards": [{"q": "Why should you not place Get Records or Update Records inside a Loop?", "a": "Each iteration runs a separate query or DML statement, so governor limits are quickly exceeded in bulk. Use a collection variable and one DML element after the loop."}, {"q": "What does a fault path do?", "a": "It is a Fault connector on an element that can fail. It runs when the element errors, so you can show a message, send an email or log the error, instead of an unhandled failure."}, {"q": "How many versions of a flow can be active at once?", "a": "Only one. To roll back, activate an earlier version."}, {"q": "What tool converts Workflow Rules and Process Builder to Flow?", "a": "The Migrate to Flow tool in Setup. Review and test the result, then deactivate the old automation."}, {"q": "Which Flow Builder feature tests a flow before activation?", "a": "Debug, which can run in Rollback mode so changes to data are not saved."}]}, "questions": [{"id": "f842250e2bb344acbaadf4a637047466", "stem": "Northwind Outfitters has a record-triggered flow that runs when an Opportunity is closed won. The flow gets all open Tasks related to the Opportunity, then uses a Loop element. Inside the loop, an Update Records element updates each Task. The flow fails with a governor limit error when an Opportunity has many Tasks. What should the administrator do to follow best practices?", "options": ["Add the updated Tasks to a collection variable inside the loop, then place one Update Records element after the loop that uses the collection", "Change the flow to run as a scheduled-triggered flow so that governor limits no longer apply", "Move the Update Records element into a second loop nested inside the first one", "Replace the Loop element with a Decision element that checks each Task individually"], "correct": [0], "explanations": ["Correct. Using an Assignment element inside the loop to build a collection and then performing a single Update Records after the loop keeps the DML outside the loop and avoids governor limits.", "Scheduled-triggered flows are still subject to governor limits, and a data element inside a loop remains a problem.", "Nesting loops multiplies the number of data operations and makes the limit problem worse.", "A Decision element cannot iterate over a collection, so it does not remove the per-record DML problem."], "takeaway": "Never put Get, Create, Update, or Delete Records elements inside a loop; collect records in a collection variable and run one data operation after the loop."}, {"id": "c0a4d51c06184268a7530cdab9747ba6", "stem": "Contoso Health uses a screen flow that creates a Case with a Create Records element. Sometimes the creation fails because of a validation rule, and users see a generic error message with technical details. The administrator wants users to see a friendly message and the flow to continue to a screen that offers to retry. What should the administrator configure?", "options": ["Enable Debug mode with the Rollback Mode option on the Create Records element", "Add a Decision element before the Create Records element that tests every validation rule", "Connect the fault path of the Create Records element to a Screen element that displays a custom message", "Deactivate the validation rule while the flow runs by using a custom setting"], "correct": [2], "explanations": ["Debug mode is a testing tool and does not change what end users see at runtime.", "Duplicating every validation rule in a Decision is hard to maintain and does not handle other kinds of failures.", "Correct. The fault path runs when the element fails, and it can show a Screen with a custom message, optionally using {!$Flow.FaultMessage}, and offer a retry.", "Bypassing validation rules this way weakens data quality and is not a flow error handling practice."], "takeaway": "Use fault paths on data elements to handle failures gracefully instead of letting users see an unhandled error."}, {"id": "58a8f2c3818c404b92cc9abe6017e24c", "stem": "Fabrikam Logistics has a record-triggered flow that updates Account fields. An administrator edited it and saved changes as a new version, but records are still being updated by the earlier logic. The administrator confirms the new version was saved without errors. What explains this behavior and what is the fix?", "options": ["The administrator must delete the older version before the new version can be saved", "Only the active version of a flow runs, so the administrator must activate the new version", "Flows can only have one version, so the administrator must clone the flow", "New versions run automatically after 24 hours, so the administrator must wait"], "correct": [1], "explanations": ["Older versions do not need to be deleted for the new version to be saved, and an active version cannot be deleted anyway.", "Correct. Saving creates a new version but it is not active by default, and only one version can be active at a time. Activating the new version makes it run.", "Flows support multiple versions, so cloning is unnecessary.", "There is no delay; versions run only when activated."], "takeaway": "Saving a flow creates a new version, but only the activated version runs, and only one version can be active at a time."}, {"id": "d86bebaca15c415eafb647a7b854326b", "stem": "Adventure Works still has several active Process Builder processes and workflow rules. The administrator wants to move them to Flow with the least manual effort. Which two actions are appropriate? (Choose 2)", "options": ["Keep both the original process and the new flow active at the same time to be safe", "Use the Migrate to Flow tool in Setup to convert supported workflow rules and processes into flows", "Test the converted flow in a sandbox before activating it and deactivating the original automation", "Open each process in Process Builder and click Convert to Apex Trigger", "Export the processes as metadata and import them into Flow Builder directly"], "correct": [1, 2], "explanations": ["Running both would cause duplicate updates, emails, or records, so it is not a safe practice.", "Correct. The Migrate to Flow tool converts supported workflow rules and Process Builder processes into flows.", "Correct. Testing in a sandbox and then deactivating the original automation when activating the flow validates behavior and ensures the logic is not duplicated.", "There is no such conversion to Apex triggers in Process Builder.", "Metadata cannot be imported into Flow Builder as a conversion method; the migration tool is the supported path."], "takeaway": "Use the Migrate to Flow tool, test the result, and make sure the old automation is deactivated when the new flow is activated."}, {"id": "953dda55433c4d8e836bbcadb6f9162c", "stem": "Tailspin Retail has a record-triggered flow on Lead that is not producing the expected result. The administrator wants to find out which path the flow takes and what values the variables hold, without affecting production data more than necessary. Which two approaches help? (Choose 2)", "options": ["Convert the flow to a Process Builder process because it offers better debugging", "Use the Debug option in Flow Builder to run the flow and review the details of each element executed", "Delete the flow and rebuild it from scratch to eliminate hidden errors", "Review the flow interview details in the debug panel to check variable values and decision outcomes", "Add a Roll Back Mode check in the Lead page layout to simulate the flow"], "correct": [1, 3], "explanations": ["Process Builder is being retired and is not a better debugging tool.", "Correct. Debug in Flow Builder runs the flow and shows each element executed, which helps trace the path.", "Rebuilding does not identify the cause and wastes effort.", "Correct. The debug details show variable values and decision outcomes so the administrator can find where logic diverges.", "Rollback behavior is a Debug option in Flow Builder, not a page layout setting."], "takeaway": "Use Flow Builder's Debug feature and its element-by-element details to troubleshoot flow paths and variable values."}]}, {"id": "adm_approvals", "title": "Processus d'approbation", "fiche": {"one_liner": "An approval process (processus d'approbation) routes a record through defined steps where designated approvers accept or reject it, with automatic actions and record locking along the way.", "need": "Some business decisions need a human sign-off before they go ahead. Example: at a manufacturing company, any Opportunity with a discount above 20% must be approved by the regional sales director, then by the finance director. An approval process enforces this path, locks the record during review, and updates fields or sends emails automatically at each outcome.", "core": "An approval process is built on an object and has four main building blocks. Entry criteria decide which records can be submitted (for example Discount % greater than 20). Approvers define who reviews: specific users, a queue, a related user field, or the submitter's manager from the user hierarchy. Steps define the order of approval. Each step can have its own criteria, so some records skip a step or are auto-approved or auto-rejected. A step can be assigned to one approver, or to several approvers where you choose between requiring unanimous approval or accepting the first response.\n\nActions fire at specific moments. Initial submission actions run when the record is submitted. Final approval actions run when all steps are approved. Final rejection actions run when a step is rejected. Recall actions run when the submitter withdraws the request. There are also step-level actions for approval and rejection. Available action types are: Field Update, Email Alert, Task, and Outbound Message. To start another automated process, you can use Flow instead.\n\nRecord locking: once submitted, the record is locked so that regular users cannot edit it. An administrator can always edit it, and approvers can edit it only if the process is configured to allow it. The record is unlocked after final approval or rejection, or after a recall, unless a final action locks it again. A submitter can recall a pending request (Recall Approval Request), provided that recall is permitted, which unlocks the record and fires the recall actions.", "setup": ["Setup > Process Automation > Approval Processes, then choose the object and click Create New Approval Process", "Select Jump Start Wizard (quick, for simple cases) or Standard Setup Wizard (full control over steps and criteria)", "Define entry criteria, approver field and record editability, then add approval steps", "Add initial submission, final approval, final rejection and recall actions, then activate the process", "Add the Approval History related list to the page layout so users can see the status and submit requests"], "compare": [{"a": "Approval process", "b": "Flow (Record-Triggered Flow)", "diff": "An approval process involves human approvers who decide yes or no, with locking and an approval history. A flow automates actions without a human decision. Flows can also launch approval processes or be launched by their actions."}, {"a": "Approval process", "b": "Validation rule", "diff": "A validation rule blocks a save when data is invalid, with no human decision. An approval process lets someone authorize a record that meets certain conditions."}], "signals": [{"if": "Manager must approve before the record proceeds", "then": "Approval process with the manager as approver"}, {"if": "Record must not be edited while under review", "then": "Approval process (record locking)"}, {"if": "Submitter wants to withdraw a pending request", "then": "Recall Approval Request (recall action)"}, {"if": "Approval needed in sequence, e.g. manager then finance", "then": "Multiple approval steps"}], "traps": ["The record is locked after submission, but administrators can still edit it, and approvers can edit it only if the process allows it.", "Entry criteria determine whether a record can be submitted, not who approves it. Approvers are set per step.", "Final rejection does not delete the record. It unlocks it by default, and the final rejection actions run.", "Recall is a separate action from rejection: it is initiated by the submitter, not the approver."], "scenario": {"situation": "At a services firm, any Quote above $50,000 must be approved by the sales manager, and if above $100,000 also by the CFO. The Quote must not be editable during review, and the status must change to Approved or Rejected automatically.", "answer": "Create an approval process on Quote with entry criteria (amount above $50,000). Step 1 goes to the manager. Step 2 goes to the CFO, with criteria (amount above $100,000). Add Field Update actions for final approval and final rejection to set the status. Locking is automatic upon submission."}, "cards": [{"q": "What decides which records can enter an approval process?", "a": "The entry criteria, set when defining the process. Only records that meet them can be submitted."}, {"q": "Who can edit a locked record during approval?", "a": "Administrators always can. Approvers can too, if the process is configured to allow it. Other users cannot."}, {"q": "What four types of actions can an approval process run?", "a": "Field Update, Email Alert, Task, and Outbound Message."}, {"q": "When do final approval actions run?", "a": "After all steps are approved, and the record is considered fully approved."}, {"q": "What does Recall Approval Request do?", "a": "It lets the submitter (or an administrator) withdraw a pending request, unlocking the record and running the recall actions."}]}, "questions": [{"id": "b807f23c8133444380cdd17ba75e36b7", "stem": "Northwind Logistics wants discount requests on Opportunities to be approved by the sales manager. Only Opportunities with a Discount Percent greater than 15% should be submitted for approval. Reps must not be able to submit Opportunities with lower discounts. What should the administrator configure in the approval process?", "options": ["A validation rule that blocks saving when Discount Percent is 15 or lower", "Entry criteria on the approval process set to Discount Percent greater than 15", "Step criteria on the first approval step set to Discount Percent greater than 15", "An initial submission action that checks the discount and rejects the record if it is too low"], "correct": [1], "explanations": ["Incorrect. A validation rule would block saving the record altogether, not just prevent submission for approval, which would disrupt normal edits.", "Correct. Entry criteria determine which records are eligible to be submitted, so records that do not meet them cannot enter the process.", "Incorrect. Step criteria decide which records go through a step, not whether the record can be submitted. Records would still be submitted and then handled by the step's no-match option.", "Incorrect. Initial submission actions run after a record is already submitted and cannot prevent submission of ineligible records."], "takeaway": "Use entry criteria to control which records are eligible to be submitted to an approval process."}, {"id": "e3bad357493a42cdb065dce5563669f5", "stem": "Contoso Energy has a Contract approval process. When a sales rep submits a Contract, the record must be locked, and the Legal team must be notified immediately by email. The administrator is configuring the process. Where should the email alert be added?", "options": ["Final Rejection Actions", "Recall Actions", "Initial Submission Actions", "Final Approval Actions"], "correct": [2], "explanations": ["Incorrect. Final rejection actions run only when the request is finally rejected, not at submission.", "Incorrect. Recall actions run only when the submitter recalls the request, which is not the event described.", "Correct. Initial submission actions run as soon as the record is submitted, so the email alert to Legal is sent at that moment.", "Incorrect. Final approval actions run only after all approval steps are approved, not at submission."], "takeaway": "Actions that must happen when a record enters the process belong in Initial Submission Actions, and the record is locked automatically at this point."}, {"id": "adafdf6bea0141069834ed3fb0bcb200", "stem": "Fabrikam Services has an approval process on Expense Report records. After a manager rejects a report, the submitter must be able to correct it and resubmit. The record must also be marked Rejected in a Status field. Which configuration meets the requirement?", "options": ["Configure Recall Actions to set Status to Rejected", "Add a Field Update to Final Rejection Actions setting Status to Rejected, and let the record unlock on rejection", "Add a Field Update to Initial Submission Actions setting Status to Rejected", "Add a Field Update to Final Approval Actions setting Status to Rejected"], "correct": [1], "explanations": ["Incorrect. Recall actions run only when the submitter pulls the request back, not when an approver rejects it.", "Correct. Final rejection actions run when the request is finally rejected, and the default behavior of the process is to unlock the record after rejection so the submitter can edit and resubmit.", "Incorrect. Initial submission actions run when the request is submitted, so the status would be set to Rejected before any decision was made.", "Incorrect. Final approval actions run only if the request is approved, so rejected records would never be updated."], "takeaway": "Use Final Rejection Actions for what must happen after a final rejection, such as updating a status field."}, {"id": "f5b7cea676784c7089bf19e0d1dba85f", "stem": "Adventure Works requires Purchase Order approval. Orders under $10,000 need approval from the department manager only. Orders of $10,000 or more need approval from the department manager and then from the Finance Director. The administrator wants a single approval process. Which two configurations should the administrator use?", "options": ["Create two separate approval processes, one for each amount range, both with the same entry criteria", "Create step 1 with no criteria, assigning the department manager as approver", "Use a validation rule to require a Finance Director approval field on large orders", "Create step 2 with no criteria and set the Finance Director to approve all orders", "Create step 2 with step criteria of Amount greater than or equal to 10000, assigning the Finance Director"], "correct": [1, 4], "explanations": ["Incorrect. Two processes with identical entry criteria are not needed, and when several processes are eligible for a record, only one is used, so this would not route orders by amount.", "Correct. A first step without criteria applies to every record that enters the process, so all orders go to the department manager.", "Incorrect. A validation rule does not create an approval request or route to an approver.", "Incorrect. A step with no criteria would send every order to the Finance Director, including those under $10,000.", "Correct. Step criteria on step 2 route only orders of $10,000 or more to the Finance Director, and the step can approve the record automatically when the criteria are not met if configured that way."], "takeaway": "Use step criteria to route records through different steps within a single approval process."}, {"id": "85d8b20178cb4804a4425c6fbd5d936c", "stem": "Tailspin Corp. has an approval process on Quote records. A sales rep submitted a quote by mistake and wants to pull it back before the manager acts so it can be edited. The rep is not an administrator. What allows the rep to do this?", "options": ["The rep asks the approver to reassign the request to the rep", "The rep recalls the approval request, which is allowed when the process is set to allow submitters to recall requests", "The rep edits the locked record because owners can always edit locked records", "The rep deletes the approval request from the Approval History related list"], "correct": [1], "explanations": ["Incorrect. Reassigning changes the approver and does not withdraw the request or unlock the record.", "Correct. When Allow submitters to recall approval requests is selected, the submitter can recall a pending request, which triggers the recall actions and unlocks the record.", "Incorrect. Records locked by an approval process cannot be edited by the owner. Only administrators and, if configured, approvers can edit them.", "Incorrect. Users cannot delete entries from Approval History to withdraw a request, and that list is not used for recall."], "takeaway": "Enable the option allowing submitters to recall requests so pending requests can be withdrawn and the record unlocked."}]}, {"id": "adm_formulas", "title": "Formules, cumuls et validations", "fiche": {"one_liner": "Formula fields calculate read-only values on the fly, roll-up summary fields aggregate child records on a master-detail parent, and validation rules block bad data on save.", "need": "A sales operations team at a manufacturer wants the Opportunity to show the account's industry, the Account to display the total value of all its won deals, and reps to be unable to close a deal as Closed Lost without giving a reason. Each need maps to a declarative tool: a cross-object formula, a roll-up summary, and a validation rule.", "core": "A formula field is read-only and computed each time the record is viewed, so it is always current and uses no automation. It can reference fields on the record and, through a lookup or master-detail relationship, fields on related parent records (cross-object formula, using dot notation such as Account.Industry). It cannot pull data from child records. Choose a formula over a flow when the value is a pure calculation from the record and its parents and nothing needs to be stored or written elsewhere.\n\nA roll-up summary field (champ de cumul) lives on the master object of a master-detail relationship and summarizes its detail records. The available types are COUNT, SUM, MIN and MAX. SUM, MIN and MAX require a number, currency, or date field on the detail object. You can add a filter so only some detail records count, for example Stage = Closed Won. It does not exist on a lookup relationship. If the relationship is a lookup, you need a flow, Apex, or conversion to master-detail.\n\nA validation rule evaluates a formula that returns TRUE when the data is invalid, then shows an error message and blocks the save. Useful functions: ISPICKVAL(field, \"value\") is the only way to test a picklist (never use = on a picklist). ISBLANK(field) tests for empty values. ISCHANGED(field) is TRUE when the field was edited in this save. PRIORVALUE(field) returns the value before the edit. ISCHANGED and PRIORVALUE only make sense when a record is edited, so they are used in validation rules, not in formula fields.", "setup": ["Object Manager > [Object] > Fields & Relationships > New > choose Formula, pick the return data type, then write the expression in Simple or Advanced Formula tab and Check Syntax", "Object Manager > [Master object] > Fields & Relationships > New > Roll-Up Summary (available only if the object is the master in a master-detail relationship), then choose the detail object, the summary type, the field to aggregate and optional filter criteria", "Object Manager > [Object] > Validation Rules > New: write the error condition formula, set the Error Message and choose to display it at the top of the page or next to a field", "Setup > Flows (or Flow Builder): only when the need goes beyond calculation, such as updating other records, creating records, or sending notifications"], "compare": [{"a": "Formula field", "b": "Flow (record-triggered)", "diff": "A formula displays a calculated value that is never stored as editable data and is always current. A flow is needed to write values to other records, create records, or run logic that a formula cannot express."}, {"a": "Roll-up summary field", "b": "Cross-object formula", "diff": "A roll-up summary goes from parent down to many children (master-detail only). A cross-object formula goes from child up to a parent record and cannot aggregate children."}, {"a": "Validation rule", "b": "Formula field", "diff": "A validation rule returns TRUE when the data is bad and blocks the save. A formula field returns a value to display, with no blocking."}], "signals": [{"if": "Show a parent field on the child record, read-only, always up to date", "then": "Cross-object formula field"}, {"if": "Total, count, minimum or maximum of child records on the parent, master-detail", "then": "Roll-up summary field"}, {"if": "Prevent saving unless a condition is met, or require a field only in some cases", "then": "Validation rule"}, {"if": "Detect that a field was modified or compare with its old value on save", "then": "ISCHANGED or PRIORVALUE in a validation rule"}], "traps": ["Roll-up summary fields cannot be created on a lookup relationship, only on the master side of a master-detail relationship, and AVG is not an available type.", "In a formula, testing a picklist with = fails: you must use ISPICKVAL(Field, \"Value\").", "A validation rule formula must evaluate to TRUE for the error to fire, so it describes the invalid state, not the valid one.", "A formula field cannot reference child records, and it is not editable, so it cannot be used to let users enter data."], "scenario": {"situation": "A company tracks Projects (parent) and Timesheet entries (child) in a master-detail relationship. Managers want each Project to show total hours logged and the date of the latest entry, without any code or flow.", "answer": "Create two roll-up summary fields on Project: a SUM of the Hours field on Timesheet, and a MAX of the Entry Date field. Master-detail makes roll-ups available, they update automatically, and no flow is needed. A formula could not aggregate child records."}, "cards": [{"q": "Which relationship type is required to create a roll-up summary field?", "a": "Master-detail. The roll-up is created on the master (parent) object and summarizes its detail records."}, {"q": "What are the roll-up summary types?", "a": "COUNT, SUM, MIN and MAX. SUM, MIN and MAX need a numeric, currency, or date field on the detail object."}, {"q": "How do you test the value of a picklist in a formula?", "a": "With ISPICKVAL(Field, \"Value\"). A direct comparison with = is not allowed on a picklist."}, {"q": "What does ISCHANGED(Field) return and where is it useful?", "a": "TRUE if the field value was changed in the current save. It is used in validation rules, for example to block changing a Stage once it is Closed Won."}, {"q": "When is a formula field better than a flow?", "a": "When you only need a calculated, read-only value from the record or its parent records. It is always current, needs no automation, and avoids storing duplicate data."}]}, "questions": [{"id": "b58ba9e9d6574f71989638a5be723968", "stem": "Northwind Logistics tracks shipments as child records of an Account through a lookup relationship. The sales director wants a field on the Account showing the total Weight (a number field) of all related Shipment records. The administrator must deliver this without code. What should the administrator do?", "options": ["Create a formula field on Account that references Shipment.Weight__c across the lookup", "Create a roll-up summary field on Account directly, since lookup relationships support SUM", "Change the Shipment relationship to master-detail, then create a roll-up summary field on Account using SUM", "Create a validation rule on Shipment that compares Weight to the Account total"], "correct": [2], "explanations": ["Wrong. Formula fields can reference parent fields through relationships, but they cannot aggregate values from child records.", "Wrong. Roll-up summary fields cannot be created on the parent of a lookup relationship, only of a master-detail relationship.", "Correct. Roll-up summary fields require a master-detail relationship, and SUM is supported on number fields of the detail object.", "Wrong. A validation rule only blocks saves based on a condition and does not calculate or display a total on the Account."], "takeaway": "Roll-up summary fields (COUNT, SUM, MIN, MAX) exist only on the master side of a master-detail relationship."}, {"id": "689eb2f1c4234a1db5368278cc8c8b30", "stem": "Bluepeak Insurance has an Opportunity picklist field called Stage. Managers require that when an Opportunity is saved with the Stage value 'Closed Lost', the Loss Reason text field must not be empty. The administrator will build a validation rule. Which error condition formula is correct?", "options": ["OR(ISPICKVAL(StageName, 'Closed Lost'), ISBLANK(Loss_Reason__c))", "AND(ISPICKVAL(StageName, 'Closed Lost'), Loss_Reason__c = NULL)", "AND(Stage = 'Closed Lost', ISBLANK(Loss_Reason__c))", "AND(ISPICKVAL(StageName, 'Closed Lost'), ISBLANK(Loss_Reason__c))"], "correct": [3], "explanations": ["Wrong. OR would trigger the error whenever either condition is true, including every Closed Lost record with a reason filled in.", "Wrong. Comparing a text field to NULL is unreliable in formulas, and ISBLANK is the recommended function for this check.", "Wrong. Picklist fields cannot be compared with the equals operator in a formula, and Stage is not the API name of the field.", "Correct. ISPICKVAL evaluates the picklist value and ISBLANK checks the text field, so the error shows only when both conditions are true."], "takeaway": "Use ISPICKVAL to test picklist values and ISBLANK to test for empty fields, combined with AND so the rule fires only when all conditions are met."}, {"id": "b89c9e8722c243558773103ea4806404", "stem": "Harbor Medical wants to prevent users from editing the Contract Amount field on a Contract Request record after its Status has become 'Approved', unless the user changes the Status at the same time. The administrator is writing a validation rule. Which function should be used to detect that the amount was modified?", "options": ["PRIORVALUE(Status__c)", "ISBLANK(Contract_Amount__c)", "ISCHANGED(Contract_Amount__c)", "ISNEW()"], "correct": [2], "explanations": ["Wrong. PRIORVALUE returns the earlier value of a field but does not by itself indicate whether Contract Amount was modified.", "Wrong. ISBLANK only tests whether the field is empty and says nothing about whether it was modified.", "Correct. ISCHANGED returns true when the field value differs from its saved value, which lets the rule target edits to Contract Amount.", "Wrong. ISNEW returns true only when the record is being created, so it cannot detect an edit of an existing record."], "takeaway": "ISCHANGED detects whether a field was modified on an existing record, while PRIORVALUE returns the value it had before the edit."}, {"id": "e673024171b74bffa0a70e21ae0708e6", "stem": "Lakeside Education stores a Student record with a lookup to a Campus record. The Campus object has a text field called Region__c. Advisors want Region to appear on the Student page layout and to update automatically whenever the Campus Region changes, with no automation to maintain. Which solutions meet the requirement? (Choose two options.)", "options": ["Create a formula field on Student returning text with the expression Campus__r.Region__c", "Create a validation rule on Student that displays the Campus Region", "Create a roll-up summary field on Campus that copies Region to each Student", "Create a formula field on Student that uses the cross-object reference to the Campus Region field", "Build a record-triggered flow on Campus that updates every related Student when Region changes"], "correct": [0, 3], "explanations": ["Correct. A cross-object formula using the relationship name returns the parent's Region and always reflects its current value.", "Wrong. A validation rule blocks saves with an error message and does not display a field value on the page layout.", "Wrong. Roll-up summary fields aggregate child data on a master-detail parent and cannot push a parent value down to children.", "Correct. This describes the same declarative approach: a cross-object formula field spanning the lookup, with no automation to maintain.", "Wrong. A flow could work but needs to be built and maintained, and the requirement asks for no automation when a formula is enough."], "takeaway": "When a child record only needs to display a parent field, a cross-object formula field is simpler and more maintainable than a flow."}, {"id": "59f9563e25a34d719d0a3e6e5c90c428", "stem": "Crestline Software wants a roll-up summary field on the Account. The Invoice object is the detail side of a master-detail relationship with Account, and has a Total__c currency field and an Invoice Date__c date field. The finance team needs to see the most recent invoice date and the highest invoice total for each Account. Which roll-up summary types should the administrator use? (Choose two options.)", "options": ["MAX on Invoice Date__c to show the latest invoice date", "MIN on Invoice Date__c to show the latest invoice date", "AVERAGE on Total__c to show the highest invoice total", "LAST on Invoice Date__c to show the latest invoice date", "MAX on Total__c to show the highest invoice total"], "correct": [0, 4], "explanations": ["Correct. MAX works on date fields and returns the most recent date among the detail records.", "Wrong. MIN on a date returns the earliest date, which is the opposite of what finance needs.", "Wrong. AVERAGE is not a roll-up summary type; the types are COUNT, SUM, MIN, and MAX.", "Wrong. LAST is not a roll-up summary type available in Salesforce.", "Correct. MAX on a currency field returns the largest value among the related Invoice records."], "takeaway": "Roll-up summary fields offer only COUNT, SUM, MIN, and MAX, and MIN or MAX also work on date fields."}]}]}, {"id": "agentforce", "label": "Agentforce", "weight": 8, "topics": [{"id": "adm_agent_use", "title": "Agentforce : à quoi ça sert", "fiche": {"one_liner": "Agentforce lets AI agents understand natural language and take actions on Salesforce data, while a flow remains the better choice when the rule is fixed and predictable.", "need": "A retailer's service team answers thousands of similar but differently worded questions: 'Where is my order?', 'Can I return these shoes?'. Reps lose time searching records and policies. Agentforce handles these varied requests in natural language, queries order data, and answers or hands over to a human. A fixed rule, such as 'assign every Gold case to Team A', stays in a flow.", "core": "Agentforce is Salesforce's platform for building AI agents that work with your CRM data. Two modes matter for the exam. An assistant (for example Einstein Copilot's successor, now an agent used by an employee) works alongside a user: the user asks, it drafts, summarizes, or suggests, and the user stays in control. An autonomous agent works on its own for a customer or a process: it receives a request, reasons about what to do, picks actions, and executes them without a human prompting each step, escalating to a person when needed.\n\nAn agent is built from topics (a category of job the agent can handle, with instructions and the scope of what it should do) and actions (what it can actually run: a flow, an Apex class, a prompt template, or a standard action). The Atlas Reasoning Engine interprets the request, chooses the topic and the actions. The Einstein Trust Layer protects data, for example by masking sensitive data and not retaining prompts with the LLM provider.\n\nTypical use cases. Service: an agent answers customers on a web chat, looks up order status, applies return policy from the knowledge base, creates or updates a case, and escalates to a human agent. Sales: an agent qualifies inbound leads, answers prospect questions, drafts outreach emails, summarizes an opportunity for a rep, and books meetings.\n\nAI versus flow. Choose AI when input is unstructured or varied (free text, many ways to ask the same thing) and the answer needs interpretation. Choose Flow when the logic is deterministic: same input, same output, auditable, such as 'if Amount > 50,000 then start approval'. The two combine: an agent action can call a flow, so the agent understands the request and the flow executes the exact business rule.", "setup": ["Setup > Agentforce Agents (create the agent from a template or from scratch in Agent Builder)", "Agent Builder: define the agent's role, add topics with instructions, then add actions to each topic (flow, Apex, prompt template)", "Setup > Einstein Setup: turn on Einstein, and Data Cloud where the use case requires it", "Assign permission sets (for example Agentforce-related permission sets) to the users and to the agent's running user, then test in the Builder preview and activate"], "compare": [{"a": "Autonomous agent", "b": "Assistant (employee-facing copilot)", "diff": "An assistant helps a user who stays in the loop and reviews the output. An autonomous agent acts on its own, often for customers, and carries out actions until it finishes or escalates."}, {"a": "Agentforce agent", "b": "Flow", "diff": "An agent interprets natural language and decides which action to take. A flow follows fixed logic you define in advance. Flow is predictable and auditable; the agent is flexible."}, {"a": "Topic", "b": "Action", "diff": "A topic defines the type of job and its instructions (the scope). An action is the concrete task the agent runs, such as a flow or Apex."}], "signals": [{"if": "Customers ask varied questions in free text, natural language", "then": "Agentforce agent with topics and actions"}, {"if": "Rule is exact, same result every time, needs to be auditable", "then": "Flow (record-triggered or other), not AI"}, {"if": "Rep wants help drafting emails or summarizing a record, and reviews it", "then": "Assistant (employee-facing agent) or prompt template"}, {"if": "Agent must perform a precise business step such as updating a record", "then": "Add a flow as an action of the agent"}], "traps": ["Choosing an agent for a deterministic rule (such as routing by a fixed field value): a flow is more reliable, cheaper, and predictable.", "Thinking an agent replaces flows: agents commonly call flows as actions, so they work together.", "Confusing a topic (scope and instructions) with an action (the task executed).", "Forgetting the Einstein Trust Layer when the scenario mentions data privacy, masking, or no data retention by the LLM provider."], "scenario": {"situation": "A telecom company receives many chat messages such as 'my bill is higher than usual' or 'I want to change my plan', phrased in many ways. Separately, any case flagged Priority = High must always go to the Tier 2 queue. The admin must handle both needs.", "answer": "Use an Agentforce service agent for the chat: topics for billing and plan changes, with actions that call flows to fetch data and update records, and escalation to a human. Use a flow for the Tier 2 routing, since the rule is fixed and deterministic and needs no language interpretation."}, "cards": [{"q": "What is the main difference between an autonomous agent and an assistant?", "a": "An assistant supports a user who stays in control. An autonomous agent acts on its own on a request, often for customers, and escalates to a human when needed."}, {"q": "What are the two building blocks of an Agentforce agent?", "a": "Topics (the jobs the agent handles, with instructions) and actions (what it executes: flow, Apex, prompt template)."}, {"q": "When is a flow better than an AI agent?", "a": "When the rule is deterministic: same input always gives the same output, and it must be predictable and auditable."}, {"q": "When is an AI agent better than a flow?", "a": "When requests arrive in natural language, vary widely, and need interpretation before action."}, {"q": "What does the Einstein Trust Layer do?", "a": "It protects data used with generative AI, for example by masking sensitive data and preventing the LLM provider from retaining prompts."}]}, "questions": [{"id": "b90b5d89bd564704ae78ba9ae888f24c", "stem": "Northwind Outfitters, an online retailer, receives thousands of chat messages each day about order status, return policies, and product compatibility. Customers phrase their requests in many different ways, and the support director wants an assistant that can understand them, answer from company knowledge, and take actions such as looking up an order, without a human starting each task. What should the administrator recommend?", "options": ["Configure Einstein Bots with a rigid menu of predefined dialog options for each request type", "Build an Agentforce Service Agent with topics and actions that use the company knowledge and order data", "Create an assignment rule that routes every chat to a queue of human support reps", "Create a screen flow with a fixed set of choice buttons for each possible customer question"], "correct": [1], "explanations": ["Incorrect. A rigid menu-based bot cannot interpret the many ways customers phrase requests and is the older rules-driven approach, not an autonomous agent.", "Correct. An Agentforce Service Agent interprets natural language, grounds answers in knowledge and data, and runs actions autonomously, which fits varied requests.", "Incorrect. Routing to human reps does not provide an assistant that answers and acts without a person starting each task.", "Incorrect. A screen flow with fixed choices forces customers into a menu and cannot handle widely varied free-text phrasing."], "takeaway": "Agentforce agents suit varied natural-language requests where the system must reason, answer from trusted data, and take actions on its own."}, {"id": "3d295aa76e9948dca6698472f022ca39", "stem": "Contoso Energy wants to give its sales reps help inside Salesforce. Reps would open a side panel, ask for a summary of an account, and request a draft follow-up email. A rep reviews and decides what to do with every response. Which description best fits this need?", "options": ["A scheduled flow that updates account fields every night without any user prompt", "An AI assistant that works alongside the rep on request, such as Agentforce for Sales or Einstein Copilot-style assistance in the app", "A validation rule that blocks reps from saving an account until a summary is entered", "An autonomous agent that works independently of any user and handles customer conversations on its own"], "correct": [1], "explanations": ["Incorrect. A scheduled flow runs on a timetable and does not respond to conversational requests from a rep.", "Correct. An assistant works with a user in the flow of work, responding to prompts, while the human reviews and decides.", "Incorrect. A validation rule enforces data quality and does not generate summaries or drafts.", "Incorrect. An autonomous agent acts without a user prompting each task, while here the rep drives every interaction."], "takeaway": "An assistant helps a user who is driving the work, whereas an autonomous agent handles tasks independently, often for customers."}, {"id": "a15c744ee8e3481b8f64221777e63520", "stem": "Fabrikam Insurance requires that any claim with a payout above $10,000 must be assigned to a senior adjuster and trigger an approval, with no exceptions and a full audit trail. An executive suggests using an Agentforce agent to decide this. What should the administrator do?", "options": ["Ask the agent to review each claim and decide whether to follow the rule", "Let the agent infer from claim notes whether a senior adjuster seems appropriate", "Use a prompt template so the model writes the assignment decision each time", "Use a record-triggered flow with a decision element on the payout amount and an approval process"], "correct": [3], "explanations": ["Incorrect. Letting an agent choose whether to follow a mandatory rule adds risk and removes the guarantee.", "Incorrect. Inference from notes is probabilistic, and the rule is exact and must never vary.", "Incorrect. A generated decision could vary between runs, which is unacceptable for a no-exceptions rule.", "Correct. A fixed threshold with mandatory outcomes is a deterministic rule, so a flow with a decision and an approval is reliable and auditable."], "takeaway": "When a business rule is exact and must run the same way every time, a flow is better than an AI agent."}, {"id": "49bcfc060a9d4ced8e7bbe7350e4e2d0", "stem": "Tailspin Software is evaluating where to use Agentforce in its Service Cloud and Sales Cloud orgs. Which two use cases are the best fit for an Agentforce agent rather than a flow?", "options": ["Answering customers' open-ended questions about troubleshooting by using knowledge articles", "Drafting a personalized outreach email for a lead using CRM data and recent activity", "Sending a fixed reminder email exactly three days after an opportunity is created", "Rounding a discount field to two decimal places on every quote line", "Setting the Status field to Closed when a case's resolution checkbox is selected"], "correct": [0, 1], "explanations": ["Correct. Open-ended questions vary widely and need language understanding and grounding in knowledge, which suits an agent.", "Correct. Generating personalized, context-aware text from CRM data is a strength of generative AI.", "Incorrect. A fixed-time reminder is a deterministic scheduled action best handled by a flow.", "Incorrect. Rounding is an exact calculation that a formula or flow handles reliably.", "Incorrect. A simple field update from a checkbox is deterministic and belongs in a flow."], "takeaway": "Use Agentforce for language understanding and content generation over varied inputs, and use flows or formulas for fixed, repeatable logic."}, {"id": "a2883c18cdfc4bd28fdf45235a6ec72f", "stem": "Adventure Works wants an Agentforce Service Agent to resolve customer requests such as checking a warranty and creating a return. The administrator must make sure the agent can perform these tasks reliably. Which two elements does the administrator configure for the agent?", "options": ["Validation rules on the agent user's profile that script each reply word for word", "A sharing rule that makes the agent the owner of all customer accounts", "Actions, such as a flow or Apex, that let the agent do the work like creating a return", "A page layout that displays the agent's conversation history to customers", "Topics that define the kinds of requests the agent handles, with instructions"], "correct": [2, 4], "explanations": ["Incorrect. Validation rules enforce data quality and do not script agent replies.", "Incorrect. Ownership of accounts is unrelated to giving the agent skills to resolve requests.", "Correct. Actions, which can run flows, Apex, or prompt templates, are how the agent performs tasks in Salesforce.", "Incorrect. A page layout controls record display for users and is not how an agent's capabilities are defined.", "Correct. Topics group the jobs the agent can handle and carry instructions that guide its behavior."], "takeaway": "An Agentforce agent is built from topics that scope its jobs and actions (often flows) that let it carry them out."}]}, {"id": "adm_agent_parts", "title": "Les composants d'un agent", "fiche": {"one_liner": "An Agentforce agent is built from a Role and description, Topics, Instructions and Actions (flows, Apex, prompt templates), authored in Agent Builder and exposed through deployment channels.", "need": "A company like a telecom operator wants an assistant that answers billing questions, checks order status and updates customer records without a human for every request. It needs a way to define what the agent does, what it must never do, which data and automations it can use, and where customers reach it (website, Slack, etc.).", "core": "An agent is defined by several building blocks. The Role and description tell the agent who it is and what it is for (for example, a service agent for a telecom operator). Topics (Sujets) are the categories of jobs the agent can handle, such as Order Management or Billing Questions. Each topic has a classification description (used by the reasoning engine to pick the right topic), a scope (what the topic covers and does not cover), and Instructions.\n\nInstructions are natural-language guidance within a topic: how to behave, what order to follow, when to use a given action, what to refuse. They guide the large language model (LLM), but they are not hard security controls. Actions are what the agent can actually do. An action can be backed by a Flow (Flux), Apex (invocable methods), a prompt template (Modèle d'invite, to generate text grounded in CRM data), or other supported targets such as MuleSoft or standard actions. Each action has a name and a description that the agent reads to decide when to call it. Actions are assigned to topics.\n\nAgent Builder is the workspace in Setup where you create the agent, define its topics, instructions and actions, test it in the conversation preview, and activate it. Deployment channels (canaux de déploiement) are where users meet the agent: for example Enhanced Chat (Messaging for In-App and Web) on a website, Slack, or Salesforce Lightning Experience as an employee-facing assistant. Building the agent and deploying it are separate steps.", "setup": ["Setup > Agentforce Agents > New Agent: choose the agent type and set its Role and description.", "Agent Builder: add Topics with classification description, scope and Instructions.", "Agent Builder > Topic > Actions: assign Flows, Apex or prompt templates, with clear action descriptions.", "Agent Builder: test in the conversation preview, then Activate.", "Agent Channels / Messaging Settings: connect the activated agent to a deployment channel such as Enhanced Chat or Slack."], "compare": [{"a": "Topic", "b": "Action", "diff": "A topic is a category of work with its scope and instructions. An action is a single executable task (flow, Apex, prompt template) assigned to a topic."}, {"a": "Instructions", "b": "Permissions or security settings", "diff": "Instructions guide the LLM's behavior in natural language but do not enforce access control. Data access still depends on the running user and sharing and permission settings."}], "signals": [{"if": "Agent must perform a business process or update records", "then": "Add an action based on a Flow (or Apex for complex logic)"}, {"if": "Agent must generate a personalized text grounded in CRM data", "then": "Action using a prompt template"}, {"if": "Agent picks the wrong job or goes out of scope", "then": "Refine topic classification description, scope and instructions"}, {"if": "Customers must reach the agent on the company website", "then": "Deployment channel: Enhanced Chat (Messaging for Web)"}], "traps": ["Confusing instructions with security: instructions guide behavior but do not replace permissions.", "Vague action or topic descriptions make the agent choose wrongly, because the LLM relies on those texts to reason.", "Assigning every action to one huge topic instead of splitting by job reduces accuracy.", "Forgetting that an agent must be activated and connected to a channel before customers can use it."], "scenario": {"situation": "A retailer wants its agent to check order status and cancel orders under 24 hours old, only after confirming with the customer. Currently the agent sometimes cancels older orders. Customers will use it on the company website.", "answer": "Create an Order Management topic with instructions stating the 24-hour rule and the confirmation step, plus a Flow-based cancel action that also enforces the rule in its logic. Test in Agent Builder, activate, then deploy through Enhanced Chat on the website. Hard rules belong in the flow, not only in instructions."}, "cards": [{"q": "What are the main components of an Agentforce agent?", "a": "Role and description, Topics (with classification description, scope and instructions), and Actions (flows, Apex, prompt templates), plus a deployment channel."}, {"q": "What is the purpose of a topic?", "a": "It groups a category of jobs the agent can handle and tells the reasoning engine when to use it, with its scope, instructions and assigned actions."}, {"q": "Which targets can back an agent action?", "a": "Flows, Apex, and prompt templates, among others such as MuleSoft or standard actions."}, {"q": "What is Agent Builder?", "a": "The Setup workspace where you configure topics, instructions and actions, test the agent in a preview, and activate it."}, {"q": "Do instructions guarantee that the agent follows a rule?", "a": "No. They guide the LLM but are not enforced. Critical rules should be enforced in the action logic, such as a flow, and in permissions."}]}, "questions": [{"id": "eea07a6968ea4deb98b493cc201ce663", "stem": "Northwind Outfitters is building an Agentforce Service Agent to handle order inquiries. The administrator wants the agent to handle only order status checks and return requests, and to politely decline anything else. Which agent component should the administrator define to group the actions and instructions for each of these job categories?", "options": ["Topics", "Channels", "Prompt Templates", "Permission Sets"], "correct": [0], "explanations": ["Correct. Topics define a category of jobs the agent can handle, with a classification description, scope, instructions, and the actions available for that job.", "Incorrect. Channels define where the agent is deployed (for example, Messaging or Slack), not which jobs it handles.", "Incorrect. Prompt Templates can be used by an action to generate content, but they do not organize the agent's jobs into categories.", "Incorrect. Permission sets control access to resources, but they do not define the agent's scope of work."], "takeaway": "Topics define the jobs an agent can do, each with its own scope, instructions, and actions."}, {"id": "fed1ec8873404fe89618581b004945fe", "stem": "Contoso Bank has an agent with a topic called Card Disputes. During testing, the agent sometimes answers questions about mortgage rates under this topic. The administrator wants to keep the topic's behavior focused on disputes only. What should the administrator do first?", "options": ["Refine the topic's classification description and scope so it clearly excludes mortgage questions", "Increase the number of actions assigned to the topic", "Add a Flow action that deletes mortgage-related cases", "Create a new channel for mortgage questions"], "correct": [0], "explanations": ["Correct. The classification description and scope guide the agent in choosing the topic and in what it handles within it, so clarifying them keeps the topic focused.", "Incorrect. Adding actions expands what the agent can do, and does not narrow its scope.", "Incorrect. Deleting records does not address the agent's topic selection and would be a harmful side effect.", "Incorrect. Channels control where the agent is available, not how it interprets requests or which topic applies."], "takeaway": "Tune a topic's classification description and scope to control which requests the agent routes to it."}, {"id": "d031655d5a574280935fa89ae81153e2", "stem": "Fabrikam Energy wants its agent to look up a customer's open invoices from Salesforce records and then have the agent apply a multi-step business process that updates several objects. The administrator prefers a low-code approach. Which two action types can the administrator add to the topic? (Select the correct options.)", "options": ["A Flow", "A workflow rule", "A validation rule", "An Apex class", "A Process Builder process"], "correct": [0, 3], "explanations": ["Correct. Autolaunched flows can be used as agent actions and are the low-code choice for multi-step logic and record updates.", "Incorrect. Workflow rules are retired and are not agent action types.", "Incorrect. Validation rules enforce data quality on save, and are not agent actions.", "Correct. Apex (invocable methods) can be used as an agent action when custom code is needed.", "Incorrect. Process Builder is retired and cannot be used as an agent action."], "takeaway": "Agent actions can run Flows, Apex, or Prompt Templates, among other types, and never retired automation tools."}, {"id": "9755bd7296a24a409e56021caeae3d3e", "stem": "Litware Retail has created an agent and wants to build it visually: create topics, assign actions, write instructions, and then test the conversation in a preview panel before activating it. Which tool should the administrator use?", "options": ["Flow Builder", "Lightning App Builder", "Agent Builder", "Experience Builder"], "correct": [2], "explanations": ["Incorrect. Flow Builder creates the flows used as actions, but it does not configure the agent or provide a conversation preview.", "Incorrect. Lightning App Builder designs Lightning pages and does not configure agents.", "Correct. Agent Builder is where you configure topics, instructions, and actions, and test the agent in a conversation preview.", "Incorrect. Experience Builder designs Experience Cloud sites, not agent logic."], "takeaway": "Use Agent Builder to define an agent's topics, instructions, and actions and to test it before activation."}, {"id": "8cabb93bd0af49c499d8d36504ba5880", "stem": "Adventure Works wants its Agentforce Service Agent to answer customers directly on its website chat widget and also assist through its Slack workspace for employees. The administrator is planning the deployment. Which two statements describe how to do this correctly? (Select the correct options.)", "options": ["Rebuild the agent's topics separately for each channel", "Use Omni-Channel flows or a messaging channel configuration to route conversations to the agent where needed", "Create a separate Salesforce org for each channel", "Connect the agent to the relevant channels, such as Enhanced Chat or Messaging, and Slack", "Deploy the agent only as a Lightning page component"], "correct": [1, 3], "explanations": ["Incorrect. Topics and actions are defined once on the agent and reused across channels, so rebuilding is unnecessary.", "Correct. Routing configuration, such as an Omni-Channel flow for messaging, directs incoming conversations to the agent.", "Incorrect. Channels are configured within one org, and multiple orgs are not needed.", "Correct. Channels determine where an agent is available, and one agent can be deployed to several channels.", "Incorrect. Agents are not limited to Lightning page components, and this would not cover the website chat or Slack."], "takeaway": "Build an agent once, then deploy it to multiple channels and use routing configuration to send conversations to it."}]}, {"id": "adm_agent_trust", "title": "Sécurité et confiance", "fiche": {"one_liner": "An Agentforce agent acts with its running user's permissions and sharing, and the Einstein Trust Layer masks sensitive data, prevents the model provider from retaining it, and detects toxicity.", "need": "A bank deploys an Agentforce service agent to answer questions about customer accounts. Leadership fears two things: the agent revealing records that a given person should never see, and confidential data (card numbers, names) leaking to an external AI model provider. Salesforce security controls and the Einstein Trust Layer address both concerns.", "core": "An agent does not have special powers. It runs as a user, and everything it can read or change is limited by that user's access: profile, permission sets (ensemble d'autorisations), object and field permissions, and record-level sharing (organization-wide defaults, role hierarchy, sharing rules). Depending on the agent type, the running user is either the person talking to it (an employee agent) or a dedicated user the agent is configured to run as (a service agent facing customers). In both cases, if that user cannot see a record or a field, the agent cannot use it in its answers or actions. The principle is least privilege: give the agent's user only what its topics and actions need.\n\nThe Einstein Trust Layer is the set of protections placed between Salesforce and the large language model (LLM). Before a prompt leaves Salesforce, secure data retrieval (which respects permissions) grounds it with CRM data, then data masking replaces sensitive values, such as names, emails, or card numbers, with placeholders. The prompt goes to the model provider under a zero data retention agreement: the provider does not store the prompt or the response, and does not use them to train its models. When the response returns, the placeholders are swapped back for the real values (demasking), and toxicity detection scores the output for harmful content. An audit trail records the interaction.\n\nKey point for the exam: the Trust Layer does not replace sharing and permissions. Permissions decide what data the agent may access. The Trust Layer protects that data once it is sent to the model and checks the response.", "setup": ["Setup > Agentforce Agents (Agent Builder): choose the agent and set the user it runs as (for a service agent, the 'Run as' user). Employee agents run as the person using them.", "Setup > Permission Sets: create a dedicated permission set with only the object and field access the agent's actions need, then assign it to the agent's user.", "Setup > Sharing Settings: check organization-wide defaults and sharing rules, since they determine which records the agent's user can see.", "Setup > Einstein Generative AI > Einstein Trust Layer (Einstein Setup): turn on Einstein, then configure data masking, which is the Trust Layer feature administrators can adjust, and review audit trail and feedback settings."], "compare": [{"a": "Permissions and sharing (access control)", "b": "Einstein Trust Layer", "diff": "Permissions and sharing control which data the agent's user can access. The Trust Layer protects the data sent to and returned by the LLM (masking, zero data retention, toxicity detection). One does not replace the other."}, {"a": "Data masking", "b": "Zero data retention", "diff": "Masking hides sensitive values in the prompt before it leaves Salesforce. Zero data retention is a contractual commitment: the model provider does not store the prompt or response and does not train on them."}], "signals": [{"if": "The agent must not show records or fields a user cannot normally see", "then": "Running user's permissions and sharing settings (profile, permission sets, field-level security, sharing rules)"}, {"if": "Sensitive data (PII, card numbers) must not be exposed to the external LLM", "then": "Einstein Trust Layer data masking"}, {"if": "The LLM provider must not store prompts or train on customer data", "then": "Zero data retention in the Einstein Trust Layer"}, {"if": "Filter offensive or harmful generated responses", "then": "Toxicity detection in the Einstein Trust Layer"}], "traps": ["Believing the agent can see all data because it is AI: it is limited by its running user's permissions and sharing.", "Thinking the Trust Layer replaces sharing rules and field-level security: it does not control record access.", "Confusing masking with encryption or deletion: masked values are replaced by placeholders, then restored in the response shown to the user.", "Giving the agent's user a System Administrator profile for convenience: this violates least privilege and widens what the agent can expose."], "scenario": {"situation": "A company launches a service agent that looks up orders. During testing, the agent mentions a discount field that sales reps can see but customers should not. The security team also asks that card numbers never reach the external model provider.", "answer": "Fix the field exposure by adjusting the agent's running user: remove field access from its permission set and check sharing, because the agent only inherits that user's access. For card numbers, rely on Einstein Trust Layer data masking, backed by zero data retention. Both layers are needed."}, "cards": [{"q": "Whose access limits what an Agentforce agent can read and do?", "a": "Its running user's access: profile, permission sets, field-level security, and record sharing. The agent has no extra privileges."}, {"q": "Name three protections of the Einstein Trust Layer.", "a": "Data masking of sensitive values, zero data retention by the model provider, and toxicity detection. It also provides an audit trail."}, {"q": "What happens to masked data when the LLM response comes back?", "a": "It is demasked: placeholders are replaced with the original values before the response is shown to the user."}, {"q": "Does zero data retention mean Salesforce stores nothing?", "a": "No. It means the external model provider does not store prompts or responses and does not train on them. Salesforce can still keep an audit trail."}, {"q": "Why give an agent's user a dedicated permission set?", "a": "To apply least privilege: only the objects and fields its actions require, limiting what the agent could expose."}]}, "questions": [{"id": "85caa0892a4b4092bb35106c85503ae2", "stem": "Northwind Insurance deploys an Agentforce Service Agent in an Experience Cloud site so customers can ask about their claims. The agent is configured to run as a specific running user. The administrator wants the agent to retrieve only the claim data that this agent's user is permitted to see. What should the administrator do?", "options": ["Create a validation rule on Claim that blocks the agent from reading records", "Assign the agent's user a profile and permission sets that grant access only to the objects and fields the agent needs", "Give the agent's user the Modify All Data permission so that actions never fail", "Enable Einstein Trust Layer data masking so that the agent cannot read claim records"], "correct": [1], "explanations": ["Incorrect. Validation rules fire on saving data, not on reading it, so they cannot restrict what the agent retrieves.", "Correct. The agent acts with the permissions of its assigned user, so least-privilege profiles and permission sets define what data and actions it can reach.", "Incorrect. Modify All Data bypasses sharing and field restrictions, which violates least privilege and exposes far more data than needed.", "Incorrect. Data masking hides sensitive values in prompts sent to the LLM. It is not an access control mechanism for records."], "takeaway": "An agent can only access the data and perform the actions that its running user's permissions allow, so apply least privilege to that user."}, {"id": "d4b36268de634af6aef9659b4c0f1dec", "stem": "At Contoso Retail, an employee-facing Agentforce agent answers questions about Opportunities. Opportunity organization-wide default is Private, and sales reps see only their own deals. A rep asks the agent to summarize the Q3 pipeline for the whole team. What will the agent do when it queries Opportunity data on behalf of this rep?", "options": ["Return only the Opportunities the rep can access under the sharing model", "Return all Opportunities but mask the Amount field with the Einstein Trust Layer", "Return no data, because agents cannot query records with a Private default", "Return all team Opportunities because the agent runs with system-level access to bypass sharing"], "correct": [0], "explanations": ["Correct. The agent respects the sharing model, so a rep with Private access sees only the records they own or that are shared with them.", "Incorrect. Masking protects sensitive data sent to the LLM. It does not grant access to records the user cannot see.", "Incorrect. Agents can query data with a Private default. They simply return only the records visible to the user.", "Incorrect. Agents do not bypass sharing when they act for a user. They respect the record access that user has."], "takeaway": "Agents honor object permissions, field-level security, and sharing, so users never see records through an agent that they could not see otherwise."}, {"id": "ff496ad27f144727b1482cde072340bf", "stem": "Fabrikam Health wants its sales team to use generative AI to draft emails that reference customer details such as names and phone numbers. The security officer worries that this personal data will be seen by the external LLM provider. Which Einstein Trust Layer capability addresses this concern directly?", "options": ["Audit trail, which records every prompt in the Setup Audit Trail", "Toxicity detection, which scores each response for harmful content", "Data masking, which replaces sensitive values with placeholders before the prompt is sent to the LLM", "Dynamic grounding, which adds CRM data to the prompt for more relevant answers"], "correct": [2], "explanations": ["Incorrect. Auditing logs AI interactions for review but does not hide data from the model provider.", "Incorrect. Toxicity detection evaluates content for harm, not the exposure of personal data.", "Correct. The Trust Layer masks sensitive data such as personal information with placeholders before sending the prompt, then restores the values in the response.", "Incorrect. Grounding actually adds CRM data to the prompt, which increases the data sent rather than protecting it."], "takeaway": "Data masking in the Einstein Trust Layer replaces sensitive values with placeholders before prompts reach the external LLM."}, {"id": "ceab8e31ab094ce7be83a983c400411c", "stem": "Litware Bank is evaluating Agentforce and its compliance team asks how the Einstein Trust Layer handles prompts and responses sent to a third-party model provider. Which two statements accurately describe the Trust Layer? (Choose two.)", "options": ["Masked data stays masked in the final response shown to the user", "Responses are scanned by toxicity detection, and the scores are stored in the audit trail", "The Trust Layer removes the need to set up permissions, since all data is masked automatically", "Customers must review every response manually, because the Trust Layer cannot evaluate content", "The model provider operates under a zero data retention agreement, so prompts and responses are not stored or used to train the model"], "correct": [1, 4], "explanations": ["Incorrect. Masked values are demasked after the response returns, so the user sees the real data in the final output.", "Correct. The Trust Layer detects toxic or harmful content in responses and records the scores in the audit trail.", "Incorrect. Masking does not replace permissions, sharing, or field-level security, which still control what data the agent can access.", "Incorrect. The Trust Layer evaluates content automatically with toxicity detection. Manual review is not required.", "Correct. Salesforce has zero data retention agreements with its LLM providers, so prompts and outputs are not retained or used for training."], "takeaway": "The Einstein Trust Layer combines zero data retention with the LLM provider, masking, toxicity detection, and auditing, but it does not replace Salesforce access controls."}, {"id": "4f3ce103094e4811a74b795cc557aa80", "stem": "Adventure Works launches an Agentforce agent that updates Case records for support staff. A security review finds that the agent's user has a profile with edit access to every Case field, including a confidential Legal Notes field. The administrator must make sure the agent cannot read or edit that field. Which two actions meet this requirement? (Choose two.)", "options": ["Remove read and edit access to Legal Notes in the field-level security for the agent user's profile and permission sets", "Turn on data masking in the Einstein Trust Layer for the Legal Notes field only", "Move the Legal Notes field to the Case page layout's hidden section", "Use a permission set for the agent user that grants only required field access, and remove the broader profile access", "Remove the Legal Notes field from the agent's topic instructions and rely on the agent not to use it"], "correct": [0, 3], "explanations": ["Correct. Field-level security determines what the agent's user can read or edit, and the agent respects it.", "Incorrect. Masking limits what the LLM sees in prompts, but it does not stop the agent's user from accessing or editing the field.", "Incorrect. Page layouts control UI display only and do not enforce security on data accessed through the agent.", "Correct. Granting only required access through permission sets, with a minimal base profile, enforces least privilege for the agent user.", "Incorrect. Instructions guide behavior but are not a security control. Access must be enforced through permissions."], "takeaway": "Control what an agent can reach with field-level security and least-privilege permission sets on its user, not with instructions or page layouts."}]}, {"id": "adm_agent_test", "title": "Tester et maintenir un agent", "fiche": {"one_liner": "Test an Agentforce agent in Conversation Preview, run it at scale in Testing Center, refine its prompts and instructions, then monitor real usage and quality with analytics.", "need": "A retailer deploys a service agent to handle order status questions. Before launch, the admin must confirm the agent picks the right topic and action. After launch, she must see whether customers get answers, spot failures, and fix instructions without breaking what already works. Testing and maintenance cover that full cycle.", "core": "Conversation Preview (Aperçu de conversation) is the built-in chat panel in Agentforce Builder. You type as a customer would and see the agent's replies. You can turn on tracing (the plan tracer) to see which topic was selected, which actions ran, the inputs and outputs, and the reasoning. It is the tool for debugging a single conversation while you build. It can run against real data or in simulated mode, where actions are mocked, so you can test without touching records.\n\nTesting Center (Centre de test) is for testing at scale. You create test cases, each with an utterance (what the user says) and expected results such as the expected topic, expected actions and expected response. You run the batch and get pass/fail results. It can also generate test cases for you. Use it for regression testing: after changing instructions, rerun the same suite to confirm nothing broke.\n\nPrompt Builder lets you create prompt templates (modèles d'invite). Types include Sales Email, Field Generation, Record Summary and Flex. You write the text and insert merge fields (champs de fusion) from records, Flows or Apex to ground the prompt in your data. You can preview the resolved prompt and the model's response before activating it. Templates can be reused by agents, flows and Apex.\n\nTo adjust instructions, edit topic descriptions, classification, topic instructions and action descriptions. The agent chooses topics and actions based on that text, so vague or overlapping wording causes wrong routing. Make small changes, retest in Preview, then rerun Testing Center.\n\nTo track usage, use Agentforce analytics (Analytics) and the Agentforce Studio dashboards. They show conversation volume, topic and action usage, and outcomes. Use them to find where the agent fails or escalates, then loop back to instructions and tests.", "setup": ["Setup > Agentforce Agents > open the agent in Agentforce Builder > Conversation Preview panel (turn on tracing to inspect topic and actions)", "Setup > Testing Center (or Agentforce Studio > Testing Center) > New Test > add utterances with expected topic, actions and response > Run", "Setup > Prompt Builder > New Prompt Template > choose type, write the prompt, insert merge fields > Preview > Activate", "Agentforce Builder > Topics > edit the topic description, instructions and action descriptions, then save and retest", "Agentforce Studio > Analytics (Agentforce analytics dashboards) to monitor conversations, topics and actions"], "compare": [{"a": "Conversation Preview", "b": "Testing Center", "diff": "Preview is manual, one conversation at a time, used to debug while building. Testing Center runs many saved test cases in batch with expected outcomes, used for regression and validation."}, {"a": "Prompt template (Prompt Builder)", "b": "Agent topic instructions", "diff": "A prompt template is a reusable, grounded prompt for a specific generation task. Topic instructions guide the agent's reasoning and behavior within a topic."}], "signals": [{"if": "See which topic and action the agent chose for one message, debug step by step", "then": "Conversation Preview with tracing"}, {"if": "Run hundreds of utterances and check expected topic, actions and responses, regression after changes", "then": "Testing Center"}, {"if": "Reusable prompt with merge fields from record data, preview before activating", "then": "Prompt Builder prompt template"}, {"if": "Measure conversation volume, topic usage and where the agent fails after launch", "then": "Agentforce analytics dashboards"}], "traps": ["Conversation Preview is not a scale or regression tool: for repeatable batch testing, choose Testing Center.", "Wrong topic selection is usually fixed by clearer topic descriptions and instructions, not by adding more actions.", "Preview in simulated mode does not run real actions, so a good result there does not prove real data access works.", "After editing instructions, you must retest: changes can break previously passing cases, so rerun the Testing Center suite."], "scenario": {"situation": "An admin updates the instructions of the Order Status topic so the agent also handles returns. She worries the agent may now misroute order status questions. She has 200 sample customer messages and wants a repeatable check with expected topics.", "answer": "Use Testing Center. Create test cases with the 200 utterances and the expected topic and actions, run them, and compare pass/fail results against the earlier run. Conversation Preview is only good for spot checks. After launch, use analytics to monitor real routing."}, "cards": [{"q": "What does Conversation Preview show when tracing is on?", "a": "The selected topic, actions run with inputs and outputs, and the agent's reasoning for that conversation."}, {"q": "What does a Testing Center test case contain?", "a": "An utterance plus expected results: the expected topic, expected actions and expected response."}, {"q": "Which prompt template types exist in Prompt Builder?", "a": "Sales Email, Field Generation, Record Summary and Flex, among others."}, {"q": "Why can an agent choose the wrong topic?", "a": "Topic descriptions or instructions are vague or overlap, since the agent routes using that text."}, {"q": "What is the maintenance loop for an agent?", "a": "Monitor analytics, adjust instructions, retest in Preview, rerun the Testing Center suite, then redeploy."}]}, "questions": [{"id": "977743c30a1948b2941fbf234d1c2cc7", "stem": "Northwind Outfitters is building an Agentforce Service Agent in Agentforce Builder. Before activating it, the administrator wants to type sample customer messages, watch how the agent reasons, and see which topic and actions it selects for each turn. What should the administrator use?", "options": ["Debug Logs in Setup with a trace flag on the agent user", "Flow Debug in Flow Builder", "Conversation Preview in Agentforce Builder", "Einstein Usage dashboard in Analytics"], "correct": [2], "explanations": ["Debug logs capture Apex and automation execution, but they do not give an interactive chat interface showing the agent's topic and action selection.", "Flow Debug tests a single flow, not the agent's overall reasoning across topics and actions.", "Correct. Conversation Preview lets you chat with the agent while building and shows the reasoning, the selected topic, and the actions invoked.", "Usage analytics show aggregated adoption and consumption trends after the agent is in use. They cannot test a single conversation interactively."], "takeaway": "Use Conversation Preview for interactive, turn-by-turn testing of an agent while you build it."}, {"id": "95181a3195e8414f8d75019cfc94dd8b", "stem": "Contoso Telecom has built an agent with 12 topics. The administrator needs to run a large set of realistic utterances against the agent at once, define the expected topic and action for each, and compare the results after every change to the instructions. Which tool fits this need?", "options": ["Prompt Builder template preview", "Testing Center in Agentforce Studio", "Sandbox with a Full copy refresh", "Conversation Preview, repeating each utterance manually"], "correct": [1], "explanations": ["Prompt Builder previews test a prompt template, not an agent's topic and action routing across many utterances.", "Correct. Testing Center runs batches of test cases with expected topics, actions, and responses, and reports results so you can rerun after changes.", "A Full sandbox provides an environment for testing changes safely but not a way to run and score batches of agent utterances.", "Conversation Preview is interactive and manual, so it does not scale to batch testing with expected outcomes."], "takeaway": "Use Testing Center for batch, repeatable agent tests with expected results, and Conversation Preview for ad hoc checks."}, {"id": "1315918e3489437d8d125aeb5e425353", "stem": "Fabrikam Bank uses a prompt template created in Prompt Builder to draft case summaries. The summaries sometimes include irrelevant details because the template merges in too many fields. The administrator wants to check the output with real case data before saving a new version. What should the administrator do?", "options": ["Create a Flow Trigger Explorer entry for the template", "Activate the template and review the first live summaries", "Use the preview in Prompt Builder, select a sample case record, and review the resolved prompt and response", "Run Testing Center against the Case object"], "correct": [2], "explanations": ["Flow Trigger Explorer lists flows on an object and has nothing to do with prompt templates.", "Activating first exposes users to poor output. Prompt Builder lets you check results before activation.", "Correct. Prompt Builder preview lets you pick a record, see the resolved prompt with merged data, and view the generated response, so you can refine the template.", "Testing Center tests agents, not object data or a prompt template's merge fields."], "takeaway": "Preview a prompt template in Prompt Builder with a real record to see the resolved prompt and response before activating."}, {"id": "a1301ef55aac4c1b9a6285b5554d4436", "stem": "Adventure Works has had an agent live for a month. Leadership asks the administrator to understand how the agent is being used and where it struggles, so the team can decide which instructions to improve. Which two approaches help? (Choose two.)", "options": ["Wait for users to file cases about the agent and tune only then", "Check Setup Audit Trail to see what customers asked the agent", "Examine session and conversation data from the agent's interactions to find topics where requests go unresolved", "Recreate the agent from scratch every month", "Review the Agentforce analytics dashboards for usage and conversation trends"], "correct": [2, 4], "explanations": ["A reactive approach ignores the available analytics and delays improvement.", "Setup Audit Trail logs configuration changes by admins, not customer questions.", "Correct. Reviewing interaction data reveals which topics or requests the agent fails to handle, which guides instruction changes.", "Rebuilding monthly discards tested configuration and is unnecessary. Iterative tuning is the recommended approach.", "Correct. Agentforce analytics provide usage metrics and trends that show adoption and performance over time."], "takeaway": "Use analytics and interaction data after launch to find weak topics, then tune instructions and retest in a continuous cycle."}, {"id": "730711a2c60f4a798b6e07c05b734758", "stem": "Litware Insurance has built an Agentforce Service Agent. During testing in Agentforce Builder, the administrator notices that the agent selects the wrong topic for questions about policy renewals and answers with a topic meant for billing. The administrator wants to see which topic was chosen and which actions were considered, step by step, for a specific test message. What should the administrator use?", "options": ["The Setup Audit Trail", "A Flow debug log in Flow Builder", "A report on the Agentforce Conversation object", "The plan tracer in the conversation preview panel"], "correct": [3], "explanations": ["Incorrect. Setup Audit Trail records configuration changes made by admins, not the runtime reasoning of an agent.", "Incorrect. A Flow debug run shows the execution of a flow, not the agent's topic selection and reasoning.", "Incorrect. A report on conversation records would not show step-by-step reasoning for a single test message.", "Correct. The plan tracer in Agentforce Builder shows how the agent reasoned, including the selected topic, instructions and actions, for a given preview turn."], "takeaway": "Use the plan tracer in the conversation preview to diagnose why an agent picked a topic or action."}, {"id": "f0c8bb9bd2494cd282b48dc584766b66", "stem": "Tailspin Toys is rolling out an Agentforce Service Agent. The administrator wants to run the same set of 200 test utterances every time a topic's instructions change, to confirm that earlier behavior still works as expected and no regressions appear. Which approach best meets this need?", "options": ["Review the Einstein Trust Layer audit trail weekly", "Ask users to report problems through Chatter", "Create a test suite in Testing Center and rerun it after each change", "Re-type each utterance manually in the conversation preview after every change"], "correct": [2], "explanations": ["Incorrect. The audit trail records trust layer data such as prompts and masking, and it does not run repeatable tests with expected outcomes.", "Incorrect. Chatter feedback is reactive and cannot systematically validate expected behavior before release.", "Correct. Testing Center lets the admin save a batch of test cases with expected topics, actions and responses, and rerun them after each change to catch regressions.", "Incorrect. Manual preview is useful for quick checks, but it is slow and inconsistent for 200 utterances."], "takeaway": "Testing Center is the tool for repeatable, batch regression testing of agent behavior against expected results."}, {"id": "2dd615a62df5456dbdce7f47f9ba69da", "stem": "Wingtip Retail uses a prompt template in Prompt Builder to generate product descriptions. The administrator edited the template and wants to check the output using real product records before making the new version available to the team. What should the administrator do?", "options": ["Export the template to a spreadsheet and review the text", "Activate the template and watch the first live results", "Use the Preview in Prompt Builder with a selected product record to review the resolved prompt and response", "Create a validation rule on the Product object"], "correct": [2], "explanations": ["Incorrect. A static export shows neither merged record data nor a generated response.", "Incorrect. Activating first exposes users to untested output, which is the opposite of testing before release.", "Correct. Preview lets the admin choose a record, see the resolved prompt with merged field values, and review the generated response before activating.", "Incorrect. A validation rule enforces data quality on records and does not test a prompt template's output."], "takeaway": "Preview a prompt template against a real record in Prompt Builder before activating a new version."}, {"id": "4a4f585335d94d998a92397aafa8c635", "stem": "Proseware Logistics has an Agentforce Service Agent live. Analysis shows that customers often ask about delivery windows, but the agent gives vague answers. The administrator wants to improve the responses. Which two actions should the administrator take?", "options": ["Delete the agent and rebuild it from scratch each time a problem appears", "Disable the Einstein Trust Layer so the agent can respond more freely", "Increase the number of users with the Agentforce license to improve accuracy", "Test the revised instructions in the conversation preview and then rerun a Testing Center suite before activating the update", "Make the topic instructions for delivery questions more specific, such as stating which data to cite and how to phrase the answer"], "correct": [3, 4], "explanations": ["Incorrect. Rebuilding is unnecessary and discards configuration. Targeted instruction tuning is the recommended approach.", "Incorrect. The Einstein Trust Layer protects data and is not a setting that improves answer quality; it should not be disabled for this purpose.", "Incorrect. License counts do not affect answer quality.", "Correct. After adjusting instructions, the admin should validate the change in preview and with a Testing Center suite to confirm improvement and avoid regressions.", "Correct. Clear, specific topic instructions guide the agent's reasoning and typically fix vague answers."], "takeaway": "Improve agent behavior by refining topic instructions, then validate with preview and Testing Center before activating."}, {"id": "e73d7489b73e4bf69642db5bfb881f30", "stem": "Alpine Ski House wants to monitor its production agent over time. The administrator needs to see how many conversations the agent handles and spot patterns that suggest where instructions or actions need improvement. Which two capabilities should the administrator use?", "options": ["Sandbox refresh to compare agent versions", "Agentforce analytics, such as the Agentforce Studio analytics dashboards, to review usage trends", "Lightning Usage App to measure agent response accuracy", "Field History Tracking on the User object", "Optimizer to review how topics and requests are grouped and where the agent struggles"], "correct": [1, 4], "explanations": ["Incorrect. A sandbox refresh copies metadata and data to a sandbox and does not provide production usage insights.", "Correct. Agentforce analytics provides usage metrics such as conversation volumes and trends over time.", "Incorrect. Lightning Usage App reports on user adoption of Lightning Experience, not agent response quality.", "Incorrect. Field History Tracking records field value changes and has no relation to agent usage.", "Correct. Optimizer analyzes agent interactions, groups them by topic and surfaces patterns that help the admin find gaps to improve."], "takeaway": "Use Agentforce analytics and Optimizer to monitor live agent usage and find where to refine it."}]}]}], "built": "12/11/2026"};
